-- The Law of Love — Lesson 1: What Love Looks Like (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000894';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $tlol_l1_reader$
{"openingThought":"Everyone talks about love.\n\nBut not everyone defines love the same way.\n\nFor some, love is a feeling.\nFor others, love is attraction.\nFor others, love is approval.\nFor others, love means letting people do whatever they want.\n\nBut Scripture gives love a clearer shape.\n\nLove is not merely something God feels.\n\nLove is who God is.\n\nAnd because God is love, His law shows what love looks like when it becomes action.\n\nThe Ten Commandments are not random restrictions.\n\nThey are a picture of life lived in love toward God and toward others.","scriptureRef":"Matthew 22:37-40","scriptureQuote":"Jesus said unto him, Thou shalt love the Lord thy God with all thy heart, and with all thy soul, and with all thy mind. This is the first and great commandment. And the second is like unto it, Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself. On these two commandments hang all the law and the prophets.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Jesus does not replace the law with vague sentiment.\n\nHe shows what holds it together: love for God and love for neighbor.\n\nThe whole law hangs on these two loves.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"1 John 4:8","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"He that loveth not knoweth not God; for God is love.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Love is not just one quality God has.\n\nLove is the foundation of His character.\n\nEverything God commands must be understood through who He is.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Exodus 20:1-17","quote":"And God spake all these words, saying, I am the LORD thy God, which have brought thee out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondage."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Romans 13:10","quote":"Love worketh no ill to his neighbour: therefore love is the fulfilling of the law."}],"teachingPoints":["1. God is love\n\nFirst John says, \"God is love.\"\n\nThis means love is not just one quality God has.\n\nLove is the foundation of His character.\n\nEverything God commands must be understood through who He is.\n\nIf God is love, then His law is not designed to destroy freedom.\n\nIt is designed to show what love looks like in real life.","2. The law shows love toward God\n\nJesus said the first great commandment is to love God with all the heart, soul, and mind.\n\nThe first part of the Ten Commandments shows what love toward God looks like.\n\nNo rival gods.\nNo false worship.\nNo misuse of His name.\nRemembering the sacred time He set apart.\n\nLove toward God is not vague emotion.\n\nIt becomes loyalty, worship, reverence, and trust.","3. The law shows love toward people\n\nJesus said the second great commandment is to love your neighbor as yourself.\n\nThe last part of the Ten Commandments shows what love toward people looks like.\n\nHonor.\nFaithfulness.\nRespect for life.\nTruthfulness.\nContentment.\nRespect for what belongs to others.\n\nLove does not harm another person.\n\nLove protects relationship.","4. God's law describes the best kind of life\n\nImagine a world where no one lied, stole, cheated, abused, murdered, envied, or betrayed.\n\nImagine every relationship marked by truth, faithfulness, respect, worship, and love.\n\nThat is not bondage.\n\nThat is freedom.\n\nGod's law describes the kind of life human beings were made for."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Law","lemma":"νόμος","transliteration":"nomos","meaning":"Instruction, direction, or revealed will.","strongs":"G3551","reference":"Romans 13:10","note":"God's law is not merely a list of rules.\n\nIt is instruction from the God who is love.\n\nThe law reveals what love looks like vertically toward God and horizontally toward other people."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"Many people misunderstand God's law because they separate it from God's character.\n\nIf God were controlling, His law would be control.\n\nIf God were selfish, His law would be selfish.\n\nBut God is love.\n\nThat means His law reflects love.\n\nJesus makes this clear when He says the whole law hangs on love for God and love for neighbor.\n\nThe law does not replace love.\n\nThe law describes love.\n\nIt shows us that love is not only a feeling.\n\nLove tells the truth.\nLove honors life.\nLove protects trust.\nLove refuses betrayal.\nLove worships God above idols.\nLove respects what belongs to others.\nLove does no harm to a neighbor.\n\nGod's law is love in action."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"Love in two directions","accent":"sage","payload":{"variant":"comparison","leftTitle":"Love toward God","rightTitle":"Love toward others","leftItems":["Worship","Loyalty","Reverence","Sacred time"],"rightItems":["Honor","Faithfulness","Truth","Respect","Contentment"]},"footnote":"The Ten Commandments show love in two directions: toward God and toward people."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Love in Action","references":[{"reference":"1 John 4:8"},{"reference":"Exodus 20:1-17"},{"reference":"Matthew 22:36-40"},{"reference":"Romans 13:10"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: God's law is not opposed to love. It shows what love looks like in action toward God and others."}],"reflectPrompt":"When you hear the word \"law,\" do you tend to think of love, restriction, protection, or pressure?\n\nBe honest. Think about your past experiences with rules, religion, authority, and how those experiences may affect the way you view God's commandments.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, choose one commandment and ask:\n\n\"What does this teach me about love?\"\n\nDo not start with fear.\n\nStart with God's character.\n\nFor example:\n\nTruthfulness teaches love protects trust.\n\nFaithfulness teaches love protects commitment.\n\nWorship teaches love gives God the highest place.\n\nContentment teaches love does not grasp for what belongs to another.\n\nLet God reshape how you see His law.\n\nNot as cold restriction.\n\nAs love made visible.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You that Your law reflects Your heart. Help me see Your commandments through the truth that You are love. Teach me to love You with all my heart and to love others as myself. Remove fear-based religion from my heart and replace it with trust, obedience, and love shaped by Jesus. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"God's law is not random restriction. Because God is love, His commandments show what love looks like in action toward Him and toward others.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"tlol-l1-q1","prompt":"According to 1 John 4:8, what is God?","options":["Love","Confusion","Fear","Selfishness"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"First John says plainly that God is love. His law must be understood in light of His loving character."},{"id":"tlol-l1-q2","prompt":"According to Matthew 22:37-40, what two loves summarize the law?","options":["Love for God and love for neighbor","Love for money and success","Love for power and control","Love for comfort and popularity"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus said the law and the prophets hang on loving God with all the heart and loving your neighbor as yourself."},{"id":"tlol-l1-q3","prompt":"According to Romans 13:10, what is the fulfilling of the law?","options":["Love","Pride","Fear","Control"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Romans 13:10 says love does no harm to a neighbor, therefore love is the fulfilling of the law."},{"id":"tlol-l1-q4","prompt":"What does this lesson teach about the Ten Commandments?","options":["They show what love looks like toward God and people","They are random restrictions with no purpose","They replace the need for love","They are unrelated to God's character"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Ten Commandments reveal love in action vertically toward God and horizontally toward others."},{"id":"tlol-l1-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"Which commandment helps you see love more clearly right now, and why?","explanation":"God's law becomes clearer when we see each command as a picture of love protecting relationship."}]}}
$tlol_l1_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'what-love-looks-like'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007a';

-- The Law of Love — Lesson 2: The Problem Within Us (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000895';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $tlol_l2_reader$
{"openingThought":"If God's law is love in action, then the real problem is not the law.\n\nThe problem is us.\n\nWe may admire love, but we do not naturally live it perfectly.\n\nWe know honesty is good, but we still hide.\nWe know faithfulness matters, but we still drift.\nWe know selfishness damages people, but we still choose ourselves.\nWe know love is right, but we still fall short of it.\n\nThe Bible does not flatter the human heart.\n\nIt tells the truth.\n\nGod's law is good, but sin bends us away from love.","scriptureRef":"Romans 7:12","scriptureQuote":"Wherefore the law is holy, and the commandment holy, and just, and good.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"The law is not the villain.\n\nGod's commandments are not corrupt, selfish, or broken.\n\nThey reveal what is right because they reflect the character of the God who is love.\n\nThe problem is not that God's law is flawed.\n\nThe problem is that sin has damaged us.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Romans 7:14-15","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"For we know that the law is spiritual: but I am carnal, sold under sin. For that which I do I allow not: for what I would, that do I not; but what I hate, that do I.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Paul describes the human struggle honestly.\n\nWe may know what is right and still fail to do it.\n\nWe may hate what is wrong and still fall into it.\n\nThe problem runs deeper than information.\n\nWe do not only need better instructions.\n\nWe need a new heart.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"1 John 3:4","quote":"Whosoever committeth sin transgresseth also the law: for sin is the transgression of the law."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Romans 13:10","quote":"Love worketh no ill to his neighbour: therefore love is the fulfilling of the law."}],"teachingPoints":["1. God's law is holy, just, and good\n\nPaul says the law is holy, just, and good.\n\nThat means the law is not the villain.\n\nGod's commandments are not corrupt, selfish, or broken.\n\nThey reveal what is right because they reflect the character of the God who is love.\n\nThe problem is not that God's law is flawed.\n\nThe problem is that sin has damaged us.","2. Sin is lawlessness\n\nFirst John says sin is the transgression of the law.\n\nIf the law is love in action, then sin is the violation of love.\n\nSin is not merely breaking a rule.\n\nSin is moving against love.\n\nIt damages trust, wounds relationships, distorts worship, and turns the heart inward.","3. We are naturally bent toward self\n\nRomans 7 describes the human struggle honestly.\n\nWe may know what is right and still fail to do it.\n\nWe may hate what is wrong and still fall into it.\n\nThis shows that the problem runs deeper than information.\n\nWe do not only need better instructions.\n\nWe need a new heart.","4. Love cannot be produced by willpower alone\n\nRomans 13 says love fulfills the law.\n\nBut fallen human beings cannot manufacture perfect love from within themselves.\n\nWe can improve behavior temporarily.\n\nWe can manage appearances.\n\nWe can try harder.\n\nBut the kind of love God's law describes requires more than self-effort.\n\nIt requires God's saving grace."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Sin","lemma":"ἁμαρτία","transliteration":"hamartia","meaning":"Transgression of God's law, a violation of love, and a turning away from God's design.","strongs":"G266","reference":"1 John 3:4","note":"Sin is not just bad behavior.\n\nBecause God's law is love, sin is whatever moves against love.\n\nIt bends the heart away from God and toward selfishness."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"Romans 7 helps us stop pretending.\n\nIt shows us that the human problem is not lack of advice.\n\nIt is moral and spiritual brokenness.\n\nGod's law says love.\n\nBut sin says self.\n\nGod's law says truth.\n\nBut sin says hide.\n\nGod's law says faithfulness.\n\nBut sin says take what you want.\n\nGod's law says worship God.\n\nBut sin says worship anything that serves you.\n\nThis is why self-improvement cannot save us.\n\nThe law can reveal what love looks like, but it cannot create love in a heart that is sold under sin.\n\nWe need more than rules.\n\nWe need rescue."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"Law and sin","accent":"stone","payload":{"variant":"comparison","leftTitle":"God's law","rightTitle":"Sin within us","leftItems":["love","truth","faithfulness","worship","life"],"rightItems":["selfishness","hiding","betrayal","idolatry","death"]},"footnote":"The law reveals love. Sin reveals our need for a Savior."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"The Problem Within","references":[{"reference":"Romans 7:12"},{"reference":"Romans 7:14-15"},{"reference":"1 John 3:4"},{"reference":"Romans 13:10"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: God's law is good, but sin bends the human heart away from love. We need more than instruction. We need rescue."}],"reflectPrompt":"Where do you see the struggle of Romans 7 in your own life?\n\nThink about an area where you know what is right, but still struggle to live it consistently.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, be honest about one area where willpower has not been enough.\n\nDo not excuse it.\n\nDo not hide it.\n\nDo not pretend it is smaller than it is.\n\nBring it to God and say:\n\n\"Lord, Your way is good, but I need Your grace to live it.\"\n\nThat is not weakness.\n\nThat is truth.\n\nThe person who admits the problem is finally ready for rescue.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You that Your law is holy, just, and good. Help me stop blaming Your commands for what sin has broken in me. Show me where my heart is bent away from love. I confess that willpower is not enough. I need Your grace, Your Spirit, and Your saving work in Jesus. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"God's law is good, but sin bends the heart away from love. The problem is not God's command. The problem is our need for rescue.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"tlol-l2-q1","prompt":"According to Romans 7:12, how does Paul describe the law?","options":["Holy, just, and good","Evil and useless","Confusing and selfish","Temporary and meaningless"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Paul says the law is holy, and the commandment is holy, just, and good."},{"id":"tlol-l2-q2","prompt":"According to 1 John 3:4, what is sin?","options":["Transgression of the law","A harmless mistake only","A lack of intelligence","Something God ignores completely"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"First John defines sin as transgression of the law. Since the law is love in action, sin violates love."},{"id":"tlol-l2-q3","prompt":"What does Romans 7:14-15 reveal about the human condition?","options":["We can know what is right and still struggle to do it","Humans naturally obey perfectly","God's law is the problem","Sin is easy to overcome without grace"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Paul describes the struggle of wanting to do what is right but still doing what he hates."},{"id":"tlol-l2-q4","prompt":"According to Romans 13:10, what fulfills the law?","options":["Love","Pride","Fear","Self-protection"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Romans 13:10 says love is the fulfilling of the law."},{"id":"tlol-l2-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"Why do you think admitting your need is the first step toward receiving grace?","explanation":"The law reveals what love looks like, but our failure shows our need for Jesus. Honesty opens the heart to rescue."}]}}
$tlol_l2_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'the-problem-within-us'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007a';

-- The Law of Love — Lesson 3: Why We Need a Savior (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000896';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $tlol_l3_reader$
{"openingThought":"The law shows us what love looks like.\n\nBut the law cannot save us.\n\nThat may sound strange at first. If God's law is good, why can't it fix us?\n\nBecause a mirror can show dirt, but it cannot wash your face.\n\nGod's law can reveal where we are out of harmony with love, but it cannot create a new heart by itself.\n\nThe problem is deeper than needing better rules.\n\nWe need rescue.\n\nWe need righteousness outside of ourselves.\n\nWe need a Savior.","scriptureRef":"Romans 7:24-25","scriptureQuote":"O wretched man that I am! who shall deliver me from the body of this death? I thank God through Jesus Christ our Lord.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Paul does not end in despair.\n\nHe cries out for deliverance, then thanks God through Jesus Christ our Lord.\n\nThe law drove him to see his need.\n\nChrist became the answer.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Romans 5:19","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"For as by one man's disobedience many were made sinners, so by the obedience of one shall many be made righteous.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Adam's failure brought brokenness into the human story.\n\nChrist's faithfulness brings righteousness, grace, and life.\n\nWe are not saved by trying to become our own savior.\n\nWe are saved by receiving Jesus as the Savior we need.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Isaiah 64:6","quote":"But we are all as an unclean thing, and all our righteousnesses are as filthy rags; and we all do fade as a leaf; and our iniquities, like the wind, have taken us away."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Jeremiah 23:6","quote":"In his days Judah shall be saved, and Israel shall dwell safely: and this is his name whereby he shall be called, THE LORD OUR RIGHTEOUSNESS."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Knowing the law is not enough\n\nRomans 7 shows a person who knows what is right but still feels trapped by what is wrong.\n\nThat is painfully honest.\n\nInformation can show us the path, but it cannot give us power to walk it.\n\nGod's law can diagnose the problem.\n\nBut diagnosis is not the same as healing.","2. Human righteousness cannot save\n\nIsaiah says our righteousnesses are like filthy rags.\n\nThat does not mean good choices are meaningless.\n\nIt means our best efforts cannot erase sin, heal the heart, or make us righteous before God.\n\nIf salvation depends on our moral record, we are finished.\n\nThat is why the gospel is necessary.","3. Jesus is our righteousness\n\nJeremiah gives the coming Savior a powerful title:\n\n\"The Lord our righteousness.\"\n\nThis means righteousness does not begin inside us.\n\nIt comes to us as a gift from Christ.\n\nJesus lived the life of love we failed to live.\n\nHe becomes the righteousness we could never manufacture.","4. Christ's obedience restores what human disobedience broke\n\nRomans 5 contrasts Adam's disobedience with Christ's obedience.\n\nAdam's failure brought brokenness into the human story.\n\nChrist's faithfulness brings righteousness, grace, and life.\n\nWe are not saved by trying to become our own savior.\n\nWe are saved by receiving Jesus as the Savior we need."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Righteousness","lemma":"δικαιοσύνη","transliteration":"dikaiosynē","meaning":"Right relationship, right standing, and a life aligned with God's love.","strongs":"G1343","reference":"Jeremiah 23:6","note":"Biblical righteousness is not self-made perfection.\n\nIt is received from Christ and then reflected through a transformed life.\n\nJesus is \"The Lord our righteousness\" because He provides what we could never produce on our own."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The moment we understand God's law as love, we also understand why we need Jesus.\n\nThe law says:\n\nLove God completely.\nLove people faithfully.\nTell the truth.\nProtect life.\nHonor relationships.\nLive without selfish grasping.\n\nThen we look at ourselves and realize:\n\nWe have not loved perfectly.\nWe have not trusted fully.\nWe have not lived consistently.\nWe have not kept ourselves pure from selfishness.\n\nSo what do we do?\n\nWe do not lower the law.\n\nWe do not pretend sin is small.\n\nWe come to Christ.\n\nJesus does not erase God's law of love.\n\nHe fulfills it, reveals it, and writes it into the hearts of those who trust Him."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"The law shows the need","accent":"sage","payload":{"variant":"sequence","steps":[{"label":"The law reveals","subtitle":"God's love and our need."},{"label":"Human effort cannot","subtitle":"Erase guilt, heal the heart, or create righteousness."},{"label":"Jesus provides","subtitle":"Forgiveness, righteousness, and new life."}]},"footnote":"The law shows the need. Jesus provides the rescue."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"The Savior We Need","references":[{"reference":"Romans 7:24-25"},{"reference":"Isaiah 64:6"},{"reference":"Jeremiah 23:6"},{"reference":"Romans 5:19"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: God's law reveals our need, but Jesus provides the righteousness and rescue we could never create ourselves."}],"reflectPrompt":"Where have you been trying to become righteous by your own strength instead of receiving Christ as your righteousness?\n\nThink about performance, guilt, comparison, religious image, or trying to prove you are good enough for God.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, stop trying to be your own savior.\n\nThat does not mean stop caring about obedience.\n\nIt means stop making your obedience the foundation of your acceptance.\n\nWhen you see your failure, do not run from God.\n\nRun to Christ.\n\nSay:\n\n\"Jesus, You are my righteousness.\"\n\nThen ask Him to teach you to live from that gift.\n\nNot for approval.\n\nFrom approval.\n\nNot to earn love.\n\nBecause love has already reached you.\n\nPrayer:\n\nJesus, thank You for being the Savior I need. I confess that my own righteousness cannot save me. Thank You for living the life of love I failed to live and for offering me righteousness as a gift. Help me stop trusting my performance and start resting in You. Write Your love into my heart. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"The law reveals our need, but Jesus provides the rescue. We do not become righteous by saving ourselves. Christ is the Lord our righteousness.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"tlol-l3-q1","prompt":"According to Romans 7:24-25, who delivers us?","options":["Jesus Christ our Lord","Our own willpower","Religious image","Human comparison"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Paul cries out for deliverance and then thanks God through Jesus Christ our Lord."},{"id":"tlol-l3-q2","prompt":"According to Isaiah 64:6, how does Scripture describe human righteousness apart from God?","options":["As filthy rags","As perfect enough to save","As greater than Christ's righteousness","As unnecessary to discuss"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Isaiah says our righteousnesses are as filthy rags, showing that human effort cannot save us."},{"id":"tlol-l3-q3","prompt":"What title does Jeremiah 23:6 give to the coming Savior?","options":["The Lord our righteousness","The Lord our confusion","The Lord our fear","The Lord our performance"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jeremiah identifies the Savior as \"The Lord our righteousness,\" showing that righteousness comes from Him."},{"id":"tlol-l3-q4","prompt":"According to Romans 5:19, what comes through Christ's obedience?","options":["Many shall be made righteous","Sin becomes stronger","Grace becomes useless","Humanity saves itself"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Romans 5 contrasts Adam's disobedience with Christ's obedience, through which many are made righteous."},{"id":"tlol-l3-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What would change if you trusted Jesus as your righteousness instead of trying to prove yourself?","explanation":"Jesus provides the righteousness we cannot create. Faith receives His gift and learns to live from His grace."}]}}
$tlol_l3_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'why-we-need-a-savior'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007a';

-- The Law of Love — Lesson 4: Righteousness as a Gift (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000897';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $tlol_l4_reader$
{"openingThought":"Most people think righteousness is something you achieve.\n\nDo enough good.\nAvoid enough bad.\nTry hard enough.\nProve yourself long enough.\n\nBut Scripture presents righteousness differently.\n\nRighteousness is not something sinners manufacture.\n\nIt is something God gives through Jesus.\n\nThat does not make obedience meaningless.\n\nIt puts obedience in the right order.\n\nWe do not become accepted because we finally produce enough righteousness.\n\nWe receive righteousness from Christ, and then His grace begins reshaping the life.","scriptureRef":"Romans 3:24","scriptureQuote":"Being justified freely by his grace through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Freely does not mean cheaply.\n\nIt means we do not purchase it.\n\nJesus paid the cost.\n\nGrace gives what human effort could never earn.\n\nJustification is God's declaration that we are accepted in Christ, not because of our record, but because of His redemption.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Romans 5:17","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"For if by one man's offence death reigned by one; much more they which receive abundance of grace and of the gift of righteousness shall reign in life by one, Jesus Christ.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Righteousness is a gift.\n\nA gift is received, not earned.\n\nIf righteousness came by our performance, it would not be a gift.\n\nIn Christ, God gives us what we lack.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Jeremiah 23:6","quote":"In his days Judah shall be saved, and Israel shall dwell safely: and this is his name whereby he shall be called, THE LORD OUR RIGHTEOUSNESS."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Philippians 3:9","quote":"And be found in him, not having mine own righteousness, which is of the law, but that which is through the faith of Christ, the righteousness which is of God by faith."}],"teachingPoints":["1. We are justified freely by grace\n\nRomans says we are justified freely by God's grace.\n\nFreely does not mean cheaply.\n\nIt means we do not purchase it.\n\nJesus paid the cost.\n\nGrace gives what human effort could never earn.\n\nJustification is God's declaration that we are accepted in Christ, not because of our record, but because of His redemption.","2. Righteousness is a gift\n\nRomans 5 calls righteousness a gift.\n\nThat word matters.\n\nA gift is received, not earned.\n\nIf righteousness came by our performance, it would not be a gift.\n\nIn Christ, God gives us what we lack.\n\nHe covers us with the righteousness of Jesus and begins forming righteousness within us by His Spirit.","3. Jesus is our righteousness\n\nJeremiah calls the promised Savior \"The Lord our righteousness.\"\n\nThis means righteousness is not first a personal achievement.\n\nIt is found in a Person.\n\nJesus is the righteous One.\n\nWhen we trust Him, we stand before God in Him.\n\nOur confidence is not our spiritual résumé.\n\nOur confidence is Christ.","4. Faith receives Christ's righteousness\n\nPaul says he does not want to be found having his own righteousness, but the righteousness that comes through faith.\n\nFaith lets go of self-trust.\n\nFaith stops trying to impress God.\n\nFaith receives Jesus and rests in His righteousness."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Justified","lemma":"δικαιόω","transliteration":"dikaioō","meaning":"Declared right, accepted, or counted righteous before God.","strongs":"G1344","reference":"Romans 3:24","note":"Justification is not God pretending sin does not matter.\n\nIt is God accepting the believer because of Jesus.\n\nThrough Christ's redemption, God gives righteousness as a gift and brings sinners into right standing with Him."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"Righteousness as a gift protects us from two mistakes.\n\nThe first mistake is pride.\n\nPride says, \"I am righteous because I performed well.\"\n\nThe second mistake is despair.\n\nDespair says, \"I can never be righteous because I have failed too much.\"\n\nThe gospel corrects both.\n\nTo pride, the gospel says:\n\nYou did not earn this.\n\nTo despair, the gospel says:\n\nYou can receive this.\n\nChrist is the center.\n\nHe gives righteousness for our standing before God.\n\nThen He works righteousness into our lives through grace and the Spirit.\n\nThe gift comes first.\n\nThe transformation follows."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"Two kinds of righteousness","accent":"stone","payload":{"variant":"comparison","leftTitle":"Self-made righteousness","rightTitle":"Christ's righteousness","leftItems":["performance","pride","fear","insecurity"],"rightItems":["gift","grace","acceptance","transformation"]},"footnote":"Righteousness is received in Christ before it is reflected in the life."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"The Gift of Righteousness","references":[{"reference":"Romans 3:24"},{"reference":"Romans 5:17"},{"reference":"Jeremiah 23:6"},{"reference":"Philippians 3:9"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: God gives righteousness through Jesus. Faith receives the gift, and grace begins transforming the life."}],"reflectPrompt":"Which do you struggle with more: pride in your performance or despair over your failure?\n\nBoth can keep your eyes on self. The gospel calls you to look to Christ as your righteousness.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, practice receiving righteousness as a gift.\n\nWhen you do well, say:\n\n\"Thank You, Jesus. Any good in me is because of Your grace.\"\n\nWhen you fail, say:\n\n\"Jesus, You are still my righteousness. Lead me back into Your grace.\"\n\nDo not let success make you proud.\n\nDo not let failure make you hide.\n\nReturn to Christ either way.\n\nHe is the foundation.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for justifying me freely by Your grace. Thank You for giving righteousness through Jesus instead of leaving me to create it myself. Deliver me from pride when I do well and despair when I fail. Help me receive Christ as my righteousness and live a transformed life through Your Spirit. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"Righteousness is not manufactured by human effort. It is given through Jesus, received by faith, and reflected through a life transformed by grace.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"tlol-l4-q1","prompt":"According to Romans 3:24, how are we justified?","options":["Freely by God's grace through redemption in Christ","By proving we are better than others","By never needing mercy","By ignoring sin"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Romans 3:24 says we are justified freely by His grace through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus."},{"id":"tlol-l4-q2","prompt":"According to Romans 5:17, what does God give?","options":["The gift of righteousness","The gift of fear","The gift of pride","The gift of self-salvation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Romans 5:17 calls righteousness a gift received through Jesus Christ."},{"id":"tlol-l4-q3","prompt":"What title does Jeremiah 23:6 give to the promised Savior?","options":["The Lord our righteousness","The Lord our performance","The Lord our confusion","The Lord our shame"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jeremiah calls the promised Savior \"The Lord our righteousness,\" showing that righteousness is found in Him."},{"id":"tlol-l4-q4","prompt":"What kind of righteousness did Paul desire in Philippians 3:9?","options":["The righteousness through faith in Christ","His own righteousness apart from Christ","Righteousness based on pride","Righteousness without grace"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Paul wanted to be found in Christ, not having his own righteousness, but the righteousness that comes through faith."},{"id":"tlol-l4-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"How can receiving righteousness as a gift change the way you respond to success and failure?","explanation":"The gospel frees us from pride when we succeed and despair when we fail because our standing rests in Christ."}]}}
$tlol_l4_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'righteousness-as-a-gift'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007a';

-- The Law of Love — Lesson 5: The New Covenant (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000898';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $tlol_l5_reader$
{"openingThought":"God's goal was never to leave His law only on stone.\n\nHe wanted His love written in human hearts.\n\nThat is the heart of the new covenant.\n\nThe problem was not that God's law was bad. The problem was that humanity was broken. We needed more than commandments outside of us. We needed transformation inside of us.\n\nSo God promised something deeper.\n\n\"I will put my law in their inward parts.\"\n\nThe new covenant is not God lowering His standard.\n\nIt is God changing the heart so love can be lived from within.","scriptureRef":"Jeremiah 31:33","scriptureQuote":"But this shall be the covenant that I will make with the house of Israel; After those days, saith the LORD, I will put my law in their inward parts, and write it in their hearts; and will be their God, and they shall be my people.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"God is not interested in surface-level religion.\n\nHe wants inward restoration.\n\nThe new covenant deals with the place where motives, desires, thoughts, and choices are formed.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Hebrews 8:10","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"For this is the covenant that I will make with the house of Israel after those days, saith the Lord; I will put my laws into their mind, and write them in their hearts: and I will be to them a God, and they shall be to me a people.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"The new covenant does not say God destroys His law.\n\nIt says He writes it within His people.\n\nGod's law still reflects His character of love.\n\nBut instead of being only an external command, God wants it to become an internal desire.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Romans 7:6","quote":"But now we are delivered from the law, that being dead wherein we were held; that we should serve in newness of spirit, and not in the oldness of the letter."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Ezekiel 36:26-27","quote":"A new heart also will I give you, and a new spirit will I put within you: and I will take away the stony heart out of your flesh, and I will give you an heart of flesh. And I will put my spirit within you, and cause you to walk in my statutes, and ye shall keep my judgments, and do them."}],"teachingPoints":["1. The new covenant reaches the heart\n\nJeremiah says God will write His law in the heart.\n\nThat means God is not interested in surface-level religion.\n\nHe wants inward restoration.\n\nThe new covenant deals with the place where motives, desires, thoughts, and choices are formed.","2. God's law is not removed\n\nThe new covenant does not say God destroys His law.\n\nIt says He writes it within His people.\n\nThat matters.\n\nGod's law still reflects His character of love.\n\nBut instead of being only an external command, God wants it to become an internal desire.","3. The Spirit creates new obedience\n\nRomans says we serve in newness of spirit.\n\nEzekiel says God gives a new heart and puts His Spirit within us.\n\nThis means obedience in the new covenant is not powered by fear-based striving.\n\nIt is the result of God's Spirit working within the heart.","4. Relationship is the goal\n\nGod says, \"I will be their God, and they shall be my people.\"\n\nThe new covenant is not merely about improved behavior.\n\nIt is about restored relationship.\n\nGod writes His law in the heart because He wants His people to live close to Him, shaped by His love."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Covenant","lemma":"διαθήκη","transliteration":"diathēkē","meaning":"A binding relationship promise.","strongs":"G1242","reference":"Jeremiah 31:33","note":"The new covenant is God's promise to restore His people from the inside out.\n\nHe does not merely command love from a distance.\n\nHe writes His law of love in the heart through His Spirit, creating a life that reflects Him."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The new covenant helps us avoid two mistakes.\n\nThe first mistake is thinking God's law no longer matters.\n\nBut God says He writes His law in the heart.\n\nThe second mistake is thinking obedience is produced by human effort alone.\n\nBut God says He gives a new heart and puts His Spirit within us.\n\nThe new covenant is better than both errors.\n\nGod keeps His law of love.\n\nGod transforms the heart.\n\nGod restores relationship.\n\nThis is what grace does.\n\nGrace does not erase love.\n\nGrace writes love deeper."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"Law on the heart","accent":"sage","payload":{"variant":"comparison","leftTitle":"Old way","rightTitle":"New covenant","leftItems":["law outside","heart resistant","obedience by fear"],"rightItems":["law within","heart renewed","obedience by the Spirit"]},"footnote":"God does not lower love. He writes it within."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Written on the Heart","references":[{"reference":"Jeremiah 31:31-34"},{"reference":"Hebrews 8:10"},{"reference":"Ezekiel 36:26-27"},{"reference":"Romans 7:6"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: In the new covenant, God writes His law of love in the heart and creates obedience through the Spirit."}],"reflectPrompt":"Where do you need God to write His love deeper into your heart?\n\nThink about your motives, reactions, relationships, habits, private thoughts, or the places where obedience still feels forced instead of Spirit-led.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, ask God for deeper than behavior change.\n\nAsk for heart change.\n\nPray this simple prayer each day:\n\n\"Lord, write Your love in me.\"\n\nThen pay attention to one area where your heart resists love.\n\nMaybe it is patience.\nMaybe it is honesty.\nMaybe it is forgiveness.\nMaybe it is purity.\nMaybe it is surrender.\n\nDo not only ask, \"What rule am I breaking?\"\n\nAsk, \"Where is God trying to restore love in me?\"\n\nThat is new covenant living.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for the new covenant promise. Thank You for not leaving Your law only outside of me, but for writing Your love in my heart. Give me a new heart and renew my spirit. Teach me to serve in newness of spirit, not fear-based religion. Make my life reflect the love of Jesus. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"The new covenant does not erase God's law. It brings His law of love into the heart through the Spirit, creating a life changed from within.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"tlol-l5-q1","prompt":"According to Jeremiah 31:33, where does God promise to write His law?","options":["In the heart","On Roman coins","In the clouds only","On city walls only"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jeremiah says God will put His law in His people's inward parts and write it in their hearts."},{"id":"tlol-l5-q2","prompt":"What does the new covenant show about God's law?","options":["God wants His law written within His people","God's law was evil","God's law has no connection to love","God removed all desire for obedience"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The new covenant does not remove God's law. It writes His law of love in the heart."},{"id":"tlol-l5-q3","prompt":"According to Ezekiel 36:26-27, what does God promise to give?","options":["A new heart and His Spirit","A reason to hide from Him","A life without transformation","A lawless heart"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Ezekiel says God will give a new heart and put His Spirit within His people."},{"id":"tlol-l5-q4","prompt":"According to Romans 7:6, how are believers called to serve?","options":["In newness of spirit","In pride and fear","In oldness without love","In self-salvation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Romans says believers serve in newness of spirit, showing that the new life is Spirit-led."},{"id":"tlol-l5-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What would change if God's law felt less like pressure outside of you and more like love being written within you?","explanation":"The new covenant is about inward transformation. God writes His love in the heart so obedience flows from restored relationship."}]}}
