update public.lessons
set reader_content = $mnic_l2_reader$
{"openingThought":"The gospel does not begin with your progress.\n\nIt begins with Christ's accomplishment.\n\nBefore you prayed perfectly, Jesus had already come.\n\nBefore you understood grace, Jesus had already died.\n\nBefore you cleaned yourself up, Jesus had already risen.\n\nBefore you could earn anything, salvation had already been accomplished in Him.\n\nThat is why the gospel is so different from self-improvement.\n\nSelf-improvement says, \"Become something.\"\n\nThe gospel says, \"Receive what Christ has already done, then become who you are in Him.\"","scriptureRef":"Ephesians 2:4","scriptureQuote":"But God, who is rich in mercy, for his great love wherewith he loved us, even when we were dead in sins, hath quickened us together with Christ.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Salvation begins with God's initiative.\n\nWe did not climb up to Him.\n\nHe came down to us.\n\nThe gospel starts with mercy, not human achievement.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Ephesians 2:5-6","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"By grace ye are saved. And hath raised us up together, and made us sit together in heavenly places in Christ Jesus.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Grace saves.\n\nChrist raises.\n\nBelievers are seated with Him in heavenly places.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Ephesians 1:7","quote":"In whom we have redemption through his blood, the forgiveness of sins, according to the riches of his grace."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"2 Corinthians 5:14-15","quote":"For the love of Christ constraineth us; because we thus judge, that if one died for all, then were all dead: And that he died for all, that they which live should not henceforth live unto themselves, but unto him which died for them, and rose again."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"1 Corinthians 15:21-22","quote":"For since by man came death, by man came also the resurrection of the dead. For as in Adam all die, even so in Christ shall all be made alive."}],"teachingPoints":["1. God acted while we were still dead in sin\n\nEphesians says God made us alive with Christ while we were dead in sins.\n\nThat means salvation begins with God's initiative.\n\nWe did not climb up to Him.\n\nHe came down to us.\n\nThe gospel starts with mercy, not human achievement.","2. Redemption is already in Christ\n\nEphesians says we have redemption through His blood.\n\nRedemption is not something we manufacture.\n\nIt is something already accomplished in Jesus.\n\nHis blood secured forgiveness.\n\nHis grace opened the way.\n\nFaith receives what Christ has done.","3. Jesus represented humanity\n\nPaul says that if One died for all, then all died.\n\nThis means Jesus did not die as a private individual only.\n\nHe died as our representative.\n\nHe entered our humanity, stood in our place, and acted on behalf of the human race.\n\nWhat He accomplished matters for us because He did it for us.","4. Christ gives a new humanity\n\nIn Adam, humanity fell into sin and death.\n\nIn Christ, humanity receives life and restoration.\n\nJesus is not only a helper for broken people.\n\nHe is the beginning of a new humanity.\n\nIn Him, God starts the human story again."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Redemption","lemma":"ἀπολύτρωσις","transliteration":"apolutrosis","meaning":"Rescue, release, or deliverance through a paid cost.","strongs":"G629","reference":"Ephesians 1:7","note":"Redemption means Jesus has already paid the cost to rescue us.\n\nWe are not trying to create redemption through good behavior.\n\nWe receive redemption through faith in Christ, whose blood secured forgiveness and freedom."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The gospel is built on what Jesus has already accomplished.\n\nHe became human.\n\nHe lived faithfully.\n\nHe died for our sins.\n\nHe rose from the dead.\n\nHe ascended in victory.\n\nHe represents a new humanity before the Father.\n\nThat means salvation is not hanging in uncertainty waiting for you to complete it.\n\nJesus has already accomplished the saving work.\n\nYour response matters deeply, but your response does not create the gospel.\n\nFaith says yes to the reality already established in Christ.\n\nThis is why grace produces security.\n\nNot laziness.\n\nSecurity.\n\nBecause the foundation is not your spiritual mood, your best week, or your ability to perform.\n\nThe foundation is Jesus."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"What Jesus has already done","accent":"sage","description":"Salvation rests on what Jesus has already accomplished.","payload":{"variant":"sequence","steps":[{"label":"Christ came","subtitle":"God entered humanity in mercy."},{"label":"Christ lived faithfully","subtitle":"Jesus fulfilled the life we could not live."},{"label":"Christ died for our sins","subtitle":"He stood in our place at the cross."},{"label":"Christ rose again","subtitle":"Death could not hold the Lord of life."},{"label":"Christ represents a new humanity","subtitle":"In Him, God begins the human story again."}]},"footnote":"Salvation rests on what Jesus has already done."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Already Accomplished in Christ","references":[{"reference":"Ephesians 1:7"},{"reference":"Ephesians 2:4-6"},{"reference":"2 Corinthians 5:14-15"},{"reference":"1 Corinthians 15:21-22"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Jesus accomplished salvation before we responded. Faith receives and rests in what is already true in Christ."}],"reflectPrompt":"Where do you struggle to believe that Jesus has already accomplished what you could never earn?\n\nThink about forgiveness, acceptance, identity, salvation, or the pressure to prove yourself to God.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, practice starting with Christ's finished work.\n\nWhen you feel spiritually behind, say:\n\n\"Jesus has already accomplished salvation.\"\n\nWhen you feel unworthy, say:\n\n\"Redemption is in Christ, not in my performance.\"\n\nWhen you feel pressure to prove yourself, say:\n\n\"I respond by faith to what Jesus has already done.\"\n\nThen take one faithful step from that place.\n\nRead Scripture.\nConfess honestly.\nPray simply.\nServe someone.\nObey the next truth God shows you.\n\nNot to earn salvation.\n\nBecause grace has already reached you.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for what Jesus has already finished. Thank You that salvation rests on His life, death, resurrection, and victory, not on my ability to prove myself. Help me receive redemption by faith. Teach me to live from Christ's accomplishment with humility, confidence, and gratitude. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"Jesus has already accomplished salvation through His life, death, resurrection, and victory. Faith receives what grace has already provided in Christ.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"mnic-l2-q1","prompt":"According to Ephesians 2:4-6, what did God do while we were dead in sins?","options":["Made us alive together with Christ","Waited for us to become perfect","Ignored humanity forever","Told us to save ourselves"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Ephesians says God made us alive together with Christ while we were dead in sins, showing salvation begins with mercy."},{"id":"mnic-l2-q2","prompt":"According to Ephesians 1:7, where do we have redemption?","options":["In Christ","In self-improvement","In human pride","In religious comparison"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Ephesians 1:7 says we have redemption through Christ's blood, according to the riches of His grace."},{"id":"mnic-l2-q3","prompt":"What does 2 Corinthians 5:14-15 teach about Jesus' death?","options":["He died for all","He died only as an example with no saving effect","His death did not matter","Humanity must replace His work"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Paul says One died for all, showing Christ's representative death on behalf of humanity."},{"id":"mnic-l2-q4","prompt":"What contrast does 1 Corinthians 15 make?","options":["In Adam all die, in Christ all shall be made alive","Adam saves and Christ destroys","Death is stronger than resurrection","Sin has no consequence"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Paul contrasts Adam and Christ. In Adam came death, but in Christ comes resurrection and life."},{"id":"mnic-l2-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"How would your faith change if you trusted Christ's accomplishment more than your spiritual performance?","explanation":"The gospel rests on what Jesus has already done. Faith receives His finished work and lives from grace."}]}}
$mnic_l2_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'what-jesus-already-finished'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000079';

-- Made New in Christ — Lesson 3: The New Representative (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000889';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $mnic_l3_reader$
{"openingThought":"Every human story begins somewhere.\n\nThe Bible says our fallen story is connected to Adam.\n\nThrough Adam, humanity entered a broken condition marked by sin, guilt, and death.\n\nBut the gospel does not leave us there.\n\nJesus came as the new representative of humanity.\n\nHe did what Adam failed to do.\nHe lived the faithful life humanity was meant to live.\nHe died the death sin demanded.\nHe rose into the life God always intended for us.\n\nIn Adam, humanity fell.\n\nIn Christ, humanity is restored.","scriptureRef":"1 Corinthians 15:22","scriptureQuote":"For as in Adam all die, even so in Christ shall all be made alive.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Paul places every person in one of two stories.\n\nIn Adam, death.\n\nIn Christ, life.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"1 Corinthians 15:45","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"The first man Adam was made a living soul; the last Adam was made a quickening spirit.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Adam stands at the head of the fallen story.\n\nChrist stands at the head of the restored story.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Romans 5:19","quote":"For as by one man's disobedience many were made sinners, so by the obedience of one shall many be made righteous."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"1 Corinthians 15:49","quote":"And as we have borne the image of the earthy, we shall also bear the image of the heavenly."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Adam represents fallen humanity\n\nAdam stands at the head of the human story in its fallen condition.\n\nThrough him, sin and death entered human experience.\n\nThis does not mean we blame Adam while ignoring our own choices.\n\nIt means the Bible sees humanity as connected.\n\nWe are born into a broken world and a damaged human condition.","2. Jesus is the last Adam\n\nPaul calls Jesus the last Adam.\n\nThat title matters.\n\nJesus came to begin a new humanity.\n\nHe entered our human story, but He did not repeat Adam's failure.\n\nWhere Adam distrusted God, Jesus trusted.\nWhere Adam disobeyed, Jesus obeyed.\nWhere Adam brought death, Jesus brings life.","3. Christ's obedience restores what Adam's disobedience damaged\n\nRomans 5 contrasts Adam's disobedience with Christ's obedience.\n\nAdam's failure affected humanity.\n\nChrist's faithfulness is greater.\n\nJesus does not merely patch the old life.\n\nHe becomes the source of a restored life.\n\nIn Him, righteousness, grace, and life are offered.","4. We are invited to identify with Christ\n\nThe gospel calls us to move our identity from Adam to Christ.\n\nWe no longer have to be defined by the old fallen story.\n\nIn Christ, we receive a new representative, a new identity, and a new future.\n\nWe have borne the image of the earthly.\n\nGod now calls us to bear the image of the heavenly."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Representative","lemma":"ἔσχατος Ἀδάμ","transliteration":"eschatos Adam","meaning":"One who stands in the place of others and acts on their behalf.","strongs":"G2078","reference":"1 Corinthians 15:45","note":"Jesus represents humanity before God.\n\nHe did not come only to give advice from the outside.\n\nHe entered our human nature, lived faithfully, died sacrificially, and rose victoriously as the new representative head of humanity."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The gospel is bigger than personal improvement.\n\nIt is a transfer of identity.\n\nIn Adam:\nsin, guilt, death, and brokenness mark the old human story.\n\nIn Christ:\ngrace, righteousness, life, and restoration define the new human story.\n\nThis is why salvation is not merely about trying harder.\n\nYou need more than a better mood, better habits, or better intentions.\n\nYou need a new representative.\n\nJesus becomes that representative.\n\nHe stands where Adam fell and succeeds where humanity failed.\n\nFaith means identifying with Him, trusting His victory, and receiving the new life He gives."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"From Adam to Christ","accent":"stone","payload":{"variant":"comparison","leftTitle":"In Adam","rightTitle":"In Christ","leftItems":["fall","sin","death","old identity"],"rightItems":["grace","righteousness","life","new identity"]},"footnote":"Jesus is the new representative of humanity."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"From Adam to Christ","references":[{"reference":"1 Corinthians 15:21-22"},{"reference":"1 Corinthians 15:45-49"},{"reference":"Romans 5:17-19"},{"reference":"2 Corinthians 5:17"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Adam represents humanity's fall, but Jesus represents humanity's restoration. In Christ, a new human story begins."}],"reflectPrompt":"Where do you still define yourself by the old story instead of your new identity in Christ?\n\nThink about guilt, shame, failure, family patterns, old habits, fear, or the belief that you can never really change.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, practice identifying with Christ.\n\nWhen the old story speaks, answer it with the gospel.\n\nOld story:\n\"I am defined by my failure.\"\n\nGospel truth:\n\"I am in Christ, and He is making me new.\"\n\nOld story:\n\"I can never change.\"\n\nGospel truth:\n\"Jesus is the source of a new life.\"\n\nOld story:\n\"Sin and death have the final word.\"\n\nGospel truth:\n\"In Christ, I am made alive.\"\n\nDo not build your identity on Adam's failure or your own record.\n\nBuild it on Christ.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for sending Jesus as the new representative of humanity. Thank You that I do not have to be defined by the old story of sin, guilt, and death. Help me identify with Christ by faith. Restore Your image in me and teach me to live from the new life You have given in Him. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"Adam represents the old fallen story, but Jesus begins a new one. In Christ, humanity receives a new representative, a new identity, and a restored future.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"mnic-l3-q1","prompt":"According to 1 Corinthians 15:22, what happens in Adam and in Christ?","options":["In Adam all die, in Christ all shall be made alive","In Adam all live, in Christ all are lost","Adam and Christ do the same work","Humanity does not need Christ"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Paul says that in Adam all die, but in Christ all shall be made alive."},{"id":"mnic-l3-q2","prompt":"What title does 1 Corinthians 15:45 give to Jesus?","options":["The last Adam","The first sinner","The hidden prophet","The second Pharaoh"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Paul calls Jesus the last Adam, showing that Christ becomes the new representative head of humanity."},{"id":"mnic-l3-q3","prompt":"According to Romans 5:19, what comes through Christ's obedience?","options":["Many shall be made righteous","Sin becomes stronger","Death wins forever","Grace becomes unnecessary"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Romans 5:19 contrasts Adam's disobedience with Christ's obedience, through which many are made righteous."},{"id":"mnic-l3-q4","prompt":"What does this lesson teach about salvation?","options":["Salvation includes receiving a new identity in Christ","Salvation is only self-improvement","Salvation means Adam saves us","Salvation removes the need for Jesus"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The gospel gives us more than self-improvement. It gives us a new representative and a new identity in Christ."},{"id":"mnic-l3-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What part of your old identity do you need to surrender to your new identity in Christ?","explanation":"Jesus becomes the new representative of humanity, and faith means learning to live from who we are in Him."}]}}
$mnic_l3_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'the-new-representative'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000079';

-- Made New in Christ — Lesson 4: All Things New (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000890';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $mnic_l4_reader$
{"openingThought":"The gospel does not only change your future.\n\nIt changes your whole life.\n\nWhen a person comes into Christ, God does more than adjust behavior. He begins a new creation.\n\nOld ways of seeing God begin to change.\nOld ways of seeing yourself begin to change.\nOld ways of seeing people begin to change.\nOld motives begin to lose their power.\n\nThe love of Christ does not leave the heart untouched.\n\nIt reconciles.\nIt restores.\nIt renews.\n\nIn Jesus, God begins making all things new.","scriptureRef":"2 Corinthians 5:17","scriptureQuote":"Therefore if any man be in Christ, he is a new creature: old things are passed away; behold, all things are become new.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"This does not mean believers instantly become flawless.\n\nIt means a new reality has begun.\n\nThe old life no longer has final authority.\n\nGod is creating a new life through union with Jesus.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"2 Corinthians 5:14","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"For the love of Christ constraineth us.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Christ's love becomes the power that moves the life.\n\nFear may pressure people.\nGuilt may push people.\nPride may perform for people.\n\nBut the gospel creates a better motive: love received from Jesus.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"2 Corinthians 5:18-19","quote":"And all things are of God, who hath reconciled us to himself by Jesus Christ, and hath given to us the ministry of reconciliation; To wit, that God was in Christ, reconciling the world unto himself, not imputing their trespasses unto them."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Galatians 2:20","quote":"I am crucified with Christ: nevertheless I live; yet not I, but Christ liveth in me: and the life which I now live in the flesh I live by the faith of the Son of God, who loved me, and gave himself for me."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Colossians 1:13-14","quote":"Who hath delivered us from the power of darkness, and hath translated us into the kingdom of his dear Son: In whom we have redemption through his blood, even the forgiveness of sins."}],"teachingPoints":["1. In Christ, a new creation begins\n\nPaul says that if anyone is in Christ, he is a new creature.\n\nThis does not mean believers instantly become flawless.\n\nIt means a new reality has begun.\n\nThe old life no longer has final authority.\n\nGod is creating a new life through union with Jesus.","2. The love of Christ becomes the new motive\n\nPaul says the love of Christ constrains us.\n\nThat means Christ's love becomes the power that moves the life.\n\nFear may pressure people.\n\nGuilt may push people.\n\nPride may perform for people.\n\nBut the gospel creates a better motive: love received from Jesus.","3. God reconciles us to Himself\n\nReconciliation means the broken relationship is restored.\n\nIn Christ, God is not holding our sins over us as if He is looking for a reason to reject us.\n\nHe is bringing us back to Himself.\n\nThe gospel announces that God has acted in Jesus to restore relationship.","4. New life changes how we see people\n\nPaul says we no longer regard people according to the flesh.\n\nThat means the gospel changes our vision.\n\nWe stop reducing people to their past, status, weakness, appearance, usefulness, or failure.\n\nWe begin seeing people through the possibility of reconciliation in Christ."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Reconciliation","lemma":"καταλλαγή","transliteration":"katallagē","meaning":"The restoration of a broken relationship.","strongs":"G2643","reference":"2 Corinthians 5:18","note":"Sin broke humanity's relationship with God.\n\nIn Christ, God reconciles us to Himself.\n\nThis means the gospel is not only about legal forgiveness. It is about restored relationship, renewed identity, and a new way of living with God and others."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"Second Corinthians 5 shows how deeply the gospel transforms a person.\n\nChrist died for all.\n\nThat means we no longer live for ourselves.\n\nGod reconciled us to Himself.\n\nThat means we no longer live as spiritual outsiders.\n\nChrist makes us new.\n\nThat means the old life does not get the final word.\n\nThis is not self-improvement with Bible language.\n\nIt is new creation.\n\nGod's love reaches the heart, changes the center, and begins reshaping the whole life from the inside out.\n\nThe gospel does not merely tell us to become better people.\n\nIt brings us into Christ, where new life begins."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"A new life begins","accent":"sage","payload":{"variant":"comparison","leftTitle":"Before","rightTitle":"In Christ","leftItems":["self-centered living","guilt","old identity","broken relationship"],"rightItems":["new creation","reconciliation","forgiveness","love-driven life"]},"footnote":"The gospel does not just improve the old life. It begins a new one."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"All Things New","references":[{"reference":"2 Corinthians 5:14-19"},{"reference":"Galatians 2:20"},{"reference":"Colossians 1:13-14"},{"reference":"Romans 8:1"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: In Christ, God reconciles us to Himself, gives us a new identity, and begins making the whole life new."}],"reflectPrompt":"What part of your life most needs to be made new by the love of Christ?\n\nThink about your motives, relationships, self-image, habits, fears, or the way you see other people.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, watch what is motivating you.\n\nAsk:\n\n\"Am I being moved by fear, guilt, pride, or the love of Christ?\"\n\nWhen you notice the old motive, pause and return to the gospel.\n\nSay:\n\n\"Jesus, let Your love become the motive here.\"\n\nThen choose one response that reflects new creation.\n\nSpeak with grace.\nForgive instead of rehearsing offense.\nServe without needing applause.\nTell the truth without hiding.\nPray instead of spiraling.\n\nNew creation is practiced in real moments.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for reconciling me to Yourself through Jesus. Thank You that in Christ, old things do not have the final word. Let the love of Christ shape my motives, relationships, thoughts, and actions. Make me new from the inside out, and teach me to live as someone restored by grace. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"In Christ, God begins a new creation. The gospel reconciles us to God, changes our motives, and teaches us to live from love instead of fear.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"mnic-l4-q1","prompt":"According to 2 Corinthians 5:17, what is true of anyone in Christ?","options":["They are a new creature","They must save themselves","Nothing can change","They are beyond grace"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Paul says that anyone in Christ is a new creature and that old things are passed away."},{"id":"mnic-l4-q2","prompt":"According to 2 Corinthians 5:14, what constrains or moves the believer?","options":["The love of Christ","Fear of people","Pride in self","Confusion about God"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Paul says the love of Christ constrains us, meaning Christ's love becomes the new motive of life."},{"id":"mnic-l4-q3","prompt":"What does reconciliation mean?","options":["Restoration of a broken relationship","Ignoring sin forever","Trying harder without grace","Hiding from God"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Reconciliation means a broken relationship is restored. In Christ, God reconciles us to Himself."},{"id":"mnic-l4-q4","prompt":"According to Colossians 1:13-14, what has God done for us in Christ?","options":["Delivered us from darkness and given redemption and forgiveness","Left us in darkness","Removed the need for grace","Told us to earn forgiveness alone"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Colossians says God has delivered us from the power of darkness and that in Christ we have redemption and forgiveness."},{"id":"mnic-l4-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"Where do you need the love of Christ to replace fear, guilt, or pride as your motive?","explanation":"The gospel creates a new life by changing the heart's center. Christ's love becomes the motive for how we live."}]}}
$mnic_l4_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'all-things-new'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000079';

-- Made New in Christ — Lesson 5: Faith Receives the Facts (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000891';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $mnic_l5_reader$
{"openingThought":"Faith does not make the gospel true.\n\nThe gospel is already true because of what Jesus has done.\n\nFaith receives it.\n\nThat matters because many people treat faith like a spiritual performance. They think faith means feeling strong enough, believing perfectly enough, or reaching a certain emotional level.\n\nBut biblical faith is not about producing salvation.\n\nFaith rests in salvation already accomplished in Christ.\n\nJesus lived.\nJesus died.\nJesus rose.\nJesus saves.\n\nFaith says, \"I trust Him.\"","scriptureRef":"Galatians 2:20","scriptureQuote":"I live by the faith of the Son of God, who loved me, and gave himself for me.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Faith is not vague positivity.\n\nFaith has an object.\n\nIt rests in Jesus.\n\nThe heart of faith is trusting the One who loved us enough to give Himself for us.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"2 Timothy 1:9-10","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"Who hath saved us, and called us with an holy calling, not according to our works, but according to his own purpose and grace, which was given us in Christ Jesus before the world began, But is now made manifest by the appearing of our Saviour Jesus Christ, who hath abolished death, and hath brought life and immortality to light through the gospel.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"God saved us, not according to our works.\n\nOur effort does not purchase acceptance.\n\nOur obedience does not force God to love us.\n\nSalvation begins with God's grace in Christ.\n\nChrist abolished death and brought life and immortality to light through the gospel.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Titus 3:5","quote":"Not by works of righteousness which we have done, but according to his mercy he saved us, by the washing of regeneration, and renewing of the Holy Ghost."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Titus 3:7","quote":"That being justified by his grace, we should be made heirs according to the hope of eternal life."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Faith rests in Christ's love\n\nPaul says he lives by faith in the Son of God, who loved him and gave Himself for him.\n\nFaith is not vague positivity.\n\nFaith has an object.\n\nIt rests in Jesus.\n\nThe heart of faith is trusting the One who loved us enough to give Himself for us.","2. Salvation is not according to our works\n\nSecond Timothy says God saved us, not according to our works.\n\nThat is direct.\n\nOur works do not create salvation.\n\nOur effort does not purchase acceptance.\n\nOur obedience does not force God to love us.\n\nSalvation begins with God's grace in Christ.","3. Faith receives mercy\n\nTitus says God saved us according to His mercy.\n\nMercy comes before spiritual achievement.\n\nThis is hard for pride to accept and hard for shame to believe.\n\nPride wants to earn.\n\nShame assumes it is too late.\n\nFaith receives what mercy gives.","4. Grace gives us a new inheritance\n\nTitus says we are justified by grace and made heirs according to the hope of eternal life.\n\nAn heir receives because of relationship, not because they built the inheritance themselves.\n\nIn Christ, believers receive a new standing and a new future.\n\nFaith receives the facts of grace and begins to live from them."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Faith","lemma":"πίστις","transliteration":"pistis","meaning":"Trust, confidence, reliance, or dependence.","strongs":"G4102","reference":"Galatians 2:20","note":"Faith is not spiritual self-confidence.\n\nFaith is confidence in Jesus.\n\nIt does not create the facts of the gospel. It receives them, rests in them, and begins to live from them."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"Faith is often misunderstood.\n\nSome people think faith is a feeling.\n\nOthers think faith is mental pressure.\n\nOthers think faith is pretending to be certain when they are not.\n\nBut biblical faith is simpler and deeper.\n\nFaith trusts Jesus.\n\nFaith receives what grace has already provided.\n\nFaith says:\n\n\"Christ loved me.\"\n\"Christ gave Himself for me.\"\n\"Christ saved me by mercy.\"\n\"Christ abolished death.\"\n\"Christ gives eternal life.\"\n\nFaith does not stare at itself to see if it is strong enough.\n\nFaith looks to Jesus.\n\nThat is where peace begins."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"What faith is","accent":"stone","payload":{"variant":"comparison","leftTitle":"Weak view of faith","rightTitle":"Biblical faith","leftItems":["I must believe hard enough to make God accept me."],"rightItems":["I trust what Jesus has already done by grace."]},"footnote":"Faith receives. Grace provides."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Faith Receives Grace","references":[{"reference":"Galatians 2:20"},{"reference":"2 Timothy 1:9-10"},{"reference":"Titus 3:5-7"},{"reference":"Ephesians 2:8-9"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Faith does not earn salvation. Faith receives and rests in the mercy, grace, and finished work of Jesus."}],"reflectPrompt":"Where do you tend to make faith about your strength instead of Christ's finished work?\n\nThink about doubt, guilt, emotional pressure, trying to believe perfectly, or feeling like your faith is never enough.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, stop measuring faith by how strong you feel.\n\nMeasure it by where you are looking.\n\nWhen you feel weak, look to Jesus.\n\nWhen you feel uncertain, look to Jesus.\n\nWhen guilt says your faith is not enough, answer:\n\n\"My hope is not the strength of my faith. My hope is the strength of my Savior.\"\n\nThen practice a simple faith statement:\n\n\"Jesus, I trust what You have done.\"\n\nSay it when you pray.\n\nSay it when you feel anxious.\n\nSay it when shame gets loud.\n\nFaith receives the facts of grace one moment at a time.\n\nPrayer:\n\nJesus, help me trust You more than I trust my feelings. Thank You for loving me and giving Yourself for me. Thank You that salvation rests on Your mercy, not my performance. Teach me to receive grace by faith and live from what You have already done. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"Faith does not create salvation. Faith receives what grace has already provided in Jesus and learns to rest in His finished work.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"mnic-l5-q1","prompt":"According to Galatians 2:20, who loved Paul and gave Himself for him?","options":["The Son of God","Adam","Caesar","Moses"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Paul says he lives by faith in the Son of God, who loved him and gave Himself for him."},{"id":"mnic-l5-q2","prompt":"According to 2 Timothy 1:9, how has God saved us?","options":["Not according to our works","By our perfect record","By our natural strength","By comparison with others"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Second Timothy says God saved us and called us, not according to our works."},{"id":"mnic-l5-q3","prompt":"According to Titus 3:5, why did God save us?","options":["According to His mercy","According to our works of righteousness","According to our popularity","According to our ability to impress Him"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Titus says God saved us according to His mercy, not by works of righteousness we have done."},{"id":"mnic-l5-q4","prompt":"What does faith do according to this lesson?","options":["Receives and rests in what grace has provided","Creates salvation by emotional effort","Replaces Jesus","Makes works unnecessary forever"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Faith receives what Christ has already accomplished. It rests in grace rather than trying to earn salvation."},{"id":"mnic-l5-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"How can you practice looking to Jesus when your faith feels weak?","explanation":"Faith is not confidence in itself. Faith is confidence in Jesus, His mercy, and His finished work."}]}}
$mnic_l5_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'faith-receives-the-facts'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000079';

-- Made New in Christ — Lesson 6: Grace Trains the Life (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000892';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $mnic_l6_reader$
{"openingThought":"Grace does not only forgive.\n\nGrace teaches.\n\nSome people hear \"saved by grace\" and think obedience no longer matters.\n\nBut Scripture says the opposite.\n\nGrace saves us from trying to earn God's love, but it also trains us to live differently.\n\nThe gospel does not leave us trapped in the old life.\n\nIt creates a new one.\n\nGood works do not make God accept us.\n\nBut once we receive grace, our lives begin to change because grace has reached the heart.","scriptureRef":"Titus 2:11-12","scriptureQuote":"For the grace of God that bringeth salvation hath appeared to all men, Teaching us that, denying ungodliness and worldly lusts, we should live soberly, righteously, and godly, in this present world.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Salvation begins with God's gift, not human achievement.\n\nGrace comes first.\n\nThe same grace that saves also teaches.\n\nGrace trains us to deny ungodliness and live in a way that reflects God.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Ephesians 2:10","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"For we are his workmanship, created in Christ Jesus unto good works, which God hath before ordained that we should walk in them.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Good works are not the root of salvation.\n\nThey are the fruit of salvation.\n\nGod does not save us because of our works.\n\nHe saves us and then begins shaping our lives for love, service, honesty, purity, justice, and mercy.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Philippians 2:13","quote":"For it is God which worketh in you both to will and to do of his good pleasure."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Titus 2:14","quote":"Who gave himself for us, that he might redeem us from all iniquity, and purify unto himself a peculiar people, zealous of good works."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Grace brings salvation\n\nTitus says the grace of God brings salvation.\n\nThat means salvation begins with God's gift, not human achievement.\n\nGrace comes first.\n\nBefore we can live differently, we must receive what Jesus has done.\n\nThe new life grows from grace, not from fear.","2. Grace teaches us how to live\n\nThe same grace that saves also teaches.\n\nGrace trains us to deny ungodliness and live in a way that reflects God.\n\nThis is not legalism.\n\nLegalism tries to earn acceptance.\n\nGrace-based obedience grows because we have already been accepted in Christ.","3. We are created for good works\n\nEphesians says we are God's workmanship, created in Christ Jesus unto good works.\n\nGood works are not the root of salvation.\n\nThey are the fruit of salvation.\n\nGod does not save us because of our works.\n\nHe saves us and then begins shaping our lives for love, service, honesty, purity, justice, and mercy.","4. God works in us\n\nPhilippians says God works in us both to will and to do His good pleasure.\n\nThis means transformation is not self-powered.\n\nGod changes desires.\n\nGod strengthens obedience.\n\nGod forms a new life within us.\n\nWe cooperate, but He supplies the power."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Train","lemma":"παιδεύω","transliteration":"paideuō","meaning":"To teach, discipline, guide, or form over time.","strongs":"G3811","reference":"Titus 2:12","note":"Grace is not passive.\n\nGrace trains the life.\n\nIt teaches the heart to turn away from what destroys and walk in what reflects God's love.\n\nThe grace that saves also shapes."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The order of the gospel matters.\n\nNot:\n\nObey so God will save you.\n\nBut:\n\nGod saves you by grace, then grace teaches you how to live.\n\nThat order protects us from two dangerous errors.\n\nThe first error is legalism.\n\nLegalism says, \"My obedience earns God's acceptance.\"\n\nThe second error is careless grace.\n\nCareless grace says, \"Since I am forgiven, obedience does not matter.\"\n\nThe gospel rejects both.\n\nGrace saves freely.\n\nGrace transforms deeply.\n\nJesus gave Himself not only to forgive us, but to redeem us from iniquity and purify a people who reflect His character."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"How grace shapes the life","accent":"sage","payload":{"variant":"sequence","steps":[{"label":"Grace received","subtitle":"Salvation begins with God's gift in Christ."},{"label":"Heart renewed","subtitle":"Grace reaches the center of life."},{"label":"Desires changed","subtitle":"God begins reshaping what we want."},{"label":"Life trained","subtitle":"Grace teaches us to deny ungodliness and live godly."},{"label":"Good works flow","subtitle":"Obedience grows as fruit, not as payment."}]},"footnote":"Grace saves first, then grace shapes the life."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Grace That Trains","references":[{"reference":"Titus 2:11-14"},{"reference":"Ephesians 2:10"},{"reference":"Philippians 2:12-13"},{"reference":"John 15:5"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Grace does not earn salvation through works. Grace saves us in Christ and then trains us to live a new life."}],"reflectPrompt":"Which error are you more tempted toward: trying to earn God's acceptance or treating grace like obedience does not matter?\n\nBe honest. Some people live under pressure. Others avoid surrender. The gospel corrects both by showing that grace saves and grace trains.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, choose one area where grace needs to train you.\n\nNot shame.\n\nGrace.\n\nAsk:\n\n\"Lord, what are You teaching me to deny?\"\n\nThen ask:\n\n\"What are You teaching me to become?\"\n\nMaybe grace is training your speech.\nMaybe it is training your habits.\nMaybe it is training your motives.\nMaybe it is training your relationships.\nMaybe it is training how you use your time.\n\nDo not obey to become loved.\n\nObey because you are loved in Christ.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for the grace that saves and the grace that trains. Keep me from trying to earn Your acceptance, and keep me from treating obedience lightly. Work in me both to will and to do Your good pleasure. Shape my desires, my habits, and my life through Jesus. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"Grace saves freely, but it does not leave us unchanged. The grace of God trains the heart and shapes a new life in Christ.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"mnic-l6-q1","prompt":"According to Titus 2:11, what has appeared to bring salvation?","options":["The grace of God","Human pride","Fear of people","Self-improvement"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Titus says the grace of God that brings salvation has appeared."},{"id":"mnic-l6-q2","prompt":"According to Titus 2:12, what does grace do?","options":["Teaches us how to live","Makes obedience meaningless","Encourages sin","Removes the need for Jesus"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Titus says grace teaches us to deny ungodliness and live soberly, righteously, and godly."},{"id":"mnic-l6-q3","prompt":"According to Ephesians 2:10, what are believers created for in Christ Jesus?","options":["Good works","Self-salvation","Spiritual pride","Fear-based religion"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Ephesians says we are God's workmanship, created in Christ Jesus unto good works."},{"id":"mnic-l6-q4","prompt":"According to Philippians 2:13, who works in believers to will and to do His good pleasure?","options":["God","Human pride","The old life","Fear"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Philippians says God works in believers both to will and to do His good pleasure."},{"id":"mnic-l6-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What is one area where grace is training you to live differently?","explanation":"Grace does not only forgive. It teaches, renews, and shapes the life to reflect Christ."}]}}
$mnic_l6_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'grace-trains-the-life'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000079';

-- Made New in Christ — Lesson 7: Living as a New Creation (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000893';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $mnic_l7_reader$
{"openingThought":"The gospel does not leave you where it found you.\n\nIn Christ, you are not just forgiven.\n\nYou are made new.\n\nThat does not mean every struggle disappears overnight. It does not mean you never feel temptation, weakness, fear, or old patterns pulling at you.\n\nBut it does mean the old life no longer owns you.\n\nYou have a new identity.\nA new source.\nA new motive.\nA new future.\n\nThe Christian life is learning to live from what is already true in Jesus.","scriptureRef":"2 Corinthians 5:17","scriptureQuote":"Therefore if any man be in Christ, he is a new creature: old things are passed away; behold, all things are become new.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"The new life does not begin with self-confidence.\n\nIt begins with union with Jesus.\n\nIn Him, the old story of guilt, condemnation, and separation no longer has the final word.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"1 John 4:19","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"We love him, because he first loved us.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"This is the new rhythm of the Christian life.\n\nWe are not trying to earn God's love.\n\nWe are responding to the love we have already received in Christ.\n\nGrace changes the heart's reason for obedience.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Romans 8:1","quote":"There is therefore now no condemnation to them which are in Christ Jesus, who walk not after the flesh, but after the Spirit."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Romans 8:3-4","quote":"For what the law could not do, in that it was weak through the flesh, God sending his own Son in the likeness of sinful flesh, and for sin, condemned sin in the flesh: That the righteousness of the law might be fulfilled in us, who walk not after the flesh, but after the Spirit."}],"teachingPoints":["1. New creation begins in Christ\n\nPaul says anyone in Christ is a new creature.\n\nThe new life does not begin with self-confidence.\n\nIt begins with union with Jesus.\n\nIn Him, the old story of guilt, condemnation, and separation no longer has the final word.","2. Love becomes the new motive\n\nFirst John says we love because He first loved us.\n\nThis is the new rhythm of the Christian life.\n\nWe are not trying to earn God's love.\n\nWe are responding to the love we have already received in Christ.\n\nGrace changes the heart's reason for obedience.","3. Condemnation is broken in Christ\n\nRomans 8 says there is no condemnation to those who are in Christ Jesus.\n\nThat does not mean sin is harmless.\n\nIt means Christ has dealt with sin.\n\nBelievers do not live under the crushing verdict of condemnation.\n\nThey live under the grace, power, and leadership of Jesus.","4. The Spirit leads the new life\n\nRomans 8 says God's purpose is fulfilled in those who walk after the Spirit.\n\nThe new creation life is not powered by human will alone.\n\nGod's Spirit works within us, teaching us to desire what is good, reject what destroys, and reflect the love of Christ."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"New Creation","lemma":"καινὴ κτίσις","transliteration":"kainē ktisis","meaning":"A new work of God in a person's life, where the old identity no longer has final authority and life is restored in Christ.","strongs":"G2937","reference":"2 Corinthians 5:17","note":"New creation does not mean pretending the past never happened.\n\nIt means the past no longer gets to define the future.\n\nIn Christ, God begins a new life that is shaped by grace, love, truth, and the Spirit."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"Made New in Christ has followed one clear gospel path:\n\nThe gospel is good news, not good advice.\n\nJesus already finished the saving work.\n\nChrist is the new representative of humanity.\n\nIn Him, God makes all things new.\n\nFaith receives what grace has provided.\n\nGrace trains the life.\n\nNow we live as new creations.\n\nThis is the balance Scripture gives us.\n\nWe do not work to become accepted.\n\nWe work because grace has reached us.\n\nWe do not obey to make God love us.\n\nWe obey because He first loved us.\n\nWe do not fight sin to earn a new identity.\n\nWe fight sin because we have one.\n\nThe gospel changes both our standing and our walk."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"Made New in Christ","accent":"stone","payload":{"variant":"sequence","steps":[{"label":"Good news announced","subtitle":"The gospel is news, not advice."},{"label":"Salvation finished","subtitle":"Jesus has already accomplished the saving work."},{"label":"New representative given","subtitle":"Christ is the new head of humanity."},{"label":"New creation begun","subtitle":"In Him, God makes all things new."},{"label":"Faith receives","subtitle":"We rest in what grace has provided."},{"label":"Grace trains","subtitle":"Grace saves and teaches the life."},{"label":"New life lived","subtitle":"We walk as new creations by the Spirit."}]},"footnote":"The Christian life is lived from grace, not for acceptance."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Living New","references":[{"reference":"2 Corinthians 5:17"},{"reference":"Romans 8:1-4"},{"reference":"1 John 4:19"},{"reference":"Galatians 2:20"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: In Christ, believers receive a new identity, live from God's love, and walk by the Spirit as new creations."}],"reflectPrompt":"What would change if you lived this week from your identity in Christ instead of your fear, failure, or performance?\n\nThink about your thoughts, decisions, relationships, habits, work, private life, and how you respond when you fall short.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, practice living from your new identity.\n\nWhen condemnation speaks, answer:\n\n\"There is no condemnation in Christ.\"\n\nWhen fear says you are not enough, answer:\n\n\"I am loved before I perform.\"\n\nWhen old habits pull at you, answer:\n\n\"That is not my master anymore.\"\n\nThen take one Spirit-led step.\n\nConfess.\nPray.\nChoose honesty.\nServe someone.\nTurn away from the old pattern.\nReturn to Scripture.\nAsk God for a renewed heart.\n\nDo not live like someone trying to become loved.\n\nLive like someone who has been made new in Christ.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for making me new in Jesus. Help me live from grace instead of fear, from love instead of performance, and from truth instead of condemnation. Teach me to walk by Your Spirit. Shape my thoughts, motives, habits, and relationships so my life reflects the new creation You have begun in me. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"You have completed Made New in Christ. The gospel is good news about what Jesus has already done. In Him, you are forgiven, reconciled, made new, trained by grace, and invited to live from a restored identity.","seriesCompleteScreen":{"title":"Study Complete","message":"You have completed Made New in Christ. The gospel is good news about what Jesus has already done. In Him, you are forgiven, reconciled, made new, trained by grace, and invited to live from a restored identity.","primaryCta":{"label":"Back to Foundations Path","href":"/paths/foundations"},"secondaryCta":{"label":"Review this study","href":"/study-library/made-new-in-christ"}},"lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"mnic-l7-q1","prompt":"According to 2 Corinthians 5:17, what is true of anyone in Christ?","options":["They are a new creature","They are beyond grace","They must save themselves","They cannot change"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Paul says that anyone in Christ is a new creature, and old things are passed away."},{"id":"mnic-l7-q2","prompt":"According to 1 John 4:19, why do we love Him?","options":["Because He first loved us","Because we earned His love","Because fear saves us","Because works replace grace"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"John says we love Him because He first loved us. God's love becomes the source of our love and obedience."},{"id":"mnic-l7-q3","prompt":"According to Romans 8:1, what is true for those in Christ Jesus?","options":["There is no condemnation","There is no hope","There is no need for grace","There is no need for Jesus"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Romans 8:1 says there is no condemnation to those who are in Christ Jesus."},{"id":"mnic-l7-q4","prompt":"What does this lesson teach about the new life?","options":["It is lived from grace and led by the Spirit","It is earned by performance","It is powered by pride","It ignores obedience completely"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The new creation life is lived from grace, shaped by love, and led by the Spirit."},{"id":"mnic-l7-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What is one old identity or pattern you need to stop letting define you?","explanation":"In Christ, the old life no longer has final authority. God calls believers to live from their new identity in Jesus."}]}}
$mnic_l7_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'living-as-a-new-creation'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000079';

-- When Death Died — Lesson 1: He Entered Death (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000880';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $wdd_l1_reader$
{"openingThought":"Jesus did not save humanity from a safe distance.\n\nHe entered our darkness.\n\nIn Gethsemane, the weight of what He was about to carry pressed so heavily on Him that He said His soul was \"exceeding sorrowful, even unto death.\"\n\nThis was not fear of physical pain only.\n\nJesus was beginning to feel the full weight of sin, guilt, separation, and death.\n\nHe stepped into what sin does to the human soul so He could rescue us from it.","scriptureRef":"Matthew 26:38","scriptureQuote":"Then saith he unto them, My soul is exceeding sorrowful, even unto death.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Gethsemane shows that Jesus' suffering began before the nails.\n\nHe was facing more than execution.\n\nHe was facing the cup of sin, guilt, separation, and judgment on behalf of humanity.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Matthew 26:39","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"O my Father, if it be possible, let this cup pass from me: nevertheless not as I will, but as thou wilt.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Jesus did not deny the cost.\n\nHe surrendered in love.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Isaiah 53:4-6","quote":"Surely he hath borne our griefs, and carried our sorrows: yet we did esteem him stricken, smitten of God, and afflicted. But he was wounded for our transgressions, he was bruised for our iniquities: the chastisement of our peace was upon him; and with his stripes we are healed. All we like sheep have gone astray; we have turned every one to his own way; and the LORD hath laid on him the iniquity of us all."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"2 Corinthians 5:21","quote":"For he hath made him to be sin for us, who knew no sin."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Jesus felt the weight before the nails\n\nGethsemane shows that Jesus' suffering began before the cross.\n\nHis sorrow was so deep He described it as being \"unto death.\"\n\nHe was not only facing physical execution.\n\nHe was facing the cup of sin, guilt, separation, and judgment on behalf of humanity.","2. Jesus carried what was not His\n\nIsaiah says He bore our griefs and carried our sorrows.\n\nJesus did not suffer because He had sinned.\n\nHe suffered because He chose to carry what belonged to us.\n\nOur sin.\nOur shame.\nOur guilt.\nOur brokenness.\n\nHe entered the burden so we could be freed from it.","3. Jesus became sin for us\n\n2 Corinthians 5:21 says Jesus was made to be sin for us, though He knew no sin.\n\nThat does not mean Jesus became sinful in character.\n\nIt means He stood in our place and bore the weight, consequence, and condemnation of sin.\n\nThe sinless One entered the death-path of sinners.","4. Jesus surrendered in love\n\nIn Gethsemane, Jesus prayed, \"Not as I will, but as thou wilt.\"\n\nThis was not weakness.\n\nThis was surrender.\n\nHe could see the cost, feel the weight, and still choose love.\n\nJesus entered death willingly because saving us mattered to Him."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Cup","lemma":"ποτήριον","transliteration":"potērion","meaning":"In Scripture, a \"cup\" can represent an experience someone must receive or pass through, often connected with suffering, judgment, or deep trial.","strongs":"G4221","reference":"Matthew 26:39","note":"When Jesus prayed for the cup to pass, He was facing more than physical pain.\n\nHe was facing the full weight of sin and separation.\n\nYet He surrendered to the Father's will so humanity could be rescued."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"Gethsemane helps us understand the cross more deeply.\n\nJesus was not simply walking toward Roman execution.\n\nHe was entering the full consequence of sin.\n\nSin separates.\nSin crushes.\nSin brings guilt.\nSin leads to death.\n\nJesus stepped into that reality for us.\n\nHe did not minimize death.\n\nHe entered it.\n\nHe did not avoid the weight of sin.\n\nHe carried it.\n\nThis is why His love is not shallow sentiment.\n\nIt is costly, deliberate, self-giving rescue."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"What sin brought and what Jesus chose","accent":"stone","payload":{"variant":"comparison","leftTitle":"What sin brought","rightTitle":"What Jesus chose","leftItems":["guilt","sorrow","separation","death"],"rightItems":["surrender","love","sacrifice","rescue"]},"footnote":"Jesus entered the full weight of sin and death so humanity could be rescued through His self-giving love."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"He Entered Our Burden","references":[{"reference":"Matthew 26:38-39"},{"reference":"Isaiah 53:4-6"},{"reference":"2 Corinthians 5:21"},{"reference":"Hebrews 2:9"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Jesus entered the full weight of sin and death so humanity could be rescued through His self-giving love."}],"reflectPrompt":"What does it mean to you that Jesus did not avoid the weight of sin, but entered it for you?\n\nThink about guilt, grief, shame, fear, or anything that has made you feel spiritually crushed.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, when guilt or shame feels heavy, do not carry it alone.\n\nBring it to Jesus.\n\nHe understands the weight.\n\nHe entered it.\n\nHe carried what was not His so you would not have to be destroyed by what was yours.\n\nPray honestly:\n\n\"Jesus, I am bringing this weight to You.\"\n\nThen name it.\n\nDo not hide it.\n\nThe Savior who entered death for you can carry what is crushing you now.\n\nPrayer:\n\nJesus, thank You for entering the weight of sin and death for me. Thank You for carrying what I could never carry and choosing love when the cost was unbearable. Help me bring my guilt, shame, grief, and fear to You. Teach me to trust Your surrender, Your sacrifice, and Your rescuing love. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"Jesus entered the weight of sin and death for us. He did not save from a distance. He stepped into the darkness to rescue humanity through love.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"wdd-l1-q1","prompt":"Where did Jesus experience deep sorrow before the cross?","options":["Gethsemane","Babylon","Egypt","Mount Carmel"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"In Gethsemane, Jesus said His soul was exceeding sorrowful, even unto death."},{"id":"wdd-l1-q2","prompt":"According to Isaiah 53, what did Jesus bear and carry?","options":["Our griefs and sorrows","His own sin","Roman wealth","Human applause"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Isaiah 53 says Jesus bore our griefs and carried our sorrows."},{"id":"wdd-l1-q3","prompt":"According to 2 Corinthians 5:21, what was Jesus made to be for us?","options":["Sin for us","A sinner in character","A political ruler","An angel"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus was made to be sin for us, meaning He stood in our place and bore sin's weight though He knew no sin."},{"id":"wdd-l1-q4","prompt":"What did Jesus pray in Gethsemane?","options":["\"Not as I will, but as thou wilt\"","\"I will not save them\"","\"Sin is harmless\"","\"The grave is stronger than God\""],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus surrendered to the Father's will, choosing the path of sacrifice and rescue."},{"id":"wdd-l1-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What burden do you need to bring to Jesus instead of carrying it alone?","explanation":"Jesus entered the weight of sin and death so we could bring our guilt, shame, grief, and fear to Him."}]}}
$wdd_l1_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'he-entered-death'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000078';


-- When Death Died — Lesson 2: The Weight of Sin (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000881';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $wdd_l2_reader$
{"openingThought":"Sin is heavier than most people realize.\n\nIt is not just a mistake.\nIt is not just a bad habit.\nIt is not just breaking a rule.\n\nSin separates the heart from God, damages the conscience, bends love into selfishness, and ends in death.\n\nAt the cross, Jesus carried the full weight of what sin does.\n\nHe carried guilt that was not His.\nHe entered sorrow He did not deserve.\nHe bore shame He did not create.\n\nThe weight of sin fell on the sinless One so the guilty could be restored.","scriptureRef":"Matthew 27:46","scriptureQuote":"And about the ninth hour Jesus cried with a loud voice, saying, Eli, Eli, lama sabachthani? that is to say, My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me?","scriptureAfterPrimary":"This was not theater.\n\nJesus was entering the terrible weight of sin's separation.\n\nHe had lived in perfect fellowship with the Father, yet now He bore the darkness sin brings.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Isaiah 53:10","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"Yet it pleased the LORD to bruise him; he hath put him to grief.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Isaiah points to deep inward suffering, not wounds on the surface only.\n\nThe cross reveals that sin is crushing.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Isaiah 53:11","quote":"He shall see of the travail of his soul, and shall be satisfied: by his knowledge shall my righteous servant justify many; for he shall bear their iniquities."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Psalm 88:3-4","quote":"For my soul is full of troubles: and my life draweth nigh unto the grave. I am counted with them that go down into the pit."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Jesus entered the feeling of separation\n\nOn the cross, Jesus cried, \"My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me?\"\n\nThis was not theater.\n\nJesus was entering the terrible weight of sin's separation.\n\nHe had lived in perfect fellowship with the Father, yet now He bore the darkness sin brings.\n\nHe stepped into the place humanity deserved so humanity could be brought back to God.","2. Sin crushes the soul\n\nIsaiah speaks of the \"travail of his soul.\"\n\nThis points to deep inward suffering.\n\nJesus did not only experience wounds on His body.\n\nHe carried anguish in His soul.\n\nThe cross reveals that sin is not light, casual, or harmless.\n\nIt is crushing.","3. Jesus carried guilt that was not His\n\nJesus had no sin of His own.\n\nHe did not lie, rebel, abuse, deceive, or turn from the Father.\n\nYet He carried human guilt as our substitute.\n\nThe innocent One stood in the place of the guilty.\n\nThis is the heart of the gospel.","4. Jesus bore the weight to rescue us\n\nThe weight of sin did not fall on Jesus by accident.\n\nHe chose to carry it.\n\nHe carried what would have destroyed us so that we could receive forgiveness, cleansing, and life.\n\nThe cross shows both the seriousness of sin and the depth of God's love."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Forsaken","lemma":"ἐγκαταλείπω","transliteration":"egkataleipō","meaning":"Abandoned, left alone, or given over.","strongs":"G1459","reference":"Matthew 27:46","note":"When Jesus cried, \"Why hast thou forsaken me?\" He was expressing the terrifying experience of sin's separation.\n\nHe entered that darkness for us.\n\nThe Son felt forsaken so sinners could be brought near."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The cross shows us what sin really costs.\n\nSin does not merely create bad behavior.\n\nIt creates separation from God, heaviness in the soul, guilt before heaven, and death.\n\nJesus carried that full reality.\n\nHe did not carry a symbol.\n\nHe carried the burden.\n\nThis is why the cross must never be treated lightly.\n\nIt reveals two truths at once:\n\nSin is worse than we imagined.\n\nGod's love is deeper than we believed.\n\nJesus entered the crushing weight of sin so that the weight would not have the final word over us."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"The weight that fell on Him","accent":"stone","payload":{"variant":"comparison","leftTitle":"Humanity","rightTitle":"Jesus","leftItems":["sin","guilt","shame","separation","death"],"rightItems":["bears","carries","suffers","restores"]},"footnote":"The weight that belonged to us fell on Him."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"The Weight He Carried","references":[{"reference":"Matthew 27:46"},{"reference":"Isaiah 53:10-12"},{"reference":"Psalm 88:3-4"},{"reference":"1 Peter 2:24"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Jesus bore the full weight of sin, guilt, and separation so humanity could be restored to God."}],"reflectPrompt":"What does the weight Jesus carried reveal about how serious sin is and how deep His love is?\n\nHold both truths together. Sin is not small, and God's love is not weak.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, stop treating sin casually and stop treating grace cheaply.\n\nBring both honesty and hope to Jesus.\n\nName one sin, burden, or guilt you have minimized.\n\nThen bring it to the cross.\n\nSay:\n\n\"Jesus, this is heavier than I admitted. Thank You for carrying what I could not.\"\n\nDo not hide from the weight.\n\nLet the weight lead you to the Savior.\n\nPrayer:\n\nJesus, thank You for carrying the weight of sin for me. Help me see sin honestly without running from Your mercy. Thank You for entering separation so I could be brought near. Teach me to hate what destroys and trust the love that restores. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"Jesus carried the weight of sin, guilt, shame, and separation. The cross shows how serious sin is and how deeply God loves.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"wdd-l2-q1","prompt":"What did Jesus cry out on the cross in Matthew 27:46?","options":["\"My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me?\"","\"I have no love for them\"","\"Sin is harmless\"","\"Death has won\""],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus cried, \"My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me?\" showing the deep experience of sin's separation."},{"id":"wdd-l2-q2","prompt":"What does Isaiah 53 describe Jesus experiencing?","options":["The travail of His soul","Personal guilt for His own sin","Political victory only","A painless death"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Isaiah 53 speaks of the travail of His soul, showing the inward suffering Jesus carried."},{"id":"wdd-l2-q3","prompt":"Why did Jesus carry guilt?","options":["Because He stood in humanity's place","Because He had sinned","Because He was forced by Rome only","Because He failed the Father"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus had no sin of His own. He carried guilt as our substitute, standing in humanity's place."},{"id":"wdd-l2-q4","prompt":"What does the cross reveal?","options":["Sin is serious and God's love is deep","Sin does not matter","God abandoned His plan","Death is stronger than Christ"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The cross reveals both the seriousness of sin and the depth of God's self-giving love."},{"id":"wdd-l2-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What burden have you been carrying that Jesus invites you to bring to the cross?","explanation":"Jesus carried the weight of sin and separation so we do not have to hide under guilt and shame."}]}}
