-- When Death Died — Lesson 6: Victory Secured (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000885';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $wdd_l6_reader$
{"openingThought":"The resurrection of Jesus was not only His victory.\n\nIt was a promise of ours.\n\nWhen Jesus rose from the grave, He proved that death does not have the final word over those who belong to Him.\n\nThe resurrection is not just something Christians look back on.\n\nIt is something we look forward from.\n\nBecause Jesus lives, His people will live.\n\nBecause Jesus rose, the dead in Christ will rise.\n\nBecause death could not hold Him, death will not hold His people forever.\n\nVictory is not wishful thinking.\n\nVictory has already been secured in Christ.","scriptureRef":"1 Corinthians 15:54","scriptureQuote":"Death is swallowed up in victory.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"That is stronger than saying death is ignored.\n\nDeath is not explained away.\n\nDeath is overcome.\n\nEverything death has stolen will be answered by the life and victory of Christ.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"1 Corinthians 15:52","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"For the trumpet shall sound, and the dead shall be raised incorruptible, and we shall be changed.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Resurrection is not a return to the same broken condition.\n\nGod gives life that death cannot decay.\n\nThe future hope is lasting restoration.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Revelation 20:6","quote":"Blessed and holy is he that hath part in the first resurrection: on such the second death hath no power."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Revelation 2:11","quote":"He that overcometh shall not be hurt of the second death."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"John 11:25","quote":"Jesus said unto her, I am the resurrection, and the life."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Jesus' resurrection guarantees future resurrection\n\nThe resurrection of Jesus is not an isolated miracle.\n\nIt is the foundation of Christian hope.\n\nBecause He rose, His people will rise.\n\nThe Bible does not teach vague survival after death as our final hope.\n\nIt teaches resurrection.\n\nGod will raise the dead, restore life, and complete what Jesus began.","2. Death will be swallowed up in victory\n\nPaul says death is swallowed up in victory.\n\nDeath is not explained away.\n\nDeath is overcome.\n\nEverything death has stolen will be answered by the life and victory of Christ.","3. The redeemed are safe from the second death\n\nRevelation says the second death has no power over those who share in the first resurrection.\n\nJesus did not only defeat the first death.\n\nHe saves His people from the final death.\n\nThose who trust Him are secure in His victory.","4. Resurrection means complete change\n\nPaul says the dead will be raised incorruptible and we shall be changed.\n\nResurrection is not a return to the same broken condition.\n\nGod gives life that death cannot decay.\n\nThe future hope is not temporary repair.\n\nIt is lasting restoration."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Victory","lemma":"νίκη","transliteration":"nikē","meaning":"Triumph, conquest, or the final defeat of an enemy.","strongs":"G3529","reference":"1 Corinthians 15:54","note":"When Scripture says death is swallowed up in victory, it means death is not merely delayed.\n\nIt is conquered.\n\nJesus' resurrection secures the final victory that His people will experience when He raises the dead and makes all things new."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The resurrection changes the way we read the whole story.\n\nDeath entered through sin.\n\nJesus entered death.\n\nDeath could not hold Him.\n\nNow His resurrection becomes the guarantee of what is coming.\n\nThe first death will be interrupted by resurrection.\n\nThe second death has no power over the redeemed.\n\nThe grave will not be the final home of God's people.\n\nThis is why Christian hope is so concrete.\n\nIt is not floating away into uncertainty.\n\nIt is Christ returning, the trumpet sounding, the dead being raised, and death being swallowed up in victory."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"Victory secured in Christ","accent":"sage","description":"The resurrection of Jesus secures the future resurrection of His people.","payload":{"variant":"sequence","steps":[{"label":"Christ rose","subtitle":"Death could not hold the Lord of life."},{"label":"The dead in Christ rise","subtitle":"The trumpet sounds and the graves open."},{"label":"The redeemed are changed","subtitle":"Raised incorruptible, not merely repaired."},{"label":"Death is swallowed up","subtitle":"Victory answers everything death stole."},{"label":"Life remains forever","subtitle":"The second death has no power over the redeemed."}]},"footnote":"The resurrection of Jesus secures the future resurrection of His people."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Death Swallowed Up","references":[{"reference":"John 11:25"},{"reference":"1 Corinthians 15:52-55"},{"reference":"Revelation 20:6"},{"reference":"Revelation 2:11"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Jesus' resurrection secures the future resurrection and final victory over death for all who belong to Him."}],"reflectPrompt":"What does it mean to you that Jesus' resurrection is not only His victory, but the promise of your future resurrection?\n\nThink about grief, fear, hope, and the difference between temporary life and lasting restoration.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, let resurrection hope reshape how you see loss.\n\nLoss is real.\n\nGrief is real.\n\nDeath is real.\n\nBut none of them are final in Christ.\n\nWhen fear rises, speak this truth:\n\n\"Death is swallowed up in victory.\"\n\nWhen grief feels heavy, remember:\n\n\"The trumpet shall sound, and the dead shall be raised.\"\n\nWhen the future feels uncertain, anchor yourself here:\n\n\"Jesus is the resurrection and the life.\"\n\nHope is not denial.\n\nHope is confidence in the risen Christ.\n\nPrayer:\n\nJesus, thank You that Your resurrection secures victory over death. Thank You that the grave is not the final word for those who belong to You. Help me live with resurrection hope. Strengthen my faith when grief or fear feels heavy, and keep my heart anchored in Your promise of life forever. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"Jesus' resurrection secures the future resurrection of His people. Death will be swallowed up in victory, and the second death has no power over those who belong to Christ.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"wdd-l6-q1","prompt":"According to 1 Corinthians 15:54, what is swallowed up in victory?","options":["Death","Faith","Hope","Love"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Paul says death is swallowed up in victory, showing that death will be fully overcome through Christ."},{"id":"wdd-l6-q2","prompt":"According to 1 Corinthians 15:52, what will happen when the trumpet sounds?","options":["The dead shall be raised incorruptible","The dead will remain forgotten","Hope will end","Death will become stronger"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Paul says the trumpet will sound and the dead will be raised incorruptible."},{"id":"wdd-l6-q3","prompt":"According to Revelation 20:6, what has no power over those in the first resurrection?","options":["The second death","Prayer","Scripture","God's love"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Revelation 20:6 says the second death has no power over those who have part in the first resurrection."},{"id":"wdd-l6-q4","prompt":"What does Jesus call Himself in John 11:25?","options":["The resurrection and the life","The end of mercy","The closed door","The fear of death"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus says, \"I am the resurrection, and the life,\" placing resurrection hope directly in Himself."},{"id":"wdd-l6-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"How can resurrection hope help you face grief or fear without pretending those things are easy?","explanation":"Biblical hope does not deny grief. It anchors grief in Christ's victory over death."}]}}
$wdd_l6_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'victory-secured'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000078';

-- When Death Died — Lesson 7: Live From Victory (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000886';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $wdd_l7_reader$
{"openingThought":"The resurrection is not only something to believe.\n\nIt is something to live from.\n\nJesus defeated death, but many people still live as if fear has the final word. Fear of loss. Fear of judgment. Fear of failure. Fear of being forgotten. Fear that death will undo everything.\n\nBut the gospel gives us a better center.\n\nJesus died.\nJesus rose.\nJesus lives forever.\nJesus holds the future.\n\nBecause death has been defeated, believers can live with courage, hope, love, and confidence.\n\nNot because life is easy.\n\nBecause Christ is alive.","scriptureRef":"Romans 8:38","scriptureQuote":"For I am persuaded, that neither death, nor life, nor angels, nor principalities, nor powers, nor things present, nor things to come, nor height, nor depth, nor any other creature, shall be able to separate us from the love of God, which is in Christ Jesus our Lord.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Death feels like separation.\n\nBut for those who belong to Jesus, death cannot break God's hold.\n\nThe grave cannot cancel His love.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"1 John 4:19","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"We love him, because he first loved us.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"The victory of Jesus is not only about the future.\n\nIt reshapes the present.\n\nBecause we are loved, we can love.\n\nBecause we are secure, we can serve.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"John 11:25-26","quote":"Jesus said unto her, I am the resurrection, and the life: he that believeth in me, though he were dead, yet shall he live: And whosoever liveth and believeth in me shall never die."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"2 Timothy 1:7","quote":"For God hath not given us the spirit of fear; but of power, and of love, and of a sound mind."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Death cannot separate us from God's love\n\nRomans 8 says neither death nor life can separate us from the love of God in Christ.\n\nThat is massive.\n\nDeath feels like separation.\n\nBut for those who belong to Jesus, death cannot break God's hold.\n\nThe grave cannot cancel His love.","2. Victory removes fear's final authority\n\nFear may still speak, but it no longer gets the final word.\n\nJesus has already passed through death and come out victorious.\n\nThat means believers do not have to live controlled by fear.\n\nWe can face life honestly while trusting the risen Christ.","3. God's love teaches us how to live\n\n1 John says we love because He first loved us.\n\nBecause we are loved, we can love.\n\nBecause we are secure, we can serve.\n\nBecause Christ lives, we can live with open hands instead of clenched fists.","4. Resurrection hope gives courage now\n\nJesus said, \"I am the resurrection, and the life.\"\n\nThat truth does not only matter at funerals.\n\nIt matters in daily life.\n\nWhen grief feels heavy.\nWhen obedience feels costly.\nWhen the future feels uncertain.\nWhen fear tries to lead.\n\nThe risen Christ gives courage for today."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Persuaded","lemma":"πείθω","transliteration":"peithō","meaning":"Fully convinced, settled, or deeply assured.","strongs":"G3982","reference":"Romans 8:38","note":"Paul does not say he is guessing that nothing can separate us from God's love.\n\nHe says he is persuaded.\n\nResurrection faith gives deep assurance.\n\nBecause Jesus defeated death, believers can become settled in the love of God."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"When Death Died has shown one clear gospel movement:\n\nJesus entered death.\nJesus carried sin's weight.\nJesus chose the cross.\nJesus broke death's claim.\nJesus shattered death's power.\nJesus secured resurrection victory.\nJesus now invites us to live from that victory.\n\nThis does not mean Christians never grieve.\n\nIt means grief does not get the throne.\n\nThis does not mean Christians never feel fear.\n\nIt means fear does not get to become lord.\n\nThis does not mean death is harmless.\n\nIt means death is defeated.\n\nThe risen Jesus changes how we live now.\n\nWe can live forgiven.\nWe can live loved.\nWe can live free from condemnation.\nWe can live with resurrection hope.\nWe can live knowing that death will not have the last word."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"When Death Died","accent":"sage","description":"The gospel movement of this study, from the cross to resurrection hope.","payload":{"variant":"sequence","steps":[{"label":"Sin was carried","subtitle":"Jesus bore the weight that belonged to us."},{"label":"Love was revealed","subtitle":"He chose the cross willingly."},{"label":"Death was broken","subtitle":"The grave could not hold Him."},{"label":"Resurrection was secured","subtitle":"Victory for all who belong to Christ."},{"label":"Hope became alive","subtitle":"Believers live from His victory now."}]},"footnote":"Because Jesus lives, death does not get the final word."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Living From Victory","references":[{"reference":"John 11:25-26"},{"reference":"Romans 8:38-39"},{"reference":"1 John 4:19"},{"reference":"2 Timothy 1:7"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Because Jesus defeated death, believers can live with love, courage, confidence, and resurrection hope."}],"reflectPrompt":"Where do you need to live more from Christ's victory instead of fear?\n\nThink about death, grief, uncertainty, guilt, judgment, relationships, obedience, or the future.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, identify one fear that has been shaping your decisions.\n\nName it clearly.\n\nThen place it under the victory of Jesus.\n\nSay:\n\n\"Jesus is alive, and this fear does not get the final word.\"\n\nThen take one faithful step.\n\nNot a dramatic step.\n\nA real one.\n\nPray honestly.\nForgive someone.\nObey what God has shown you.\nStop rehearsing condemnation.\nEncourage someone who is grieving.\nReturn to Scripture when fear gets loud.\n\nVictory is not only something you celebrate later.\n\nIt is something you practice now.\n\nPrayer:\n\nJesus, thank You for defeating death and giving me resurrection hope. Teach me to live from Your victory instead of fear. Help me trust that nothing can separate me from Your love. Give me courage, a sound mind, and a heart shaped by Your love. Let my life show that death does not have the final word. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"You have completed When Death Died. Jesus entered death, carried sin's weight, chose the cross, rose in victory, and secured resurrection hope. Because He lives, death does not get the final word.","seriesCompleteScreen":{"title":"Study Complete","message":"You have completed When Death Died. Jesus entered death, carried sin's weight, chose the cross, rose in victory, and secured resurrection hope. Because He lives, death does not get the final word.","primaryCta":{"label":"Back to Foundations Path","href":"/paths/foundations"},"secondaryCta":{"label":"Review this study","href":"/study-library/when-death-died"}},"lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"wdd-l7-q1","prompt":"According to Romans 8:38-39, what cannot separate us from the love of God in Christ?","options":["Neither death nor life","Only small mistakes","Only difficult emotions","Only human opinions"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Romans 8 says neither death nor life can separate believers from the love of God in Christ Jesus."},{"id":"wdd-l7-q2","prompt":"According to 1 John 4:19, why do we love Him?","options":["Because He first loved us","Because we earned His love","Because fear saved us","Because death is stronger than God"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"1 John 4:19 says we love Him because He first loved us."},{"id":"wdd-l7-q3","prompt":"What does Jesus call Himself in John 11:25?","options":["The resurrection and the life","The fear of death","The final defeat","The closed door"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus says He is the resurrection and the life, making Him the center of our hope."},{"id":"wdd-l7-q4","prompt":"According to 2 Timothy 1:7, what has God not given us?","options":["The spirit of fear","Power","Love","A sound mind"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"2 Timothy 1:7 says God has not given us the spirit of fear, but of power, love, and a sound mind."},{"id":"wdd-l7-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What would change this week if you lived like death truly does not have the final word?","explanation":"The victory of Jesus is not only future hope. It reshapes present courage, love, obedience, and trust."}]}}
$wdd_l7_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'live-from-victory'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000078';






-- What Happens When We Die? — Lesson 7: Forgiven and Free (guided reader JSON).
update public.lessons
set reader_content = $wh_l7_reader$
{"openingThought":"Death is not only something people fear in the future.\n\nSometimes death shows up in the heart now.\n\nGuilt can make a person feel buried.\nShame can make hope feel impossible.\nSin can make someone feel too far gone.\n\nBut Jesus came to free us from more than the grave.\n\nHe came to free us from guilt, condemnation, and the power of sin.\n\nHe tasted death for everyone so that forgiveness could be real, freedom could be offered, and eternal life could become our hope.","scriptureRef":"Hebrews 2:9","scriptureQuote":"But we see Jesus, who was made a little lower than the angels for the suffering of death... that he by the grace of God should taste death for every man.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Jesus did not save us from a distance.\n\nHe entered death itself so we could receive life.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Colossians 1:13-14","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"Who hath delivered us from the power of darkness, and hath translated us into the kingdom of his dear Son.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Forgiveness is not vague comfort.\n\nIt is deliverance from darkness and translation into Christ's kingdom.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Colossians 1:14","quote":"In whom we have redemption through his blood, even the forgiveness of sins."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Psalm 32:1","quote":"Blessed is he whose transgression is forgiven, whose sin is covered."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Psalm 32:2","quote":"Blessed is the man unto whom the LORD imputeth not iniquity."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"2 Corinthians 5:10","quote":"For we must all appear before the judgment seat of Christ."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Jesus tasted death for everyone\n\nHebrews says Jesus tasted death for every person.\n\nThat means He entered the place humanity feared most.\n\nHe did not save us from a distance.\n\nHe stepped into death so we could receive life.","2. Forgiveness is real in Christ\n\nColossians says we have redemption and forgiveness through Jesus.\n\nForgiveness is not God pretending sin never happened.\n\nIt is God dealing with sin through Christ and releasing the sinner from guilt.\n\nJesus does not offer vague comfort.\n\nHe offers real pardon.","3. The forgiven person is blessed\n\nPsalm 32 describes the blessing of forgiven sin.\n\nGuilt is heavy.\n\nForgiveness lifts the weight.\n\nThe gospel does not leave people buried under shame.\n\nIt brings them into honest freedom before God.","4. Judgment is not hopeless for those in Christ\n\n2 Corinthians says every life will appear before Christ.\n\nThat is serious.\n\nBut the believer does not face judgment without a Savior.\n\nThe One who judges is also the One who died, forgives, restores, and represents His people.\n\nIn Jesus, truth and mercy meet."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Redemption","lemma":"ἀπολύτρωσις","transliteration":"apolutrosis","meaning":"Rescue, release, or deliverance through a paid cost.","strongs":"G629","reference":"Colossians 1:14","note":"Redemption means Jesus paid the cost to rescue us from the power of darkness.\n\nWe are not freed because sin was cheap.\n\nWe are freed because Christ gave Himself.\n\nForgiveness is costly grace."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"This study began with death, but it ends with Jesus.\n\nThat is the right order.\n\nThe Bible's teaching on death is not meant to make us obsessed with the grave.\n\nIt is meant to lead us to Christ.\n\nJesus tasted death.\nJesus forgives sin.\nJesus breaks the power of darkness.\nJesus raises the dead.\nJesus ends sin forever.\nJesus gives eternal life.\n\nThat means the believer can live forgiven and free.\n\nNot careless.\nNot proud.\nNot pretending sin does not matter.\n\nFree.\n\nFree to come to God honestly.\nFree to receive mercy.\nFree to reject deception.\nFree to grieve with hope.\nFree to wait for resurrection.\nFree to trust that death will not have the last word."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"deathResurrectionFlow","title":"From Death to Life in Christ","subtitle":"The full path from life, through death and resurrection, to judgment and eternal hope in Jesus.","highlightedIndices":[0,1,2,3,4]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Jesus is the center of the whole hope. He forgives, raises, judges with truth, and gives eternal life to those who trust Him."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Forgiven and Free","references":[{"reference":"Hebrews 2:9"},{"reference":"Colossians 1:13-14"},{"reference":"Psalm 32:1-2"},{"reference":"Romans 8:1"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Jesus tasted death for everyone so that sinners can be forgiven, freed from guilt, and restored to hope."}],"reflectPrompt":"Where do you most need to receive the freedom Jesus gives: guilt, fear of death, shame, spiritual confusion, or fear of judgment?\n\nChoose one area honestly. Jesus does not invite you to hide it. He invites you to bring it to Him.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, practice living as someone Christ has forgiven.\n\nWhen guilt speaks, answer with Scripture.\n\n\"There is therefore now no condemnation to them which are in Christ Jesus.\"\n\nWhen fear of death rises, answer with Jesus.\n\n\"I am the resurrection, and the life.\"\n\nWhen shame says you are too far gone, remember:\n\nJesus tasted death for every person.\n\nThat includes you.\n\nDo not rehearse what Christ has forgiven more than you receive what Christ has done.\n\nPrayer:\n\nJesus, thank You for tasting death for me. Thank You for forgiveness, redemption, and resurrection hope. Free me from guilt, fear, shame, and confusion. Help me trust Your word more than my feelings. Teach me to live forgiven, honest, and anchored in Your promise of eternal life. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"You have completed What Happens When We Die? The Bible's answer is clear: death is sleep, resurrection is hope, deception should be rejected, and Jesus gives forgiveness, freedom, and eternal life.","seriesCompleteScreen":{"title":"Study Complete","message":"You have completed What Happens When We Die? The Bible's answer is clear: death is sleep, resurrection is hope, deception should be rejected, and Jesus gives forgiveness, freedom, and eternal life.","primaryCta":{"label":"Back to Foundations Path","href":"/paths/foundations"},"secondaryCta":{"label":"Review this study","href":"/study-library/what-happens-when-we-die"}},"lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"wh-l7-q1","prompt":"According to Hebrews 2:9, what did Jesus taste for every person?","options":["Death","Wealth","Confusion","Pride"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Hebrews 2:9 says Jesus tasted death for every person by the grace of God."},{"id":"wh-l7-q2","prompt":"According to Colossians 1:13-14, what do we have in Christ?","options":["Redemption and forgiveness of sins","Fear without hope","Confusion about God","A reason to stay in darkness"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Colossians says we have redemption through Christ's blood and the forgiveness of sins."},{"id":"wh-l7-q3","prompt":"According to Psalm 32, who is blessed?","options":["The person whose sin is forgiven","The person who hides forever","The person who rejects mercy","The person who trusts deception"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Psalm 32 says blessed is the one whose transgression is forgiven and whose sin is covered."},{"id":"wh-l7-q4","prompt":"What is the center of our hope about death?","options":["Jesus","Fear","Superstition","Human guessing"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Bible's hope about death centers on Jesus, who forgives, raises the dead, and gives eternal life."},{"id":"wh-l7-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What truth from this study do you want to carry with you when fear, grief, or guilt feels heavy?","explanation":"The goal is not just information about death. The goal is hope in Jesus, who forgives, raises, restores, and gives eternal life."}]}}
$wh_l7_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'forgiven-and-free'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000077';

-- What Happens When We Die? — Lesson 6: The Resurrection Hope (guided reader JSON).
update public.lessons
set reader_content = $wh_l6_reader$
{"openingThought":"The Bible's hope is not vague.\n\nIt is resurrection.\n\nJesus does not promise that death is harmless. He promises that death will not win.\n\nOne day, His voice will reach the graves. The sleeping dead will awaken. Those who belong to Christ will rise to life.\n\nThis is why believers do not grieve like people with no hope.\n\nWe grieve, but not without promise.\n\nWe mourn, but not without resurrection.","scriptureRef":"John 5:28-29","scriptureQuote":"The hour is coming, in the which all that are in the graves shall hear his voice.\n\nAnd shall come forth.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Jesus grounds resurrection hope in His own authority.\n\nThe dead are in the graves.\n\nThey are not elsewhere.\n\nThey await His call.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"1 Thessalonians 4:16","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"For the Lord himself shall descend from heaven with a shout.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Resurrection is tied to Christ's return.\n\nThe Lord descends.\n\nThe dead in Christ rise.\n\nHope is forward-looking, not vague.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"1 Thessalonians 4:17","quote":"And so shall we ever be with the Lord."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"1 Corinthians 15:52","quote":"For the trumpet shall sound, and the dead shall be raised incorruptible."}],"teachingPoints":["1. The dead are in the graves until Christ calls\n\nJesus said all who are in the graves will hear His voice.\n\nThat is clear and grounded.\n\nThe dead are not wandering.\nThey are not watching.\nThey are not communicating.\n\nThey are resting until Jesus calls them.","2. Resurrection happens by the voice of Jesus\n\nThe hope of the dead is not human memory.\n\nIt is not superstition.\n\nIt is not spiritual guesswork.\n\nIt is the voice of Christ.\n\nThe same Jesus who called Lazarus from the tomb will call His people from the grave.","3. The resurrection happens when Jesus returns\n\n1 Thessalonians 4 connects the resurrection with the Lord descending from heaven.\n\nThis means the great hope of believers is tied to Christ's return.\n\nThe dead in Christ rise.\n\nThe living faithful are gathered.\n\nAnd God's people are forever with the Lord.","4. The resurrection brings victory over death\n\n1 Corinthians 15 says the dead will be raised incorruptible.\n\nThat means resurrection is not just coming back to the same broken condition.\n\nGod gives victory over decay, weakness, and death.\n\nThe resurrection is restoration."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Incorruptible","lemma":"ἄφθαρτος","transliteration":"aphthartos","meaning":"Not subject to decay, ruin, or death.","strongs":"G862","reference":"1 Corinthians 15:52","note":"When Scripture says the dead will be raised incorruptible, it means God's resurrection life is not fragile like our present life.\n\nJesus does not raise His people into temporary repair.\n\nHe raises them into lasting victory."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The resurrection keeps the Bible's teaching on death clear.\n\nDeath is sleep.\n\nThe dead know nothing.\n\nThe dead do not communicate with the living.\n\nThe second death is final.\n\nAnd the resurrection is the hope of God's people.\n\nThis is why Scripture points us forward, not sideways.\n\nForward to Christ's return.\nForward to the trumpet.\nForward to the voice of Jesus.\nForward to the dead being raised.\nForward to being forever with the Lord.\n\nChristian hope is not escape from the body.\n\nIt is resurrection through Christ."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"deathResurrectionFlow","stages":[{"stageName":"Life Now","description":"Human beings live, choose, trust, and respond to God in this present life.","scriptures":["Deuteronomy 30:19","John 3:16"]},{"stageName":"The First Death","description":"The first death is described by Jesus as sleep, an unconscious state awaiting resurrection.","scriptures":["John 11:11-14","Ecclesiastes 9:5"]},{"stageName":"The Resurrection","description":"Jesus will call the dead from the graves, and they will be raised.","scriptures":["John 5:28-29","1 Thessalonians 4:16-17","1 Corinthians 15:52"]},{"stageName":"Judgment","description":"God brings every life into truth and settles the question of each person's response to Him.","scriptures":["2 Corinthians 5:10","Revelation 20:12"]},{"stageName":"Eternal Life or Second Death","description":"The redeemed receive eternal life with Christ, while the second death brings the final end of sin and rebellion.","scriptures":["Revelation 20:6","Revelation 20:14","Revelation 21:4"]}],"highlightedIndices":[2],"subtitle":"Jesus will call the dead from the graves, and they will be raised."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Raised by His Voice","references":[{"reference":"John 5:28-29"},{"reference":"1 Thessalonians 4:16-17"},{"reference":"1 Corinthians 15:52"},{"reference":"John 11:25"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: The dead rest until Jesus returns and calls them from the graves in the resurrection."}],"reflectPrompt":"How does the promise of resurrection change the way you think about grief, death, or the future?\n\nThink honestly. Resurrection hope does not erase grief, but it gives grief somewhere to stand.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, practice hope that looks forward.\n\nWhen death, grief, or fear feels heavy, say:\n\n\"Jesus will call the dead to life.\"\n\nLet that sentence interrupt despair.\n\nThen read 1 Thessalonians 4:16-17 slowly.\n\nDo not rush it.\n\nLet the promise settle.\n\nThe dead in Christ will rise.\n\nAnd so shall we ever be with the Lord.\n\nPrayer:\n\nJesus, thank You for the promise of resurrection. Thank You that Your voice can reach the grave. Help me grieve with hope, trust Your return, and anchor my future in Your victory over death. Keep my heart steady until the day You make all things new. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"The resurrection is the Bible's hope for the dead. Jesus will call, the graves will open, and His people will be raised to lasting life.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"wh-l6-q1","prompt":"According to John 5:28-29, who will hear Jesus' voice?","options":["All who are in the graves","Only angels","Only people alive on earth","No one"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus said all who are in the graves will hear His voice and come forth."},{"id":"wh-l6-q2","prompt":"According to 1 Thessalonians 4:16, what happens when the Lord descends from heaven?","options":["The dead in Christ rise","The dead begin haunting the living","The resurrection is cancelled","Death becomes eternal"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"1 Thessalonians 4 teaches that the dead in Christ rise when the Lord descends from heaven."},{"id":"wh-l6-q3","prompt":"According to 1 Corinthians 15:52, how will the dead be raised?","options":["Incorruptible","Still broken forever","As spirits only","Without hope"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Paul says the dead will be raised incorruptible, meaning not subject to decay or death."},{"id":"wh-l6-q4","prompt":"What is the Bible's central hope for the dead?","options":["Resurrection through Jesus","Communication with the living","Wandering the earth","Escaping Scripture"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Bible points to resurrection through Jesus as the hope for the dead."},{"id":"wh-l6-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What part of the resurrection promise gives you the most hope?","explanation":"The resurrection promise centers on Jesus' return, His voice, and His power to raise the dead to life."}]}}
$wh_l6_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'the-resurrection-hope'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000077';

-- What Happens When We Die? — Lesson 5: The Second Death (guided reader JSON).
update public.lessons
set reader_content = $wh_l5_reader$
{"openingThought":"The Bible speaks about two deaths.\n\nThe first death is temporary. Jesus described it as sleep.\n\nThe second death is different.\n\nIt is final.\n\nThe second death is not a place where God keeps people alive forever in pain. It is the final end of sin, rebellion, and separation from God.\n\nThat truth is serious.\n\nBut it also shows us something hopeful:\n\nGod will not let evil live forever.","scriptureRef":"Revelation 20:14","scriptureQuote":"And death and hell were cast into the lake of fire. This is the second death.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Revelation names this clearly.\n\nThe lake of fire is called the second death.\n\nThat means this is not the same as the first death, which is sleep until resurrection.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Matthew 10:28","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"Fear him which is able to destroy both soul and body in hell.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Jesus teaches that final judgment is complete.\n\nGod is able to destroy both soul and body.\n\nThe Bible does not picture evil as immortal.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Revelation 20:6","quote":"Blessed and holy is he that hath part in the first resurrection: on such the second death hath no power."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Romans 6:23","quote":"For the wages of sin is death; but the gift of God is eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord."}],"teachingPoints":["1. The second death is final\n\nRevelation calls the lake of fire the second death.\n\nThis is not the same as the first death.\n\nThe first death is sleep until resurrection.\n\nThe second death is the final destruction of sin and those who refuse God's mercy.\n\nIt has no resurrection after it.","2. Sin ends in death\n\nRomans 6:23 says the wages of sin is death.\n\nNot eternal life in torment.\n\nDeath.\n\nSin separates from the Source of life, and separation from God cannot produce life forever.\n\nThe final result of sin is destruction.","3. God destroys both soul and body\n\nJesus said God is able to destroy both soul and body.\n\nThat means the final judgment is complete.\n\nThe Bible does not picture evil as immortal.\n\nIt pictures God bringing evil to an end.\n\nThis protects God's character.\n\nHe is just, but He is not cruel.","4. The second death has no power over the redeemed\n\nRevelation 20:6 says the second death has no power over those who belong to Christ.\n\nThis is why Jesus matters.\n\nHe tasted death for us.\nHe conquered the grave.\nHe offers eternal life.\n\nIn Him, the final death loses its claim."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Destroy","lemma":"ἀπόλλυμι","transliteration":"apollumi","meaning":"To bring to ruin, end, or complete destruction.","strongs":"G622","reference":"Matthew 10:28","note":"Jesus said God can destroy both soul and body.\n\nThis means the final judgment is not eternal preservation in suffering.\n\nIt is the complete end of sin and rebellion.\n\nGod's goal is not endless pain.\n\nHis goal is a cleansed universe where death itself is gone."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"Many people have been taught to fear God because of confused ideas about hell.\n\nBut Scripture teaches that the second death is final destruction, not eternal conscious torture.\n\nThat does not make judgment light.\n\nIt makes it clear.\n\nSin is serious.\nRebellion is serious.\nRejecting life is serious.\n\nBut God's justice does not make Him cruel.\n\nThe same God who warns about the second death is the God who gave His Son so we could have eternal life.\n\nThe second death shows the final end of sin.\n\nThe cross shows how far God went to save us from it."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"deathResurrectionFlow","stages":[{"stageName":"Life Now","description":"Human beings live, choose, trust, and respond to God in this present life.","scriptures":["Deuteronomy 30:19","John 3:16"]},{"stageName":"The First Death","description":"The first death is described by Jesus as sleep, an unconscious state awaiting resurrection.","scriptures":["John 11:11-14","Ecclesiastes 9:5"]},{"stageName":"The Resurrection","description":"Jesus will call the dead from the graves, and they will be raised.","scriptures":["John 5:28-29","1 Thessalonians 4:16"]},{"stageName":"Judgment","description":"God brings every life into truth and settles the question of each person's response to Him.","scriptures":["2 Corinthians 5:10","Revelation 20:12"]},{"stageName":"Eternal Life or Second Death","description":"The redeemed receive eternal life with Christ, while the second death brings the final end of sin and rebellion.","scriptures":["Revelation 20:6","Revelation 20:14","Romans 6:23","Revelation 21:4"]}],"highlightedIndices":[4],"subtitle":"The redeemed receive eternal life with Christ, while the second death brings the final end of sin and rebellion."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"The Second Death","references":[{"reference":"Matthew 10:28"},{"reference":"Romans 6:23"},{"reference":"Revelation 20:6"},{"reference":"Revelation 20:14"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: The second death is the final end of sin and rebellion, but those who belong to Christ are safe in His victory."}],"reflectPrompt":"How does understanding the second death as the final end of sin affect your view of God's justice?\n\nThink about the difference between God being just and God being cruel. Scripture shows that God deals with evil fully without making evil eternal.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, let the seriousness of judgment lead you toward Jesus, not away from Him.\n\nThe point is not panic.\n\nThe point is surrender.\n\nRomans 6:23 gives both sides:\n\nThe wages of sin is death.\n\nBut the gift of God is eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord.\n\nDo not stop at the warning.\n\nReceive the gift.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, help me understand Your justice clearly. Thank You that You will bring sin, death, and evil to an end. Thank You even more for giving eternal life through Jesus. Keep me from fear-based confusion, and lead me into humble trust, surrender, and hope. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"The second death is the final end of sin and rebellion. But in Christ, eternal life is offered as a gift, and the second death has no power over the redeemed.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"wh-l5-q1","prompt":"What does Revelation 20:14 call the lake of fire?","options":["The second death","A new beginning for sin","A place where evil lives forever","The first resurrection"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Revelation 20:14 says the lake of fire is the second death."},{"id":"wh-l5-q2","prompt":"According to Romans 6:23, what are the wages of sin?","options":["Death","Eternal life","Popularity","Wisdom"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Romans 6:23 says the wages of sin is death, but the gift of God is eternal life through Jesus Christ."},{"id":"wh-l5-q3","prompt":"According to Matthew 10:28, what can God destroy?","options":["Both soul and body","Scripture","His love","The resurrection promise"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus said God is able to destroy both soul and body, showing the completeness of final judgment."},{"id":"wh-l5-q4","prompt":"Who does the second death have no power over?","options":["Those who have part in the first resurrection","Those who reject Christ forever","Those who trust superstition","Those who speak to the dead"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Revelation 20:6 says the second death has no power over those who have part in the first resurrection."},{"id":"wh-l5-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"Why is eternal life described as a gift, not something we earn?","explanation":"Romans 6:23 says eternal life is the gift of God through Jesus Christ. Our hope rests in Christ's grace, not human merit."}]}}
$wh_l5_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'the-second-death'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000077';

-- What Happens When We Die? — Lesson 4: Why God Warns Against Speaking to the Dead (guided reader JSON).
update public.lessons
set reader_content = $wh_l4_reader$
{"openingThought":"If the dead are resting unconsciously, then we need to ask another question:\n\nWho are people encountering when they believe they are speaking with the dead?\n\nThe Bible does not treat this lightly.\n\nGod warns His people not to seek messages from the dead because the dead cannot communicate. That means spiritual encounters that claim to be dead loved ones are not what they appear to be.\n\nThis warning is not given to scare us.\n\nIt is given to protect us.\n\nGod wants His people grounded in truth, not vulnerable to deception.","scriptureRef":"Deuteronomy 18:10-11","scriptureQuote":"There shall not be found among you any one that maketh his son or his daughter to pass through the fire... or a consulter with familiar spirits, or a wizard, or a necromancer.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"God lists forbidden practices together.\n\nAmong them are attempts to contact the dead through mediums, spirits, and occult arts.\n\nThis is not a minor cultural detail.\n\nIt is a serious warning.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Deuteronomy 18:12","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"For all that do these things are an abomination unto the LORD.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"God does not call these practices forbidden because He is distant.\n\nHe calls them abomination because they lead people away from truth and into deception.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Deuteronomy 32:17","quote":"They sacrificed unto devils, not to God."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"2 Corinthians 11:14","quote":"And no marvel; for Satan himself is transformed into an angel of light."}],"teachingPoints":["1. God forbids attempts to speak with the dead\n\nDeuteronomy warns against necromancy, mediums, and occult practices.\n\nNecromancy means seeking communication with the dead.\n\nGod does not forbid this because He is withholding comfort.\n\nHe forbids it because it is spiritually dangerous and deceptive.","2. The dead cannot communicate\n\nPrevious lessons showed that the dead know nothing, their thoughts perish, and they do not return to their homes.\n\nSo if the dead are unconscious, they are not sending messages.\n\nThey are not appearing to guide people.\n\nThey are not speaking through mediums.\n\nAny spiritual experience claiming to be the dead must be tested by Scripture.","3. Deception can look comforting\n\n2 Corinthians says Satan can appear as an angel of light.\n\nThat means deception does not always look dark.\n\nSometimes it looks emotional, beautiful, spiritual, or comforting.\n\nThe enemy does not need a lie to look evil.\n\nHe only needs it to look believable.","4. God protects us through His word\n\nGod's warning is an act of love.\n\nHe knows grief makes people vulnerable.\n\nWhen someone deeply misses a loved one, they may become more open to anything that feels like contact.\n\nScripture protects the grieving heart by giving clear truth.\n\nThe dead are resting.\n\nJesus will raise them.\n\nWe do not seek the dead.\n\nWe trust the Lord of life."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Necromancy","lemma":"דֹּרֵשׁ אֶל־הַמֵּתִים","transliteration":"doresh el-hametim","meaning":"Seeking communication with the dead.","strongs":"H1875","reference":"Deuteronomy 18:11","note":"The Bible warns against necromancy because the dead are unconscious and cannot communicate.\n\nSpiritual encounters claiming to be the dead are deceptive and must be rejected.\n\nGod calls His people to seek truth from Him, not messages from the grave."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The Bible connects false worship, occult practices, and demonic deception.\n\nThat may sound intense, but Scripture is direct because the danger is real.\n\nIf the dead cannot communicate, then any supernatural \"message from the dead\" is not actually from the dead.\n\nThis protects us from a common deception:\n\n\"I felt comforted, so it must be from God.\"\n\nComfort alone is not the test of truth.\n\nScripture is.\n\nGod's comfort never requires us to ignore His word.\n\nThe safest place for grief is not spiritual curiosity.\n\nIt is the presence of Jesus."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"Warning and Hope","accent":"sage","payload":{"variant":"sequence","steps":[{"label":"Warning","subtitle":"Do not seek messages from the dead."},{"label":"Why","subtitle":"The dead are unconscious."},{"label":"Hope","subtitle":"Jesus will raise the dead."}]},"footnote":"God's warning is protection, not horror. His comfort is found in Christ."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Protected From Deception","references":[{"reference":"Deuteronomy 18:10-12"},{"reference":"Ecclesiastes 9:5"},{"reference":"2 Corinthians 11:14"},{"reference":"John 5:28-29"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: God warns against speaking to the dead because the dead cannot communicate, and deceptive spiritual powers can counterfeit comfort."}],"reflectPrompt":"Why do you think grief can make people vulnerable to spiritual deception?\n\nThink honestly about longing, comfort, fear, loneliness, and the desire to hear from someone who has died.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, commit to letting Scripture define spiritual truth.\n\nNot emotion alone.\nNot supernatural experiences.\nNot culture.\nNot grief.\n\nScripture.\n\nIf something contradicts God's word, reject it, even if it feels comforting.\n\nBring grief to Jesus instead.\n\nHe understands loss.\nHe wept at a tomb.\nHe promises resurrection.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, protect me from deception. Help me trust Your word when emotions are strong and grief feels heavy. Teach me not to seek comfort from forbidden places. Keep my hope anchored in Jesus, who understands sorrow and has power to raise the dead. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"God's warning is protection. The dead rest until resurrection, and Jesus gives safer comfort than any counterfeit voice from the grave.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"wh-l4-q1","prompt":"What does Deuteronomy 18 warn against?","options":["Seeking communication with the dead","Reading Scripture","Praying to God","Trusting Jesus"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Deuteronomy 18 warns against necromancy and occult practices, including attempts to communicate with the dead."},{"id":"wh-l4-q2","prompt":"Why can the dead not communicate with the living?","options":["They are unconscious and know nothing","They are too busy","They only speak at night","They become angels"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Ecclesiastes 9:5 says the dead know nothing, and Psalm 146:4 says their thoughts perish."},{"id":"wh-l4-q3","prompt":"According to 2 Corinthians 11:14, how can Satan appear?","options":["As an angel of light","As a harmless teacher only","As a Roman soldier only","As someone who always looks evil"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Satan can appear as an angel of light, meaning deception can look spiritual or comforting."},{"id":"wh-l4-q4","prompt":"What should test spiritual experiences?","options":["Scripture","Emotion alone","Popular opinion","Curiosity"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Spiritual experiences must be tested by Scripture, not merely by how comforting or supernatural they seem."},{"id":"wh-l4-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"How can you bring grief to Jesus instead of seeking comfort in spiritually dangerous places?","explanation":"Jesus understands grief, speaks truth, and promises resurrection. He is the safe place for sorrow."}]}}
$wh_l4_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'why-god-warns-against-speaking-to-the-dead'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000077';

-- What Happens When We Die? — Lesson 3: What the Dead Know (guided reader JSON).
update public.lessons
set reader_content = $wh_l3_reader$
{"openingThought":"If death is sleep, then the next question is simple:\n\nWhat do the dead know?\n\nThe Bible answers clearly.\n\nThe dead are not watching us.\nThey are not guiding us.\nThey are not suffering in secret.\nThey are not communicating from another realm.\n\nThey are resting.\n\nThat truth may feel different from what many people have heard, but it is deeply comforting. God has not left us guessing. He tells us the dead rest until the resurrection.","scriptureRef":"Ecclesiastes 9:5","scriptureQuote":"For the living know that they shall die: but the dead know not any thing.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"This is one of Scripture's clearest statements about the state of the dead.\n\nThe living are conscious.\nThe dead, in the first death, are not.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Ecclesiastes 9:6","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"Also their love, and their hatred, and their envy, is now perished.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Earthly passions and plans do not continue in the unconscious sleep of death.\n\nThe person is not lost to God.\nBut conscious activity in this life has ceased until resurrection.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Psalm 146:4","quote":"His breath goeth forth, he returneth to his earth; in that very day his thoughts perish."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Job 7:9","quote":"He that goeth down to the grave shall come up no more."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Job 7:10","quote":"He shall return no more to his house, neither shall his place know him any more."}],"teachingPoints":["1. The dead do not know anything\n\nEcclesiastes says the dead know nothing.\n\nThat is direct.\n\nDeath is not described as a conscious life somewhere else.\n\nIt is an unconscious rest.\n\nThis agrees with Jesus calling death sleep.","2. The thoughts of the dead perish\n\nPsalm 146 says when a person dies, their thoughts perish.\n\nThis does not mean God forgets them.\n\nIt means their conscious thinking stops.\n\nTheir identity is safe with God, but they are not actively thinking, watching, or speaking.","3. The dead do not return to their homes\n\nJob says the dead do not return to their houses.\n\nThis matters because many people are afraid of spirits of the dead.\n\nBut Scripture does not teach that deceased loved ones come back to visit, haunt, warn, or guide the living.\n\nThe dead rest until resurrection.","4. Resurrection is still the hope\n\nIf the dead are unconscious, then resurrection becomes even more important.\n\nThe Bible's hope is not that people float away at death.\n\nThe hope is that Jesus will raise the dead.\n\nDeath is sleep.\n\nResurrection is awakening."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Perish","lemma":"אָבַד","transliteration":"abad","meaning":"To cease, pass away, come to an end, or stop.","strongs":"H6","reference":"Psalm 146:4","note":"Psalm 146 says a person's thoughts perish at death.\n\nThis means conscious activity stops in the first death.\n\nThe person is not lost to God, but they are resting until Christ calls them from the grave."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The Bible's teaching on death protects us from confusion.\n\nWhen we believe the dead are conscious and able to communicate, we become vulnerable to fear, superstition, and deception.\n\nBut Scripture gives a clearer picture.\n\nThe dead know nothing.\nTheir thoughts have ceased.\nThey do not return to their homes.\nThey rest until the resurrection.\n\nThis does not make death less painful.\n\nBut it does remove confusion.\n\nYour loved ones who died are not trapped, wandering, watching every painful moment, or trying to reach you.\n\nThey are resting in the sleep of death until the voice of Jesus wakes them."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"A Clearer Picture","accent":"stone","payload":{"variant":"comparison","leftTitle":"Common fear","rightTitle":"Biblical teaching","leftItems":["The dead are watching","The dead are wandering","The dead are communicating"],"rightItems":["The dead rest unconsciously","They await the resurrection","Jesus will call them from the graves"]},"footnote":"Scripture does not deny grief. It brings clarity where culture often brings confusion."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"What the Dead Know","references":[{"reference":"Ecclesiastes 9:5-6"},{"reference":"Psalm 146:4"},{"reference":"Job 7:9-10"},{"reference":"John 5:28-29"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Scripture teaches that the dead are unconscious and await the resurrection when Jesus calls them from the graves."}],"reflectPrompt":"How does it affect you to know that the dead rest unconsciously until the resurrection?\n\nThink about fear, grief, comfort, and the way Scripture brings clarity where culture often brings confusion.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, let Scripture correct fear.\n\nWhen thoughts about death become heavy or confusing, return to what God has revealed:\n\nThe dead know nothing.\nTheir thoughts have ceased.\nThey do not return to the living.\nJesus will raise the dead.\n\nDo not build your hope on fear.\n\nBuild it on Christ's promise.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for speaking clearly about death. Help me trust Your word more than fear, stories, or confusion. Comfort me with the truth that the dead rest until the resurrection. Keep my hope anchored in Jesus, who will call the dead to life. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"The dead are not wandering or watching. Scripture teaches that they rest unconsciously until Jesus calls them from the grave.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"wh-l3-q1","prompt":"According to Ecclesiastes 9:5, what do the dead know?","options":["Nothing","Everything happening on earth","Secret future events","Everyone's thoughts"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Ecclesiastes 9:5 says the dead know not anything."},{"id":"wh-l3-q2","prompt":"According to Psalm 146:4, what happens to a person's thoughts at death?","options":["They perish","They become stronger","They guide the living","They turn into angels"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Psalm 146:4 says that when a person dies, their thoughts perish."},{"id":"wh-l3-q3","prompt":"According to Job 7:9-10, do the dead return to their houses?","options":["No","Yes, often","Only at night","Only to warn people"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Job 7 teaches that the dead do not return to their houses."},{"id":"wh-l3-q4","prompt":"What is the biblical hope for the dead?","options":["Resurrection through Jesus","Haunting the living","Wandering the earth","Speaking through mediums"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Bible's hope is resurrection. Jesus will call the dead from the graves."},{"id":"wh-l3-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"How can Scripture's clarity about death help protect your mind from fear or deception?","explanation":"God's word gives a clear picture of death as unconscious rest, which protects us from confusion and false spiritual claims."}]}}
$wh_l3_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'what-the-dead-know'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000077';

-- What Happens When We Die? — Lesson 2: The First Death Is Sleep (guided reader JSON).
update public.lessons
set reader_content = $wh_l2_reader$
{"openingThought":"Jesus used a surprising word for death.\n\nSleep.\n\nThat does not mean death is fake. Death is painful and real.\n\nBut Jesus wanted us to understand that the first death is not the final ending. It is a temporary state from which God can awaken His people.\n\nWhen Jesus stood before death, He did not panic.\n\nHe spoke with authority.\n\nFor Him, the grave is not a locked door.\n\nIt is a place His voice can reach.","scriptureRef":"John 11:11","scriptureQuote":"Our friend Lazarus sleepeth; but I go, that I may awake him out of sleep.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Jesus chose sleep as a picture.\n\nDeath is real. Lazarus was not merely resting for the night.\n\nBut sleep points to something death cannot erase: God can awaken the dead.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"John 11:14","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"Then said Jesus unto them plainly, Lazarus is dead.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Jesus did not deny death.\n\nHe named it plainly.\n\nSleep was not denial. It was a picture of hope.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Luke 8:52","quote":"Weep not; she is not dead, but sleepeth."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"John 11:23","quote":"Jesus saith unto her, Thy brother shall rise again."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"John 11:25","quote":"Jesus said unto her, I am the resurrection, and the life."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Jesus described the first death as sleep\n\nWhen Lazarus died, Jesus first said he was sleeping.\n\nThen He explained plainly that Lazarus was dead.\n\nThis shows us that Jesus used sleep as a picture for the first death.\n\nDeath is real, but it is not final when God plans to awaken the dead.","2. Sleep points to unconscious rest\n\nSleep is not active work.\n\nIt is not wandering.\nIt is not haunting.\nIt is not watching over the living.\n\nIt is rest.\n\nThe Bible uses sleep to help us understand that the dead are not living a separate conscious life somewhere else.\n\nThey rest until the resurrection.","3. Resurrection is the awakening\n\nJesus told Martha that her brother would rise again.\n\nHer hope was not that Lazarus was already fully alive somewhere else.\n\nHer hope was the resurrection.\n\nThat is the Bible's consistent hope.\n\nGod will awaken the dead.","4. Jesus is the resurrection and the life\n\nThe hope of resurrection is not based on human strength.\n\nIt is based on Christ.\n\nJesus does not merely teach resurrection.\n\nHe is the resurrection and the life.\n\nThe dead are safe in His memory, His power, and His promise."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Sleep","lemma":"κοιμάω","transliteration":"koimaō","meaning":"A state of rest used in Scripture as a picture for the first death.","strongs":"G2837","reference":"John 11:11","note":"When Jesus calls death sleep, He is not minimizing grief.\n\nHe is revealing hope.\n\nThe first death is temporary because Jesus has authority to awaken the dead at the resurrection."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The Bible's teaching on death protects us from fear and confusion.\n\nIf the first death is sleep, then we do not need to imagine the dead wandering, suffering, watching, or trying to communicate.\n\nThey are resting.\n\nThey are unconscious.\n\nThey are awaiting the voice of Christ.\n\nThis makes the resurrection central.\n\nChristian hope is not built on the idea that death secretly continues life in another form.\n\nChristian hope is built on Jesus raising the dead."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"deathResurrectionFlow","stages":[{"stageName":"Life Now","description":"Human beings live, choose, trust, and respond to God in this present life.","scriptures":["Deuteronomy 30:19","John 3:16"]},{"stageName":"The First Death","description":"The first death is described by Jesus as sleep, an unconscious state awaiting resurrection.","scriptures":["John 11:11-14","Luke 8:52-53"]},{"stageName":"The Resurrection","description":"Jesus will call the dead from the graves, and they will be raised.","scriptures":["John 11:23-25","John 5:28-29"]},{"stageName":"Judgment","description":"God brings every life into truth and settles the question of each person's response to Him.","scriptures":["2 Corinthians 5:10","Revelation 20:12"]},{"stageName":"Eternal Life or Second Death","description":"The redeemed receive eternal life with Christ, while the second death brings the final end of sin and rebellion.","scriptures":["Revelation 20:6","Revelation 20:14","Revelation 21:4"]}],"highlightedIndices":[1,2],"subtitle":"Focused on the first death as sleep and the resurrection as awakening."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Death as Sleep","references":[{"reference":"John 11:11-14"},{"reference":"Luke 8:52-53"},{"reference":"John 11:23-25"},{"reference":"John 5:28-29"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Jesus describes the first death as sleep and points our hope toward the resurrection."}],"reflectPrompt":"How does Jesus describing death as sleep change the way you think about the first death?\n\nThink about fear, grief, confusion, and the hope of being awakened by Christ.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, let Jesus define death for you.\n\nNot fear.\nNot movies.\nNot superstition.\nNot culture.\n\nJesus.\n\nWhen death feels terrifying, remember:\n\nThe first death is sleep.\n\nThe resurrection is awakening.\n\nChrist is the voice that reaches the grave.\n\nLet that truth steady your mind.\n\nPrayer:\n\nJesus, thank You for speaking clearly about death. Thank You that the grave is not beyond Your voice. Help me understand death through Scripture, not fear or confusion. Teach me to trust Your promise of resurrection and to rest my hope in You. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"Jesus called the first death sleep. That means the grave is not the end. The dead rest until Christ awakens them in the resurrection.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"wh-l2-q1","prompt":"What word did Jesus use to describe Lazarus' death in John 11:11?","options":["Sleep","Celebration","Punishment only","Wandering"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus said Lazarus was sleeping, then explained plainly that Lazarus was dead. He used sleep as a picture for the first death."},{"id":"wh-l2-q2","prompt":"What did Jesus say He was going to do for Lazarus?","options":["Awake him out of sleep","Forget him","Leave him in the tomb forever","Ask Lazarus to haunt the living"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus said He was going to awaken Lazarus out of sleep, pointing to His authority over death."},{"id":"wh-l2-q3","prompt":"What did Martha expect for Lazarus?","options":["That he would rise again","That he would never be seen again","That he had become an angel","That he was already haunting Bethany"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Martha expected Lazarus to rise again, showing resurrection as the biblical hope."},{"id":"wh-l2-q4","prompt":"What does the sleep picture teach about the first death?","options":["It is an unconscious rest awaiting resurrection","It is eternal torment","It is communication with the living","It is stronger than Jesus"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Sleep points to unconscious rest. The first death waits for resurrection, not ongoing conscious activity."},{"id":"wh-l2-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"Why is resurrection a stronger hope than speculation about what happens immediately after death?","explanation":"Resurrection hope is based on Jesus' authority and promise, not human guesses or fear."}]}}
$wh_l2_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'the-first-death-is-sleep'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000077';

-- What Happens When We Die? — Lesson 1: Death Is Not the End (guided reader JSON).
update public.lessons
set reader_content = $wh_l1_reader$
{"openingThought":"Death feels final.\n\nIt stops conversations.\nIt interrupts plans.\nIt leaves empty rooms and unanswered questions.\n\nBut Scripture does not treat death as the end of the story.\n\nThe Bible is honest about death, but it is not hopeless about death. Jesus speaks of resurrection. Revelation speaks of victory. God's promise is bigger than the grave.\n\nThis lesson begins with a simple truth:\n\nFor those who trust Christ, death does not get the last word.","scriptureRef":"John 11:25","scriptureQuote":"Jesus said unto her, I am the resurrection, and the life.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Revelation 2:11","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"He that overcometh shall not be hurt of the second death.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Scripture names two deaths.\n\nThe first death is the common death all people face in this broken world.\n\nThe second death is different. It is final separation from life, mercy, and restoration.\n\nJesus speaks of resurrection and victory for those who trust Him.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Revelation 20:6","quote":"Blessed and holy is he that hath part in the first resurrection: on such the second death hath no power."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"John 11:26","quote":"And whosoever liveth and believeth in me shall never die."}],"teachingPoints":["1. The Bible speaks of more than one death\n\nRevelation mentions the \"second death.\"\n\nThat means Scripture also recognizes a first death.\n\nThe first death is the common death all human beings face in this broken world.\n\nThe second death is different. It is final separation from life, mercy, and restoration.\n\nThis study will help us understand both.","2. The first death is not final for believers\n\nJesus says, \"I am the resurrection, and the life.\"\n\nThat means the grave is not stronger than Christ.\n\nThose who die trusting Him are not beyond His reach.\n\nThe first death may stop the body for a time, but it cannot cancel God's promise of resurrection.","3. The second death has no power over the redeemed\n\nRevelation 20:6 says the second death has no power over those who share in the first resurrection.\n\nThis is a serious truth, but also a hopeful one.\n\nThe Bible does not teach that evil and death continue forever.\n\nGod will bring sin to an end.\n\nThose who are in Christ are safe in His victory.","4. Our hope is in Jesus, not speculation\n\nDeath creates many questions.\n\nPeople guess.\nCultures speculate.\nFear invents stories.\n\nBut the Christian hope is not built on rumors about the afterlife.\n\nIt is built on Jesus, who says, \"I am the resurrection, and the life.\""],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Resurrection","lemma":"ἀνάστασις","transliteration":"anastasis","meaning":"To rise again, to be raised from death to life.","strongs":"G386","reference":"John 11:25","note":"The Bible's hope is not that death is harmless.\n\nThe hope is that Jesus has authority over death.\n\nResurrection means God can call life back from the grave.\n\nDeath is real, but it is not ultimate when Christ speaks."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The Bible does not ask us to pretend death is easy.\n\nEven Jesus wept at the tomb of Lazarus.\n\nBut Jesus did not only grieve.\n\nHe also declared Himself to be the resurrection and the life.\n\nThat means Christian hope is not denial.\n\nIt is confidence in the One who has power over the grave.\n\nThis study will move carefully through what Scripture teaches about death, the unconscious state of the dead, the resurrection, the second death, and the forgiveness Jesus offers.\n\nWe are not chasing fear.\n\nWe are following Scripture."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"deathResurrectionFlow","title":"Death Is Not the End","subtitle":"The Bible shows a clear path from life, to the first death, to resurrection, judgment, and final restoration."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Hope Beyond Death","references":[{"reference":"John 11:25-26"},{"reference":"Revelation 2:11"},{"reference":"Revelation 20:6"},{"reference":"1 Corinthians 15:26"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Death is real, but Jesus is the resurrection and the life. Those who trust Him have hope beyond the grave."}],"reflectPrompt":"When you think about death, what question or fear comes up most strongly for you?\n\nBe honest. This study is not asking you to pretend death is not painful. It is inviting you to bring your questions under the light of Scripture.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, anchor your hope in Jesus' words:\n\n\"I am the resurrection, and the life.\"\n\nWhen fear of death, grief, or uncertainty rises, do not start with speculation.\n\nStart with Christ.\n\nSay:\n\n\"Jesus, You are stronger than the grave.\"\n\nThen let Scripture shape your hope one truth at a time.\n\nPrayer:\n\nJesus, thank You that death does not have the final word. Thank You for being the resurrection and the life. Help me bring my fears and questions to You honestly. Teach me what Scripture says, and anchor my hope in Your victory over sin, death, and the grave. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"Death is real, but it is not final for those who trust Christ. Jesus is the resurrection and the life, and His promise reaches beyond the grave.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"wh-l1-q1","prompt":"What does Jesus call Himself in John 11:25?","options":["The resurrection and the life","The hidden gate","The end of hope","The second death"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus says, \"I am the resurrection, and the life,\" showing that hope beyond death is centered in Him."},{"id":"wh-l1-q2","prompt":"What does Revelation 2:11 mention?","options":["The second death","The first city","The fall of Egypt","The end of Scripture"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Revelation 2:11 says the one who overcomes will not be hurt by the second death."},{"id":"wh-l1-q3","prompt":"According to Revelation 20:6, what has no power over those in the first resurrection?","options":["The second death","Human weakness","Prayer","Scripture"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Revelation 20:6 says the second death has no power over those who share in the first resurrection."},{"id":"wh-l1-q4","prompt":"What is the Christian hope about death built on?","options":["Jesus and His promise of resurrection","Guessing about the afterlife","Fear of spirits","Human imagination"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Christian hope is built on Jesus, who has authority over death and promises resurrection."},{"id":"wh-l1-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"How does knowing Jesus is the resurrection and the life affect the way you face death or grief?","explanation":"The Bible does not deny grief, but it gives hope through Christ's authority over death."}]}}
$wh_l1_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'death-is-not-the-end'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000077';

-- The Way Back to God — Lesson 1: A Map Back to God (guided reader JSON).
update public.lessons
set reader_content = $wb_map_l1_reader$
{"openingThought":"Sin leaves people feeling far from God.\n\nSometimes that distance feels obvious. Other times it hides under busyness, success, religion, or distraction. But deep down, humanity still carries the ache of separation.\n\nWe were made for life with God.\n\nThe Bible says sin has separated us from Him, but it also says God has made a way back.\n\nThat way is not a maze.\n\nIt is not a guessing game.\n\nIn the sanctuary, God gave Israel a picture of the journey from separation to restoration. Every space, symbol, and sacrifice pointed forward to Jesus.\n\nThe sanctuary is a map.\n\nJesus is the way.","scriptureRef":"Psalm 77:13","scriptureQuote":"Thy way, O God, is in the sanctuary: who is so great a God as our God?","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Isaiah 59:2","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"But your iniquities have separated between you and your God.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Scripture names both truths together.\n\nSin creates real separation.\n\nYet God reveals His way where His holiness meets mercy.","scriptureWordInsights":[{"surface":"way","lemma":"דֶּרֶךְ","transliteration":"derek","meaning":"Path, road, direction, or journey.","strongs":"H1870","reference":"Psalm 77:13","note":"Psalm 77:13 says God's \"way\" is in the sanctuary.\n\nThis means the sanctuary reveals a journey. It shows the path God has opened for sinners to come back into restored relationship with Him.\n\nIn the New Testament, Jesus brings the meaning into focus when He says, \"I am the way.\""}],"scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"John 14:6","quote":"Jesus saith unto him, I am the way, the truth, and the life: no man cometh unto the Father, but by me."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Hebrews 10:19-20","quote":"Having therefore, brethren, boldness to enter into the holiest by the blood of Jesus. By a new and living way, which he hath consecrated for us, through the veil, that is to say, his flesh."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Sin creates separation\n\nIsaiah 59:2 says sin separates humanity from God.\n\nThis does not mean God stops loving us.\n\nIt means sin damages relationship, trust, desire, and spiritual life.\n\nSeparation is the problem the plan of salvation answers.\n\nThe sanctuary begins with this reality:\n\nWe need a way back.","2. God's way is revealed in the sanctuary\n\nPsalm 77:13 says God's way is in the sanctuary.\n\nThe sanctuary was not just a religious building.\n\nIt was a symbolic path.\n\nIt showed how God would deal with sin, cleanse His people, restore relationship, and bring humanity back into His presence.","3. Jesus is the true way back to God\n\nJohn 14:6 makes the sanctuary's meaning clear.\n\nJesus is the way.\n\nHe is not merely one stop on the path.\n\nHe is the path itself.\n\nEverything in the sanctuary points to some part of His saving work.","4. The sanctuary moves from distance to nearness\n\nThe sanctuary journey moved from the outer court toward the Most Holy Place.\n\nThat movement matters.\n\nIt pictured the journey from separation to restored fellowship.\n\nGod's plan is not only to forgive us at a distance.\n\nHis plan is to bring us near."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudy":{"surface":"Way","lemma":"דֶּרֶךְ","transliteration":"derek","meaning":"Path, road, direction, or journey.","strongs":"H1870","reference":"Psalm 77:13","note":"Psalm 77:13 says God's \"way\" is in the sanctuary.\n\nThis means the sanctuary reveals a journey. It shows the path God has opened for sinners to come back into restored relationship with Him.\n\nIn the New Testament, Jesus brings the meaning into focus when He says, \"I am the way.\""},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The sanctuary helps us see the plan of salvation in motion.\n\nThe courtyard points to sacrifice and cleansing.\n\nThe Holy Place points to daily dependence, light, and prayer.\n\nThe Most Holy Place points to God's presence, His law of love, and final restoration.\n\nBut none of these symbols stand alone.\n\nThey all point to Jesus.\n\nHe is the Door.\nHe is the Lamb.\nHe is the cleansing Savior.\nHe is the Bread of Life.\nHe is the Light of the world.\nHe is our Intercessor.\nHe is our High Priest.\nHe is the One who brings us into the presence of God.\n\nThe sanctuary is not about ancient furniture first.\n\nIt is about Christ."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"sanctuaryJourneyMap","title":"The Way Back to God","subtitle":"The sanctuary shows the journey from separation to restored relationship."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"God Made a Way","references":[{"reference":"Isaiah 59:2"},{"reference":"Psalm 77:13"},{"reference":"John 14:6"},{"reference":"Hebrews 10:19-20"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Sin separated humanity from God, but Jesus is the living way back into God's presence."}],"reflectPrompt":"Where do you most feel the need for God to bring you closer to Him right now?\n\nThink about trust, prayer, obedience, peace, forgiveness, spiritual hunger, or a sense of distance from God.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, do not think of salvation as God keeping you at arm's length.\n\nThink of it as God making a way for you to come near.\n\nWhen you feel distant, pray simply:\n\n\"Jesus, lead me back to the Father.\"\n\nThen take one step.\n\nOpen Scripture.\nPray honestly.\nConfess what needs light.\nReceive grace.\nFollow the next truth God shows you.\n\nThe journey back to God is not traveled by human pride.\n\nIt is traveled by trusting Jesus as the way.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for making a way back to You through Jesus. Help me understand the sanctuary as a picture of Your saving love. When I feel far from You, remind me that Christ is the way, the truth, and the life. Lead me from distance into restored relationship with You. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"The sanctuary is a map, but Jesus is the way. God has not left humanity in separation. He has opened a living path back to Himself.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"wb-l1-q1","prompt":"According to Isaiah 59:2, what has separated humanity from God?","options":["Iniquities","Weather","Lack of intelligence","Angels"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Isaiah 59:2 says iniquities have separated between humanity and God."},{"id":"wb-l1-q2","prompt":"According to Psalm 77:13, where is God's way revealed?","options":["In the sanctuary","In Egypt's throne room","In human pride","In the serpent's lie"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Psalm 77:13 says, \"Thy way, O God, is in the sanctuary.\""},{"id":"wb-l1-q3","prompt":"What does Jesus say in John 14:6?","options":["\"I am the way, the truth, and the life\"","\"I am one road among many\"","\"No one can know the Father\"","\"The sanctuary has no meaning\""],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus identifies Himself as the way, the truth, and the life. The sanctuary path finds its meaning in Him."},{"id":"wb-l1-q4","prompt":"What is the sanctuary mainly pointing us toward?","options":["Jesus and His work of salvation","Ancient furniture only","Human achievement","A building with no spiritual meaning"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The sanctuary symbols point forward to Jesus and the path God opened for humanity's restoration."},{"id":"wb-l1-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What step toward God do you sense Jesus inviting you to take this week?","explanation":"The sanctuary shows a journey back to God. Jesus leads us one step at a time into restored relationship."}]}}
$wb_map_l1_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'a-map-back-to-god'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000076';

-- The Way Back to God — Lesson 2: The Door and the Sacrifice (guided reader JSON).
update public.lessons
set reader_content = $wb_door_l2_reader$
{"openingThought":"The sanctuary journey began with entrance.\n\nBefore a person reached the altar, the laver, the Holy Place, or the Most Holy Place, they first had to come through the door.\n\nThat detail matters.\n\nGod was teaching Israel that the way back to Him is not self-made. We do not create our own path into His presence.\n\nJesus makes the meaning clear when He says, \"I am the door.\"\n\nThe sanctuary begins where salvation begins:\n\nAccess through Christ.\n\nAnd once inside the courtyard, the first major symbol was sacrifice.\n\nThe way back to God is opened by Jesus, and the cost of that way is the life He gave for us.","scriptureRef":"John 10:9","scriptureQuote":"I am the door: by me if any man enter in, he shall be saved, and shall go in and out, and find pasture.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Jesus is naming something essential.\n\nSinners do not design their own path into God's presence.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"John 1:29","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"Behold the Lamb of God, which taketh away the sin of the world.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"The Lamb language continues the courtyard story.\n\nSacrifice speaks of substitution, seriousness, and the cost God would bear.","scriptureWordInsights":[{"surface":"door","lemma":"θύρα","transliteration":"thyra","meaning":"An entrance, access point, or way in.","strongs":"G2374","reference":"John 10:9","note":"When Jesus says, \"I am the door,\" He is saying that restored relationship with God begins through Him.\n\nThe sanctuary door was not just architecture.\n\nIt pointed to the truth that sinners need access, and Christ provides it."}],"scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Hebrews 10:19-20","quote":"Having therefore, brethren, boldness to enter into the holiest by the blood of Jesus. By a new and living way, which he hath consecrated for us, through the veil, that is to say, his flesh."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"1 Peter 1:18-19","quote":"Ye were not redeemed with corruptible things, as silver and gold. But with the precious blood of Christ, as of a lamb without blemish and without spot."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Jesus is the door into salvation\n\nJesus says, \"I am the door.\"\n\nThat is not vague religious language.\n\nIt means access to God comes through Him.\n\nThe sanctuary door pointed forward to Christ as the entrance into restored relationship.\n\nWe do not enter by pride, performance, ancestry, intelligence, or religious image.\n\nWe enter through Jesus.","2. The altar showed the cost of sin\n\nInside the courtyard stood the altar of sacrifice.\n\nThe altar taught that sin is serious.\n\nSin is not a small misunderstanding or harmless mistake.\n\nIt brings death, separation, guilt, and brokenness.\n\nThe sacrifice showed that restoration would come at a cost.","3. Jesus is the Lamb of God\n\nJohn the Baptist saw Jesus and said, \"Behold the Lamb of God.\"\n\nThe sanctuary sacrifices pointed beyond themselves.\n\nThey pointed to Christ.\n\nAnimals could not ultimately remove sin, but they taught the lesson of substitution.\n\nThe innocent would stand in the place of the guilty.\n\nJesus fulfilled that symbol completely.","4. The blood of Jesus opens the way\n\nHebrews says we have boldness to enter by the blood of Jesus.\n\nThat means the way back to God is not built on denial, excuses, or self-improvement.\n\nIt is built on Christ's sacrifice.\n\nThe cross opens the way into God's presence."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudy":{"surface":"Door","lemma":"θύρα","transliteration":"thyra","meaning":"An entrance, access point, or way in.","strongs":"G2374","reference":"John 10:9","note":"When Jesus says, \"I am the door,\" He is saying that restored relationship with God begins through Him.\n\nThe sanctuary door was not just architecture.\n\nIt pointed to the truth that sinners need access, and Christ provides it."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The courtyard gives us two major truths:\n\nJesus is the way in.\n\nJesus is the sacrifice that makes the way possible.\n\nThe door tells us we need access.\n\nThe altar tells us access is costly.\n\nThis keeps us from two wrong ideas.\n\nFirst, we should not think we can save ourselves.\n\nSecond, we should not think God forgives by pretending sin does not matter.\n\nGod takes sin seriously.\n\nGod takes love seriously too.\n\nAt the cross, both truths meet.\n\nSin is judged.\n\nLove is revealed.\n\nThe way back to God is opened."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"sanctuaryJourneyMap","highlightedIndices":[0,1],"subtitle":"Focused on the courtyard entrance and the altar: Jesus is how we enter, and Jesus is the Lamb who bore the cost."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"The Way In","references":[{"reference":"John 10:9"},{"reference":"John 1:29"},{"reference":"Hebrews 10:19-20"},{"reference":"1 Peter 1:18-19"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Jesus is both the door into salvation and the Lamb whose sacrifice opens the way back to God."}],"reflectPrompt":"Where are you most tempted to try to enter God's presence by your own effort instead of trusting Jesus?\n\nThink about performance, guilt, religious image, comparison, or trying to make yourself \"good enough\" before coming to God.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, come to God through Jesus instead of through self-measurement.\n\nWhen you feel unworthy, do not wait until you feel impressive.\n\nCome through the Door.\n\nWhen guilt tells you to stay away, remember the Lamb.\n\nSay:\n\n\"Jesus, You are my way in.\"\n\nThe Christian life does not begin with proving yourself.\n\nIt begins with trusting the One who gave Himself for you.\n\nPrayer:\n\nJesus, thank You for being the door and the sacrifice. Thank You for opening the way back to the Father through Your blood. Help me stop trusting my performance and start trusting Your finished work. Teach me to come near with humility, faith, and gratitude. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"The sanctuary journey begins with Christ. Jesus is the Door who gives access and the Lamb whose sacrifice opens the way back to God.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"wb-l2-q1","prompt":"What does Jesus say in John 10:9?","options":["I am the door","I am only a symbol","Find your own way","There is no way back"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus says, \"I am the door,\" showing that access to salvation and restored relationship with God comes through Him."},{"id":"wb-l2-q2","prompt":"What did John the Baptist call Jesus in John 1:29?","options":["The Lamb of God","The builder of Rome","The hidden prophet","The golden altar"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"John called Jesus the Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world."},{"id":"wb-l2-q3","prompt":"What did the altar of sacrifice teach?","options":["Sin is serious and restoration comes at a cost","Sin does not matter","Humans can save themselves","Sacrifice was unrelated to Jesus"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The altar showed the seriousness of sin and pointed forward to the cost of redemption in Christ."},{"id":"wb-l2-q4","prompt":"According to Hebrews 10:19-20, what gives us boldness to enter?","options":["The blood of Jesus","Our popularity","Our perfect record","Our intelligence"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Hebrews says we have boldness to enter by the blood of Jesus."},{"id":"wb-l2-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"How does seeing Jesus as both the Door and the Lamb change the way you approach God?","explanation":"Jesus gives access and provides the sacrifice. We come to God through His grace, not our own worthiness."}]}}
$wb_door_l2_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'the-door-and-the-sacrifice'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000076';

-- The Way Back to God — Lesson 3: Washed and Made New (guided reader JSON).
update public.lessons
set reader_content = $wb_laver_l3_reader$
{"openingThought":"After the altar came the laver.\n\nThe altar pointed to sacrifice.\n\nThe laver pointed to cleansing.\n\nGod was teaching something simple but powerful: salvation is not only about forgiveness. It is also about renewal.\n\nJesus does not simply open the way back to God and leave us unchanged. He cleanses what sin has stained. He renews what sin has damaged. He begins restoring the heart from the inside out.\n\nThe way back to God includes being washed and made new.","scriptureRef":"Titus 3:5","scriptureQuote":"Not by works of righteousness which we have done, but according to his mercy he saved us, by the washing of regeneration, and renewing of the Holy Ghost.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Mercy reaches us before our performance qualifies us.\n\nForgiveness connects to regeneration and renewing work by the Holy Spirit.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Ezekiel 36:25-27","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"Then will I sprinkle clean water upon you, and ye shall be clean.\n\nA new heart also will I give you, and a new spirit will I put within you.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"God is not only concerned with what we have done.\n\nHe is concerned with who we are becoming in Him.","scriptureWordInsights":[{"surface":"regeneration","lemma":"παλιγγενεσία","transliteration":"paliggensia","meaning":"New birth, renewal, or being made new.","strongs":"G3824","reference":"Titus 3:5","note":"Titus 3:5 connects salvation with washing and regeneration.\n\nGod's goal is not merely to forgive the old life.\n\nHe begins a new life in us through the Holy Spirit.\n\nThe laver pointed forward to this cleansing and renewing work."}],"scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"1 John 1:9","quote":"If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just to forgive us our sins, and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness."}],"teachingPoints":["1. The laver pointed to cleansing\n\nThe laver was used for washing.\n\nAfter sacrifice, cleansing was part of the sanctuary journey.\n\nThis teaches that God does not only deal with the guilt of sin.\n\nHe also deals with the uncleanness sin leaves behind.\n\nGrace forgives.\n\nGrace also cleanses.","2. God saves by mercy, not self-repair\n\nTitus 3:5 says God saves us according to His mercy.\n\nThat matters because cleansing is not something we manufacture.\n\nWe do not scrub ourselves into spiritual life.\n\nGod washes, renews, and restores through the Holy Spirit.","3. God promises a new heart\n\nEzekiel 36 says God will give His people a new heart and put His Spirit within them.\n\nThis is deeper than behavior management.\n\nGod wants to change the inner life.\n\nHe renews desire, trust, love, and obedience from the heart.","4. Confession brings sin into the light\n\n1 John 1:9 says God forgives and cleanses when we confess.\n\nConfession is not informing God of something He does not know.\n\nIt is agreeing with God about what is true.\n\nWhen we stop hiding, grace can begin washing what shame tried to cover."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudy":{"surface":"Regeneration","lemma":"παλιγγενεσία","transliteration":"paliggensia","meaning":"New birth, renewal, or being made new.","strongs":"G3824","reference":"Titus 3:5","note":"Titus 3:5 connects salvation with washing and regeneration.\n\nGod's goal is not merely to forgive the old life.\n\nHe begins a new life in us through the Holy Spirit.\n\nThe laver pointed forward to this cleansing and renewing work."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The sanctuary journey shows a beautiful order.\n\nFirst, the sacrifice.\n\nThen, the washing.\n\nThis order matters.\n\nWe are not cleansed so that Jesus will accept us.\n\nJesus receives us and then begins cleansing us.\n\nThat means transformation is not the price of coming to God.\n\nTransformation is the result of coming to God.\n\nThe laver points to the Holy Spirit's work of renewal.\n\nGod forgives the record.\n\nThen He begins restoring the person."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"sanctuaryJourneyMap","highlightedIndices":[2],"subtitle":"Focused on cleansing and renewal: the laver points to the Holy Spirit washing and renewing the heart."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Washed and Renewed","references":[{"reference":"Titus 3:5"},{"reference":"Ezekiel 36:25-27"},{"reference":"1 John 1:9"},{"reference":"Psalm 51:10"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: God does not only forgive sin. He cleanses, renews, and creates a new heart through the Holy Spirit."}],"reflectPrompt":"Where do you need God's cleansing and renewal right now?\n\nThink about guilt, habits, thoughts, desires, shame, or an area where you have been trying to change yourself without surrendering it to God.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, practice honest confession without hiding.\n\nBring one specific thing to God and say:\n\n\"Lord, I agree with You about this. Cleanse me and renew my heart.\"\n\nDo not stop at guilt.\n\nAsk for renewal.\n\nDo not only ask God to forgive what happened.\n\nAsk Him to restore what sin damaged.\n\nThe same grace that brings you through the door also washes you at the laver.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for saving me according to Your mercy. Thank You for forgiveness, cleansing, and renewal. Wash what sin has stained. Give me a new heart and a willing spirit. Help me stop hiding and let Your grace make me new through the Holy Spirit. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"The laver reminds us that God does more than forgive. He washes, renews, and begins making the heart new through the Holy Spirit.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"wb-l3-q1","prompt":"What did the laver point to in the sanctuary journey?","options":["Cleansing and renewal","Pride and performance","Political power","The end of prayer"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The laver was used for washing and pointed to God's work of cleansing and renewal."},{"id":"wb-l3-q2","prompt":"According to Titus 3:5, how does God save us?","options":["According to His mercy","According to our perfection","According to our popularity","According to our excuses"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Titus 3:5 says God saves us according to His mercy, by the washing of regeneration and renewing of the Holy Ghost."},{"id":"wb-l3-q3","prompt":"What does God promise in Ezekiel 36?","options":["A new heart and a new spirit","A life without choices","A reason to keep hiding","A replacement for Jesus"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Ezekiel 36 promises that God will give His people a new heart and put His Spirit within them."},{"id":"wb-l3-q4","prompt":"According to 1 John 1:9, what does God do when we confess our sins?","options":["Forgives and cleanses us","Rejects us forever","Pretends sin is harmless","Stops listening"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"1 John 1:9 says God is faithful and just to forgive our sins and cleanse us from all unrighteousness."},{"id":"wb-l3-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"How does it help you to know that God cleanses after He receives you?","explanation":"Transformation is not the price of coming to God. It is the result of coming to Him through Jesus."}]}}
$wb_laver_l3_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'washed-and-made-new'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000076';

-- The Way Back to God — Lesson 4: Bread, Light, and Prayer (guided reader JSON).
update public.lessons
set reader_content = $wb_holy_l4_reader$
{"openingThought":"The sanctuary journey did not stop in the courtyard.\n\nAfter sacrifice and cleansing, the priest entered the Holy Place.\n\nInside were three powerful symbols:\n\nBread.\n\nLight.\n\nIncense.\n\nThese were not random decorations. They pointed to the daily life of faith.\n\nGod does not only forgive and cleanse us. He sustains us, guides us, and invites us into prayer.\n\nThe way back to God is not a one-time moment.\n\nIt is a daily walk with Jesus.","scriptureRef":"John 6:35","scriptureQuote":"And Jesus said unto them, I am the bread of life: he that cometh to me shall never hunger; and he that believeth on me shall never thirst.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Bread language here is richer than metaphor.\n\nJesus is naming Himself as spiritual life itself.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"John 8:12","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"Then spake Jesus again unto them, saying, I am the light of the world: he that followeth me shall not walk in darkness, but shall have the light of life.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Light contrasts with wandering in confusion and moral darkness.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Revelation 8:3-4","quote":"And another angel came and stood at the altar, having a golden censer; and there was given unto him much incense, that he should offer it with the prayers of all saints upon the golden altar which was before the throne.\n\nAnd the smoke of the incense, which came with the prayers of the saints, ascended up before God out of the angel's hand."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Hebrews 7:25","quote":"Wherefore he is able also to save them to the uttermost that come unto God by him, seeing he ever liveth to make intercession for them."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Jesus is the Bread of Life\n\nThe table of bread pointed to Jesus.\n\nBread sustains physical life.\n\nJesus sustains spiritual life.\n\nHe feeds the soul with His presence, His word, and His grace.\n\nWe do not live by spiritual excitement alone.\n\nWe live by daily dependence on Christ.","2. Jesus is the Light of the world\n\nThe lampstand gave light in the Holy Place.\n\nJesus said, \"I am the light of the world.\"\n\nSin darkens perception.\n\nJesus restores sight.\n\nHe shows us who God is, who we are, and where the path of life leads.\n\nFollowing Him means we do not have to walk in darkness.","3. Prayer rises before God\n\nThe altar of incense pointed to prayer and intercession.\n\nRevelation connects incense with the prayers of the saints.\n\nThis means prayer is not wasted breath.\n\nThrough Christ, our prayers rise before God.\n\nEven weak prayers, honest prayers, and tearful prayers matter.","4. Jesus ministers for us now\n\nHebrews says Jesus lives to make intercession.\n\nThat means Jesus is not only the sacrifice who died.\n\nHe is the living Savior who ministers for us.\n\nHe sustains, guides, and intercedes as we continue the journey."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudy":{"surface":"Intercession","lemma":"ἔντευξις","transliteration":"enteuxis","meaning":"To plead, represent, or act on behalf of another.","strongs":"G1783","reference":"Hebrews 7:25","note":"Jesus intercedes for us.\n\nThis does not mean the Father is unwilling to love us and Jesus has to convince Him.\n\nIt means Jesus represents us before the Father and applies the benefits of His saving work to our lives.\n\nHis ministry is ongoing, personal, and faithful."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The Holy Place shows the daily rhythm of life with God.\n\nBread points to dependence.\n\nLight points to guidance.\n\nIncense points to prayer.\n\nThis is not complicated, but it is deep.\n\nA restored relationship with God grows through daily receiving, daily following, and daily communion.\n\nJesus is not only the door we enter through.\n\nHe is the life we continue with.\n\nHe feeds us.\nHe lights the way.\nHe carries our prayers.\nHe ministers for us.\n\nThe Christian journey is sustained by Christ from beginning to end."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"sanctuaryJourneyMap","highlightedIndices":[3,4,5],"subtitle":"Focused on the Holy Place: bread, light, and prayer point to Jesus as our daily Shepherd in word, truth, and intercession."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Daily Life With Jesus","references":[{"reference":"John 6:35"},{"reference":"John 8:12"},{"reference":"Revelation 8:3-4"},{"reference":"Hebrews 7:25"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Jesus sustains us as the Bread of Life, guides us as the Light of the world, and ministers for us as our living Intercessor."}],"reflectPrompt":"Which part of your daily walk with God needs attention right now: being fed by His word, following His light, or coming to Him in prayer?\n\nChoose one area honestly. The goal is not guilt. The goal is noticing where Jesus is inviting you deeper.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, build a simple Holy Place rhythm.\n\nBread:\nRead one short passage of Scripture each day.\n\nLight:\nAsk, \"Jesus, what truth do You want me to walk in today?\"\n\nPrayer:\nBring one honest prayer to God before the day gets loud.\n\nKeep it simple.\n\nThe point is not to impress God.\n\nThe point is to stay near Him.\n\nPrayer:\n\nJesus, thank You for sustaining me, guiding me, and carrying my prayers before the Father. Feed me with Your word. Lead me by Your light. Teach me to pray honestly and consistently. Help me walk with You daily, not only in big moments, but in ordinary ones too. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"The Holy Place reminds us that Jesus sustains, guides, and intercedes for us. The way back to God becomes a daily walk with Christ.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"wb-l4-q1","prompt":"What did Jesus call Himself in John 6:35?","options":["The bread of life","The closed gate","The hidden altar","The final prophet only"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus said, \"I am the bread of life,\" showing that He sustains spiritual life."},{"id":"wb-l4-q2","prompt":"What did the lampstand point to?","options":["Jesus as the Light of the world","Human pride","The end of prayer","The serpent's deception"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The lampstand pointed to Christ as the Light who guides His people."},{"id":"wb-l4-q3","prompt":"What is incense connected with in Revelation 8:3-4?","options":["The prayers of the saints","The fall of Babylon only","The creation of animals","The walls of Jericho"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Revelation connects incense with the prayers of the saints ascending before God."},{"id":"wb-l4-q4","prompt":"According to Hebrews 7:25, what does Jesus live to do?","options":["Make intercession","Hide from sinners","Stop forgiving","End Scripture"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Hebrews says Jesus ever lives to make intercession for those who come to God through Him."},{"id":"wb-l4-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What simple daily rhythm could help you stay closer to Jesus this week?","explanation":"The Holy Place points to daily dependence, guidance, and prayer. Small faithful steps matter."}]}}
$wb_holy_l4_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'bread-light-and-prayer'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000076';

-- The Way Back to God — Lesson 5: God's Love Written Within (guided reader JSON).
update public.lessons
set reader_content = $wb_mhp_l5_reader$
{"openingThought":"The sanctuary journey moved toward the Most Holy Place.\n\nThere, behind the veil, was the ark of the covenant.\n\nInside the ark was God's law.\n\nBut God's goal was never to keep His law only on stone. His desire was to write His law in human hearts.\n\nThat matters.\n\nThe way back to God is not just about being forgiven.\n\nIt is about being restored so deeply that God's love begins shaping how we think, desire, choose, and live.\n\nGod wants His love written within us.","scriptureRef":"Hebrews 10:16","scriptureQuote":"This is the covenant that I will make with them after those days, saith the Lord, I will put my laws into their hearts, and in their minds will I write them;","scriptureAfterPrimary":"This promise ties the sanctuary symbols to inward transformation.\n\nGod is speaking about a covenant that reshapes desire and direction from within.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Jeremiah 31:33","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"But this shall be the covenant that I will make with the house of Israel; After those days, saith the LORD, I will put my law in their inward parts, and write it in their hearts; and will be their God, and they shall be my people.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"God's law moves from outward testimony toward inward relationship.\n\nHeart language is intimacy language.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Exodus 25:21-22","quote":"And thou shalt put the mercy seat above upon the ark; and in the ark thou shalt put the testimony that I shall give thee.\n\nAnd there I will meet with thee, and I will commune with thee from above the mercy seat, from between the two cherubims which are upon the ark of the testimony, of all things which I will give thee in commandment unto the children of Israel."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Romans 13:10","quote":"Love worketh no ill to his neighbour: therefore love is the fulfilling of the law."}],"teachingPoints":["1. The ark held God's law\n\nThe ark of the covenant was placed in the Most Holy Place.\n\nInside it were the tables of the law.\n\nThis showed that God's law is not a side issue.\n\nHis law reflects His character and His way of love.\n\nGod's presence and God's law belong together because God's government is built on love.","2. The mercy seat covered the law\n\nThe mercy seat was placed above the ark.\n\nThat picture matters.\n\nGod's law was not separated from mercy.\n\nMercy did not erase the law.\n\nMercy met humanity where the law revealed the seriousness of sin.\n\nIn Jesus, justice and mercy meet perfectly.","3. God wants His law written in the heart\n\nJeremiah and Hebrews both speak of God writing His law within His people.\n\nThis is not forced religion.\n\nIt is inward restoration.\n\nGod wants His love to become part of how we think, choose, and live.\n\nHe does not only want obedience around us.\n\nHe wants love within us.","4. Love fulfills the law\n\nRomans 13:10 says love is the fulfilling of the law.\n\nThis means God's law is not cold control.\n\nIt is the shape of self-giving love.\n\nWhen God writes His law in the heart, He is restoring us into the life we were created for."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudy":{"surface":"Covenant","lemma":"διαθήκη","transliteration":"diathēkē","meaning":"A binding relationship promise.","strongs":"G1242","reference":"Hebrews 10:16","note":"God's covenant is not merely a contract.\n\nIt is His faithful promise to restore relationship.\n\nWhen God says He will write His law in our hearts, He is promising deeper restoration than outward behavior.\n\nHe is promising a heart shaped by love."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The Most Holy Place shows us something beautiful about God.\n\nAt the center was His presence.\n\nAt the center was His law.\n\nAt the center was mercy.\n\nThat combination tells the truth about God's character.\n\nHe is holy.\nHe is loving.\nHe is just.\nHe is merciful.\n\nThe sanctuary does not show a God who ignores sin.\n\nIt shows a God who makes a way to restore sinners.\n\nThe goal is not just forgiveness from guilt.\n\nThe goal is a heart made new.\n\nJesus brings us near to God, cleanses us, sustains us, intercedes for us, and writes love into us by His Spirit."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"sanctuaryJourneyMap","highlightedIndices":[6],"subtitle":"Focused on the Most Holy Place: the ark pictures God's presence, His law of love, and His promise to write that law within His people."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Written on the Heart","references":[{"reference":"Exodus 25:21-22"},{"reference":"Jeremiah 31:33"},{"reference":"Hebrews 10:16"},{"reference":"Romans 13:10"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: God's goal is not only outward obedience. He wants to write His law of love in the heart."}],"reflectPrompt":"Where do you need God to write His love more deeply into your heart?\n\nThink about your thoughts, habits, relationships, desires, reactions, or the way you respond when life presses you.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, ask God for more than behavior change.\n\nAsk Him for heart change.\n\nPray:\n\n\"Lord, write Your love in me.\"\n\nThen watch one area carefully.\n\nHow do you respond when interrupted?\nHow do you treat people when you are tired?\nHow do you think about someone who hurt you?\nHow do you handle temptation when no one sees?\n\nGod is not only forming better habits.\n\nHe is forming a heart that reflects His love.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for wanting more than outward religion. Write Your law of love in my heart and mind. Shape my thoughts, desires, choices, and relationships by Your Spirit. Bring me near to You, and make my life reflect the love revealed in Jesus. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"The Most Holy Place reveals God's goal: not only forgiven people, but restored hearts. God wants His law of love written within us.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"wb-l5-q1","prompt":"What was placed inside the ark of the covenant?","options":["God's law","Roman coins","A map of Egypt","A crown for Pharaoh"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The ark contained the tables of the law, showing the importance of God's character and government of love."},{"id":"wb-l5-q2","prompt":"Where does Hebrews 10:16 say God will write His laws?","options":["In hearts and minds","On city walls only","In the clouds only","On Roman scrolls"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Hebrews 10:16 says God will put His laws into hearts and write them in minds."},{"id":"wb-l5-q3","prompt":"What does Romans 13:10 say fulfills the law?","options":["Love","Fear","Pride","Performance"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Romans 13:10 says love is the fulfilling of the law."},{"id":"wb-l5-q4","prompt":"What does the mercy seat show us?","options":["God's mercy meets humanity where His law reveals sin","God does not care about sin","God's law has no meaning","Prayer is unnecessary"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The mercy seat above the ark shows that God's mercy and justice meet in His plan to restore sinners."},{"id":"wb-l5-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What would change if God's love shaped your reactions more deeply this week?","explanation":"God's goal is inward restoration. He wants His love to shape not only behavior, but the heart behind it."}]}}
$wb_mhp_l5_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'gods-love-written-within'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000076';

-- The Way Back to God — Lesson 6: The Daily Path of Grace (guided reader JSON).
update public.lessons
set reader_content = $wb_daily_l6_reader$
{"openingThought":"The sanctuary was not only about one big moment.\n\nIt had a daily rhythm.\n\nDay after day, sacrifices were offered. Day after day, priests ministered. Day after day, the sanctuary reminded Israel that sin was serious, grace was available, and God was making a way back.\n\nThis daily service pointed beyond itself.\n\nIt pointed to Jesus.\n\nHe is not only the Lamb who died once for all. He is also our living Savior who continues to minister for us.\n\nThe way back to God is sustained by daily grace.","scriptureRef":"Leviticus 4:31","scriptureQuote":"And the priest shall burn it upon the altar for a sweet savour unto the LORD; and the priest shall make an atonement for him, and it shall be forgiven him.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Forgiveness is named plainly.\n\nGrace met real people in ordinary days, again and again.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Hebrews 9:22","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"And almost all things are by the law purged with blood; and without shedding of blood is no remission.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Sin is costly.\n\nGod does not pretend otherwise.\n\nMercy arrives through God's provided way, not denial.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"1 Peter 1:18-19","quote":"Ye were not redeemed with corruptible things, as silver and gold.\n\nBut with the precious blood of Christ, as of a lamb without blemish and without spot."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Hebrews 7:25","quote":"Wherefore he is able also to save them to the uttermost that come unto God by him, seeing he ever liveth to make intercession for them."}],"teachingPoints":["1. The daily service showed the seriousness of sin\n\nThe daily sacrifices taught Israel that sin was not harmless.\n\nSin damages relationship.\nSin brings guilt.\nSin requires atonement.\n\nThe sanctuary did not minimize sin.\n\nIt exposed the cost of it.\n\nBut it also showed that God had provided a way for forgiveness.","2. A substitute stood in the sinner's place\n\nThe sacrifice pointed to substitution.\n\nThe innocent stood in place of the guilty.\n\nThis was not because God enjoyed death.\n\nIt was because God was teaching that sin leads to death, and only a provided substitute could open the way to forgiveness.\n\nEvery sacrifice pointed forward to Jesus, the true Lamb.","3. Forgiveness was part of the daily path\n\nLeviticus says atonement was made and the person was forgiven.\n\nThis matters because God's grace met people in real life.\n\nThe daily service reminded Israel that God was not waiting to destroy anyone who failed.\n\nHe was providing a way for sinners to come honestly and receive mercy.","4. Jesus continues to minister for us\n\nHebrews says Jesus lives to make intercession.\n\nThat means His work for us did not end with the cross.\n\nHis sacrifice is complete, but His ministry continues.\n\nHe applies the benefits of His sacrifice, represents us before the Father, and carries us forward in grace."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudy":{"surface":"Atonement","lemma":"כָּפַר","transliteration":"kāphar","meaning":"The act of making reconciliation, covering sin, and restoring relationship.","strongs":"H3722","reference":"Leviticus 4:31","note":"Atonement is not just a religious word.\n\nIt is about God dealing with sin so relationship can be restored.\n\nIn the sanctuary, atonement pointed forward to Jesus, who brings forgiveness, cleansing, and restored nearness to God."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The daily sanctuary service teaches balance.\n\nSin is serious.\n\nGrace is real.\n\nBoth truths matter.\n\nIf we only talk about sin, we can become afraid of God.\n\nIf we only talk about grace without the cost of sin, we can treat evil lightly.\n\nThe sanctuary holds both together.\n\nSin brings death.\n\nGod provides a substitute.\n\nThe sinner comes honestly.\n\nThe priest ministers.\n\nForgiveness is given.\n\nJesus fulfills the whole pattern.\n\nHe is the Lamb who gave His life.\n\nHe is the Priest who ministers for us.\n\nHe is the grace that keeps the way back to God open."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"sanctuaryJourneyMap","stages":[{"stageName":"The Door","location":"Entrance to the Courtyard","symbol":"Access through Christ","meaning":"Jesus is the way into restored relationship with God.","scriptures":["John 10:9","John 14:6"]},{"stageName":"The Altar of Sacrifice","location":"Courtyard","symbol":"The Lamb","meaning":"Jesus gave His life for our sins.","scriptures":["Leviticus 4:31","1 Peter 1:18-19"]},{"stageName":"The Laver","location":"Courtyard","symbol":"Washing","meaning":"God cleanses and renews us through the Holy Spirit.","scriptures":["Titus 3:5","Ezekiel 36:25-27"]},{"stageName":"The Table of Bread","location":"Holy Place","symbol":"Bread of Life","meaning":"Jesus sustains us with His life and word.","scriptures":["John 6:35","Matthew 4:4"]},{"stageName":"The Lampstand","location":"Holy Place","symbol":"Light","meaning":"Jesus is the Light of the world and the Spirit illuminates our path.","scriptures":["John 8:12","Revelation 4:5"]},{"stageName":"The Altar of Incense","location":"Holy Place","symbol":"Prayer and intercession","meaning":"Through Christ, our prayers rise before God.","scriptures":["Revelation 8:3-4","Hebrews 7:25"]},{"stageName":"The Ark of the Covenant","location":"Most Holy Place","symbol":"God's presence and law of love","meaning":"God desires to dwell with His people and write His law in their hearts.","scriptures":["Hebrews 10:16","Jeremiah 31:33","Exodus 25:21-22","Romans 13:10"]},{"stageName":"The Day of Atonement","location":"Most Holy Place ministry","symbol":"Final cleansing","meaning":"God completes the work of cleansing, judgment, restoration, and the complete removal of evil.","scriptures":["Leviticus 16:15-22","Daniel 7:9-10","Revelation 14:6-7"]},{"stageName":"Priestly Ministry","location":"Sanctuary service","symbol":"Ongoing mediation","meaning":"Jesus continues to minister and intercede for His people.","scriptures":["Hebrews 7:25","Hebrews 9:24"]}],"highlightedIndices":[1,8],"subtitle":"Daily sacrifice in the courtyard pointed to substitution; priestly ministry points to Jesus continuing to minister and intercede for His people."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Daily Grace","references":[{"reference":"Leviticus 4:31"},{"reference":"Hebrews 9:22"},{"reference":"1 Peter 1:18-19"},{"reference":"Hebrews 7:25"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: The daily service showed the seriousness of sin and the availability of grace, pointing to Jesus as both sacrifice and intercessor."}],"reflectPrompt":"Which truth do you need to take more seriously right now: the seriousness of sin or the availability of grace?\n\nBe honest. Some people minimize sin. Others struggle to believe grace is still available. Bring your real answer to God.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, practice daily grace.\n\nAt the end of each day, take two minutes with God.\n\nAsk:\n\n\"Where did I step outside Your love today?\"\n\nThen ask:\n\n\"Where did I see Your grace today?\"\n\nConfess honestly.\n\nReceive mercy.\n\nThen rest in Christ's ongoing ministry for you.\n\nYou are not saved by pretending you had a perfect day.\n\nYou are saved by trusting the perfect Savior.\n\nPrayer:\n\nJesus, thank You for being my sacrifice and my living intercessor. Help me take sin seriously without running from Your grace. Teach me to confess honestly, receive mercy, and walk with You daily. Keep me close to the Father through Your faithful ministry. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"The daily service pointed to Jesus as both sacrifice and intercessor. Sin is serious, but grace is available every day through Christ.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"wb-l6-q1","prompt":"What did the daily sanctuary service teach about sin?","options":["Sin is serious and requires atonement","Sin is harmless","Sin does not affect relationship","Sin saves humanity"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The daily service showed that sin is serious, damages relationship, and requires atonement."},{"id":"wb-l6-q2","prompt":"What did the sacrifice point to?","options":["A substitute standing in the sinner's place","Human pride","A way to ignore sin","A replacement for prayer"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The sacrifice pointed to substitution, ultimately fulfilled in Jesus, the Lamb of God."},{"id":"wb-l6-q3","prompt":"According to 1 Peter 1:18-19, what redeems us?","options":["The precious blood of Christ","Silver and gold","Personal popularity","Human perfection"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Peter says we are redeemed with the precious blood of Christ, as of a lamb without blemish and without spot."},{"id":"wb-l6-q4","prompt":"According to Hebrews 7:25, what does Jesus live to do?","options":["Make intercession","Hide from sinners","Stop forgiving","End all prayer"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Hebrews says Jesus ever lives to make intercession for those who come to God through Him."},{"id":"wb-l6-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"How can daily confession help you stay close to Jesus instead of hiding from Him?","explanation":"Daily confession brings sin into the light and helps us receive grace from the Savior who continues to minister for us."}]}}
$wb_daily_l6_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'the-daily-path-of-grace'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000076';

-- The Way Back to God — Lesson 7: The Final Cleansing (guided reader JSON).
update public.lessons
set reader_content = $wb_final_l7_reader$
{"openingThought":"The sanctuary journey leads somewhere.\n\nIt does not stop at forgiveness.\nIt does not stop at daily grace.\n\nIt moves toward complete restoration.\n\nOnce a year, Israel observed the Day of Atonement.\n\nIt was a solemn day.\n\nA day of cleansing.\nA day of truth.\nA day of judgment.\nA day of putting away sin.\n\nThis final step pointed forward to God's last work in dealing with sin completely.\n\nGod's plan is not only to forgive sin.\n\nIt is to remove it.","scriptureRef":"Leviticus 16:30","scriptureQuote":"For on that day shall the priest make an atonement for you, to cleanse you, that ye may be clean from all your sins before the LORD.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Complete cleansing is the goal.\n\nGod was teaching that sin would not remain forever.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Daniel 7:9-10","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"I beheld till the thrones were cast down, and the Ancient of days did sit.\n\nThe judgment was set, and the books were opened.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Judgment in Scripture is not only about exposure.\n\nIt is about setting things right.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Revelation 14:6-7","quote":"And I saw another angel fly in the midst of heaven, having the everlasting gospel to preach unto them that dwell on the earth.\n\nSaying with a loud voice, Fear God, and give glory to him; for the hour of his judgment is come: and worship him that made heaven, and earth, and the sea, and the fountains of waters."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Hebrews 10:22","quote":"Let us draw near with a true heart in full assurance of faith, having our hearts sprinkled from an evil conscience, and our bodies washed with pure water."}],"teachingPoints":["1. The Day of Atonement was about cleansing\n\nLeviticus 16 says the purpose of this day was cleansing.\n\nNot partial cleansing.\n\nComplete cleansing.\n\nGod was teaching that sin would not remain forever.\n\nThere would be a final dealing with evil.","2. It was a day of truth and honesty\n\nOn this day, the people were called to examine their hearts.\n\nThis was not about fear-driven performance.\n\nIt was about honesty before God.\n\nNo hiding.\nNo pretending.\nNo covering.\n\nGod's cleansing work happens in truth.","3. Judgment reveals God's justice and mercy\n\nDaniel 7 and Revelation 14 speak of judgment.\n\nFor some, that word feels heavy.\n\nBut in Scripture, judgment is not only about exposure.\n\nIt is about setting things right.\n\nIt shows that God takes sin seriously and that He is just, fair, and faithful in dealing with it.","4. God's goal is the removal of sin\n\nThe sanctuary services moved toward this final step.\n\nSin would not only be forgiven.\n\nIt would be removed.\n\nThe brokenness of the world would not last forever.\n\nThrough Jesus, God is bringing history toward restoration."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudy":{"surface":"Cleanse","lemma":"טָהֵר","transliteration":"taher","meaning":"To make pure, remove defilement, and restore to what is right.","strongs":"H2891","reference":"Leviticus 16:30","note":"Cleansing in the sanctuary was not only outward.\n\nIt pointed to God's desire to cleanse the heart, remove sin, and restore full relationship.\n\nThe final cleansing shows that God's plan includes complete restoration, not partial healing."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The Day of Atonement brings the sanctuary message into focus.\n\nGod is not ignoring sin.\n\nGod is dealing with it fully.\n\nThe same God who:\n\nCame looking in Eden\nPromised a Deliverer\nSent His Son\nProvided daily grace\n\nIs also the God who will finish the work.\n\nThis is good news.\n\nIt means evil does not win.\nSin does not last forever.\nBrokenness is not the end of the story.\n\nJesus is not only the Lamb.\n\nHe is the High Priest who completes the work of restoration."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"sanctuaryJourneyMap","stages":[{"stageName":"The Door","location":"Entrance to the Courtyard","symbol":"Access through Christ","meaning":"Jesus is the way into restored relationship with God.","scriptures":["John 10:9","John 14:6"]},{"stageName":"The Altar of Sacrifice","location":"Courtyard","symbol":"The Lamb","meaning":"Jesus gave His life for our sins.","scriptures":["John 1:29","1 Peter 1:18-19"]},{"stageName":"The Laver","location":"Courtyard","symbol":"Washing","meaning":"God cleanses and renews us through the Holy Spirit.","scriptures":["Titus 3:5","Ezekiel 36:25-27"]},{"stageName":"The Table of Bread","location":"Holy Place","symbol":"Bread of Life","meaning":"Jesus sustains us with His life and word.","scriptures":["John 6:35","Matthew 4:4"]},{"stageName":"The Lampstand","location":"Holy Place","symbol":"Light","meaning":"Jesus is the Light of the world and the Spirit illuminates our path.","scriptures":["John 8:12","Revelation 4:5"]},{"stageName":"The Altar of Incense","location":"Holy Place","symbol":"Prayer and intercession","meaning":"Through Christ, our prayers rise before God.","scriptures":["Revelation 8:3-4","Hebrews 7:25"]},{"stageName":"The Ark of the Covenant","location":"Most Holy Place","symbol":"God's presence and law of love","meaning":"God desires to dwell with His people and write His law in their hearts.","scriptures":["Hebrews 10:16","Jeremiah 31:33","Exodus 25:21-22","Romans 13:10"]},{"stageName":"The Day of Atonement","location":"Most Holy Place ministry","symbol":"Final cleansing","meaning":"God completes the work of cleansing, judgment, restoration, and the complete removal of evil.","scriptures":["Leviticus 16:30","Daniel 7:9-10","Revelation 14:6-7","Hebrews 10:22"]}],"highlightedIndices":[7],"subtitle":"The final stage of the sanctuary journey: God completes cleansing, judgment, restoration, and the removal of evil through Christ."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"The Final Cleansing","references":[{"reference":"Leviticus 16:30"},{"reference":"Daniel 7:9-10"},{"reference":"Revelation 14:6-7"},{"reference":"Hebrews 10:22"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: God's plan does not end with forgiveness. He will fully cleanse, restore, and remove sin through Jesus."}],"reflectPrompt":"How does it change your perspective to know that God's plan includes the complete removal of sin, not just temporary forgiveness?\n\nThink about pain, injustice, personal struggle, and the hope of full restoration.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, practice living in the light.\n\nThe Day of Atonement was a time of honesty before God.\n\nDo not carry hidden things.\n\nBring everything into the open with Him.\n\nAsk:\n\n\"Lord, is there anything I am holding onto that You want to cleanse?\"\n\nThen trust His grace.\n\nGod's goal is not to expose you to shame you.\n\nIt is to cleanse you and restore you.\n\nYou can live with an open heart before Him.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You that Your plan does not stop at forgiveness. Thank You that You are bringing all things toward restoration. Search my heart and cleanse what needs to be made new. Help me live honestly before You, trusting Your grace and Your justice. Keep my hope anchored in Jesus, who will complete the work You have begun. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"You have completed The Way Back to God. From the door to the final cleansing, God has revealed a clear path through Jesus.","seriesCompleteScreen":{"title":"Study Complete","message":"You have completed The Way Back to God. From the door to the final cleansing, God has revealed a clear path through Jesus. He forgives, cleanses, restores, and will one day remove sin completely.","primaryCta":{"label":"Back to Foundations Path","href":"/paths/foundations"},"secondaryCta":{"label":"Review this study","href":"/study-library/the-way-back-to-god"}},"lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"wb-l7-q1","prompt":"What was the main purpose of the Day of Atonement?","options":["Cleansing from sin","Building new cities","Increasing wealth","Avoiding prayer"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Leviticus 16:30 shows that the Day of Atonement was about cleansing from all sin."},{"id":"wb-l7-q2","prompt":"What does Daniel 7:9-10 describe?","options":["A scene of judgment","The creation of animals","The fall of Egypt","A wedding feast"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Daniel 7 describes a judgment scene where the books are opened."},{"id":"wb-l7-q3","prompt":"According to Revelation 14:6-7, what has come?","options":["The hour of God's judgment","The end of Scripture","The removal of prayer","The destruction of love"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Revelation 14 says the hour of God's judgment has come."},{"id":"wb-l7-q4","prompt":"What is God's ultimate goal regarding sin?","options":["To fully remove it and restore what was broken","To ignore it forever","To make it stronger","To leave it unresolved"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The sanctuary points to complete restoration, not partial forgiveness."},{"id":"wb-l7-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What would it look like for you to live openly before God instead of hiding anything?","explanation":"God's cleansing work happens in truth. Living openly before Him allows His grace to restore deeply."}]}}
$wb_final_l7_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'the-final-cleansing'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000076';



