update public.lessons
set reader_content = $mfm_l1_reader$
{"openingThought":"Every person eventually asks the same question.\n\nWho am I?\n\nNot just,\n\nWhat is my name?\n\nNot just,\n\nWhat do I do?\n\nBut,\n\nWhat am I?\n\nWhy am I here?\n\nDo I matter?\n\nThe world gives different answers.\n\nSome say human life is only biology.\n\nA short life.\n\nA few urges.\n\nA little pleasure.\n\nA little pain.\n\nThen death.\n\nBut Scripture tells a different story.\n\nHumanity is not an accident.\n\nYou are not merely a survival machine.\n\nYou were created by God.\n\nAnd if that is true,\n\nthen your life carries dignity,\n\npurpose,\n\nresponsibility,\n\nand hope.","scriptureRef":"Psalm 8:3 to 5","scriptureQuote":"When I consider Your heavens,\n\nthe work of Your fingers,\n\nthe moon and the stars,\n\nwhich You have ordained,\n\nwhat is man that You are mindful of him,\n\nand the son of man that You visit him?\n\nFor You have made him a little lower than the angels,\n\nand You have crowned him with glory and honor.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Genesis 1:1","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.","scriptureWordInsights":[{"surface":"mindful","lemma":"זָכַר","transliteration":"zakar","meaning":"to remember, call to mind, be mindful","strongs":"H2142","reference":"Psalm 8:4","language":"Hebrew","note":"In Psalm 8,\n\nDavid asks,\n\n\"What is man that You are mindful of him?\"\n\nThe idea is not that God occasionally remembers humanity,\n\nas if He almost forgot.\n\nIt points to God's attentive care.\n\nThe Creator of the heavens notices human beings.\n\nHe is mindful of us.\n\nThat means your life is not invisible to God.\n\nYou are seen.\n\nKnown.\n\nConsidered.\n\nAnd valued."}],"scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Genesis 1:26","quote":"Then God said,\n\nLet Us make man in Our image,\n\naccording to Our likeness."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Acts 17:26 to 28","quote":"And He has made from one blood every nation of men\n\nto dwell on all the face of the earth,\n\nand has determined their preappointed times\n\nand the boundaries of their dwellings,\n\nso that they should seek the Lord,\n\nin the hope that they might grope for Him\n\nand find Him,\n\nthough He is not far from each one of us;\n\nfor in Him we live and move and have our being."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Scripture begins with God as Creator.\n\nThat means human identity begins with Him."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Made for More","references":[{"reference":"Psalm 8:3 to 5"},{"reference":"Genesis 1:1"},{"reference":"Genesis 1:26"},{"reference":"Acts 17:26 to 28"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Human life is not random or disposable. Scripture begins our identity with the Creator who sees, values, and calls us to seek Him."}],"teachingPoints":["The Bible asks the human identity question.\n\nPsalm 8 looks at the vastness of creation and asks why God cares about humanity.","Human life begins with God.\n\nGenesis does not begin with accident, chaos, or meaninglessness. It begins with the Creator.","Humanity carries dignity.\n\nPsalm 8 says God crowns human beings with glory and honor.","Humanity was made for relationship with God.\n\nActs 17 says God arranged life so people would seek Him and find Him.","The Bible gives a better story than survival.\n\nWe are not merely living, consuming, and dying. We are made by God and for God."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"Psalm 8 uses a careful Hebrew word when it speaks about God being \"mindful\" of us. Take a closer look.","wordStudyInsights":[{"surface":"mindful","lemma":"זָכַר","transliteration":"zakar","meaning":"to remember, call to mind, be mindful","strongs":"H2142","reference":"Psalm 8:4","language":"Hebrew","note":"In Psalm 8,\n\nDavid asks,\n\n\"What is man that You are mindful of him?\"\n\nThe idea is not that God occasionally remembers humanity,\n\nas if He almost forgot.\n\nIt points to God's attentive care.\n\nThe Creator of the heavens notices human beings.\n\nHe is mindful of us.\n\nThat means your life is not invisible to God.\n\nYou are seen.\n\nKnown.\n\nConsidered.\n\nAnd valued."}],"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"doctrine_clarity","body":"There are two very different ways to tell the story of humanity.\n\nOne story says we are here by accident.\n\nWe live for a little while.\n\nWe follow desire.\n\nWe compete.\n\nWe survive if we can.\n\nThen we disappear.\n\nThe Bible tells a different story.\n\nIt says creation has a Creator.\n\nHuman life has meaning.\n\nEvery person carries worth.\n\nOur choices matter.\n\nLove matters.\n\nJustice matters.\n\nMercy matters.\n\nWhy?\n\nBecause human beings were made by God.\n\nThat does not answer every question in one lesson.\n\nBut it gives us the right starting place.\n\nYou are not random.\n\nYou are not disposable.\n\nYou are not merely what you feel,\n\nwhat you own,\n\nwhat you achieve,\n\nor what others think of you.\n\nYou were made for more."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"Two Stories of Humanity","accent":"stone","payload":{"variant":"comparison","leftTitle":"Mere Survival","rightTitle":"Made by God","leftItems":["accident","appetite","competition","death"],"rightItems":["dignity","purpose","relationship","hope"]},"footnote":"Mere survival: This story leaves human life without lasting meaning. Made by God: Scripture says humanity begins with the Creator."}],"reflectPrompt":"When you think about human life,\n\ndo you usually see it as random or purposeful?\n\nWhat does Psalm 8 show you about God's view of humanity?\n\nHave you ever struggled to believe your life matters?\n\nWhat changes when your identity begins with God instead of achievement,\n\nappearance,\n\nsuccess,\n\nor other people's opinions?","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week,\n\nread Psalm 8 slowly.\n\nDo not rush through the question.\n\n\"What is man that You are mindful of him?\"\n\nLet that question sit with you.\n\nThen answer it from Scripture.\n\nYou are seen by God.\n\nYou are known by God.\n\nYou were created by God.\n\nYou were made for more than survival.","prayer":"","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"mfm-l1-q1","prompt":"What question does Psalm 8 ask about humanity?","options":["Why are humans better at building cities?","What is man that God is mindful of him?","Why do humans need no guidance?","What nation is strongest?"],"correctIndex":1},{"id":"mfm-l1-q2","prompt":"How does Genesis 1:1 begin the story of reality?","options":["With human achievement","With God as Creator","With meaninglessness","With Israel's kings"],"correctIndex":1},{"id":"mfm-l1-q3","prompt":"According to Psalm 8, God crowns humanity with what?","options":["Glory and honor","Fear and confusion","Wealth and control","Silence and distance"],"correctIndex":0},{"id":"mfm-l1-q4","kind":"multi","prompt":"Which statements fit this lesson?","options":["Human identity begins with God","Scripture gives humanity dignity","Human life is only meaningless survival","God is mindful of humanity"],"correctIndices":[0,1,3]},{"id":"mfm-l1-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What does it mean to you that your life begins with God's purpose rather than accident?","explanation":"This reflection helps the learner connect Scripture's teaching on humanity with personal identity and worth."}]},"completionSummary":"You began with a simple idea: Scripture gives humanity a story that is deeper than survival. Keep that anchor as you move forward."}
$mfm_l1_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'more-than-mere-survival';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $mfm_l2_reader$
{"openingThought":"The Bible does not describe human beings as accidents.\n\nIt does not begin our identity with status,\n\nmoney,\n\nappearance,\n\nrace,\n\ntalent,\n\nachievement,\n\nor failure.\n\nIt begins deeper.\n\nHumanity was created in the image of God.\n\nThat means every person carries dignity before they accomplish anything.\n\nBefore a resume.\n\nBefore a title.\n\nBefore applause.\n\nBefore success.\n\nBefore anyone knows their name.\n\nHuman worth begins with the Creator.\n\nTo be human is to carry meaning.\n\nNot because we made ourselves important,\n\nbut because God made us in His image.","scriptureRef":"Genesis 1:26 to 28","scriptureQuote":"Then God said,\n\nLet Us make man in Our image,\n\naccording to Our likeness;\n\nlet them have dominion over the fish of the sea,\n\nover the birds of the air,\n\nand over the cattle,\n\nover all the earth\n\nand over every creeping thing that creeps on the earth.\n\nSo God created man in His own image;\n\nin the image of God He created him;\n\nmale and female He created them.\n\nThen God blessed them,\n\nand God said to them,\n\nBe fruitful and multiply;\n\nfill the earth and subdue it;\n\nhave dominion over the fish of the sea,\n\nover the birds of the air,\n\nand over every living thing that moves on the earth.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Genesis 5:1 to 2","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"In the day that God created man,\n\nHe made him in the likeness of God.\n\nHe created them male and female,\n\nand blessed them\n\nand called them Mankind\n\nin the day they were created.","scriptureWordInsights":[{"surface":"image","lemma":"צֶלֶם אֱלֹהִים","transliteration":"tselem Elohim","meaning":"image of God","strongs":"H6754","reference":"Genesis 1:26 to 27","language":"Hebrew","note":"The Hebrew word tselem can mean image,\n\nlikeness,\n\nor representation.\n\nGenesis says humanity was made in the image of God.\n\nThis does not mean we are God.\n\nIt means human beings were created to reflect something about Him.\n\nWe were made to represent His character in the world.\n\nHis love.\n\nHis wisdom.\n\nHis care.\n\nHis justice.\n\nHis creativity.\n\nHis relational nature.\n\nTo bear God's image is a gift,\n\nbut it is also a calling."}],"scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"James 3:9","quote":"With it we bless our God and Father,\n\nand with it we curse men,\n\nwho have been made in the similitude of God."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Scripture grounds human dignity in creation.\n\nHuman beings matter because they bear God's image."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Created in God's Image","references":[{"reference":"Genesis 1:26 to 28"},{"reference":"Genesis 5:1 to 2"},{"reference":"James 3:9"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Every human being carries God-given dignity because humanity was created in the image and likeness of God."}],"teachingPoints":["Humanity was created by God.\n\nOur identity begins with the Creator, not with human opinion.","Humanity was made in God's image.\n\nThis gives every person dignity, value, and purpose.","Male and female are both included.\n\nGenesis says God created humanity in His image, male and female.","God blessed humanity.\n\nHuman life begins under divine blessing, not meaninglessness.","The image of God affects how we treat people.\n\nJames warns against cursing human beings who are made in God's likeness."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"Genesis ties human dignity to one striking Hebrew phrase. Take a closer look at what \"image\" means here.","wordStudyInsights":[{"surface":"image","lemma":"צֶלֶם אֱלֹהִים","transliteration":"tselem Elohim","meaning":"image of God","strongs":"H6754","reference":"Genesis 1:26 to 27","language":"Hebrew","note":"The Hebrew word tselem can mean image,\n\nlikeness,\n\nor representation.\n\nGenesis says humanity was made in the image of God.\n\nThis does not mean we are God.\n\nIt means human beings were created to reflect something about Him.\n\nWe were made to represent His character in the world.\n\nHis love.\n\nHis wisdom.\n\nHis care.\n\nHis justice.\n\nHis creativity.\n\nHis relational nature.\n\nTo bear God's image is a gift,\n\nbut it is also a calling."}],"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"doctrine_clarity","body":"Genesis 1 gives humanity a high calling.\n\nGod creates a world filled with life,\n\nbeauty,\n\norder,\n\nand purpose.\n\nThen He creates human beings in His image.\n\nThat means people are not disposable.\n\nNo human being is merely a body.\n\nNo human being is merely a number.\n\nNo human being is merely useful or useless.\n\nEvery person carries God-given worth.\n\nThis truth also gives responsibility.\n\nIf we are made in God's image,\n\nthen our lives are meant to reflect Him.\n\nThe way we speak matters.\n\nThe way we treat others matters.\n\nThe way we use power matters.\n\nThe way we care for creation matters.\n\nThe way we love matters.\n\nBeing made in God's image is not only about feeling valuable.\n\nIt is about living as someone created to reflect the God who made you."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"Image of God","accent":"sage","payload":{"variant":"sequence","steps":[{"label":"Dignity","subtitle":"Every person has value because God made them."},{"label":"Relationship","subtitle":"We were made to know God and love others."},{"label":"Responsibility","subtitle":"We are called to represent God's character."},{"label":"Purpose","subtitle":"Human life is meant to reflect God in the world."}]},"footnote":"The image of God means human life has worth, calling, and responsibility."}],"reflectPrompt":"What usually shapes the way people define their worth?\n\nHow does Genesis 1 correct shallow views of human identity?\n\nWhat changes when you see every person as made in God's image?\n\nWhere do you need to treat yourself or someone else with more dignity?","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week,\n\npractice seeing people through Genesis 1.\n\nThe person in traffic.\n\nThe coworker.\n\nThe stranger.\n\nThe difficult family member.\n\nThe person you disagree with.\n\nThe person in the mirror.\n\nAsk:\n\nHow would I treat this person if I remembered they were made in God's image?","prayer":"","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"mfm-l2-q1","prompt":"According to Genesis 1:26 to 27, humanity was created in whose image?","options":["The image of angels","The image of God","The image of animals","The image of kings"],"correctIndex":1},{"id":"mfm-l2-q2","prompt":"What does being made in God's image give every person?","options":["God-given dignity and purpose","Permission to mistreat others","Freedom from responsibility","Worth only after success"],"correctIndex":0},{"id":"mfm-l2-q3","prompt":"What does James 3:9 warn about?","options":["Cursing people made in God's likeness","Reading Genesis too slowly","Naming animals","Forgetting geography"],"correctIndex":0},{"id":"mfm-l2-q4","kind":"multi","prompt":"Which statements fit this lesson?","options":["Humanity was created by God","Male and female are both included in God's image","The image of God affects how we treat people","Human worth depends only on achievement"],"correctIndices":[0,1,2]},{"id":"mfm-l2-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What is one way you can honor the image of God in someone this week?","explanation":"This reflection helps the learner apply human dignity to real relationships and daily choices."}]},"completionSummary":"You anchored dignity where Scripture anchors it: in God's image. Carry that lens into how you see yourself and others."}
$mfm_l2_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'created-in-gods-image';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $mfm_l4_reader$
{"openingThought":"Love cannot be forced.\n\nObedience can be forced.\n\nBehavior can be controlled.\n\nWords can be repeated.\n\nActions can be pressured.\n\nBut love must be chosen.\n\nThat is why freedom matters.\n\nGod did not create human beings as machines.\n\nHe did not make us puppets.\n\nHe created us with the ability to think,\n\nrespond,\n\ntrust,\n\nchoose,\n\nand love.\n\nThat freedom makes love meaningful.\n\nBut it also makes rebellion possible.\n\nThe same freedom that allows love to be real\n\nalso allows sin to be chosen.\n\nThat is serious.\n\nBut without freedom,\n\nthere can be no real relationship.","scriptureRef":"Deuteronomy 30:19","scriptureQuote":"I call heaven and earth as witnesses today against you,\n\nthat I have set before you life and death,\n\nblessing and cursing;\n\ntherefore choose life,\n\nthat both you and your descendants may live.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Joshua 24:15","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"And if it seems evil to you to serve the LORD,\n\nchoose for yourselves this day whom you will serve.","scriptureWordInsights":[{"surface":"choose","lemma":"בָּחַר","transliteration":"bachar","meaning":"to choose, select, decide","strongs":"H977","reference":"Joshua 24:15","language":"Hebrew","note":"Joshua tells the people,\n\n\"Choose for yourselves this day whom you will serve.\"\n\nThe Hebrew word bachar points to a real act of choosing.\n\nGod does not treat people as machines.\n\nHe calls them to respond.\n\nThis matters because love,\n\nworship,\n\nand loyalty only have meaning when they are chosen.\n\nGod invites.\n\nGod warns.\n\nGod teaches.\n\nBut He does not force the heart."}],"scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Genesis 2:16 to 17","quote":"And the LORD God commanded the man,\n\nsaying,\n\nOf every tree of the garden you may freely eat;\n\nbut of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil you shall not eat,\n\nfor in the day that you eat of it\n\nyou shall surely die."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"John 14:15","quote":"If you love Me,\n\nkeep My commandments."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"God gives commands,\n\nbut He also gives meaningful choice.\n\nLove responds freely."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Choose Life","references":[{"reference":"Deuteronomy 30:19"},{"reference":"Joshua 24:15"},{"reference":"Genesis 2:16 to 17"},{"reference":"John 14:15"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Love requires freedom, and Scripture calls us to use that freedom to choose life, trust, and relationship with God."}],"teachingPoints":["God created humans with moral freedom.\n\nWe are able to choose, respond, trust, obey, or reject.","Choice makes love meaningful.\n\nForced love is not love. Real love requires freedom.","God clearly shows the path of life.\n\nHe does not leave people without guidance.","Freedom comes with responsibility.\n\nOur choices matter because they shape life, character, and relationship.","Obedience is meant to flow from love.\n\nJesus said, \"If you love Me, keep My commandments.\""],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"Joshua uses a Hebrew word when he asks Israel whom they will serve. Take a closer look.","wordStudyInsights":[{"surface":"choose","lemma":"בָּחַר","transliteration":"bachar","meaning":"to choose, select, decide","strongs":"H977","reference":"Joshua 24:15","language":"Hebrew","note":"Joshua tells the people,\n\n\"Choose for yourselves this day whom you will serve.\"\n\nThe Hebrew word bachar points to a real act of choosing.\n\nGod does not treat people as machines.\n\nHe calls them to respond.\n\nThis matters because love,\n\nworship,\n\nand loyalty only have meaning when they are chosen.\n\nGod invites.\n\nGod warns.\n\nGod teaches.\n\nBut He does not force the heart."}],"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"doctrine_clarity","body":"Freedom is one of the most powerful gifts God gave humanity.\n\nBut freedom is not the same as independence from God.\n\nA fish is free in water,\n\nnot on dry land.\n\nA bird is free in the sky,\n\nnot underwater.\n\nHuman beings are free when they live in harmony with the God who made them.\n\nSin presents freedom as doing whatever we want.\n\nScripture presents freedom as choosing life.\n\nChoosing truth.\n\nChoosing love.\n\nChoosing the Creator.\n\nThat is why God's commands are not random restrictions.\n\nThey are boundaries that protect life and love.\n\nGod gives choice,\n\nbut He also tells the truth about where choices lead.\n\nLife or death.\n\nBlessing or curse.\n\nTrust or rebellion.\n\nLove cannot be forced.\n\nBut love can be invited."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"Choice and Direction","accent":"sky","payload":{"variant":"comparison","leftTitle":"Life","rightTitle":"Death","leftItems":["trust","love","obedience","relationship"],"rightItems":["distrust","rebellion","selfishness","separation"]},"footnote":"God invites us to choose life. Sin promises freedom but leads away from life."}],"reflectPrompt":"Why does love require freedom?\n\nHave you ever thought of God's commands as protection rather than control?\n\nWhere are you currently facing a meaningful choice?\n\nWhat would it look like to choose life in that area?","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week,\n\npay attention to one choice that keeps coming up.\n\nIt may be a habit.\n\nA relationship.\n\nA temptation.\n\nA thought pattern.\n\nA decision you keep delaying.\n\nAsk:\n\nDoes this choice lead me closer to life with God,\n\nor farther from it?\n\nThen choose one step toward life.\n\nPrayer:\n\nLord,\n\nThank You for creating me with the ability to choose.\n\nTeach me to use freedom wisely.\n\nHelp me see Your commands as love,\n\nnot control.\n\nGive me courage to choose life,\n\ntruth,\n\nobedience,\n\nand relationship with You.\n\nShape my heart so my choices reflect Your love.\n\nAmen.","prayer":"","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"mfm-l4-q1","prompt":"Why does freedom matter for love?","options":["Because love must be chosen","Because love works best when forced","Because choices do not matter","Because God wants confusion"],"correctIndex":0},{"id":"mfm-l4-q2","prompt":"What does Deuteronomy 30:19 tell people to choose?","options":["Popularity","Life","Fear","Wealth"],"correctIndex":1},{"id":"mfm-l4-q3","prompt":"What does the Hebrew word bachar mean?","options":["To choose or decide","To hide","To destroy","To forget"],"correctIndex":0},{"id":"mfm-l4-q4","kind":"multi","prompt":"Which statements fit this lesson?","options":["God created humans with moral freedom","Choice makes love meaningful","Freedom comes with responsibility","God forces the heart to love Him"],"correctIndices":[0,1,2]},{"id":"mfm-l4-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What is one area where God is inviting you to choose life?","explanation":"This reflection helps the learner apply moral freedom to a real decision."}]},"completionSummary":"You saw that love requires freedom, and that God invites us to choose life, trust, and obedience in relationship with Him."}
$mfm_l4_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'free-moral-agents';



update public.lessons
set reader_content = $json_ta_l1$
{
  "openingThought": "The world does not feel stable.\n\nNations rise and fall.\nEconomies shake.\nDisasters come without warning.\nFear spreads quickly.\nTruth feels harder to recognize.\n\nMany people can sense that something is wrong.\n\nBut they do not know where history is going.\n\nRevelation 14 speaks directly into that moment.\n\nIt is not given to satisfy curiosity.\n\nIt is given to prepare a people.\n\nBefore the final harvest of the earth, God sends one last message to the world.\n\nA message of gospel.\nA message of worship.\nA message of warning.\nA message of decision.\n\nThe question is not whether Revelation 14 matters.\n\nThe question is whether we are listening.",
  "scriptureRef": "Revelation 14:6 to 7",
  "scriptureQuote": "\"And I saw another angel fly in the midst of heaven, having the everlasting gospel to preach unto them that dwell on the earth,\n\nand to every nation, and kindred, and tongue, and people,\n\nSaying with a loud voice,\n\nFear God, and give glory to him;\n\nfor the hour of his judgment is come:\n\nand worship him that made heaven, and earth, and the sea, and the fountains of waters.\"",
  "scriptureAfterPrimary": "Revelation 14 begins with a message moving across the whole world.\n\nIt is not quiet.\n\nIt is not local.\n\nIt is not optional.\n\nIt is global, urgent, and centered on worship.",
  "teachingPoints": [
    "The three angels represent God's final message to the world.\nThe word angel means messenger, and in Scripture God often uses human beings to carry His message.",
    "The message comes from heaven.\nThe angels are seen flying in the midst of heaven because the message does not originate from human wisdom.",
    "The message is given with a loud voice.\nThis means it carries power, urgency, and authority.",
    "The message goes to every nation, kindred, tongue, and people.\nNo group is excluded. This is a worldwide call.",
    "The message comes before the final harvest.\nRevelation 14 shows Christ returning after these messages are proclaimed."
  ],
  "studyInsight": {
    "placement": "after_teaching",
    "title": "Study Insight",
    "body": "Revelation 14 is not random.\n\nThe messages come in order.\n\nFirst, the everlasting gospel is proclaimed.\n\nThen Babylon's fall is announced.\n\nThen the warning against the beast, his image, and his mark is given.\n\nThat order matters.\n\nYou cannot understand the warning if you skip the gospel.\n\nYou cannot understand Babylon's fall if you do not know what truth she rejected.\n\nGod's final message does not begin with fear.\n\nIt begins with the everlasting gospel.\n\nBefore God warns the world,\n\nHe offers salvation."
  },
  "reflectPrompt": "When you think about prophecy,\n\nwhat do you usually feel?\n\nFear?\nConfusion?\nCuriosity?\nAvoidance?\n\nWhat if prophecy was not given to scare you,\n\nbut to prepare you?\n\nWhat would change if you saw Revelation 14\n\nas a message of mercy before it is a message of warning?",
  "lifeApplication": "This week, read Revelation 14:6 to 12 slowly.\n\nDo not try to understand every symbol yet.\n\nJust notice the flow.\n\nLook for what God is emphasizing.\n\nAsk yourself:\n\nWhat is God calling the world to see?\n\nWhat is God calling me to take seriously?\n\nLet the message become personal before it becomes technical.",
  "prayer": "Lord,\n\nHelp me approach prophecy with humility.\n\nNot with fear.\n\nNot with pride.\n\nNot with argument.\n\nOpen my heart to understand Your final message.\n\nShow me the gospel at the center of Revelation.\n\nTeach me to listen,\n\nand prepare me to respond.\n\nAmen."
}
$json_ta_l1$::jsonb
where slug = 'why-revelation-14-matters';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $json_ta_l2$
{
  "openingThought": "Revelation 14 does not begin with the beast.\n\nIt does not begin with the mark.\n\nIt does not begin with fear.\n\nIt begins with the gospel.\n\nThat matters.\n\nBefore God warns the world,\n\nHe reminds the world of good news.\n\nThe final message to humanity is not merely about crisis.\n\nIt is about Christ.\n\nIt is about a plan that did not begin after sin entered the world.\n\nIt reaches back before Eden.\n\nBefore failure.\n\nBefore shame.\n\nBefore the first sacrifice.\n\nThe gospel is called everlasting because it was not an emergency plan.\n\nIt was God's plan to save before humanity knew it needed saving.",
  "scriptureRef": "Revelation 14:6",
  "scriptureQuote": "\"And I saw another angel fly in the midst of heaven, having the everlasting gospel to preach unto them that dwell on the earth.\"",
  "scriptureSecondaryRef": "1 Peter 1:18 to 20",
  "scriptureSecondaryQuote": "\"Forasmuch as ye know that ye were not redeemed with corruptible things, as silver and gold,\n\nbut with the precious blood of Christ, as of a lamb without blemish and without spot:\n\nWho verily was foreordained before the foundation of the world.\"",
  "scriptureAfterSecondary": "Romans 6:23\n\n\"For the wages of sin is death;\n\nbut the gift of God is eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord.\"\n\nThe gospel is not temporary.\n\nIt is not shallow.\n\nIt is not merely religious advice.\n\nIt is the announcement that Christ has done for us what we could never do for ourselves.",
  "teachingPoints": [
    "The everlasting gospel began before the foundation of the world.\nGod was not surprised by sin. The plan of salvation was already in place.",
    "Sin brings death, but Christ brings life.\nThe law reveals the seriousness of sin, but the gospel reveals the mercy of God.",
    "The sacrifices in the Old Testament pointed forward to Jesus.\nThe lambs could not remove sin by themselves. They pointed to the true Lamb.",
    "Jesus is both the perfect Priest and the perfect Sacrifice.\nHe lived the life we failed to live and died the death sin demanded.",
    "The gospel must be received personally.\nA gift can be purchased, offered, and placed before someone, but it must still be accepted."
  ],
  "studyInsight": {
    "placement": "after_teaching",
    "title": "Study Insight",
    "body": "The gospel is not only forgiveness.\n\nIt is restoration.\n\nWhen Adam and Eve sinned, they tried to cover themselves.\n\nBut human effort could not fix what sin had broken.\n\nGod provided the covering.\n\nThat picture matters.\n\nThe gospel is not humanity reaching high enough to reach God.\n\nIt is God coming low enough to reach humanity.\n\nChrist covers our guilt.\n\nChrist restores our standing.\n\nChrist begins changing the life.\n\nThe everlasting gospel is not only about being forgiven for the past.\n\nIt is about being remade for the future."
  },
  "reflectPrompt": "When you think of the gospel,\n\ndo you mostly think of pardon,\n\nor transformation?\n\nDo you see salvation as something Christ gives,\n\nor something you are still trying to earn?\n\nWhat would change if you truly believed\n\nthat Jesus has already provided what you cannot produce?",
  "lifeApplication": "This week, read Romans 6:23 slowly.\n\nPause over both sides of the verse.\n\nThe wages of sin.\n\nThe gift of God.\n\nDo not rush past either one.\n\nThen ask yourself:\n\nAm I trying to cover myself,\n\nor am I receiving what Christ has provided?\n\nLet that question search your heart honestly.",
  "prayer": "Lord,\n\nThank You for the everlasting gospel.\n\nThank You that salvation was not an afterthought.\n\nThank You for Christ,\n\nthe Lamb without blemish,\n\nthe One who lived for me,\n\ndied for me,\n\nand now lives to save completely.\n\nHelp me stop trusting in my own covering.\n\nTeach me to receive Your gift fully,\n\nand let that gift change my life.\n\nAmen."
}
$json_ta_l2$::jsonb
where slug = 'the-everlasting-gospel';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $json_ta_l3$
{
  "openingThought": "When people hear the phrase \"fear God,\" they often think it means to be afraid of Him.\n\nTo hide.\n\nTo shrink back.\n\nTo expect punishment.\n\nBut that is not how Scripture uses the phrase at its deepest level.\n\nTo fear God is to recognize who He is.\n\nCreator.\nJudge.\nFather.\nRedeemer.\nKing.\n\nIt means awe.\n\nReverence.\n\nTrust.\n\nObedience.\n\nNot terror that drives us away,\n\nbut holy respect that draws us near.\n\nThe first command in the three angels' messages is not asking us to panic.\n\nIt is calling us to live before God with our eyes open.",
  "scriptureRef": "Revelation 14:7",
  "scriptureQuote": "\"Saying with a loud voice,\n\nFear God, and give glory to him;\n\nfor the hour of his judgment is come.\"",
  "scriptureSecondaryRef": "Proverbs 9:10",
  "scriptureSecondaryQuote": "\"The fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom:\n\nand the knowledge of the holy is understanding.\"",
  "scriptureAfterSecondary": "Ecclesiastes 12:13\n\n\"Let us hear the conclusion of the whole matter:\n\nFear God, and keep his commandments:\n\nfor this is the whole duty of man.\"\n\nThe fear of God is not spiritual panic.\n\nIt is the beginning of wisdom.\n\nIt teaches us to see life clearly.",
  "teachingPoints": [
    "To fear God means to reverence Him.\nIt is awe before His greatness, holiness, and mercy.",
    "The fear of God is connected to wisdom.\nA person cannot live wisely while ignoring the One who made them.",
    "The fear of God leads to obedience.\nIn Scripture, reverence is never just an emotion. It shapes the life.",
    "The fear of God also affects worship.\nWe do not come before God casually, proudly, or carelessly.",
    "The fear of God changes how we treat people.\nThose who reverence God learn to honor the weak, the poor, and the vulnerable."
  ],
  "studyInsight": {
    "placement": "after_teaching",
    "title": "Study Insight",
    "body": "The fear of God holds two truths together.\n\nGod is high and holy.\n\nAnd God is near.\n\nIf we only see His holiness, we may become afraid.\n\nIf we only see His nearness, we may become casual.\n\nScripture gives us both.\n\nThe God who fills heaven also listens to the humble.\n\nThe God who judges also saves.\n\nThe God who commands also gives mercy.\n\nTo fear God is to live with that awareness.\n\nHe is not to be handled lightly.\n\nAnd He is not to be avoided.\n\nHe is to be trusted, honored, and obeyed."
  },
  "reflectPrompt": "How do you usually think about God?\n\nDistant?\nStrict?\nCasual?\nNear?\n\nDoes your view of God produce reverence,\n\nor indifference?\n\nIf someone looked at your choices,\n\nwould they see that God carries weight in your life?",
  "lifeApplication": "This week, choose one area of your life and bring it under the fear of God.\n\nYour speech.\nYour worship.\nYour habits.\nYour private thoughts.\nYour treatment of others.\n\nAsk one simple question:\n\nDoes this show reverence for God?\n\nDo not answer quickly.\n\nLet the question do its work.",
  "prayer": "Lord,\n\nTeach me what it means to fear You.\n\nNot to run from You,\n\nbut to honor You.\n\nGive me reverence without terror,\n\nnearness without casualness,\n\nobedience without pride.\n\nLet my life show that You are holy,\n\nmerciful,\n\nand worthy of my trust.\n\nAmen."
}
$json_ta_l3$::jsonb
where slug = 'what-it-means-to-fear-god';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $json_ta_l4$
{
  "openingThought": "The first angel's message does not only say,\n\nFear God.\n\nIt also says,\n\nGive glory to Him.\n\nThat sounds simple until we ask what it means.\n\nIs glory only praise?\n\nIs it only worship music?\n\nIs it only something we say with our lips?\n\nScripture gives a deeper answer.\n\nTo glorify God is to reveal what He is like.\n\nIn our worship.\n\nIn our character.\n\nIn our choices.\n\nIn our bodies.\n\nIn the way we treat people.\n\nGod's glory is not meant to stay hidden in heaven.\n\nIt is meant to be reflected on earth through a surrendered life.",
  "scriptureRef": "Revelation 14:7",
  "scriptureQuote": "\"Saying with a loud voice,\n\nFear God, and give glory to him;\n\nfor the hour of his judgment is come.\"",
  "scriptureSecondaryRef": "Matthew 5:16",
  "scriptureSecondaryQuote": "\"Let your light so shine before men,\n\nthat they may see your good works,\n\nand glorify your Father which is in heaven.\"",
  "scriptureAfterSecondary": "1 Corinthians 6:19 to 20\n\n\"Do you not know that your body is the temple of the Holy Spirit who is in you,\n\nwhom you have from God,\n\nand you are not your own?\n\nFor you were bought at a price;\n\ntherefore glorify God in your body and in your spirit,\n\nwhich are God's.\"\n\n1 Corinthians 10:31\n\n\"Therefore, whether you eat or drink,\n\nor whatever you do,\n\ndo all to the glory of God.\"\n\nTo give God glory is not only to speak well of Him.\n\nIt is to live in a way that points back to Him.",
  "teachingPoints": [
    "God is glorified when His character is reflected.\nWhen mercy, purity, justice, humility, and love are seen in His people, His name is honored.",
    "God is glorified through good works.\nNot works that save us, but works that reveal the One who is changing us.",
    "God is glorified in the body and spirit.\nOur habits, appetites, minds, and choices matter because we belong to Him.",
    "God is glorified when we bear fruit.\nThe fruit of the Spirit shows that His life is working within us.",
    "God is glorified when we remain faithful under pressure.\nEven suffering can reveal the worth of God when His people trust Him."
  ],
  "studyInsight": {
    "placement": "after_teaching",
    "title": "Study Insight",
    "body": "The Bible connects glory with character.\n\nWhen Moses asked to see God's glory, God revealed His goodness, mercy, patience, and justice.\n\nThat matters.\n\nGod's glory is not merely brightness.\n\nIt is who He is.\n\nSo when Revelation says,\n\nGive glory to Him,\n\nit is calling for more than religious words.\n\nIt is calling for a life that reflects God's character.\n\nThis includes the visible parts of life.\n\nWhat we say.\n\nWhat we watch.\n\nWhat we eat.\n\nHow we care for the body.\n\nHow we treat others.\n\nHow we respond when no one is watching.\n\nThe body affects the mind.\n\nThe mind affects the spiritual life.\n\nAnd the spiritual life is where God speaks to us.\n\nGlory is not performance.\n\nIt is reflection."
  },
  "reflectPrompt": "When people look at your life,\n\nwhat picture of God are they seeing?\n\nDo your words reflect Him?\n\nDo your habits reflect Him?\n\nDo your private choices reflect Him?\n\nWhere is God inviting you to give Him glory more fully?",
  "lifeApplication": "This week, choose one area where your life can better reflect God.\n\nYour speech.\nYour entertainment.\nYour eating.\nYour rest.\nYour attitude.\nYour treatment of others.\n\nDo not try to fix everything at once.\n\nChoose one area.\n\nBring it honestly before God.\n\nAsk:\n\nDoes this help me reflect Your character?\n\nThen take one practical step.",
  "prayer": "Lord,\n\nTeach me what it means to give You glory.\n\nNot only with words,\n\nbut with my life.\n\nLet my choices reflect Your character.\n\nLet my body and mind be surrendered to You.\n\nChange what needs to change.\n\nPurify what needs to be purified.\n\nHelp others see something of You in me.\n\nAmen."
}
$json_ta_l4$::jsonb
where slug = 'how-to-glorify-god';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $json_ta_l5$
{
  "openingThought": "Judgment is not a comfortable word.\n\nFor many people, it sounds cold.\n\nSevere.\n\nThreatening.\n\nBut Scripture presents judgment as more than punishment.\n\nJudgment is where wrong is exposed.\n\nWhere truth is made clear.\n\nWhere God shows that He is just.\n\nAnd for those who are in Christ,\n\njudgment is not a reason to hide.\n\nIt is a reason to hold tightly to the Advocate.\n\nThe first angel does not say the judgment might come someday.\n\nIt says,\n\nThe hour of His judgment has come.\n\nThat means we are not dealing with theory.\n\nWe are dealing with reality.",
  "scriptureRef": "Revelation 14:7",
  "scriptureQuote": "\"Saying with a loud voice,\n\nFear God, and give glory to him;\n\nfor the hour of his judgment is come.\"",
  "scriptureSecondaryRef": "Daniel 7:9 to 10",
  "scriptureSecondaryQuote": "\"I beheld till the thrones were cast down,\n\nand the Ancient of days did sit.\n\nThe judgment was set,\n\nand the books were opened.\"",
  "scriptureAfterSecondary": "1 John 2:1\n\n\"My little children, these things write I unto you,\n\nthat ye sin not.\n\nAnd if any man sin,\n\nwe have an advocate with the Father,\n\nJesus Christ the righteous.\"\n\n1 John 1:9\n\n\"If we confess our sins,\n\nhe is faithful and just to forgive us our sins,\n\nand to cleanse us from all unrighteousness.\"\n\nThe judgment is real.\n\nThe records are real.\n\nBut so is the Advocate.",
  "teachingPoints": [
    "Revelation presents judgment as already begun before Christ returns.\nThe message says the hour of His judgment has come before the harvest scene that follows.",
    "Judgment is connected with records.\nDaniel sees the books opened, showing that God's decisions are not careless or arbitrary.",
    "Satan accuses, but Christ represents His people.\nOur hope is not that our record is clean by itself, but that Jesus stands for those who trust Him.",
    "Confession and repentance matter.\nSin is not ignored. It is forgiven, cleansed, and removed through Christ.",
    "The judgment reveals who is truly in Christ.\nThose who belong to Him receive the reward when He comes."
  ],
  "studyInsight": {
    "placement": "after_teaching",
    "title": "Study Insight",
    "body": "Many people think of judgment as a single moment.\n\nBut Revelation shows a process.\n\nThere is a judgment before the Second Coming.\n\nThere is a judgment during the millennium.\n\nThere is a final judgment after the millennium.\n\nThis lesson focuses on the judgment before Jesus returns.\n\nThat matters because Revelation 14 announces judgment before the final harvest.\n\nThe good news is that God has not left us alone in that judgment.\n\nJesus is not only the Sacrifice.\n\nHe is also the Advocate.\n\nHe lived the perfect life we failed to live.\n\nHe died the death sin deserved.\n\nAnd He now represents those who come to Him in repentance, confession, and trust.\n\nThe safest place in the judgment is not denial.\n\nIt is Christ."
  },
  "reflectPrompt": "When you hear the word judgment,\n\nwhat rises in you first?\n\nFear?\nDefensiveness?\nAvoidance?\nConfidence in Christ?\n\nDo you try to hide your sin,\n\nor bring it honestly to Jesus?\n\nWhat would change if you saw the judgment\n\nnot as a reason to run from God,\n\nbut as a reason to surrender fully to Christ?",
  "lifeApplication": "This week, take time for honest confession.\n\nDo not be vague.\n\nAsk God to show you anything you have been excusing.\n\nThen bring it to Christ.\n\nName it.\n\nConfess it.\n\nTurn from it.\n\nTrust His forgiveness.\n\nThe goal is not to live terrified.\n\nThe goal is to live clean before God,\n\ncovered by Christ,\n\nand responsive to His Spirit.",
  "prayer": "Lord,\n\nThank You that judgment does not leave me hopeless.\n\nThank You for Jesus,\n\nmy Sacrifice,\n\nmy Advocate,\n\nand my righteousness.\n\nSearch my heart.\n\nShow me what needs to be confessed.\n\nGive me repentance where I have been careless.\n\nHelp me trust Your forgiveness,\n\nand stand in Christ with a clean heart.\n\nAmen."
}
$json_ta_l5$::jsonb
where slug = 'the-hour-of-gods-judgment';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $json_ta_l6$
{
  "openingThought": "The first angel's message ends with a call to worship.\n\nNot just worship in general.\n\nWorship the Creator.\n\nThat matters because the final conflict is not merely about belief.\n\nIt is about worship.\n\nWho receives your loyalty?\n\nWho defines truth?\n\nWho has authority over your life?\n\nRevelation does not leave that question vague.\n\nIt calls the whole world back to the One who made heaven, earth, the sea, and the fountains of waters.\n\nTrue worship begins with creation.\n\nBecause the One who made us\n\nhas the right to call us back to Himself.",
  "scriptureRef": "Revelation 14:7",
  "scriptureQuote": "\"Saying with a loud voice,\n\nFear God, and give glory to him;\n\nfor the hour of his judgment is come:\n\nand worship him that made heaven, and earth, and the sea, and the fountains of waters.\"",
  "scriptureSecondaryRef": "Colossians 1:15 to 17",
  "scriptureSecondaryQuote": "\"He is the image of the invisible God,\n\nthe firstborn over all creation.\n\nFor by Him all things were created,\n\nthat are in heaven,\n\nand that are in earth,\n\nvisible and invisible.\"",
  "scriptureAfterSecondary": "Psalm 95:6\n\n\"O come, let us worship and bow down:\n\nlet us kneel before the Lord our maker.\"\n\nRevelation 4:11\n\n\"Thou art worthy, O Lord,\n\nto receive glory and honour and power:\n\nfor thou hast created all things.\"\n\nWe worship God because He is Creator.\n\nAnd Scripture identifies Christ as central in that work of creation.",
  "teachingPoints": [
    "Worship is the central issue in Revelation.\nThe contrast is between those who worship the Creator and those who worship the beast and his image.",
    "Creation is the foundation of true worship.\nGod is worthy because He made all things and sustains all things.",
    "Christ is not separate from creation.\nAll things were created through Him and for Him.",
    "Worship involves loyalty.\nIt is not only what we sing, but who we obey and trust.",
    "Revelation's call to worship points us back to creation language.\nThe wording echoes the fourth commandment and the memorial of creation."
  ],
  "studyInsight": {
    "placement": "after_teaching",
    "title": "Study Insight",
    "body": "Genesis says God rested on the seventh day.\n\nThe Hebrew word connected with this rest is **shabbat**.\n\nIt means to cease, stop, or rest.\n\nThat matters.\n\nThe Sabbath was not introduced after sin.\n\nIt was placed at creation.\n\nBefore Israel.\n\nBefore Sinai.\n\nBefore the fall.\n\nGod made the world in six days.\n\nThen He rested.\n\nBlessed the seventh day.\n\nAnd made it holy.\n\nRevelation 14 calls the world to worship the Creator.\n\nThat call points back to creation.\n\nAnd at creation, God placed a memorial of His work.\n\nThe Sabbath is not merely a day off.\n\nIt is a sign that the Creator is worthy of worship."
  },
  "reflectPrompt": "When you think about worship,\n\ndo you think mostly of songs,\n\nor surrender?\n\nDo you see worship as something you attend,\n\nor something that orders your life?\n\nWhat does it mean for you personally\n\nto worship the Creator?",
  "lifeApplication": "This week, slow down and notice creation.\n\nThe sky.\nThe trees.\nYour breath.\nYour body.\nThe rhythm of time.\n\nLet creation remind you that your life is not self-made.\n\nThen ask:\n\nAm I living as though God is Creator,\n\nor as though I belong to myself?\n\nChoose one act of worship this week that reflects loyalty to Him.\n\nNot just words.\n\nA real decision.",
  "prayer": "Lord,\n\nYou are my Creator.\n\nYou made all things,\n\nand You hold all things together.\n\nTeach me to worship You with more than words.\n\nCall my heart back to Your authority.\n\nHelp me remember that I belong to You.\n\nMake my worship faithful,\n\nhumble,\n\nand true.\n\nAmen."
}
$json_ta_l6$::jsonb
where slug = 'the-foundation-of-true-worship';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $json_ta_l7$
{
  "openingThought": "The second angel's message introduces a name that carries heavy meaning.\n\nBabylon.\n\nIt is not just a place on a map.\n\nIt is a symbol.\n\nA system.\n\nA spirit of confusion and rebellion against God.\n\nThe story begins long before Revelation.\n\nAt Babel, humanity tried to build upward without surrendering to God.\n\nA tower.\n\nA name.\n\nA system centered on human greatness.\n\nBut the result was confusion.\n\nRevelation uses that history to show us something deeper.\n\nEnd time Babylon is not merely ancient history repeated.\n\nIt is a final system that unites the world in rebellion while claiming to offer stability, truth, and worship.\n\nThe danger of Babylon is not that it looks obviously evil.\n\nThe danger is that it can look religious while leading people away from God.",
  "scriptureRef": "Revelation 14:8",
  "scriptureQuote": "\"And another angel followed, saying,\n\nBabylon is fallen, is fallen, that great city,\n\nbecause she made all nations drink of the wine of the wrath of her fornication.\"",
  "scriptureSecondaryRef": "Genesis 11:4",
  "scriptureSecondaryQuote": "\"And they said,\n\nCome, let us build us a city and a tower,\n\nwhose top may reach unto heaven;\n\nand let us make us a name.\"",
  "scriptureAfterSecondary": "Revelation 17:5\n\n\"And upon her forehead was a name written,\n\nMystery, Babylon The Great,\n\nThe Mother Of Harlots And Abominations Of The Earth.\"\n\nRevelation 17:15\n\n\"The waters which thou sawest, where the whore sitteth,\n\nare peoples, and multitudes, and nations, and tongues.\"\n\nBabylon is more than one city.\n\nIt represents a worldwide system of confusion, false worship, and human authority set against the authority of God.",
  "teachingPoints": [
    "Babylon begins with human pride.\nAt Babel, people tried to reach heaven by their own effort and make a name for themselves.",
    "Babylon produces confusion.\nThe name Babel is tied to confusion, and Revelation shows final Babylon confusing the nations spiritually.",
    "Babylon is worldwide in influence.\nRevelation says she sits on many waters, meaning peoples, multitudes, nations, and tongues.",
    "Babylon is religious, political, and economic.\nRevelation shows her connected with kings, merchants, nations, and worship.",
    "Babylon opposes true worship.\nHer influence leads people away from the Creator and toward false worship."
  ],
  "studyInsight": {
    "placement": "after_teaching",
    "title": "Study Insight",
    "body": "Revelation 14 and Revelation 17 are connected.\n\nThe first angel's message goes to every nation, kindred, tongue, and people.\n\nBabylon also sits on peoples, multitudes, nations, and tongues.\n\nThat means two messages are moving through the world.\n\nOne comes from heaven.\n\nOne comes from Babylon.\n\nOne calls people to fear God, give Him glory, and worship the Creator.\n\nThe other intoxicates the nations with false teaching and misplaced loyalty.\n\nThis is why the second angel's message matters.\n\nGod is not only exposing Babylon.\n\nHe is preparing people to come out of confusion."
  },
  "reflectPrompt": "Babylon is not only about ancient history.\n\nIt is about the temptation to build life without surrendering to God.\n\nWhere do you see that temptation today?\n\nIn culture?\nIn religion?\nIn ambition?\nIn yourself?\n\nAre there places where you want God's name,\n\nbut not God's authority?\n\nWhat would it mean to come out of confusion\n\nand stand fully with truth?",
  "lifeApplication": "This week, ask God to reveal any confusion you have accepted as normal.\n\nNot all confusion looks dark.\n\nSome of it sounds spiritual.\n\nSome of it feels popular.\n\nSome of it comes dressed as success.\n\nTake one belief, habit, or influence in your life and ask:\n\nDoes this lead me closer to God's truth,\n\nor deeper into confusion?\n\nThen be willing to follow what God shows you.",
  "prayer": "Lord,\n\nKeep me from spiritual confusion.\n\nTeach me to recognize the difference between what comes from You\n\nand what comes from human pride.\n\nGive me humility to receive truth.\n\nGive me courage to leave what is false.\n\nHelp me worship You clearly,\n\nfaithfully,\n\nand fully.\n\nAmen."
}
$json_ta_l7$::jsonb
where slug = 'mystery-babylon-the-great';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $json_ta_l8$
{
  "openingThought": "Revelation says Babylon makes the nations drunk.\n\nNot with literal wine.\n\nWith teaching.\n\nWith influence.\n\nWith ideas that distort truth.\n\nThat image matters.\n\nWine affects the mind.\n\nIt changes judgment.\n\nIt blurs what is clear.\n\nThat is what false teaching does spiritually.\n\nIt may still use religious language.\n\nIt may still speak of God.\n\nIt may even sound comforting.\n\nBut if it leads people away from Scripture,\n\naway from God's commandments,\n\naway from Christ's work,\n\nand away from true worship,\n\nthen it is not harmless.\n\nBabylon's wine is dangerous because people drink it without realizing they are losing clarity.",
  "scriptureRef": "Revelation 14:8",
  "scriptureQuote": "\"And another angel followed, saying,\n\nBabylon is fallen, is fallen, that great city,\n\nbecause she made all nations drink of the wine of the wrath of her fornication.\"",
  "scriptureSecondaryRef": "Revelation 17:2",
  "scriptureSecondaryQuote": "\"With whom the kings of the earth have committed fornication,\n\nand the inhabitants of the earth have been made drunk with the wine of her fornication.\"",
  "scriptureAfterSecondary": "Jeremiah 51:7\n\n\"Babylon hath been a golden cup in the Lord's hand,\n\nthat made all the earth drunken:\n\nthe nations have drunken of her wine;\n\ntherefore the nations are mad.\"\n\nJohn 17:17\n\n\"Sanctify them through thy truth:\n\nthy word is truth.\"\n\nTruth sanctifies.\n\nFalsehood intoxicates.\n\nThat is the contrast.",
  "teachingPoints": [
    "Babylon's wine represents false teaching.\nIt confuses the mind and leads people away from the clear Word of God.",
    "False teaching is dangerous because it can sound religious.\nThe issue is not whether something uses spiritual language, but whether it agrees with Scripture.",
    "Babylon falls because she rejects God's message.\nIf Babylon received the everlasting gospel, true worship, judgment, and obedience, she would not fall.",
    "Beliefs shape behavior.\nWhat people believe about God, sin, death, judgment, worship, and obedience changes how they live.",
    "God's answer to Babylon's wine is truth.\nThe Word of God must test every teaching, tradition, and spiritual claim."
  ],
  "studyInsight": {
    "placement": "after_teaching",
    "title": "Study Insight",
    "body": "Revelation does not say Babylon keeps her wine to herself.\n\nShe gives it to the nations.\n\nThat means her teachings spread.\n\nThey influence churches, cultures, governments, and ordinary people.\n\nSome false teachings weaken obedience.\n\nSome make sin feel safe.\n\nSome replace God's commandments with human tradition.\n\nSome confuse what happens after death.\n\nSome turn judgment into something distant or unimportant.\n\nSome present worship in a way that ignores the Creator's authority.\n\nThe issue is not merely having wrong information.\n\nWrong teaching produces wrong worship.\n\nAnd wrong worship leads the heart away from God.\n\nThis is why the Bible must remain the test.\n\nNot tradition.\n\nNot popularity.\n\nNot emotion.\n\nScripture."
  },
  "reflectPrompt": "What teachings have shaped your view of God?\n\nDid they come from Scripture,\n\nor from assumption?\n\nAre there beliefs you have accepted because they were familiar,\n\nnot because you studied them?\n\nWhere might God be asking you to test what you believe\n\nby His Word?",
  "lifeApplication": "This week, choose one belief you have always assumed was true.\n\nDo not attack it.\n\nTest it.\n\nOpen Scripture.\n\nPray honestly.\n\nAsk:\n\nWhere does the Bible teach this?\n\nDoes this belief lead me toward Christ,\n\nobedience,\n\ntruth,\n\nand worship of the Creator?\n\nIf it does, hold it with confidence.\n\nIf it does not, be willing to let it go.",
  "prayer": "Lord,\n\nProtect me from confusion.\n\nTeach me to love truth more than comfort.\n\nHelp me test every teaching by Your Word.\n\nRemove what is false.\n\nStrengthen what is true.\n\nGive me a clear mind,\n\na humble heart,\n\nand courage to follow Scripture wherever it leads.\n\nAmen."
}
$json_ta_l8$::jsonb
where slug = 'babylons-abominable-wine';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $json_ta_l9$
{
  "openingThought": "Revelation uses strong language for a reason.\n\nFornication.\n\nA harlot.\n\nKings of the earth.\n\nA religious power joined to political power.\n\nThis is not random symbolism.\n\nIn Scripture, God's people are often pictured as a woman.\n\nFaithfulness to God is purity.\n\nUnfaithfulness is spiritual adultery.\n\nSo when Revelation shows a harlot joined with the kings of the earth, the picture is serious.\n\nReligion has reached for the power of the state.\n\nSpiritual influence has become political control.\n\nWorship is no longer free.\n\nConscience is no longer protected.\n\nThat is Babylon's fornication.\n\nIt is the union of religious authority with civil power to enforce false worship.",
  "scriptureRef": "Revelation 17:1 to 2",
  "scriptureQuote": "\"Come hither;\n\nI will shew unto thee the judgment of the great whore that sitteth upon many waters:\n\nWith whom the kings of the earth have committed fornication,\n\nand the inhabitants of the earth have been made drunk with the wine of her fornication.\"",
  "scriptureSecondaryRef": "Revelation 18:3",
  "scriptureSecondaryQuote": "\"For all nations have drunk of the wine of the wrath of her fornication,\n\nand the kings of the earth have committed fornication with her.\"",
  "scriptureAfterSecondary": "Matthew 22:21\n\n\"Render therefore unto Caesar the things which are Caesar's;\n\nand unto God the things that are God's.\"\n\nJohn 18:36\n\n\"My kingdom is not of this world.\"\n\nJesus recognized the difference between the kingdom of God and the kingdoms of this world.\n\nWhen religion uses the state to control worship,\n\nit crosses a line Christ never crossed.",
  "teachingPoints": [
    "In Bible prophecy, a woman often represents a religious body.\nA pure woman represents faithfulness. A corrupt woman represents spiritual unfaithfulness.",
    "Babylon's fornication is union with the kings of the earth.\nThis points to religious power joining itself to political power.",
    "Christ's kingdom is not built by force.\nJesus never used civil power to compel worship or obedience.",
    "Forced worship is false worship.\nGod desires love, faith, and willing obedience, not outward conformity produced by pressure.",
    "When church and state unite to enforce religious practice, conscience is endangered.\nThis is one of the central warnings of Revelation."
  ],
  "studyInsight": {
    "placement": "after_teaching",
    "title": "Study Insight",
    "body": "Jesus was clear.\n\nThere are things that belong to Caesar.\n\nAnd there are things that belong to God.\n\nCivil government has a proper role.\n\nIt can protect order.\n\nIt can punish crime.\n\nIt can defend basic justice.\n\nBut it has no authority to command worship.\n\nWorship belongs to God alone.\n\nThe moment religious power reaches for the sword of the state, persecution follows.\n\nHistory has shown this again and again.\n\nRevelation shows that it will happen again at the end.\n\nBabylon's fornication is not merely moral corruption.\n\nIt is a system where religion and political power unite against the conscience of those who keep God's commandments and follow Jesus."
  },
  "reflectPrompt": "How do you think about religious freedom?\n\nDo you value it only when it protects people who agree with you?\n\nOr do you value it because worship must be free?\n\nHave you ever confused outward religious control\n\nwith genuine spiritual faithfulness?\n\nWhat does it mean to follow Christ\n\nwithout using force,\n\npressure,\n\nor political power to control another person's conscience?",
  "lifeApplication": "This week, examine your own spirit.\n\nIt is easy to condemn religious control in history\n\nwhile practicing pressure in smaller ways.\n\nAsk yourself:\n\nDo I try to control people,\n\nor persuade with truth and love?\n\nDo I respect conscience,\n\neven when I disagree?\n\nDo I trust God's Word and Spirit\n\nmore than force?\n\nChoose one relationship or conversation where you can practice the way of Christ.\n\nClear truth.\n\nHumble spirit.\n\nNo coercion.",
  "prayer": "Lord,\n\nTeach me to honor truth without using force.\n\nHelp me understand the danger of false worship\n\nand the misuse of power.\n\nGive me courage to stand for conscience.\n\nGive me humility to respect it in others.\n\nKeep me faithful to Your kingdom,\n\nwhich is not built by pressure,\n\nbut by truth,\n\nlove,\n\nand the Spirit.\n\nAmen."
}
$json_ta_l9$::jsonb
where slug = 'babylons-filthy-fornication';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $json_ta_l10$
{
  "openingThought": "Revelation 13 introduces a beast rising from the sea.\n\nThat image can feel strange at first.\n\nA beast.\n\nA sea.\n\nHorns.\n\nHeads.\n\nBlasphemy.\n\nAuthority.\n\nWar against the saints.\n\nBut prophecy does not use symbols to hide truth from honest seekers.\n\nIt uses symbols to reveal truth carefully.\n\nThe sea beast is not a monster from imagination.\n\nIt represents a real power in history.\n\nA power that receives authority.\n\nA power that claims what belongs to God.\n\nA power that persecutes the faithful.\n\nA power that receives a deadly wound,\n\nand later rises again to global influence.\n\nTo understand the final warning,\n\nwe must understand the system Revelation is warning about.",
  "scriptureRef": "Revelation 13:1 to 2",
  "scriptureQuote": "\"And I stood upon the sand of the sea,\n\nand saw a beast rise up out of the sea,\n\nhaving seven heads and ten horns,\n\nand upon his horns ten crowns,\n\nand upon his heads the name of blasphemy.\n\nAnd the beast which I saw was like unto a leopard,\n\nand his feet were as the feet of a bear,\n\nand his mouth as the mouth of a lion:\n\nand the dragon gave him his power,\n\nand his seat,\n\nand great authority.\"",
  "scriptureSecondaryRef": "Daniel 7:3",
  "scriptureSecondaryQuote": "\"And four great beasts came up from the sea,\n\ndiverse one from another.\"",
  "scriptureAfterSecondary": "Daniel 7:17\n\n\"These great beasts,\n\nwhich are four,\n\nare four kings,\n\nwhich shall arise out of the earth.\"\n\nRevelation 13 borrows language from Daniel.\n\nIn prophecy, beasts represent kingdoms or ruling powers.\n\nThe sea beast is not symbolic chaos.\n\nIt is a prophetic system with historical features.",
  "teachingPoints": [
    "A beast in Bible prophecy represents a kingdom or ruling power.\nDaniel gives the interpretive key before Revelation expands the picture.",
    "The sea represents populated areas and nations in conflict.\nThis power rises among peoples, kingdoms, and political struggle.",
    "Revelation 13 combines features from Daniel 7.\nThe lion, bear, leopard, and dreadful beast point back to the sequence of world empires.",
    "The dragon gives this beast power, throne, and authority.\nThis shows a transfer of political and religious influence.",
    "The beast speaks blasphemy and persecutes the saints.\nIts issue is not merely political power, but religious claims that belong to God alone."
  ],
  "studyInsight": {
    "placement": "after_teaching",
    "title": "Study Insight",
    "body": "Daniel 7 and Revelation 13 belong together.\n\nDaniel shows a sequence of powers:\n\nBabylon.\n\nMedo Persia.\n\nGreece.\n\nRome.\n\nDivided Rome.\n\nThe little horn.\n\nRevelation 13 looks back at that same prophetic line and focuses on the power that follows Rome's division.\n\nThis power speaks great words.\n\nIt persecutes God's people.\n\nIt claims religious authority.\n\nIt continues for a prophetic period.\n\nAnd it receives a deadly wound.\n\nThe issue is not whether a system is religious.\n\nThe issue is whether that system honors God's authority,\n\nor takes authority that belongs to Him.\n\nRevelation identifies a power that uses religion,\n\nbut turns worship and conscience away from Scripture.\n\nThat is why the warning is serious."
  },
  "reflectPrompt": "When you think about religious power,\n\ndo you assume it is always safe because it uses spiritual language?\n\nHow can something sound religious,\n\nyet still oppose God's truth?\n\nAre there places where you have trusted tradition,\n\nauthority,\n\nor popularity\n\nmore than Scripture?\n\nWhat would it mean to let the Bible test every religious claim?",
  "lifeApplication": "This week, practice discernment.\n\nChoose one belief, practice, or tradition you have accepted without much thought.\n\nDo not approach it with pride.\n\nApproach it with prayer.\n\nAsk:\n\nWhere does Scripture teach this?\n\nDoes this lead me to worship God more faithfully?\n\nDoes it honor Christ's authority?\n\nDoes it agree with God's commandments?\n\nTruth does not need fear to survive examination.\n\nLet Scripture be the test.",
  "prayer": "Lord,\n\nTeach me to understand prophecy with humility.\n\nProtect me from confusion,\n\nfear,\n\nand pride.\n\nHelp me test every claim by Your Word.\n\nGive me courage to follow truth,\n\neven when it challenges what is familiar.\n\nKeep my worship centered on You,\n\nand my conscience surrendered to Scripture.\n\nAmen."
}
$json_ta_l10$::jsonb
where slug = 'revelations-sea-beast';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $json_ta_l11$
{
  "openingThought": "Revelation does not stop with the sea beast.\n\nAnother beast appears.\n\nThis one does not rise from the sea.\n\nIt rises from the earth.\n\nIt looks different.\n\nIt has two horns like a lamb.\n\nBut it speaks like a dragon.\n\nThat is the warning.\n\nSomething can begin with gentle principles\n\nand later speak with the voice of coercion.\n\nSomething can claim liberty\n\nand later use power to control conscience.\n\nThe land beast is not introduced as openly hostile at first.\n\nIts danger is contradiction.\n\nLamb like appearance.\n\nDragon like voice.\n\nReligious freedom in profession.\n\nReligious control in practice.",
  "scriptureRef": "Revelation 13:11",
  "scriptureQuote": "\"And I beheld another beast coming up out of the earth;\n\nand he had two horns like a lamb,\n\nand he spake as a dragon.\"",
  "scriptureSecondaryRef": "Revelation 13:12",
  "scriptureSecondaryQuote": "\"And he exerciseth all the power of the first beast before him,\n\nand causeth the earth and them which dwell therein\n\nto worship the first beast,\n\nwhose deadly wound was healed.\"",
  "scriptureAfterSecondary": "Revelation 12:16\n\n\"And the earth helped the woman,\n\nand the earth opened her mouth,\n\nand swallowed up the flood which the dragon cast out of his mouth.\"\n\nMatthew 22:21\n\n\"Render therefore unto Caesar the things which are Caesar's;\n\nand unto God the things that are God's.\"\n\nThis power rises from the earth,\n\nappears lamb like,\n\nbut eventually speaks like the dragon.\n\nIts final role is to lead the world back toward the authority of the first beast.",
  "teachingPoints": [
    "The land beast rises from the earth.\nUnlike the sea, the earth points to a less populated area and a different kind of rise.",
    "It appears after the first beast receives its deadly wound.\nThe timing matters in identifying its role in prophecy.",
    "It has two horns like a lamb.\nThis points to gentle, Christ like principles in appearance.",
    "It speaks like a dragon.\nIts speech reveals a change from liberty to coercion.",
    "It causes the world to worship the first beast.\nIts final role is not neutral. It restores influence to the wounded power."
  ],
  "studyInsight": {
    "placement": "after_teaching",
    "title": "Study Insight",
    "body": "The two horns matter.\n\nIn prophecy, horns often represent powers or kingdoms.\n\nThis beast has two lamb like horns,\n\nbut no crowns.\n\nThat picture suggests a nation with two key principles,\n\nnot a monarchy.\n\nThe PDF connects this with the idea of two kingdoms existing within one nation:\n\ncivil authority\n\nand religious liberty.\n\nJesus recognized this distinction when He said to give Caesar what belongs to Caesar,\n\nand give God what belongs to God.\n\nThat separation protects conscience.\n\nBut Revelation warns that this power eventually speaks like a dragon.\n\nIt keeps its lamb like appearance,\n\nbut uses dragon like methods.\n\nThis is the danger.\n\nA nation can keep the language of liberty\n\nwhile beginning to enforce worship."
  },
  "reflectPrompt": "Why does forced religion always become dangerous?\n\nCan worship still be true if it is pressured?\n\nHave you seen people confuse outward religious behavior\n\nwith genuine surrender to God?\n\nWhere do you need to protect your own conscience,\n\nand also respect the conscience of others?",
  "lifeApplication": "This week, examine how you use influence.\n\nNot just political influence.\n\nPersonal influence.\n\nFamily influence.\n\nSpiritual influence.\n\nAsk yourself:\n\nDo I pressure people,\n\nor persuade with truth and love?\n\nDo I respect conscience,\n\nor try to control outcomes?\n\nDo I trust God's Spirit,\n\nor rely on force?\n\nChoose one conversation where you can practice Christ's way.\n\nClear truth.\n\nPatient spirit.\n\nNo coercion.",
  "prayer": "Lord,\n\nTeach me to value the conscience You gave.\n\nHelp me recognize the danger of religious force.\n\nKeep me from using pressure where You call for truth and love.\n\nGive me courage to stand for what is right,\n\nand humility to respect the freedom of others.\n\nMake my faith sincere,\n\nnot forced,\n\nand my witness faithful,\n\nnot controlling.\n\nAmen."
}
$json_ta_l11$::jsonb
where slug = 'revelations-land-beast';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $json_ta_l12$
{
  "openingThought": "An image is a copy.\n\nA likeness.\n\nA reproduction.\n\nRevelation says an image will be made to the beast.\n\nThat means something will be formed that resembles the first beast's system of power.\n\nNot necessarily the same organization.\n\nBut the same pattern.\n\nReligious authority joined with civil power.\n\nWorship enforced.\n\nConscience pressured.\n\nDissent punished.\n\nThis is why the image of the beast matters.\n\nIt is not only about what happened in history.\n\nIt is about what happens when the same principles return.\n\nWhen religion reaches for the power of the state,\n\nthe image begins to form.",
  "scriptureRef": "Revelation 13:14 to 15",
  "scriptureQuote": "\"Saying to them that dwell on the earth,\n\nthat they should make an image to the beast,\n\nwhich had the wound by a sword,\n\nand did live.\n\nAnd he had power to give life unto the image of the beast,\n\nthat the image of the beast should both speak,\n\nand cause that as many as would not worship the image of the beast\n\nshould be killed.\"",
  "scriptureSecondaryRef": "Revelation 13:12",
  "scriptureSecondaryQuote": "\"And he exerciseth all the power of the first beast before him,\n\nand causeth the earth and them which dwell therein\n\nto worship the first beast.\"",
  "scriptureAfterSecondary": "Daniel 3:4 to 6\n\n\"Then an herald cried aloud,\n\nTo you it is commanded,\n\nO people, nations, and languages,\n\nthat at what time ye hear the sound of the cornet,\n\nflute,\n\nharp,\n\nsackbut,\n\npsaltery,\n\ndulcimer,\n\nand all kinds of music,\n\nye fall down and worship the golden image.\"\n\nAn image connected with forced worship is not new.\n\nDaniel 3 helps us recognize the pattern.",
  "teachingPoints": [
    "An image is a likeness of something else.\nThe image of the beast reflects the principles and methods of the first beast.",
    "The first beast used religious authority joined with civil power.\nThe image repeats that pattern.",
    "The image speaks.\nIn prophecy, this points to official action, law, and command.",
    "The image causes.\nIt does not merely invite worship. It pressures and enforces.",
    "The issue is worship and conscience.\nThose who refuse false worship become the target of coercive power."
  ],
  "studyInsight": {
    "placement": "after_teaching",
    "title": "Study Insight",
    "body": "Daniel 3 gives a clear preview.\n\nA global empire.\n\nAn image.\n\nA command to worship.\n\nMusic and public pressure.\n\nA death decree for those who refuse.\n\nThe issue was not whether the Hebrews believed in God privately.\n\nThe issue was whether they would bow publicly.\n\nRevelation shows the same pattern at the end.\n\nFalse worship will not remain only a belief.\n\nIt will become enforced practice.\n\nThe image of the beast forms when religious influence gains civil power to enforce worship.\n\nThat is why Scripture calls God's people to patience,\n\nfaith,\n\ncommandment keeping,\n\nand loyalty to Jesus.\n\nThe final crisis reveals who owns the conscience."
  },
  "reflectPrompt": "What would make false worship powerful?\n\nFear?\n\nPressure?\n\nConvenience?\n\nDesire to fit in?\n\nWould you recognize false worship if it was popular,\n\nlegal,\n\nand presented as good for society?\n\nWhere are you learning now\n\nto obey God over pressure?",
  "lifeApplication": "This week, practice small faithfulness.\n\nDo not wait for a major crisis to learn obedience.\n\nChoose one area where pressure tempts you to compromise.\n\nIt may be private.\n\nIt may be public.\n\nIt may seem small.\n\nAsk:\n\nAm I bowing because it is easier,\n\nor standing because it is true?\n\nFaithfulness grows through repeated choices.\n\nChoose truth now,\n\nso your conscience is trained for later.",
  "prayer": "Lord,\n\nGive me a conscience loyal to You.\n\nTeach me to recognize false worship,\n\neven when it looks acceptable.\n\nKeep me from bowing to pressure.\n\nStrengthen my faith in small decisions,\n\nso I can stand in greater tests.\n\nHelp me worship You freely,\n\nfaithfully,\n\nand without compromise.\n\nAmen."
}
$json_ta_l12$::jsonb
where slug = 'the-image-of-the-beast';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $json_ta_l13$
{
  "openingThought": "Few symbols in Revelation have created more speculation than 666.\n\nPeople have guessed names.\n\nWatched headlines.\n\nChased theories.\n\nBut Revelation does not give this number so we can play with fear.\n\nIt gives it so we can understand a system.\n\nThe number is connected to the beast.\n\nTo worship.\n\nTo authority.\n\nTo a name.\n\nTo a claim that belongs only to God.\n\nSo the question is not,\n\nWho can we accuse?\n\nThe better question is,\n\nWhat kind of authority is Revelation warning us about?\n\nBecause 666 is not mainly about curiosity.\n\nIt is about counterfeit authority.",
  "scriptureRef": "Revelation 13:18",
  "scriptureQuote": "\"Here is wisdom.\n\nLet him that hath understanding count the number of the beast:\n\nfor it is the number of a man;\n\nand his number is Six hundred threescore and six.\"",
  "scriptureSecondaryRef": "Revelation 13:5 to 6",
  "scriptureSecondaryQuote": "\"And there was given unto him a mouth speaking great things and blasphemies.\n\nAnd he opened his mouth in blasphemy against God,\n\nto blaspheme his name,\n\nand his tabernacle,\n\nand them that dwell in heaven.\"",
  "scriptureAfterSecondary": "2 Thessalonians 2:3 to 4\n\n\"That man of sin be revealed,\n\nthe son of perdition;\n\nwho opposeth and exalteth himself above all that is called God,\n\nor that is worshipped.\"\n\nThe number is tied to a power that speaks blasphemy,\n\nexalts itself,\n\nand claims authority that belongs to God.",
  "teachingPoints": [
    "Revelation says the number calls for wisdom.\nThis is not meant for wild guessing, but careful spiritual discernment.",
    "The number belongs to the beast system.\nIt must be studied in the same context as worship, authority, blasphemy, and the mark.",
    "Revelation says it is the number of a man.\nThis points to a system centered in human authority rather than God's authority.",
    "The beast's name is blasphemous.\nIt claims religious authority and prerogatives that belong only to Christ.",
    "In this prophetic interpretation, the number is connected with Rome and Latin titles of religious authority.\nThe issue is not personal insult, but a system that places human authority where Christ alone belongs."
  ],
  "studyInsight": {
    "placement": "after_teaching",
    "title": "Study Insight",
    "body": "Revelation 13 points us toward a name that carries a number.\n\nIn ancient languages, letters often carried numerical value.\n\nBecause the beast is connected with Rome,\n\nthe PDF source argues that the number should be understood through Latin.\n\nThe title often discussed in this interpretation is:\n\n**Vicarius Filii Dei**\n\nIt means,\n\nVicar of the Son of God.\n\nThe concern is not simply the words themselves.\n\nThe concern is the claim behind them.\n\nA representative who stands in Christ's place on earth.\n\nA religious authority claiming power to act for Christ,\n\neven where Scripture gives no permission.\n\nThat is why this lesson matters.\n\nThe number 666 is not meant to distract us with fear.\n\nIt is meant to expose a system that centers human authority in the place of Christ's authority."
  },
  "reflectPrompt": "When you think about spiritual authority,\n\nwhat do you trust most?\n\nScripture?\n\nTradition?\n\nReligious office?\n\nPopular belief?\n\nHuman explanation?\n\nAre there places where you have allowed human authority\n\nto outweigh the Word of God?\n\nWhat would it mean to let Christ alone hold the highest authority in your faith?",
  "lifeApplication": "This week, practice testing authority.\n\nChoose one belief or practice you have accepted.\n\nAsk:\n\nWhere does Scripture teach this?\n\nDoes this exalt Christ,\n\nor human authority?\n\nDoes this lead me to obey God,\n\nor merely follow tradition?\n\nDo not study with suspicion.\n\nStudy with humility.\n\nChrist is not threatened by honest questions.\n\nTruth can be examined.",
  "prayer": "Lord,\n\nGive me wisdom.\n\nTeach me to recognize the difference between Your authority\n\nand human authority.\n\nKeep me from fear,\n\nspeculation,\n\nand pride.\n\nLead me back to Scripture.\n\nKeep Christ at the center of my worship,\n\nmy obedience,\n\nand my trust.\n\nAmen."
}
$json_ta_l13$::jsonb
where slug = '666-the-number-of-the-beast';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $json_ta_l14$
{
  "openingThought": "The mark of the beast is not introduced in a vacuum.\n\nIt comes after a long conflict.\n\nA conflict over worship.\n\nA conflict over authority.\n\nA conflict over obedience.\n\nMany people want to know what the mark is,\n\nbut Revelation first shows us what the conflict is about.\n\nIt is not merely about technology.\n\nIt is not merely about politics.\n\nIt is not merely about a visible sign.\n\nIt is about loyalty.\n\nWho has the right to define worship?\n\nWho has the right to command conscience?\n\nWho has the right to shape obedience?\n\nBefore we identify the mark,\n\nwe must understand the law, worship, and the seal of God.",
  "scriptureRef": "Revelation 14:9 to 12",
  "scriptureQuote": "\"If any man worship the beast and his image,\n\nand receive his mark in his forehead,\n\nor in his hand,\n\nthe same shall drink of the wine of the wrath of God.\"\n\n\"Here is the patience of the saints:\n\nhere are they that keep the commandments of God,\n\nand the faith of Jesus.\"",
  "scriptureSecondaryRef": "Revelation 12:17",
  "scriptureSecondaryQuote": "\"And the dragon was wroth with the woman,\n\nand went to make war with the remnant of her seed,\n\nwhich keep the commandments of God,\n\nand have the testimony of Jesus Christ.\"",
  "scriptureAfterSecondary": "Hebrews 8:10\n\n\"I will put my laws into their mind,\n\nand write them in their hearts:\n\nand I will be to them a God,\n\nand they shall be to me a people.\"\n\nIsaiah 8:16\n\n\"Bind up the testimony,\n\nseal the law among my disciples.\"\n\nRevelation contrasts two groups.\n\nOne receives the mark of the beast.\n\nThe other keeps the commandments of God and the faith of Jesus.",
  "teachingPoints": [
    "The final conflict is connected with worship.\nRevelation repeatedly shows the issue as worship of God or worship of the beast.",
    "The final conflict is connected with God's commandments.\nThe faithful are described as those who keep the commandments of God.",
    "The seal of God is connected with the mind and the heart.\nGod writes His law inwardly, not merely outwardly.",
    "The forehead and hand matter symbolically.\nThe forehead points to conviction. The hand points to action.",
    "The mark of the beast is a counterfeit of God's seal.\nIt involves false worship, human authority, and obedience against God's command."
  ],
  "studyInsight": {
    "placement": "after_teaching",
    "title": "Study Insight",
    "body": "Think of the final issue like three circles.\n\nThe largest circle is the law of God.\n\nThe smaller circle is the first table of the law,\n\nthe commandments that deal directly with worship and loyalty to God.\n\nThe smallest circle is the one commandment that identifies the Creator.\n\nThe Sabbath commandment.\n\nThat commandment contains the elements of a seal:\n\nthe name,\n\nthe title,\n\nand the territory of authority.\n\nThe Lord your God.\n\nCreator.\n\nHeaven and earth.\n\nThis is why the mark of the beast cannot be understood apart from worship and law.\n\nThe issue is not simply a day.\n\nIt is authority.\n\nGod's authority,\n\nor human authority.\n\nGod's command,\n\nor human substitution."
  },
  "reflectPrompt": "When you hear \"the mark of the beast,\"\n\ndo you think first of fear,\n\nor worship?\n\nHave you studied this from Scripture,\n\nor mostly from assumptions?\n\nWhat does your obedience reveal about whose authority you trust?\n\nAre there places where you follow what is convenient\n\ninstead of what is written?",
  "lifeApplication": "This week, read Revelation 14:9 to 12 carefully.\n\nNotice the contrast.\n\nMark of the beast.\n\nCommandments of God.\n\nFaith of Jesus.\n\nDo not rush.\n\nAsk:\n\nWhat does this passage say the final issue is?\n\nWhat kind of people does God call faithful?\n\nWhere is God calling me to deeper loyalty?\n\nLet Scripture define the issue before culture does.",
  "prayer": "Lord,\n\nHelp me understand this warning with humility.\n\nRemove fear,\n\nspeculation,\n\nand confusion.\n\nTeach me to see the real issue.\n\nWorship.\n\nAuthority.\n\nObedience.\n\nWrite Your law in my heart and mind.\n\nStrengthen my faith in Jesus.\n\nMake my loyalty clear,\n\nnot only in words,\n\nbut in my life.\n\nAmen."
}
$json_ta_l14$::jsonb
where slug = 'the-mark-of-the-beast-part-1';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $json_ta_l15$
{
  "openingThought": "If the final conflict centers on worship,\n\nthen the question becomes practical.\n\nWhat worship does God command?\n\nWhat worship has human authority substituted?\n\nRevelation points us back to creation.\n\nTo the One who made heaven,\n\nearth,\n\nthe sea,\n\nand the fountains of waters.\n\nThat language is not accidental.\n\nIt echoes the fourth commandment.\n\nThe Sabbath stands as a memorial of creation,\n\na sign of God's authority,\n\nand a test of loyalty when human authority claims the right to change what God has blessed.\n\nThe mark of the beast is not about an accidental misunderstanding.\n\nIt is about choosing human authority over God's command when the issue is made clear.",
  "scriptureRef": "Genesis 2:2 to 3",
  "scriptureQuote": "\"And on the seventh day God ended his work which he had made;\n\nand he rested on the seventh day from all his work which he had made.\n\nAnd God blessed the seventh day,\n\nand sanctified it.\"",
  "scriptureSecondaryRef": "Exodus 20:8 to 11",
  "scriptureSecondaryQuote": "\"Remember the sabbath day,\n\nto keep it holy.\n\nSix days shalt thou labour,\n\nand do all thy work:\n\nbut the seventh day is the sabbath of the Lord thy God.\"",
  "scriptureAfterSecondary": "Mark 2:27 to 28\n\n\"The sabbath was made for man,\n\nand not man for the sabbath:\n\nTherefore the Son of man is Lord also of the sabbath.\"\n\nDaniel 7:25\n\n\"He shall speak great words against the most High,\n\nand shall wear out the saints of the most High,\n\nand think to change times and laws.\"\n\nThe Bible presents the seventh day as blessed,\n\nsanctified,\n\nand connected with the authority of the Creator.",
  "teachingPoints": [
    "The Sabbath began at creation.\nIt was given before sin, before Sinai, and before any nation existed.",
    "The Sabbath points to God as Creator.\nThat is why Revelation's call to worship the Creator matters.",
    "Jesus identified Himself as Lord of the Sabbath.\nThe Sabbath belongs to Him, not to human tradition.",
    "Daniel warned of a power that would think to change times and law.\nThis connects directly with authority over worship.",
    "The mark of the beast centers on enforced false worship.\nIt becomes the mark when human authority is chosen over God's command in the final crisis."
  ],
  "studyInsight": {
    "placement": "after_teaching",
    "title": "Study Insight",
    "body": "The PDF source identifies Sunday observance as the mark of beast authority\n\nwhen it is knowingly accepted and enforced in place of God's Sabbath.\n\nThis distinction matters.\n\nA person is not marked simply because they were taught incorrectly,\n\nor because they have not yet seen the issue clearly.\n\nThe final crisis involves light,\n\nchoice,\n\npressure,\n\nand worship.\n\nThe Sabbath is God's sign of creation authority.\n\nSunday, in this prophetic understanding, becomes the sign of human religious authority when it is exalted above God's command and enforced by civil power.\n\nThe issue is not calendar preference.\n\nIt is authority.\n\nWho has the right to define holy time?\n\nGod,\n\nor man?"
  },
  "reflectPrompt": "Have you thought seriously about why you worship when you worship?\n\nIs your practice based on Scripture,\n\ntradition,\n\nfamily,\n\nculture,\n\nor convenience?\n\nIf God has blessed and sanctified the seventh day,\n\nwhat does that mean for your life?\n\nAre you willing to follow Scripture\n\neven when it challenges what is familiar?",
  "lifeApplication": "This week, study the Sabbath for yourself.\n\nRead Genesis 2:2 to 3.\n\nRead Exodus 20:8 to 11.\n\nRead Mark 2:27 to 28.\n\nRead Revelation 14:6 to 12.\n\nDo not begin with tradition.\n\nBegin with Scripture.\n\nAsk:\n\nWhat did God bless?\n\nWhat did God command?\n\nWhat did Jesus affirm?\n\nWhat does Revelation call the world back to?\n\nLet the Bible speak clearly.",
  "prayer": "Lord,\n\nGive me a heart that follows Your Word.\n\nHelp me lay aside assumption,\n\ntradition,\n\nand convenience.\n\nShow me what You have blessed.\n\nTeach me what true worship means.\n\nGive me courage to follow You\n\nwhen truth becomes clear.\n\nKeep my loyalty with Christ,\n\nand my conscience surrendered to Scripture.\n\nAmen."
}
$json_ta_l15$::jsonb
where slug = 'the-mark-of-the-beast-part-2';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $json_ta_l16$
{
  "openingThought": "The third angel's message contains one of the strongest warnings in Scripture.\n\nIt speaks of the wine of God's wrath.\n\nThat phrase is heavy.\n\nIt should not be treated carelessly.\n\nBut it should also not be misunderstood.\n\nGod's wrath is not uncontrolled anger.\n\nIt is His settled response to sin,\n\nrebellion,\n\nfalse worship,\n\nand the final rejection of mercy.\n\nBefore the wine of wrath is poured out,\n\nthe everlasting gospel has been preached.\n\nTruth has been given.\n\nWarnings have been heard.\n\nMercy has been offered.\n\nThe wine of God's wrath comes only after people choose the beast,\n\nhis image,\n\nand his mark,\n\ninstead of Christ,\n\nHis commandments,\n\nand His truth.",
  "scriptureRef": "Revelation 14:9 to 10",
  "scriptureQuote": "\"If any man worship the beast and his image,\n\nand receive his mark in his forehead,\n\nor in his hand,\n\nthe same shall drink of the wine of the wrath of God,\n\nwhich is poured out without mixture into the cup of his indignation.\"",
  "scriptureSecondaryRef": "Revelation 15:1",
  "scriptureSecondaryQuote": "\"And I saw another sign in heaven,\n\ngreat and marvellous,\n\nseven angels having the seven last plagues;\n\nfor in them is filled up the wrath of God.\"",
  "scriptureAfterSecondary": "Revelation 16:1\n\n\"And I heard a great voice out of the temple saying to the seven angels,\n\nGo your ways,\n\nand pour out the vials of the wrath of God upon the earth.\"\n\nRomans 6:23\n\n\"For the wages of sin is death;\n\nbut the gift of God is eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord.\"\n\nGod's wrath is real.\n\nBut so is His gift.\n\nThe issue is whether a person receives what Christ has provided,\n\nor chooses to stand under sin's final consequence.",
  "teachingPoints": [
    "The wine of God's wrath is connected with the final rejection of mercy.\nIt is poured out after truth has been proclaimed and choices have been made.",
    "The wrath of God is revealed in the seven last plagues.\nRevelation shows the bowls of wrath being poured out after probation closes.",
    "God's wrath is unmixed.\nThat means it is no longer softened by mercy because mercy has been finally rejected.",
    "The issue is not whether Christ paid for sin.\nThe issue is whether people receive His payment or choose to carry their own guilt.",
    "The final consequence of sin is death.\nJesus offers eternal life, but He does not force anyone to receive it."
  ],
  "studyInsight": {
    "placement": "after_teaching",
    "title": "Study Insight",
    "body": "The Bible presents two ways for sin to be dealt with.\n\nEither Christ carries it,\n\nor the sinner does.\n\nAt the cross, Jesus paid the price for the world.\n\nThe gift is real.\n\nThe sacrifice is enough.\n\nThe provision has been made.\n\nBut a gift can be refused.\n\nThat is what makes the wine of God's wrath so serious.\n\nIt is not poured out because God failed to save.\n\nIt is poured out because people finally reject the only One who can save them.\n\nThe wrath of God is not the opposite of His love.\n\nIt is what happens when love, truth, and mercy are permanently rejected."
  },
  "reflectPrompt": "When you think about God's wrath,\n\ndo you see only punishment,\n\nor do you also see rejected mercy?\n\nHave you treated grace as something ordinary?\n\nAre there areas where God has been warning you,\n\nbut you have delayed responding?\n\nWhat would it mean to receive Christ fully,\n\ninstead of resisting what He is trying to save you from?",
  "lifeApplication": "This week, take grace seriously.\n\nDo not use mercy as an excuse to delay obedience.\n\nAsk God to show you one area where you have been resisting Him.\n\nThen respond.\n\nConfess what needs to be confessed.\n\nRelease what needs to be released.\n\nObey what has already been made clear.\n\nThe safest place is not denial.\n\nThe safest place is surrender to Christ.",
  "prayer": "Lord,\n\nThank You for offering mercy before judgment.\n\nThank You for the gift of eternal life through Jesus.\n\nHelp me not to take grace lightly.\n\nShow me where I have resisted Your voice.\n\nLead me to repentance.\n\nTeach me to receive what Christ has provided,\n\nand to stand on the side of mercy,\n\ntruth,\n\nand life.\n\nAmen."
}
$json_ta_l16$::jsonb
where slug = 'the-wine-of-gods-wrath';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $json_ta_l17$
{
  "openingThought": "Some verses in Revelation sound terrifying at first reading.\n\nFire.\n\nBrimstone.\n\nTorment.\n\nSmoke rising forever.\n\nNo rest day or night.\n\nMany people read those words and assume the Bible teaches endless conscious suffering.\n\nBut Scripture must explain Scripture.\n\nRevelation uses symbols and language drawn from the Old Testament.\n\nIf we read carefully,\n\nthe picture becomes clearer.\n\nThe fire is real.\n\nThe judgment is real.\n\nThe consequences are eternal.\n\nBut the Bible does not teach that sin will live forever in a corner of God's universe.\n\nGod's final judgment destroys sin completely.\n\nThe smoke rises because the destruction is final,\n\nnot because the wicked live forever.",
  "scriptureRef": "Revelation 14:10 to 11",
  "scriptureQuote": "\"He shall be tormented with fire and brimstone\n\nin the presence of the holy angels,\n\nand in the presence of the Lamb:\n\nAnd the smoke of their torment ascendeth up for ever and ever.\"",
  "scriptureSecondaryRef": "Jude 7",
  "scriptureSecondaryQuote": "\"Sodom and Gomorrha,\n\nand the cities about them in like manner,\n\ngiving themselves over to fornication,\n\nand going after strange flesh,\n\nare set forth for an example,\n\nsuffering the vengeance of eternal fire.\"",
  "scriptureAfterSecondary": "2 Peter 2:6\n\n\"And turning the cities of Sodom and Gomorrha into ashes\n\ncondemned them with an overthrow,\n\nmaking them an ensample unto those that after should live ungodly.\"\n\nMalachi 4:1\n\n\"For, behold, the day cometh,\n\nthat shall burn as an oven;\n\nand all the proud,\n\nyea,\n\nand all that do wickedly,\n\nshall be stubble.\"\n\nSodom suffered eternal fire,\n\nbut it is not still burning today.\n\nThe result was eternal.\n\nThe cities were reduced to ashes.",
  "teachingPoints": [
    "Revelation uses Old Testament judgment language.\nTo understand \"forever and ever,\" we must compare Scripture with Scripture.",
    "Eternal fire does not mean the fire burns without end.\nJude says Sodom suffered eternal fire, yet Peter says it was turned to ashes.",
    "The punishment is eternal in result.\nThe wicked do not receive eternal life in torment. They face final destruction.",
    "The second death is final.\nRevelation 20 describes the lake of fire as the second death.",
    "God will not preserve sin forever.\nThe final judgment removes sin, rebellion, Satan, and death from the universe."
  ],
  "studyInsight": {
    "placement": "after_teaching",
    "title": "Study Insight",
    "body": "The phrase \"smoke ascending\" appears in the Old Testament as a picture of complete judgment.\n\nWhen Sodom was destroyed,\n\nAbraham saw smoke rising like the smoke of a furnace.\n\nThe smoke was evidence that the judgment had taken place.\n\nIt did not mean the city would burn forever.\n\nIt meant the destruction was complete.\n\nRevelation uses similar language.\n\nThe smoke of Babylon's torment rising forever points to a judgment whose result cannot be reversed.\n\nThe wicked do not continue living forever in suffering.\n\nThey are destroyed.\n\nSin ends.\n\nDeath ends.\n\nThe universe is cleansed.\n\nGod's justice is not eternal cruelty.\n\nIt is final judgment that makes way for eternal restoration."
  },
  "reflectPrompt": "How has your view of God been shaped by the idea of judgment?\n\nHave you pictured Him as cruel,\n\nor as just?\n\nDoes the Bible's teaching about final destruction change how you see His character?\n\nWhat does it mean to you that God will not allow sin to continue forever?",
  "lifeApplication": "This week, read Jude 7 and 2 Peter 2:6 together.\n\nNotice the connection.\n\nEternal fire.\n\nReduced to ashes.\n\nThen read Revelation 20:14 to 15.\n\nAsk:\n\nWhat is the final result of sin?\n\nDoes God preserve evil forever,\n\nor end it completely?\n\nLet Scripture correct fear,\n\nand give you a clearer picture of God's justice.",
  "prayer": "Lord,\n\nHelp me understand Your justice through Scripture.\n\nRemove false pictures of Your character.\n\nTeach me to see that You are holy,\n\nfair,\n\nmerciful,\n\nand true.\n\nThank You that sin will not last forever.\n\nThank You that death will end.\n\nHelp me trust Your judgment,\n\nand choose life in Christ.\n\nAmen."
}
$json_ta_l17$::jsonb
where slug = 'the-smoke-of-babylons-torment';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $json_ta_l18$
{
  "openingThought": "After the warning against the beast,\n\nhis image,\n\nand his mark,\n\nRevelation shows us a different kind of people.\n\nNot loud.\n\nNot powerful in the world's eyes.\n\nNot supported by earthly systems.\n\nBut faithful.\n\nThey endure.\n\nThey keep God's commandments.\n\nThey hold to the faith of Jesus.\n\nThat is what Revelation calls the patience of the saints.\n\nThis is not passive waiting.\n\nIt is steady faithfulness under pressure.\n\nIt is refusing to let go of God\n\nwhen the world makes obedience costly.",
  "scriptureRef": "Revelation 14:12",
  "scriptureQuote": "\"Here is the patience of the saints:\n\nhere are they that keep the commandments of God,\n\nand the faith of Jesus.\"",
  "scriptureSecondaryRef": "Luke 18:1",
  "scriptureSecondaryQuote": "\"And he spake a parable unto them to this end,\n\nthat men ought always to pray,\n\nand not to faint.\"",
  "scriptureAfterSecondary": "Luke 18:7 to 8\n\n\"And shall not God avenge his own elect,\n\nwhich cry day and night unto him,\n\nthough he bear long with them?\n\nI tell you that he will avenge them speedily.\n\nNevertheless when the Son of man cometh,\n\nshall he find faith on the earth?\"\n\n1 Peter 5:8\n\n\"Be sober,\n\nbe vigilant;\n\nbecause your adversary the devil,\n\nas a roaring lion,\n\nwalketh about,\n\nseeking whom he may devour.\"\n\nThe saints endure because they keep praying,\n\nkeep trusting,\n\nand keep obeying,\n\neven when the adversary presses hard.",
  "teachingPoints": [
    "The patience of the saints is endurance under pressure.\nIt is faithfulness when obedience becomes costly.",
    "The saints are God's faithful people.\nIn Daniel and Revelation, the saints are opposed, persecuted, and tested by hostile powers.",
    "Their strength is not earthly support.\nLike the widow in Luke 18, their hope is in God's intervention.",
    "Their endurance is connected with prayer.\nJesus taught His people to pray always and not lose heart.",
    "Their faithfulness is visible.\nThey keep God's commandments and hold to the faith of Jesus."
  ],
  "studyInsight": {
    "placement": "after_teaching",
    "title": "Study Insight",
    "body": "The Greek word translated \"patience\" is **hupomone**.\n\nIt means endurance,\n\nperseverance,\n\nsteadfastness,\n\nand the refusal to give up under pressure.\n\nThis is not weak patience.\n\nIt is not simply waiting quietly.\n\nIt is a stubborn faith that keeps holding on to God.\n\nThe PDF connects this with the persistent widow in Luke 18.\n\nShe had no earthly support.\n\nNo power.\n\nNo protection.\n\nBut she kept coming to the judge for justice.\n\nIn the same way,\n\nGod's faithful people will face pressure,\n\nbut they will not stop praying.\n\nThey will not stop trusting.\n\nThey will not stop obeying.\n\nThat is **hupomone**.\n\nHoly endurance."
  },
  "reflectPrompt": "How do you usually respond when obedience becomes hard?\n\nDo you pull back?\n\nCompromise?\n\nGrow tired?\n\nOr keep holding on?\n\nWhere do you need hupomone,\n\nsteady endurance,\n\nin your walk with God right now?",
  "lifeApplication": "This week, practice endurance in one small area.\n\nChoose one thing God has made clear to you.\n\nKeep doing it.\n\nEven if it feels slow.\n\nEven if no one notices.\n\nEven if it costs comfort.\n\nPray instead of quitting.\n\nObey instead of drifting.\n\nTrust instead of shrinking back.\n\nEndurance is built before the crisis,\n\nnot during it.",
  "prayer": "Lord,\n\nGive me the patience of the saints.\n\nTeach me to endure without bitterness.\n\nTeach me to pray without giving up.\n\nTeach me to obey when it costs something.\n\nStrengthen my faith in Jesus.\n\nWrite Your commandments in my heart.\n\nMake me steady,\n\nfaithful,\n\nand ready to stand with You.\n\nAmen."
}
$json_ta_l18$::jsonb
where slug = 'the-patience-of-the-saints';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $json_ta_l19$
{
  "openingThought": "Revelation does not describe God's final people vaguely.\n\nIt gives clear marks of their character.\n\nThey have patience.\n\nThey have faith.\n\nAnd they keep the commandments of God.\n\nThat phrase matters.\n\nIn a world where many people separate love from obedience,\n\nRevelation brings them back together.\n\nObedience is not the way we earn God's love.\n\nIt is the way love becomes visible.\n\nGod's commandments are not chains.\n\nThey reveal His character.\n\nThey show what love looks like toward God and toward others.\n\nThe final issue is not whether people talk about God.\n\nIt is whether they trust Him enough to obey.",
  "scriptureRef": "Revelation 14:12",
  "scriptureQuote": "\"Here is the patience of the saints:\n\nhere are they that keep the commandments of God,\n\nand the faith of Jesus.\"",
  "scriptureSecondaryRef": "Revelation 12:17",
  "scriptureSecondaryQuote": "\"And the dragon was wroth with the woman,\n\nand went to make war with the remnant of her seed,\n\nwhich keep the commandments of God,\n\nand have the testimony of Jesus Christ.\"",
  "scriptureAfterSecondary": "Ecclesiastes 12:13\n\n\"Let us hear the conclusion of the whole matter:\n\nFear God,\n\nand keep his commandments:\n\nfor this is the whole duty of man.\"\n\nJohn 14:15\n\n\"If ye love me,\n\nkeep my commandments.\"\n\nRevelation's faithful people are not lawless.\n\nThey are not legalistic either.\n\nThey love Christ,\n\nand that love produces obedience.",
  "teachingPoints": [
    "God's commandments reveal His character.\nThey show what love, loyalty, worship, truth, purity, and justice look like.",
    "Obedience does not save us.\nOnly Christ saves. But saving faith does not produce rebellion against God.",
    "God writes His law in the heart.\nThe new covenant does not erase obedience. It moves obedience inward.",
    "Revelation identifies the faithful by commandment keeping.\nThis matters because the final conflict centers on worship and authority.",
    "True obedience is relational.\nIt flows from love, trust, and surrender, not from fear or pride."
  ],
  "studyInsight": {
    "placement": "after_teaching",
    "title": "Study Insight",
    "body": "The PDF source emphasizes that God's law must be more than posted externally.\n\nIt must be written internally.\n\nA commandment can be displayed on a wall\n\nand still be absent from the heart.\n\nGod's desire is deeper.\n\nHe says He will put His law in the mind\n\nand write it in the heart.\n\nThat means obedience is not forced from the outside.\n\nIt grows from a changed life within.\n\nThis is why force can never produce true righteousness.\n\nForce creates fear.\n\nForce creates hypocrisy.\n\nBut the Spirit creates love,\n\nconviction,\n\nand willing obedience.\n\nThe commandments of God are not merely rules to defend.\n\nThey are truth to be lived."
  },
  "reflectPrompt": "How do you think about God's commandments?\n\nAs restrictions?\n\nAs protection?\n\nAs a burden?\n\nAs a reflection of His love?\n\nIs there a command of God you agree with in theory,\n\nbut resist in practice?\n\nWhat would change if obedience became your response to love,\n\nnot your attempt to earn acceptance?",
  "lifeApplication": "This week, choose one command of God and look at it differently.\n\nDo not ask only,\n\nWhat does this forbid?\n\nAsk,\n\nWhat does this reveal about God?\n\nWhat does this protect?\n\nHow does this teach me to love?\n\nThen take one practical step of obedience.\n\nNot to earn salvation.\n\nBut because you trust the One who gave the command.",
  "prayer": "Lord,\n\nWrite Your law in my heart.\n\nTeach me to see Your commandments as good,\n\nwise,\n\nand loving.\n\nDeliver me from legalism.\n\nDeliver me from rebellion.\n\nGive me a heart that trusts You enough to obey.\n\nLet my life show love,\n\nnot only in words,\n\nbut in faithful action.\n\nAmen."
}
$json_ta_l19$::jsonb
where slug = 'the-commandments-of-god';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $json_ta_l20$
{
  "openingThought": "Revelation 14:12 holds two truths together.\n\nThe commandments of God.\n\nAnd the faith of Jesus.\n\nIf we separate them,\n\nwe fall into trouble.\n\nCommandments without faith become legalism.\n\nFaith without obedience becomes empty profession.\n\nGod's final people are not described as people who choose one and reject the other.\n\nThey keep God's commandments,\n\nand they hold to the faith of Jesus.\n\nThis is the balance the final message restores.\n\nNot law without grace.\n\nNot grace without obedience.\n\nChrist at the center,\n\nand a life shaped by trust in Him.",
  "scriptureRef": "Revelation 14:12",
  "scriptureQuote": "\"Here is the patience of the saints:\n\nhere are they that keep the commandments of God,\n\nand the faith of Jesus.\"",
  "scriptureSecondaryRef": "Ephesians 2:8 to 10",
  "scriptureSecondaryQuote": "\"For by grace are ye saved through faith;\n\nand that not of yourselves:\n\nit is the gift of God:\n\nNot of works,\n\nlest any man should boast.\n\nFor we are his workmanship,\n\ncreated in Christ Jesus unto good works.\"",
  "scriptureAfterSecondary": "James 2:17\n\n\"Even so faith,\n\nif it hath not works,\n\nis dead,\n\nbeing alone.\"\n\nGalatians 2:20\n\n\"I live by the faith of the Son of God,\n\nwho loved me,\n\nand gave himself for me.\"\n\nFaith saves.\n\nFaith works.\n\nFaith follows.",
  "teachingPoints": [
    "We are saved by grace through faith.\nSalvation is God's gift, not our achievement.",
    "Good works are the fruit of salvation.\nThey do not purchase salvation, but they reveal that Christ is working in us.",
    "Legalism trusts obedience apart from Christ.\nIt may look religious, but it centers the self.",
    "Cheap grace claims faith while resisting obedience.\nIt wants Christ's pardon without Christ's lordship.",
    "The faith of Jesus keeps the balance.\nTrust in Christ leads to a life shaped by Christ."
  ],
  "studyInsight": {
    "placement": "after_teaching",
    "title": "Study Insight",
    "body": "The phrase \"**faith of Jesus**\" can be understood in two closely connected ways.\n\nIt can mean faith in Jesus.\n\nAnd it can mean the faithfulness of Jesus.\n\nBoth matter.\n\nWe trust in Jesus.\n\nAnd we receive the kind of faithful life that Jesus lived.\n\nThis keeps us out of both ditches.\n\nOne ditch says,\n\nMy obedience saves me.\n\nThe other ditch says,\n\nObedience does not matter.\n\nThe gospel says something better.\n\nChrist saves completely,\n\nand the faith that receives Him does not remain dead.\n\nIt becomes active.\n\nIt loves.\n\nIt obeys.\n\nIt endures."
  },
  "reflectPrompt": "Which ditch are you more tempted toward?\n\nTrying to prove yourself by obedience?\n\nOr using grace as a reason to avoid obedience?\n\nDo you trust Christ enough to rest in His salvation,\n\nand follow His commands?\n\nWhere does your faith need to become more active?",
  "lifeApplication": "This week, examine your faith honestly.\n\nChoose one area where you say you believe,\n\nbut your actions have not followed.\n\nDo not condemn yourself.\n\nBring it to Christ.\n\nAsk Him for faith that moves.\n\nFaith that obeys.\n\nFaith that loves.\n\nThen take one clear step.\n\nNot to earn God's favor,\n\nbut because you already have His grace.",
  "prayer": "Lord,\n\nKeep me from legalism.\n\nKeep me from empty profession.\n\nTeach me the faith of Jesus.\n\nHelp me rest in what Christ has done,\n\nand walk in what Christ commands.\n\nLet my obedience come from trust,\n\nnot pride.\n\nLet my faith be alive,\n\nnot merely spoken.\n\nMake me faithful through Jesus.\n\nAmen."
}
$json_ta_l20$::jsonb
where slug = 'the-faith-of-jesus';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $json_ta_l21$
{
  "openingThought": "Revelation shows a dragon at war.\n\nBut not with everyone in the same way.\n\nHe is angry with a woman.\n\nThen with the remnant of her seed.\n\nThat phrase matters.\n\nThe remnant is not introduced as a random group.\n\nThey belong to the woman.\n\nThey are connected to Christ.\n\nThey keep God's commandments.\n\nThey have the testimony of Jesus.\n\nIn prophecy, the final conflict is not only about systems of false worship.\n\nIt is also about a people who remain faithful to Christ when pressure rises.\n\nThe remnant is not defined by pride.\n\nIt is defined by loyalty.",
  "scriptureRef": "Revelation 12:17",
  "scriptureQuote": "\"And the dragon was wroth with the woman,\n\nand went to make war with the remnant of her seed,\n\nwhich keep the commandments of God,\n\nand have the testimony of Jesus Christ.\"",
  "scriptureSecondaryRef": "Genesis 3:15",
  "scriptureSecondaryQuote": "\"And I will put enmity between thee and the woman,\n\nand between thy seed and her seed;\n\nit shall bruise thy head,\n\nand thou shalt bruise his heel.\"",
  "scriptureAfterSecondary": "Galatians 3:16\n\n\"Now to Abraham and his seed were the promises made.\n\nHe saith not,\n\nAnd to seeds,\n\nas of many;\n\nbut as of one,\n\nAnd to thy seed,\n\nwhich is Christ.\"\n\nGalatians 3:29\n\n\"And if ye be Christ's,\n\nthen are ye Abraham's seed,\n\nand heirs according to the promise.\"\n\nThe remnant belongs to Christ.\n\nThey are His people,\n\nformed by faith,\n\nidentified by loyalty,\n\nand marked by obedience.",
  "teachingPoints": [
    "The seed of the woman points ultimately to Christ.\nGenesis 3:15 begins the great conflict between Christ and the serpent.",
    "Those who belong to Christ become part of His people.\nGalatians says that if we are Christ's, we are Abraham's seed.",
    "The remnant is connected to the woman of Revelation 12.\nThis points to God's faithful people through history.",
    "The remnant keeps the commandments of God.\nTheir loyalty is visible in obedience.",
    "The remnant has the testimony of Jesus.\nTheir witness is centered on Christ and grounded in His Word."
  ],
  "studyInsight": {
    "placement": "after_teaching",
    "title": "Study Insight",
    "body": "The PDF source emphasizes that the final remnant must be understood spiritually and worldwide.\n\nBabylon is symbolic and worldwide.\n\nHer waters are peoples, multitudes, nations, and tongues.\n\nSo the people who stand against Babylon are also worldwide.\n\nThey are not gathered around one earthly territory.\n\nThey are gathered to Christ.\n\nThe issue is not ethnicity,\n\nlocation,\n\nor national identity.\n\nThe issue is union with Jesus.\n\nA true Israelite is not merely someone in a land,\n\nbut someone who belongs to the Lord.\n\nChrist is the Seed.\n\nThose who are Christ's are counted as His seed.\n\nThe remnant is the final visible expression of that loyalty."
  },
  "reflectPrompt": "What defines your spiritual identity?\n\nHeritage?\n\nReligious label?\n\nFamily background?\n\nOr belonging to Christ?\n\nIf someone looked at your life,\n\nwould they see loyalty to Jesus?\n\nWould they see obedience to God's commandments?\n\nWould they hear the testimony of Jesus?",
  "lifeApplication": "This week, think carefully about what it means to belong to Christ.\n\nDo not define faith only by what you oppose.\n\nDefine it by who you follow.\n\nAsk:\n\nAm I Christ's?\n\nDoes my life show it?\n\nDoes my obedience come from loyalty to Him?\n\nDoes my witness point people to Jesus?\n\nChoose one way to make your loyalty more visible this week.\n\nQuietly.\n\nFaithfully.\n\nWithout pride.",
  "prayer": "Lord,\n\nI want to belong fully to Christ.\n\nNot only in name,\n\nbut in life.\n\nMake me faithful when pressure rises.\n\nWrite Your commandments in my heart.\n\nGive me the testimony of Jesus.\n\nKeep me from pride,\n\nconfusion,\n\nand compromise.\n\nLet my life show that I am Yours.\n\nAmen."
}
$json_ta_l21$::jsonb
where slug = 'the-remnant-of-her-seed';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $json_ta_l22$
{
  "openingThought": "Revelation asks a question.\n\nWho shall be able to stand?\n\nNot who can talk about prophecy.\n\nNot who can explain every symbol.\n\nNot who can win an argument.\n\nWho can stand?\n\nThat is the question behind the 144,000.\n\nThey are shown in connection with crisis,\n\nsealing,\n\nworship,\n\nand victory.\n\nThey stand when the world is divided.\n\nThey stand when false worship is enforced.\n\nThey stand because they belong fully to God.\n\nThe 144,000 are not introduced to feed speculation.\n\nThey are shown to reveal the kind of people God will have at the end.\n\nSealed.\n\nFaithful.\n\nVictorious.\n\nStanding with the Lamb.",
  "scriptureRef": "Revelation 7:3",
  "scriptureQuote": "\"Hurt not the earth,\n\nneither the sea,\n\nnor the trees,\n\ntill we have sealed the servants of our God in their foreheads.\"",
  "scriptureSecondaryRef": "Revelation 14:1",
  "scriptureSecondaryQuote": "\"And I looked,\n\nand, lo,\n\na Lamb stood on the mount Sion,\n\nand with him an hundred forty and four thousand,\n\nhaving his Father's name written in their foreheads.\"",
  "scriptureAfterSecondary": "Revelation 14:4 to 5\n\n\"These are they which follow the Lamb whithersoever he goeth.\n\nThese were redeemed from among men,\n\nbeing the firstfruits unto God and to the Lamb.\n\nAnd in their mouth was found no guile:\n\nfor they are without fault before the throne of God.\"\n\nRevelation 15:2 to 3\n\n\"And I saw as it were a sea of glass mingled with fire:\n\nand them that had gotten the victory over the beast,\n\nand over his image,\n\nand over his mark,\n\nand over the number of his name,\n\nstand on the sea of glass,\n\nhaving the harps of God.\"\n\nThe 144,000 are connected with sealing,\n\ncharacter,\n\nvictory,\n\nand worship.\n\nThey stand because they follow the Lamb.",
  "teachingPoints": [
    "The 144,000 are sealed for protection.\nRevelation 7 shows them sealed before the winds of final strife are released.",
    "They stand in contrast to those who receive the mark of the beast.\nOne group has the Father's name in the forehead. The other receives the beast's mark.",
    "Their character reflects loyalty to Christ.\nThey follow the Lamb wherever He goes.",
    "They overcome the beast, his image, his mark, and the number of his name.\nTheir victory is not political. It is spiritual.",
    "They accepted the three angels' messages.\nTheir song reflects fear of God, glory to His name, and worship of the Creator."
  ],
  "studyInsight": {
    "placement": "after_teaching",
    "title": "Study Insight",
    "body": "Revelation places the 144,000 in key moments.\n\nBefore the final winds are released,\n\nthey are sealed.\n\nAfter the final crisis,\n\nthey stand victorious.\n\nThat order matters.\n\nGod prepares His people before the crisis fully breaks.\n\nThe seal is placed in the forehead,\n\nthe place of conviction,\n\nunderstanding,\n\nand settled loyalty.\n\nThis is not surface religion.\n\nIt is truth settled deeply in the mind and heart.\n\nThe 144,000 are not strong because they are impressive.\n\nThey are strong because they are surrendered.\n\nThey follow the Lamb.\n\nThey do not follow the crowd.\n\nThey do not follow the beast.\n\nThey do not follow convenience.\n\nThey follow the Lamb."
  },
  "reflectPrompt": "When pressure rises,\n\nwhat usually guides your decisions?\n\nConviction?\n\nFear?\n\nConvenience?\n\nApproval?\n\nTruth?\n\nIs your faith being settled deeply,\n\nor only carried loosely?\n\nWhat would it mean for God's name,\n\nHis character,\n\nHis authority,\n\nand His truth\n\nto be written in your mind?",
  "lifeApplication": "This week, practice settled loyalty.\n\nChoose one truth God has already made clear to you.\n\nDo not delay it.\n\nDo not treat it casually.\n\nLive it.\n\nEven if it is small.\n\nEven if no one notices.\n\nAsk:\n\nAm I following the Lamb here?\n\nFaithfulness in the final crisis is formed through faithfulness now.",
  "prayer": "Lord,\n\nSeal my heart and mind with Your truth.\n\nTeach me to follow the Lamb wherever He leads.\n\nMake my loyalty deeper than emotion,\n\nstronger than pressure,\n\nand clearer than convenience.\n\nPrepare me to stand,\n\nnot in my own strength,\n\nbut in Christ.\n\nWrite Your character in me.\n\nAmen."
}
$json_ta_l22$::jsonb
where slug = 'the-144000';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $json_ta_l23$
{
  "openingThought": "God has never left His people without guidance.\n\nBefore great moments in sacred history,\n\nHe sent light.\n\nBefore the flood,\n\nNoah warned the world.\n\nBefore the Exodus,\n\nMoses was raised up.\n\nBefore the first coming of Christ,\n\nJohn prepared the way.\n\nBefore the final crisis,\n\nRevelation says God's final people have the testimony of Jesus.\n\nThat phrase matters.\n\nIt is not a decorative detail.\n\nIt identifies a people who are guided by Christ's witness,\n\ngrounded in Scripture,\n\nand prepared for His return.\n\nThe testimony of Jesus does not replace the Bible.\n\nIt points back to the Bible,\n\nexalts Christ,\n\nand calls people to be ready.",
  "scriptureRef": "Revelation 12:17",
  "scriptureQuote": "\"And the dragon was wroth with the woman,\n\nand went to make war with the remnant of her seed,\n\nwhich keep the commandments of God,\n\nand have the testimony of Jesus Christ.\"",
  "scriptureSecondaryRef": "Revelation 19:10",
  "scriptureSecondaryQuote": "\"For the testimony of Jesus is the spirit of prophecy.\"",
  "scriptureAfterSecondary": "Revelation 22:8 to 9\n\n\"And I John saw these things,\n\nand heard them.\n\nAnd when I had heard and seen,\n\nI fell down to worship before the feet of the angel which shewed me these things.\n\nThen saith he unto me,\n\nSee thou do it not:\n\nfor I am thy fellowservant,\n\nand of thy brethren the prophets.\"\n\n1 Thessalonians 5:20 to 21\n\n\"Despise not prophesyings.\n\nProve all things;\n\nhold fast that which is good.\"\n\nThe testimony of Jesus is connected with the spirit of prophecy.\n\nBut Scripture also commands us to test everything.\n\nTruth is never afraid of examination.",
  "teachingPoints": [
    "The remnant has the testimony of Jesus.\nRevelation connects God's final people with Christ's prophetic witness.",
    "The testimony of Jesus is the spirit of prophecy.\nThis points to God's work of guiding His people through prophetic testimony.",
    "Prophetic guidance must never replace Scripture.\nThe Bible remains the supreme test of truth.",
    "God raises up messengers to prepare people for decisive moments.\nJohn the Baptist prepared people for Christ's first coming.",
    "True prophetic witness exalts Christ.\nIt does not draw attention to itself, but points people to the Word, repentance, obedience, and readiness."
  ],
  "studyInsight": {
    "placement": "after_teaching",
    "title": "Study Insight",
    "body": "John the Baptist helps us understand the pattern.\n\nHe was not the Christ.\n\nHe was not the center.\n\nHe was a voice preparing the way.\n\nHis work was to turn hearts back to God,\n\ncall people to repentance,\n\nand point them to the Lamb.\n\nThat is the purpose of true prophetic witness.\n\nIt prepares.\n\nIt restores.\n\nIt warns.\n\nIt points to Jesus.\n\nRevelation says the final remnant has the testimony of Jesus.\n\nThat means God's last-day people are not left without guidance.\n\nBut every claim to spiritual guidance must be tested.\n\nBy Scripture.\n\nBy Christ.\n\nBy its fruit.\n\nBy whether it leads people into truth,\n\nobedience,\n\nhumility,\n\nand readiness for the Lord."
  },
  "reflectPrompt": "How do you respond when God sends correction?\n\nDo you test it by Scripture,\n\nor reject it because it challenges you?\n\nAre you willing to receive guidance\n\nif it leads you closer to Christ?\n\nDo you want truth only when it comforts you,\n\nor also when it prepares you?",
  "lifeApplication": "This week, practice spiritual testing.\n\nDo not believe every claim.\n\nDo not reject every claim.\n\nTest carefully.\n\nAsk:\n\nDoes this agree with Scripture?\n\nDoes this exalt Christ?\n\nDoes this call me to obedience?\n\nDoes this produce humility?\n\nDoes this prepare me for His coming?\n\nHold fast what is good.\n\nLet go of what is false.",
  "prayer": "Lord,\n\nTeach me to recognize Your voice.\n\nKeep me from deception.\n\nKeep me from pride.\n\nGive me a heart that tests all things by Scripture.\n\nHelp me receive what leads me closer to Christ.\n\nPrepare me for Your coming,\n\nand make my life faithful to the testimony of Jesus.\n\nAmen."
}
$json_ta_l23$::jsonb
where slug = 'the-testimony-of-jesus';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $json_ta_l24$
{
  "openingThought": "Revelation 14 does not end with endless delay.\n\nIt ends with harvest.\n\nMessages are given.\n\nChoices are made.\n\nCharacters are ripened.\n\nThen Christ comes.\n\nThe three angels' messages are not merely information to study.\n\nThey are messages that separate the world.\n\nOne group worships God and receives His seal.\n\nAnother worships the beast and receives his mark.\n\nOne group is pictured as harvest.\n\nThe other as grapes for the winepress.\n\nThis is not because God is careless.\n\nIt is because truth brings decision.\n\nAnd decision forms character.\n\nBy the time the harvest comes,\n\npeople have shown where their loyalty rests.",
  "scriptureRef": "Revelation 14:14 to 16",
  "scriptureQuote": "\"And I looked,\n\nand behold a white cloud,\n\nand upon the cloud one sat like unto the Son of man,\n\nhaving on his head a golden crown,\n\nand in his hand a sharp sickle.\n\nAnd another angel came out of the temple,\n\ncrying with a loud voice to him that sat on the cloud,\n\nThrust in thy sickle,\n\nand reap:\n\nfor the time is come for thee to reap;\n\nfor the harvest of the earth is ripe.\"",
  "scriptureSecondaryRef": "Revelation 14:18 to 19",
  "scriptureSecondaryQuote": "\"And another angel came out from the altar,\n\nwhich had power over fire;\n\nand cried with a loud cry to him that had the sharp sickle,\n\nsaying,\n\nThrust in thy sharp sickle,\n\nand gather the clusters of the vine of the earth;\n\nfor her grapes are fully ripe.\"",
  "scriptureAfterSecondary": "Matthew 13:30\n\n\"Let both grow together until the harvest:\n\nand in the time of harvest I will say to the reapers,\n\nGather ye together first the tares,\n\nand bind them in bundles to burn them:\n\nbut gather the wheat into my barn.\"\n\nThe harvest comes when character is ripe.\n\nThe question is not only what we believe,\n\nbut what we are becoming.",
  "teachingPoints": [
    "Revelation 14 moves from message to harvest.\nThe three angels' messages prepare the world for final decision.",
    "The world is divided into two groups.\nOne group receives God's seal. The other receives the mark of the beast.",
    "The righteous are pictured as harvest.\nThey are gathered to Christ.",
    "The wicked are pictured as grapes.\nThey are gathered to the winepress of God's wrath.",
    "Character ripens over time.\nRepeated choices either soften the heart toward God or harden it against Him."
  ],
  "studyInsight": {
    "placement": "after_teaching",
    "title": "Study Insight",
    "body": "The PDF source connects the final harvest with the latter rain.\n\nIn ancient Israel,\n\nthe latter rain helped ripen the grain before harvest.\n\nIn prophecy,\n\nthe Holy Spirit prepares God's people for the final harvest.\n\nThis is not emotional excitement only.\n\nIt is spiritual ripening.\n\nThe three angels' messages go to the world.\n\nThe Spirit gives power.\n\nTruth is made clear.\n\nPeople respond.\n\nSome are softened.\n\nOthers are hardened.\n\nLike Pharaoh before Moses,\n\nthe same message can lead to different outcomes depending on the heart.\n\nThe final harvest reveals what repeated choices have formed."
  },
  "reflectPrompt": "What direction is your character moving?\n\nToward surrender,\n\nor resistance?\n\nToward obedience,\n\nor delay?\n\nToward Christ,\n\nor self?\n\nAre your daily choices ripening you for the harvest of Christ,\n\nor for something else?",
  "lifeApplication": "This week, pay attention to repeated choices.\n\nNot just big decisions.\n\nSmall ones.\n\nWhat you watch.\n\nWhat you excuse.\n\nWhat you obey.\n\nWhat you ignore.\n\nWhat you return to.\n\nAsk:\n\nWhat is this forming in me?\n\nThen choose one pattern to surrender to Christ.\n\nCharacter is not formed in one moment.\n\nIt is formed by repeated response to truth.",
  "prayer": "Lord,\n\nPrepare me for Your harvest.\n\nLet Your Spirit ripen what is good in me.\n\nSoften what has become hard.\n\nRemove what resists You.\n\nTeach me to respond to truth now,\n\nnot later.\n\nMake me faithful,\n\nsettled,\n\nand ready for Your coming.\n\nAmen."
}
$json_ta_l24$::jsonb
where slug = 'the-harvest-of-the-earth-is-ripe';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $json_ta_l25$
{
  "openingThought": "The final call is not only a warning.\n\nIt is an invitation.\n\nCome out of her, my people.\n\nThose words reveal something important about God.\n\nHe still has people in places of confusion.\n\nPeople who love Him.\n\nPeople who have not yet seen everything clearly.\n\nPeople who belong to Him,\n\nbut must now respond to His voice.\n\nGod does not call them out because He hates them.\n\nHe calls them out because they are His.\n\nBabylon falls because she rejects truth.\n\nBut God's people are called before the plagues fall.\n\nMercy speaks before judgment comes.\n\nThat is the heart of the final message.",
  "scriptureRef": "Revelation 18:1 to 4",
  "scriptureQuote": "\"And after these things I saw another angel come down from heaven,\n\nhaving great power;\n\nand the earth was lightened with his glory.\n\nAnd he cried mightily with a strong voice,\n\nsaying,\n\nBabylon the great is fallen,\n\nis fallen.\n\nAnd I heard another voice from heaven,\n\nsaying,\n\nCome out of her,\n\nmy people,\n\nthat ye be not partakers of her sins,\n\nand that ye receive not of her plagues.\"",
  "scriptureSecondaryRef": "John 10:27",
  "scriptureSecondaryQuote": "\"My sheep hear my voice,\n\nand I know them,\n\nand they follow me.\"",
  "scriptureAfterSecondary": "Revelation 14:12\n\n\"Here is the patience of the saints:\n\nhere are they that keep the commandments of God,\n\nand the faith of Jesus.\"\n\nThe final call is personal.\n\nGod speaks to His people.\n\nThe question is whether they will hear and follow.",
  "teachingPoints": [
    "God still has people in Babylon.\nThe call says \"my people,\" which shows His mercy and patience.",
    "Coming out means leaving confusion and false worship.\nIt is not merely physical movement. It is spiritual separation.",
    "Babylon falls because she rejects the first angel's message.\nShe rejects the everlasting gospel, true worship, judgment, and obedience.",
    "The final call comes before the plagues.\nGod warns before judgment falls.",
    "The faithful are identified by obedience and faith in Jesus.\nThey keep God's commandments and hold to the faith of Jesus."
  ],
  "studyInsight": {
    "placement": "after_teaching",
    "title": "Study Insight",
    "body": "The PDF source reviews the whole message here.\n\nThe everlasting gospel.\n\nFear God.\n\nGive Him glory.\n\nThe hour of judgment.\n\nWorship the Creator.\n\nBabylon's fall.\n\nThe warning against the beast,\n\nhis image,\n\nhis mark,\n\nand his number.\n\nThe patience of the saints.\n\nThe commandments of God.\n\nThe faith of Jesus.\n\nThe testimony of Jesus.\n\nAll of it leads to a call.\n\nCome out.\n\nThis is not only about leaving error.\n\nIt is about coming fully to Christ.\n\nThe final message does not end with information.\n\nIt ends with decision.\n\nWhere will you stand?\n\nWhose voice will you follow?\n\nWhat will you leave behind because Jesus is calling?"
  },
  "reflectPrompt": "Where might God be calling you out of confusion?\n\nA belief?\n\nA habit?\n\nA system?\n\nA compromise?\n\nA fear?\n\nA delay?\n\nDo you hear Christ's voice clearly,\n\nor have other voices become louder?\n\nWhat would obedience look like now?\n\nNot someday.\n\nNow.",
  "lifeApplication": "This week, make the call personal.\n\nDo not only study Babylon as a symbol.\n\nAsk what God is calling you to leave.\n\nThen take one step.\n\nA clear step.\n\nA costly step if needed.\n\nA faithful step.\n\nIt may be a belief you must test.\n\nA practice you must surrender.\n\nA habit you must break.\n\nA truth you must obey.\n\nDo not wait until the call feels easy.\n\nFollow the voice of Christ.",
  "prayer": "Lord,\n\nHelp me hear Your voice.\n\nCall me out of confusion.\n\nCall me out of compromise.\n\nCall me out of fear.\n\nLead me fully to Christ.\n\nGive me courage to obey what You make clear.\n\nKeep me among those who have the patience of the saints,\n\nwho keep Your commandments,\n\nand hold to the faith of Jesus.\n\nAmen.",
  "seriesCompleteScreen": {
    "title": "Study complete",
    "message": "You have completed The Three Angels' Messages.",
    "primaryCta": {
      "label": "Review study",
      "href": "/study-library/three-angels-messages"
    },
    "secondaryCta": {
      "label": "Explore more prophecy studies",
      "href": "/paths/prophecy"
    }
  }
}
$json_ta_l25$::jsonb
where slug = 'come-out-of-her-my-people';

-- The Rest God Gives — Lesson 1: A Gift in Time (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000901';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $trgg_l1_reader$
{"openingThought":"Before God gave humanity work to manage, He gave humanity rest to receive.\n\nThat matters.\n\nThe Sabbath did not begin as a burden.\n\nIt began as a gift.\n\nAt the end of creation week, God rested, blessed the seventh day, and made it holy. Not because He was tired, but because His work was complete.\n\nHumanity's first full day was not a workday.\n\nIt was a rest day.\n\nThat means life with God begins with receiving, not achieving.\n\nThe Sabbath is a sacred reminder that we were made to live from God's completed work, not from anxious striving.","scriptureRef":"Genesis 2:2-3","scriptureQuote":"And on the seventh day God ended his work which he had made; and he rested on the seventh day from all his work which he had made.\n\nAnd God blessed the seventh day, and sanctified it: because that in it he had rested from all his work which God created and made.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"At creation, God set apart the seventh day as blessed and holy time.\n\nThis was not an afterthought.\n\nIt was part of His design for human life.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Mark 2:27-28","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"And he said unto them, The sabbath was made for man, and not man for the sabbath: Therefore the Son of man is Lord also of the sabbath.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Jesus restores the Sabbath to its original purpose.\n\nIt was made for human blessing, not human burden.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Exodus 20:8-11","quote":"Remember the sabbath day, to keep it holy. Six days shalt thou labour, and do all thy work: But the seventh day is the sabbath of the LORD thy God: in it thou shalt not do any work, thou, nor thy son, nor thy daughter, thy manservant, nor thy maidservant, nor thy cattle, nor thy stranger that is within thy gates: For in six days the LORD made heaven and earth, the sea, and all that in them is, and rested the seventh day: wherefore the LORD blessed the sabbath day, and hallowed it."}],"teachingPoints":["1. The Sabbath began at creation\n\nThe Sabbath was not first introduced at Sinai.\n\nIt appears in Genesis, before Israel existed as a nation.\n\nGod rested on the seventh day, blessed it, and sanctified it.\n\nThat means the Sabbath is rooted in creation itself.\n\nIt is part of God's original design for humanity.","2. God rested because His work was complete\n\nGod did not rest because He was exhausted.\n\nHe rested because creation was finished, good, ordered, and complete.\n\nThe seventh day was filled with God's satisfaction and delight.\n\nSabbath rest is not laziness.\n\nIt is celebration of God's completed work.","3. The Sabbath was made for humanity\n\nJesus said, \"The sabbath was made for man.\"\n\nThat means the Sabbath was created as a gift for human beings.\n\nIt was not designed to crush people with religious pressure.\n\nIt was designed to bless people with time, worship, rest, and relationship with God.","4. The Sabbath points us back to dependence\n\nExodus connects the Sabbath with God as Creator.\n\nWe rest because God made us.\n\nWe rest because our lives are not self-created.\n\nWe rest because everything begins with God's power, provision, and grace.\n\nThe Sabbath reminds us that we are creatures, not machines."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Sanctified","lemma":"קָדַשׁ","transliteration":"qadash","meaning":"Set apart, made holy, or marked for a special purpose.","strongs":"H6942","reference":"Genesis 2:3","note":"God sanctified the seventh day.\n\nThat means He set it apart as sacred time.\n\nThe Sabbath is not ordinary time with a religious label attached. It is time God Himself blessed and marked for relationship, worship, rest, and remembrance."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"scripture_connection","body":"Genesis shows a powerful pattern.\n\nGod forms the world.\nGod fills the world.\nThen God rests.\n\nHuman beings are created near the end of the sixth day, after the work of creation is already done.\n\nSo humanity wakes up to a finished world.\n\nBefore Adam and Eve work the garden, they receive life as a gift.\n\nBefore they produce anything, they rest in what God has made.\n\nThat is the spirit of the Sabbath.\n\nIt reminds us that life does not begin with our output.\n\nIt begins with God's goodness.\n\nThe Sabbath tells us every week:\n\nYou are not your productivity.\nYou are not your performance.\nYou are not the source of your own life.\n\nGod is Creator.\n\nYou are invited to rest in Him."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"creationRestPattern","title":"Creation Moves Toward Rest","caption":"Creation rises toward rest. Humanity begins life by receiving God’s completed work."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"A Gift in Time","references":[{"reference":"Genesis 2:1-3"},{"reference":"Exodus 20:8-11"},{"reference":"Mark 2:27-28"},{"reference":"Psalm 100:3"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: The Sabbath began at creation as a gift of sacred time, reminding humanity to rest in God's completed work."}],"reflectPrompt":"Do you usually see rest as a gift from God or as something you have to earn after doing enough?\n\nThink about your work, schedule, responsibilities, anxiety, and the pressure to prove your value through productivity.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, practice receiving rest as a gift.\n\nChoose one moment to stop producing, stop scrolling, stop striving, and remember:\n\nGod is Creator.\n\nYou are His creature.\n\nYour worth is not measured by how much you get done.\n\nIf Sabbath has felt like pressure, ask God to help you see it differently.\n\nNot as religious weight.\n\nAs sacred time.\n\nAs a gift.\n\nAs a weekly invitation to rest in the God who made you.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for creating the Sabbath as a gift in time. Thank You that life begins with Your goodness, not my performance. Teach me to rest in You without guilt, fear, or striving. Help me remember that You are my Creator, my Provider, and the source of my life. Make Sabbath rest a doorway into deeper trust and relationship with You. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"The Sabbath began at creation as a gift in time. God blessed the seventh day, made it holy, and invited humanity to rest in His completed work.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"trgg-l1-q1","prompt":"Where does the Sabbath first appear in Scripture?","options":["At creation in Genesis","During the reign of David","In Babylon","Only in the New Testament"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Sabbath first appears in Genesis 2, where God rests, blesses the seventh day, and sanctifies it."},{"id":"trgg-l1-q2","prompt":"Why did God rest on the seventh day?","options":["His work of creation was complete","He was physically exhausted","Humanity invented the Sabbath later","Creation had failed"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"God rested because His work was finished and complete, not because He was tired."},{"id":"trgg-l1-q3","prompt":"According to Mark 2:27, who was the Sabbath made for?","options":["Humanity","Angels only","Kings only","No one"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus said the Sabbath was made for man, showing that it was given as a gift for human beings."},{"id":"trgg-l1-q4","prompt":"What does it mean that God sanctified the seventh day?","options":["He set it apart as holy","He made it ordinary","He removed its meaning","He made it a burden"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"To sanctify means to set apart as holy or special. God marked the seventh day as sacred time."},{"id":"trgg-l1-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What would change in your life if you saw Sabbath as a gift instead of a burden?","explanation":"The Sabbath reminds us that life begins with God's completed work and that rest is received before it is earned."}]}}
$trgg_l1_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'a-gift-in-time'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007b';


-- The Rest God Gives — Lesson 2: Rest Before Work (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000902';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $trgg_l2_reader$
{"openingThought":"Most of us think life works like this:\n\nWork first.\nProve yourself first.\nEarn your place first.\nThen maybe you can rest.\n\nBut creation tells a different story.\n\nHuman beings were created near the end of the sixth day. By the time Adam and Eve opened their eyes, God's work of creation was already complete.\n\nThey did not begin life by producing.\n\nThey began life by receiving.\n\nTheir first full day was the Sabbath.\n\nBefore they worked in the garden, they rested in God's finished work.\n\nThat is not a small detail.\n\nIt shows us that life with God begins with grace.","scriptureRef":"Genesis 1:31","scriptureQuote":"And God saw every thing that he had made, and, behold, it was very good.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Creation was complete before humanity's first breath.\n\nGod looked at everything He had made and declared it very good.\n\nHuman life began inside that finished goodness.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Genesis 2:1-3","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"Thus the heavens and the earth were finished, and all the host of them.\n\nAnd on the seventh day God ended his work which he had made; and he rested on the seventh day from all his work which he had made.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"The world was finished before the Sabbath was blessed.\n\nGod rested because His work was complete, not because creation was incomplete.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Psalm 100:3","quote":"Know ye that the LORD he is God: it is he that hath made us, and not we ourselves; we are his people, and the sheep of his pasture."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Acts 17:25","quote":"Neither is worshipped with men's hands, as though he needed any thing, seeing he giveth to all life, and breath, and all things."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Humanity entered a finished creation\n\nGenesis says God saw everything He made, and it was very good.\n\nHuman beings did not wake up into a half-built world.\n\nThey woke up into a world already formed, filled, blessed, and ready for life.\n\nBefore they contributed anything, they received everything.\n\nLife began as gift.","2. Rest came before human labor\n\nAdam and Eve were created on the sixth day, and the next full day was the seventh day.\n\nThat means their first full day of life was not a workday.\n\nIt was a rest day.\n\nThis teaches a deep spiritual principle:\n\nWe do not work to create our worth.\n\nWe work from the life God has already given.","3. We are created beings, not self-made beings\n\nPsalm 100 says God made us, and not we ourselves.\n\nThat confronts the lie of self-sufficiency.\n\nWe are dependent creatures.\n\nEvery breath, ability, opportunity, and moment of life comes from God.\n\nSabbath rest reminds us that we are receivers before we are workers.","4. Work is good, but it is not our identity\n\nGod gave humanity meaningful work.\n\nBut He gave rest first.\n\nWork has dignity, but work is not our source of worth.\n\nProductivity can become an idol when we use it to prove we matter.\n\nThe Sabbath breaks that cycle by reminding us that our value begins with God's creation and care."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Finished","lemma":"כָּלָה","transliteration":"kalah","meaning":"Completed, brought to its intended fullness, lacking nothing necessary.","strongs":"H3615","reference":"Genesis 2:1","note":"Genesis says the heavens and the earth were finished.\n\nHumanity entered life after God's work was complete.\n\nThe Sabbath reminds us that we begin by receiving God's finished work before we offer Him our work."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"scripture_connection","body":"The order of creation is teaching us something.\n\nGod works.\nGod finishes.\nGod rests.\nHumanity receives.\nThen humanity lives and works from what God has already provided.\n\nThat pattern matters because sin flips the order.\n\nSin says:\n\nWork until you are worthy.\nPerform until you are accepted.\nProduce until you matter.\nStrive until you feel safe.\n\nBut Sabbath says:\n\nYou are created by God.\nYou are sustained by God.\nYou are loved before you produce.\nYou are invited to rest before you work.\n\nThis is not laziness.\n\nThis is alignment.\n\nThe healthiest work flows from rest in God, not anxiety to prove yourself."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"creationRestPattern","title":"Rest Before Work","highlightedIndices":[5,6],"caption":"Humanity's first full day was rest, not production."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Receiving Before Producing","references":[{"reference":"Genesis 1:31"},{"reference":"Genesis 2:1-3"},{"reference":"Psalm 100:3"},{"reference":"Acts 17:25"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Human beings begin as receivers of God's completed work. Sabbath rest reminds us that our worth is rooted in God, not productivity."}],"reflectPrompt":"Where do you feel the pressure to prove your worth through work, achievement, or productivity?\n\nThink about your job, goals, ministry, school, fitness, money, social media, or how you feel when you are not producing.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, practice receiving before producing.\n\nBefore you start one workday, pause for two minutes and say:\n\n\"God made me. God sustains me. My worth is not my output.\"\n\nThen begin your work from rest, not panic.\n\nIf you feel anxious, rushed, or behind, come back to the same truth:\n\nI am a creature before I am a worker.\n\nI receive before I produce.\n\nGod's finished work comes before mine.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You that I do not have to create my own worth. Thank You for making me, sustaining me, and giving me life as a gift. Teach me to work faithfully without turning productivity into my identity. Help me receive rest before work and live from trust instead of striving. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"Humanity's first full day was rest, not work. Sabbath reminds us that life begins by receiving God's completed work before producing our own.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"trgg-l2-q1","prompt":"When did humanity enter the creation story?","options":["After God had already formed and filled the world","Before God created light","Before God made the earth","After the Sabbath was removed"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Humanity was created on the sixth day, after God had already formed and filled the world."},{"id":"trgg-l2-q2","prompt":"What was humanity's first full day?","options":["The Sabbath day of rest","A day of anxious labor","A day of earning existence","A day of creating the world"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Adam and Eve were created on the sixth day, and their first full day was the seventh day, the Sabbath."},{"id":"trgg-l2-q3","prompt":"According to Psalm 100:3, what should we know?","options":["The Lord made us, and not we ourselves","We created ourselves","Our worth comes from productivity only","God does not care about creation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Psalm 100:3 says God made us, and not we ourselves. We are created beings dependent on Him."},{"id":"trgg-l2-q4","prompt":"What does this lesson teach about work?","options":["Work is good, but it is not our identity","Work is always sinful","Rest has no value","Productivity creates our worth"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Work has dignity, but Sabbath reminds us that our worth comes from God, not our productivity."},{"id":"trgg-l2-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What would change if you began your week from rest in God instead of pressure to prove yourself?","explanation":"Sabbath rest teaches that we receive life from God before we produce work for God or others."}]}}
$trgg_l2_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'rest-before-work'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007b';


-- The Rest God Gives — Lesson 3: Remember the Creator (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000903';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $trgg_l3_reader$
{"openingThought":"The Sabbath begins with one word:\n\nRemember.\n\nThat word matters because human beings forget quickly.\n\nWe forget where life comes from.\nWe forget who made us.\nWe forget that we are dependent.\nWe forget that the world is not held together by our effort.\n\nThe Sabbath calls us back to reality.\n\nGod is Creator.\n\nWe are created.\n\nHe is the source.\n\nWe are receivers.\n\nEvery seventh day, God gives humanity a sacred reminder that life did not begin with our work. It began with His.","scriptureRef":"Exodus 20:8-11","scriptureQuote":"Remember the sabbath day, to keep it holy.\n\nFor in six days the LORD made heaven and earth, the sea, and all that in them is, and rested the seventh day: wherefore the LORD blessed the sabbath day, and hallowed it.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"The Sabbath command begins with memory.\n\nGod ties sacred rest to creation itself.\n\nHe made all things in six days and rested the seventh.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Psalm 95:6-7","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"O come, let us worship and bow down: let us kneel before the LORD our maker.\n\nFor he is our God; and we are the people of his pasture, and the sheep of his hand.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Worship flows from remembering who made us.\n\nWe belong to the God who formed us and cares for us.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Revelation 14:6-7","quote":"Saying with a loud voice, Fear God, and give glory to him; for the hour of his judgment is come: and worship him that made heaven, and earth, and the sea, and the fountains of waters."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Genesis 2:3","quote":"And God blessed the seventh day, and sanctified it: because that in it he had rested from all his work which God created and made."}],"teachingPoints":["1. The Sabbath calls us to remember\n\nGod says, \"Remember the sabbath day.\"\n\nThe command begins with memory because forgetfulness is spiritually dangerous.\n\nWhen we forget God as Creator, we start living as if we are self-made, self-sustained, and self-owned.\n\nSabbath restores our memory.\n\nIt brings us back to the God who made us.","2. Sabbath worship is rooted in creation\n\nExodus gives creation as the reason for Sabbath rest.\n\nGod made heaven, earth, the sea, and everything in them.\n\nThen He rested the seventh day.\n\nThe Sabbath is not an empty ritual.\n\nIt is a weekly act of worship that acknowledges God as Creator.","3. Creation gives us identity\n\nPsalm 95 says, \"He is our God\" and \"we are the people of his pasture.\"\n\nBecause God made us, our identity begins with Him.\n\nWe are not accidents.\n\nWe are not machines.\n\nWe are not merely consumers, workers, or performers.\n\nWe are creatures made for relationship with our Maker.","4. The final call to worship points back to the Creator\n\nRevelation calls the world to worship the One who made heaven, earth, sea, and fountains of waters.\n\nThat language echoes the Sabbath command.\n\nAt the end of history, God calls people back to true worship.\n\nThe Sabbath helps us remember who deserves our worship and why."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Remember","lemma":"זָכַר","transliteration":"zakar","meaning":"To call back to mind, keep present, and act in light of what is true.","strongs":"H2142","reference":"Exodus 20:8","note":"In Scripture, remembering is not just mental recall.\n\nTo remember the Sabbath is to live in light of the truth that God is Creator.\n\nIt means setting apart sacred time to worship, rest, and return to dependence on Him."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"scripture_connection","body":"The Sabbath protects us from spiritual amnesia.\n\nWithout regular remembrance, we start believing lies.\n\n\"I made myself.\"\n\n\"I sustain myself.\"\n\n\"My worth comes from my output.\"\n\n\"My life belongs to me.\"\n\n\"My time is mine alone.\"\n\nBut Sabbath tells the truth:\n\nGod made me.\nGod sustains me.\nGod gives me life.\nGod owns time.\nGod is worthy of worship.\n\nThis is why the Sabbath is more than rest from activity.\n\nIt is a return to reality.\n\nIt teaches the soul to bow before the Creator and receive life as a gift."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"creationRestPattern","title":"Remember the Creator","highlightedIndices":[6],"caption":"The Sabbath reminds us that God is Creator and we live by His gift."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Worship the Creator","references":[{"reference":"Genesis 2:1-3"},{"reference":"Exodus 20:8-11"},{"reference":"Psalm 95:6-7"},{"reference":"Revelation 14:6-7"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: The Sabbath calls us to remember, worship, and rest in the God who created all things."}],"reflectPrompt":"Where are you most tempted to live as if everything depends on you?\n\nThink about control, work, money, planning, ministry, family, goals, or the pressure to hold your life together by yourself.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, use the Sabbath command as a reset.\n\nPause and say:\n\n\"God is Creator. I am created.\"\n\nThen name one area where you have been acting like the source instead of trusting the Source.\n\nMaybe you have been carrying work like your worth depends on it.\n\nMaybe you have been managing your future like God is absent.\n\nMaybe you have treated your time as if it all belongs to you.\n\nLet Sabbath remembrance call you back.\n\nYou are not the Creator.\n\nThat is good news.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for being my Creator. Thank You that I do not have to live as if everything depends on me. Help me remember the Sabbath as sacred time with You. Teach me to worship, rest, and live from dependence instead of self-sufficiency. Bring my heart back to You, the Maker and Sustainer of my life. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"The Sabbath calls us to remember the Creator. It brings us back to worship, dependence, and the truth that life begins with God's completed work.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"trgg-l3-q1","prompt":"What is the first word of the Sabbath commandment in Exodus 20:8?","options":["Remember","Forget","Ignore","Replace"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Sabbath commandment begins with \"Remember,\" calling God's people to keep sacred time in view."},{"id":"trgg-l3-q2","prompt":"According to Exodus 20:11, why is the Sabbath connected to creation?","options":["Because God made heaven and earth in six days and rested the seventh day","Because humans invented rest later","Because creation was unfinished","Because God needed help creating"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Exodus grounds the Sabbath in God's six-day work of creation and His rest on the seventh day."},{"id":"trgg-l3-q3","prompt":"According to Psalm 95:6-7, why should we worship and bow down?","options":["The Lord is our Maker","We created ourselves","Worship is unnecessary","God is distant and unknown"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Psalm 95 calls us to worship because the Lord is our Maker and we are His people."},{"id":"trgg-l3-q4","prompt":"What does Revelation 14:7 call people to do?","options":["Worship Him who made heaven, earth, sea, and fountains of waters","Worship human achievement","Forget the Creator","Replace God's commandments"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Revelation calls the world to worship the Creator, using language that echoes the Sabbath commandment."},{"id":"trgg-l3-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"How can remembering God as Creator help you release anxiety or self-sufficiency?","explanation":"The Sabbath reminds us that God is the source of life. We can rest because we are created, sustained, and cared for by Him."}]}}
$trgg_l3_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'remember-the-creator'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007b';


-- The Rest God Gives — Lesson 4: Remember the Redeemer (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000904';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $trgg_l4_reader$
{"openingThought":"The Sabbath does not only point backward to creation.\n\nIt also points to redemption.\n\nGod did not only make us.\n\nHe rescues us.\n\nIn Exodus, the Sabbath command reminds us that God created the world. In Deuteronomy, the same command reminds Israel that God delivered them from slavery.\n\nThat means Sabbath rest carries two great truths:\n\nGod is Creator.\n\nGod is Redeemer.\n\nWe rest because life is His gift.\n\nWe rest because salvation is His work.\n\nThe Sabbath reminds us every week that we are not saved by anxious striving, but by the power and grace of God.","scriptureRef":"Deuteronomy 5:15","scriptureQuote":"And remember that thou wast a servant in the land of Egypt, and that the LORD thy God brought thee out thence through a mighty hand and by a stretched out arm: therefore the LORD thy God commanded thee to keep the sabbath day.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"The Sabbath is not only about creation.\n\nIt is also about deliverance.\n\nIsrael was told to remember slavery and God's mighty rescue.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Ephesians 2:8-9","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Salvation is received, not earned.\n\nGrace comes before boasting.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Ephesians 2:10","quote":"For we are his workmanship, created in Christ Jesus unto good works, which God hath before ordained that we should walk in them."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Psalm 51:10-12","quote":"Create in me a clean heart, O God; and renew a right spirit within me.\n\nRestore unto me the joy of thy salvation."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Titus 3:5","quote":"Not by works of righteousness which we have done, but according to his mercy he saved us, by the washing of regeneration, and renewing of the Holy Ghost."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Sabbath remembers deliverance\n\nIn Deuteronomy, the Sabbath is connected to Israel's deliverance from Egypt.\n\nGod tells them to remember that they were slaves and that He brought them out by His mighty hand.\n\nThey did not free themselves.\n\nGod delivered them.\n\nThe Sabbath became a weekly reminder that redemption is God's work.","2. Salvation is a gift, not a wage\n\nEphesians says we are saved by grace through faith, not by works.\n\nThat is the heartbeat of redemption.\n\nWe do not earn salvation like a paycheck.\n\nWe receive it as a gift.\n\nThe Sabbath trains the soul to stop striving and remember that God saves by grace.","3. Redemption creates new life\n\nEphesians also says we are created in Christ Jesus unto good works.\n\nThat means grace does not leave us unchanged.\n\nGod saves first.\n\nThen He creates a new life within us.\n\nGood works do not purchase salvation.\n\nThey flow from the salvation God has already given.","4. God creates clean hearts\n\nPsalm 51 uses creation language for spiritual renewal:\n\n\"Create in me a clean heart.\"\n\nThe God who created the world is the same God who recreates the heart.\n\nSabbath reminds us that both creation and redemption depend on God's power.\n\nWe cannot create ourselves.\n\nWe cannot save ourselves.\n\nWe receive both life and salvation from Him."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Redeemer","lemma":"גֹּאֵל","transliteration":"goel","meaning":"One who rescues, delivers, or buys back someone from bondage.","strongs":"H1350","reference":"Deuteronomy 5:15","note":"God is not only our Creator.\n\nHe is our Redeemer.\n\nThe Sabbath reminds us that the God who made us is also the God who saves us from bondage, restores the heart, and gives salvation as a gift of grace."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"scripture_connection","body":"The Sabbath is a protest against self-salvation.\n\nEvery week, it tells the truth:\n\nYou did not create yourself.\n\nYou cannot redeem yourself.\n\nYou cannot earn God's favor by working hard enough.\n\nThat does not mean obedience does not matter.\n\nIt means obedience belongs in the right place.\n\nGrace comes first.\n\nSalvation comes first.\n\nGod's mighty work comes first.\n\nThen good works flow from a heart made new.\n\nThe Sabbath protects us from legalism because it points to a finished work we receive, not a ladder we climb.\n\nIt reminds us that God delivers before He asks us to walk in freedom."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"Sabbath remembers both","accent":"sage","description":"Sabbath rest remembers both the gift of life and the gift of salvation.","payload":{"variant":"comparison","leftTitle":"Creation","rightTitle":"Redemption","leftItems":["God made us."],"rightItems":["God saves us."]},"footnote":"The Sabbath points backward to creation and forward to redemption every week."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Remember the Redeemer","references":[{"reference":"Deuteronomy 5:12-15"},{"reference":"Ephesians 2:8-10"},{"reference":"Psalm 51:10-12"},{"reference":"Titus 3:5"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: The Sabbath reminds us that salvation is God's work. We rest in grace before we walk in good works."}],"reflectPrompt":"Where are you tempted to treat salvation like something you must earn instead of something God gives?\n\nThink about guilt, spiritual pressure, performance, fear of not doing enough, or trying to prove you are worthy of God's love.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, let Sabbath remind you of redemption.\n\nPause and say:\n\n\"God saves by grace.\"\n\nThen ask yourself:\n\n\"Am I working from grace, or am I working to earn grace?\"\n\nThere is a difference.\n\nWorking to earn grace produces anxiety.\n\nWorking from grace produces gratitude.\n\nChoose one act of obedience this week, but do it from the right place.\n\nNot to make God love you.\n\nBecause He already has loved you in Christ.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for being my Redeemer. Thank You for saving by grace, not by my works. Teach me to rest in Your finished work instead of striving to prove myself. Create in me a clean heart and restore the joy of Your salvation. Let my obedience flow from gratitude, trust, and love. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"The Sabbath remembers the Redeemer. It reminds us that salvation is God's finished work, received by grace, and followed by a life made new.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"trgg-l4-q1","prompt":"According to Deuteronomy 5:15, what was Israel told to remember?","options":["That they were servants in Egypt and God delivered them","That they freed themselves from Egypt","That Sabbath had no meaning","That work saves the soul"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Deuteronomy connects the Sabbath with Israel's deliverance from Egypt by God's mighty hand."},{"id":"trgg-l4-q2","prompt":"According to Ephesians 2:8-9, how are we saved?","options":["By grace through faith","By works alone","By earning enough favor","By comparing ourselves to others"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Ephesians says we are saved by grace through faith, not of works. Salvation is the gift of God."},{"id":"trgg-l4-q3","prompt":"According to Psalm 51:10, what does David ask God to create?","options":["A clean heart","A better reputation only","A new nation only","A stronger human effort"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"David asks God to create a clean heart, showing that spiritual renewal depends on God's creative power."},{"id":"trgg-l4-q4","prompt":"What does the Sabbath remind us about redemption?","options":["Salvation is God's work and gift","Salvation is earned by anxiety","God only creates but never saves","Grace removes the need for Jesus"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Sabbath points to redemption by reminding us that God delivers and saves by His grace."},{"id":"trgg-l4-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"How can Sabbath rest help you move from striving for acceptance to resting in God's grace?","explanation":"Sabbath rest reminds us that God's saving work comes before our response. We obey from grace, not to earn grace."}]}}
$trgg_l4_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'remember-the-redeemer'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007b';


-- The Rest God Gives — Lesson 5: It Is Finished (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000905';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $trgg_l5_reader$
{"openingThought":"Some words change everything.\n\nAt the cross, Jesus said:\n\n\"It is finished.\"\n\nHe did not say, \"It is almost done.\"\n\nHe did not say, \"Now finish what I started.\"\n\nHe declared that the work the Father gave Him was complete.\n\nThat matters for Sabbath.\n\nThe Sabbath points us to God's completed work in creation. The cross points us to Christ's completed work in redemption.\n\nBoth teach the same deep truth:\n\nWe rest because God has finished what we could never do.","scriptureRef":"John 19:30","scriptureQuote":"When Jesus therefore had received the vinegar, he said, It is finished: and he bowed his head, and gave up the ghost.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"These were not words of defeat.\n\nThey were words of completion.\n\nThe saving work the Father gave the Son had reached its goal.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"John 17:4","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"I have glorified thee on the earth: I have finished the work which thou gavest me to do.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Jesus lived with a clear mission and completed it faithfully.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Luke 23:54-56","quote":"And that day was the preparation, and the sabbath drew on.\n\nAnd they returned, and prepared spices and ointments; and rested the sabbath day according to the commandment."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Hebrews 10:12","quote":"But this man, after he had offered one sacrifice for sins for ever, sat down on the right hand of God."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Jesus finished the work the Father gave Him\n\nIn John 17, Jesus says He finished the work the Father gave Him to do.\n\nHis life was not random.\n\nHis mission was clear.\n\nHe came to reveal the Father, live faithfully, bear sin, defeat the enemy, and open the way back to God.\n\nAt the cross, He completed that saving work.","2. \"It is finished\" means redemption is complete\n\nWhen Jesus said, \"It is finished,\" He was not announcing defeat.\n\nHe was announcing completion.\n\nThe debt had been paid.\nThe sacrifice had been offered.\nThe promise had been kept.\nThe way to God had been opened.\n\nRedemption rests on Christ's finished work, not our unfinished striving.","3. Jesus rested in the tomb on the Sabbath\n\nLuke tells us Jesus died on preparation day, and the Sabbath drew on.\n\nHis followers rested the Sabbath according to the commandment.\n\nJesus rested in the tomb over the Sabbath after finishing the work of redemption.\n\nThat connection is powerful.\n\nAt creation, God rested after finishing His work.\n\nAt redemption, Christ rested after finishing His work.","4. Christ's sacrifice is enough\n\nHebrews says Jesus offered one sacrifice for sins forever.\n\nThat means His sacrifice does not need to be repeated or improved.\n\nWe do not add to His saving work.\n\nWe receive it.\n\nThe Sabbath reminds us weekly that God's completed work is enough."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Finished","lemma":"τετέλεσται","transliteration":"tetelestai","meaning":"Completed, fulfilled, brought to its intended goal.","strongs":"G5055","reference":"John 19:30","note":"When Jesus said, \"It is finished,\" He declared that His saving work had reached its goal.\n\nThe cross was not failure.\n\nIt was fulfillment.\n\nSabbath rest helps us remember that salvation is grounded in Christ's completed work, not human effort."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The Sabbath and the cross speak the same language.\n\nCreation says:\n\nGod finished.\n\nRedemption says:\n\nChrist finished.\n\nCreation rest reminds us that life is a gift.\n\nRedemption rest reminds us that salvation is a gift.\n\nThis is why Sabbath cannot be reduced to legalism.\n\nAt its heart, Sabbath is a weekly witness against anxious self-salvation.\n\nIt tells us to stop trying to be the source.\n\nStop trying to be the Savior.\n\nStop trying to finish what only Christ could complete.\n\nJesus' words still stand:\n\n\"It is finished.\""},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"Completed work","accent":"stone","description":"Sabbath rest points to completed work: creation and redemption.","payload":{"variant":"comparison","leftTitle":"Creation","rightTitle":"Redemption","leftItems":["God finished His work","God rested the seventh day"],"rightItems":["Jesus finished His work","Jesus rested in the tomb on the Sabbath"]},"footnote":"We rest because God has finished what we could never do."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"It Is Finished","references":[{"reference":"John 17:4"},{"reference":"John 19:30"},{"reference":"Luke 23:54-56"},{"reference":"Hebrews 10:12"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: The Sabbath points to rest in God's completed work. Jesus finished the work of redemption, and His sacrifice is enough."}],"reflectPrompt":"Where are you still trying to finish what Jesus has already completed?\n\nThink about guilt, acceptance, forgiveness, salvation, spiritual pressure, or the need to prove yourself to God.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, practice resting in Christ's finished work.\n\nWhen guilt says, \"Do more so God will accept you,\" answer:\n\n\"It is finished.\"\n\nWhen shame says, \"You are still not enough,\" answer:\n\n\"Christ is enough.\"\n\nWhen anxiety says, \"You must hold everything together,\" answer:\n\n\"God is the source. Jesus is the Savior.\"\n\nLet Sabbath become more than a day off.\n\nLet it become a weekly protest against self-salvation.\n\nPrayer:\n\nJesus, thank You for finishing the work of redemption. Thank You that Your sacrifice is enough and that I do not have to save myself. Teach me to rest in what You have completed. Free me from anxious striving, guilt-driven religion, and the pressure to prove myself. Help me receive Sabbath as a reminder of Your finished work. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"Jesus finished the work of redemption. Sabbath rest points us to creation completed and salvation accomplished in Christ.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"trgg-l5-q1","prompt":"What did Jesus say on the cross in John 19:30?","options":["\"It is finished\"","\"It is almost done\"","\"Finish this for Me\"","\"Redemption failed\""],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus declared, \"It is finished,\" showing that His saving work had reached completion."},{"id":"trgg-l5-q2","prompt":"According to John 17:4, what did Jesus say He had finished?","options":["The work the Father gave Him to do","The work of creating Rome","The work of avoiding suffering","The work of ending Scripture"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus said He had finished the work the Father gave Him to do."},{"id":"trgg-l5-q3","prompt":"According to Luke 23:54-56, what did Jesus' followers do as the Sabbath came?","options":["Rested the Sabbath day according to the commandment","Rejected the Sabbath","Rewrote the commandments","Pretended Jesus had not died"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Luke says Jesus' followers rested the Sabbath day according to the commandment."},{"id":"trgg-l5-q4","prompt":"According to Hebrews 10:12, how many sacrifices for sins did Jesus offer?","options":["One sacrifice for sins forever","Many incomplete sacrifices","No sacrifice at all","A temporary symbol only"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Hebrews says Jesus offered one sacrifice for sins forever, showing the sufficiency of His work."},{"id":"trgg-l5-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"How can the words \"It is finished\" help you rest from trying to earn God's acceptance?","explanation":"Christ's finished work means salvation rests on what He has completed, not what we anxiously try to prove."}]}}
$trgg_l5_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'it-is-finished'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007b';


-- The Rest God Gives — Lesson 6: The Sabbath and the New Covenant (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000906';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $trgg_l6_reader$
{"openingThought":"Some people think grace cancels obedience.\n\nBut Scripture shows something better.\n\nGrace changes where obedience comes from.\n\nIn the new covenant, God does not erase His law. He writes it in the heart.\n\nThat means Sabbath rest is not about trying to earn salvation. It is about living from a restored relationship with God.\n\nThe Sabbath belongs inside the life of grace because it reminds us that God creates, God saves, and God transforms.\n\nWe do not keep Sabbath to make God love us.\n\nWe receive Sabbath because God already does.","scriptureRef":"Jeremiah 31:33","scriptureQuote":"I will put my law in their inward parts, and write it in their hearts; and will be their God, and they shall be my people.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"This is not shallow religion.\n\nGod is restoring the inner life where motives, desires, and choices are formed.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Hebrews 8:10","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"I will put my laws into their mind, and write them in their hearts.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"The new covenant does not describe a lawless life.\n\nIt describes a transformed life.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Isaiah 56:6-7","quote":"Every one that keepeth the sabbath from polluting it, and taketh hold of my covenant.\n\nEven them will I bring to my holy mountain, and make them joyful in my house of prayer."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Acts 13:42-44","quote":"And the next sabbath day came almost the whole city together to hear the word of God."}],"teachingPoints":["1. The new covenant reaches the heart\n\nJeremiah says God writes His law in the heart.\n\nThat means the new covenant is not shallow religion.\n\nGod is not only changing outward behavior.\n\nHe is restoring the inner life where motives, desires, and choices are formed.\n\nSabbath rest becomes meaningful when the heart receives it as love, not pressure.","2. Grace does not erase God's law\n\nHebrews repeats the promise that God writes His laws in the mind and heart.\n\nThe new covenant does not describe a lawless life.\n\nIt describes a transformed life.\n\nGod's law moves from external command to inward desire through the work of His Spirit.","3. Sabbath belongs to covenant relationship\n\nIsaiah connects Sabbath keeping with taking hold of God's covenant.\n\nThis shows that Sabbath is relational.\n\nIt is not an empty ritual.\n\nIt is sacred time where God invites His people into worship, joy, and nearness.\n\nThe Sabbath is not only about stopping work.\n\nIt is about belonging to God.","4. The early believers still gathered on Sabbath\n\nActs shows people gathering on the Sabbath to hear the word of God.\n\nThis reminds us that Sabbath remained a meaningful time for worship, Scripture, and spiritual community.\n\nThe Sabbath was not meant to be a cold rule.\n\nIt was a weekly rhythm of grace, truth, and fellowship."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Covenant","lemma":"διαθήκη","transliteration":"diathēkē","meaning":"A binding relationship promise.","strongs":"G1242","reference":"Jeremiah 31:33","note":"A covenant is more than a contract.\n\nIt is a relationship shaped by promise, faithfulness, and love.\n\nIn the new covenant, God writes His law in the heart so obedience flows from restored relationship instead of fear-based performance."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The Sabbath makes sense inside the new covenant.\n\nWhy?\n\nBecause the new covenant is about God doing inside us what we could never produce on our own.\n\nGod writes His law in the heart.\nGod gives His Spirit.\nGod creates new desires.\nGod restores relationship.\nGod teaches obedience from love.\n\nThat means Sabbath is not a ladder to climb toward acceptance.\n\nIt is a gift received by people who are already being drawn into God's grace.\n\nThe Sabbath reminds us that holiness is not self-manufactured.\n\nSacred time is received.\n\nRest is received.\n\nSalvation is received.\n\nTransformation is received.\n\nThen obedience becomes response."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"New covenant life","accent":"sage","description":"The new covenant writes God's love within.","payload":{"variant":"comparison","leftTitle":"Old misunderstanding","rightTitle":"New covenant reality","leftItems":["obedience to earn acceptance"],"rightItems":["grace received","heart renewed","obedience from love"]},"footnote":"Sabbath is received by grace and lived from a heart being renewed by God."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Sabbath in the New Covenant","references":[{"reference":"Jeremiah 31:31-34"},{"reference":"Hebrews 8:10"},{"reference":"Isaiah 56:6-7"},{"reference":"Acts 13:42-44"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: The Sabbath belongs within new covenant life as sacred time received by grace and lived from a heart being renewed by God."}],"reflectPrompt":"Do you tend to see Sabbath obedience as pressure, privilege, or relationship?\n\nThink honestly about whether Sabbath feels like a burden to perform, a gift to receive, or a weekly invitation into deeper trust with God.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, ask God to shift Sabbath from outward routine to inward relationship.\n\nBefore Sabbath begins, pray:\n\n\"Lord, write Your love deeper in my heart.\"\n\nThen choose one simple way to receive the day differently.\n\nWorship without rushing.\nRead Scripture slowly.\nPut down unnecessary work.\nSpend time with people intentionally.\nNotice what God has already done.\nRest without guilt.\n\nDo not treat Sabbath like a spiritual scoreboard.\n\nTreat it like sacred time with the God who saves and renews you.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for the new covenant promise. Thank You for writing Your law in hearts and minds through Your Spirit. Help me receive the Sabbath as a gift of grace, not a burden of performance. Teach me to rest, worship, and obey from love. Make my heart new in Jesus. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"The Sabbath belongs inside the new covenant life. God writes His law in the heart so rest, worship, and obedience flow from grace instead of fear.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"trgg-l6-q1","prompt":"According to Jeremiah 31:33, where does God promise to write His law?","options":["In the heart","On Roman coins","In the clouds only","On city walls only"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jeremiah says God will put His law in His people's inward parts and write it in their hearts."},{"id":"trgg-l6-q2","prompt":"What does Hebrews 8:10 say God will put into His people's minds and hearts?","options":["His laws","Fear of grace","Confusion","Self-salvation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Hebrews repeats the new covenant promise that God will write His laws in the mind and heart."},{"id":"trgg-l6-q3","prompt":"What does Isaiah 56 connect with taking hold of God's covenant?","options":["Keeping the Sabbath","Rejecting worship","Ignoring prayer","Forgetting creation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Isaiah speaks of those who keep the Sabbath and take hold of God's covenant."},{"id":"trgg-l6-q4","prompt":"What happened on the next Sabbath in Acts 13?","options":["Almost the whole city came together to hear the word of God","No one gathered for worship","Scripture was rejected completely","Sabbath was called meaningless"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Acts 13 says almost the whole city came together on the next Sabbath to hear the word of God."},{"id":"trgg-l6-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"How can Sabbath become more about relationship with God than religious performance?","explanation":"The new covenant writes God's law in the heart, so Sabbath obedience flows from grace, love, and restored relationship."}]}}
$trgg_l6_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'the-sabbath-and-the-new-covenant'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007b';


-- The Rest God Gives — Lesson 7: Rest for Your Soul (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000907';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $trgg_l7_reader$
{"openingThought":"Some people stop working, but they still do not rest.\n\nThe body sits down, but the mind keeps running.\n\nThe calendar clears, but the soul stays heavy.\n\nJesus knows that kind of weariness.\n\nThat is why He does not only invite the tired to take a break.\n\nHe invites the weary to come to Him.\n\nSabbath rest points to something deeper than time off.\n\nIt points to the rest Jesus gives the soul.\n\nThe rest God gives is not escape from life.\n\nIt is life restored in His presence.","scriptureRef":"Matthew 11:28-30","scriptureQuote":"Come unto me, all ye that labour and are heavy laden, and I will give you rest.\n\nTake my yoke upon you, and learn of me.\n\nFor my yoke is easy, and my burden is light.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"This is the center of true rest.\n\nJesus does not first say, \"Fix yourself.\"\n\nHe says, \"Come.\"","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Mark 2:27-28","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"The sabbath was made for man, and not man for the sabbath.\n\nTherefore the Son of man is Lord also of the sabbath.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Sabbath rest is a gift, not a crushing burden.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Hebrews 4:9-10","quote":"There remaineth therefore a rest to the people of God.\n\nFor he that is entered into his rest, he also hath ceased from his own works, as God did from his."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Psalm 62:5","quote":"My soul, wait thou only upon God; for my expectation is from him."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Jesus invites the weary to come\n\nJesus says, \"Come unto me.\"\n\nThat is the center of true rest.\n\nHe does not first say, \"Fix yourself.\"\n\nHe says, \"Come.\"\n\nThe rest Jesus gives begins in relationship with Him.\n\nHe welcomes the burdened, the tired, the anxious, and the spiritually worn down.","2. Sabbath was made as a gift\n\nJesus said the Sabbath was made for humanity.\n\nThat means Sabbath rest is not meant to crush the soul.\n\nIt is meant to bless, restore, and reconnect us with God.\n\nThe Sabbath is sacred time where Jesus reminds us that we are not machines, slaves, or self-made beings.\n\nWe are loved creatures invited into rest.","3. God's rest means ceasing from self-saving works\n\nHebrews says the one who enters God's rest ceases from his own works.\n\nThis does not mean obedience stops.\n\nIt means anxious self-salvation stops.\n\nWe rest from trying to earn what God gives by grace.\n\nWe stop carrying the burden of proving our worth before Him.","4. True rest is found in trusting God\n\nPsalm 62 says, \"My soul, wait thou only upon God.\"\n\nSoul rest comes when the heart stops leaning on everything else as ultimate.\n\nWork cannot give this rest.\n\nSuccess cannot give this rest.\n\nEntertainment cannot give this rest.\n\nOnly God can quiet the soul at its deepest level."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Rest","lemma":"ἀναπαύω","transliteration":"anapauō","meaning":"Relief, renewal, ceasing from striving, and being settled in God's care.","strongs":"G373","reference":"Matthew 11:28","note":"Biblical rest is more than stopping activity.\n\nIt is trusting God as Creator, Redeemer, Provider, and Lord.\n\nSabbath rest points to the deeper soul rest Jesus gives to those who come to Him."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The Rest God Gives has shown one clear movement:\n\nGod created the Sabbath as a gift in time.\n\nHumanity began with rest before work.\n\nThe Sabbath calls us to remember the Creator.\n\nThe Sabbath calls us to remember the Redeemer.\n\nJesus finished the work of redemption.\n\nThe Sabbath belongs within new covenant grace.\n\nNow Jesus invites the weary into rest for the soul.\n\nThis is the heart of the study.\n\nSabbath is not merely about stopping work.\n\nIt is about remembering whose work saves us.\n\nIt is about returning to the God who made us, redeemed us, and renews us.\n\nThe rest God gives is weekly, spiritual, relational, and deeply practical.\n\nIt teaches the soul:\n\nGod is Creator.\nGod is Redeemer.\nJesus is Lord of the Sabbath.\nSalvation is finished in Christ.\nI can stop striving and come near."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"The Rest God Gives","accent":"stone","description":"Sabbath rest points to the deeper rest Jesus gives.","payload":{"variant":"sequence","steps":[{"label":"Creation completed"},{"label":"Humanity receives"},{"label":"Creator remembered"},{"label":"Redeemer remembered"},{"label":"Christ's work finished"},{"label":"New covenant heart renewed"},{"label":"Soul rest received"}]},"footnote":"God is Creator. God is Redeemer. Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath. Salvation is finished in Christ."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Rest for Your Soul","references":[{"reference":"Matthew 11:28-30"},{"reference":"Mark 2:27-28"},{"reference":"Hebrews 4:9-10"},{"reference":"Psalm 62:5"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: The Sabbath is God's gift of sacred time, pointing to the deeper soul rest Jesus gives to the weary."}],"reflectPrompt":"Where is your soul most tired right now?\n\nThink about striving, guilt, pressure, anxiety, grief, work, ministry, relationships, or trying to prove your worth.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, let Sabbath rest become soul rest.\n\nDo not only stop working.\n\nCome to Jesus.\n\nBefore or during Sabbath, take a quiet moment and pray:\n\n\"Jesus, this is what I have been carrying.\"\n\nThen name it honestly.\n\nA fear.\nA burden.\nA pressure.\nA guilt.\nA responsibility.\nA place where you feel worn down.\n\nThen receive His invitation:\n\n\"Come unto me... and I will give you rest.\"\n\nLet Sabbath become a weekly return to the One who can actually quiet the soul.\n\nPrayer:\n\nJesus, thank You for inviting the weary to come to You. Thank You for Sabbath as a gift of sacred time and for the deeper rest You give the soul. Help me stop striving for what only You can give. Teach me to rest in You as Creator, Redeemer, and Lord. Quiet my heart in Your love and lead me into the freedom of Your finished work. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"You have completed The Rest God Gives. The Sabbath is God's weekly gift of sacred time, reminding us that creation and salvation are His finished work, and that Jesus gives true rest to the soul.","seriesCompleteScreen":{"title":"Study Complete","message":"You have completed The Rest God Gives. The Sabbath is God's weekly gift of sacred time, reminding us that creation and salvation are His finished work, and that Jesus gives true rest to the soul.","primaryCta":{"label":"Back to Foundations Path","href":"/paths/foundations"},"secondaryCta":{"label":"Review this study","href":"/study-library/the-rest-god-gives"}},"lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"trgg-l7-q1","prompt":"According to Matthew 11:28, who does Jesus invite to come to Him?","options":["All who labour and are heavy laden","Only people who feel perfect","Only people with no burdens","Those who never need rest"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus invites all who labor and are heavy laden to come to Him, promising rest."},{"id":"trgg-l7-q2","prompt":"According to Mark 2:27, who was the Sabbath made for?","options":["Humanity","Angels only","No one","Kings only"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus said the Sabbath was made for man, showing it was given as a gift for human beings."},{"id":"trgg-l7-q3","prompt":"According to Hebrews 4:9, what remains for the people of God?","options":["Rest","Fear","Condemnation","Endless striving"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Hebrews says there remains a rest for the people of God."},{"id":"trgg-l7-q4","prompt":"What is the deeper meaning of Sabbath rest in this lesson?","options":["Coming to Jesus and resting in God's finished work","Proving our worth through religious effort","Ignoring God's grace","Treating rest as laziness"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Sabbath rest points to Jesus, who gives soul rest and teaches us to rest in God's completed work."},{"id":"trgg-l7-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What burden do you need to bring to Jesus so you can receive His rest?","explanation":"Jesus does not invite the weary to hide their burdens. He invites them to come to Him and receive rest for the soul."}]}}
$trgg_l7_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'rest-for-your-soul'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007b';


-- The Life That Gives — Lesson 1: God Gives First (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000908';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $tltg_l1_reader$
{"openingThought":"The Christian life does not begin with what we give to God.\n\nIt begins with what God has given to us.\n\nBefore we loved Him, He loved us.\n\nBefore we served Him, He served us.\n\nBefore we gave anything back, He gave us life, breath, mercy, grace, and Jesus.\n\nThat matters because biblical generosity is not rooted in guilt.\n\nIt is rooted in grace.\n\nGod is the Great Giver.\n\nAnd when His love reaches the heart, it begins to make us generous too.","scriptureRef":"James 1:17","scriptureQuote":"Every good gift and every perfect gift is from above, and cometh down from the Father of lights.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Every truly good thing in life begins with God's generosity.\n\nWe are receivers before we are givers.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"John 3:16","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"For God so loved the world, that he gave his only begotten Son.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"God's greatest gift reveals the depth of His love.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Romans 8:32","quote":"He that spared not his own Son, but delivered him up for us all, how shall he not with him also freely give us all things?"},{"type":"scripture","reference":"1 Timothy 2:5-6","quote":"For there is one God, and one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus.\n\nWho gave himself a ransom for all."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Every good gift begins with God\n\nJames says every good and perfect gift comes from above.\n\nThat means everything truly good in our lives begins with God's generosity.\n\nLife is a gift.\nBreath is a gift.\nProvision is a gift.\nGrace is a gift.\nSalvation is a gift.\n\nBefore we ever give anything, we are receivers.","2. God's greatest gift is Jesus\n\nJohn 3:16 says God loved the world and gave His only begotten Son.\n\nThis is the center of Christian giving.\n\nGod did not give us leftovers.\n\nHe gave His Son.\n\nThe cross reveals that God's generosity is not shallow, cheap, or distant.\n\nGod gives Himself for the salvation of others.","3. Jesus gave Himself for us\n\nFirst Timothy says Jesus gave Himself as a ransom for all.\n\nJesus was not forced into generosity.\n\nHe willingly gave Himself.\n\nHe gave His life to rescue us from sin, selfishness, fear, and death.\n\nChristian generosity begins by looking at the self-giving heart of Christ.","4. God's giving teaches us to trust Him\n\nRomans 8 says if God did not spare His own Son, we can trust Him to freely give what we truly need.\n\nThis does not mean God gives us every selfish desire.\n\nIt means His greatest gift proves His heart.\n\nIf God gave Jesus, we can trust His love, care, provision, and wisdom."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Gift","lemma":"δῶρον","transliteration":"dōron","meaning":"Something given freely, not earned or purchased by the receiver.","strongs":"G1435","reference":"James 1:17","note":"A gift reveals the heart of the giver.\n\nGod's gifts reveal His character of love.\n\nThe greatest gift is Jesus, and every act of Christian generosity is meant to flow from receiving Him."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"God's kingdom starts with giving because God Himself is the Great Giver.\n\nThe world often teaches us to get, keep, protect, and accumulate.\n\nBut the gospel begins with a God who gives.\n\nHe gives life.\nHe gives mercy.\nHe gives daily provision.\nHe gives His Son.\nHe gives salvation.\n\nAnd His giving is meant to create a new kind of life in us.\n\nWe do not give to make God generous.\n\nWe give because God has already been generous.\n\nWe do not give to earn His love.\n\nWe give because His love has reached us first."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"givingCirclePattern","title":"God Gives First","caption":"God gives first, and His grace creates a circle of blessing through us."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"The Great Giver","references":[{"reference":"James 1:17"},{"reference":"John 3:16"},{"reference":"Romans 8:32"},{"reference":"1 Timothy 2:5-6"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Every good gift comes from God, and His greatest gift is Jesus. Christian generosity begins by receiving the love God has already given."}],"reflectPrompt":"When you think about giving, do you usually start with guilt, pressure, or God's generosity?\n\nThink about money, time, service, energy, attention, and how your view of God shapes your willingness to give.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, begin with gratitude before giving.\n\nMake a short list of five gifts God has already given you.\n\nThey may be simple:\n\nLife.\nFood.\nFriendship.\nForgiveness.\nAnother day.\nA skill.\nA lesson.\nA chance to grow.\nJesus.\n\nThen ask:\n\n\"How can I reflect God's generosity today?\"\n\nStart small.\n\nEncourage someone.\nShare what you have.\nGive time without needing applause.\nHelp someone without making it about you.\n\nGenerosity grows best in the soil of gratitude.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for being the Great Giver. Every good gift comes from You, and the greatest gift is Jesus. Help me receive Your love deeply so that generosity can grow in me. Free me from guilt-driven giving, selfishness, and fear. Teach me to give because You gave first. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"God gives first. Every good gift comes from Him, and His greatest gift is Jesus. Christian generosity begins by receiving His grace.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"tltg-l1-q1","prompt":"According to James 1:17, where does every good and perfect gift come from?","options":["From above, from the Father of lights","From human pride","From selfishness","From chance only"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"James says every good and perfect gift comes from above, from the Father of lights."},{"id":"tltg-l1-q2","prompt":"According to John 3:16, what did God give because He loved the world?","options":["His only begotten Son","A temporary idea","A selfish command","A burden without hope"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"John 3:16 says God so loved the world that He gave His only begotten Son."},{"id":"tltg-l1-q3","prompt":"According to 1 Timothy 2:5-6, what did Jesus give Himself as?","options":["A ransom for all","A symbol of fear","A reason to boast","A replacement for love"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"First Timothy says Jesus gave Himself a ransom for all."},{"id":"tltg-l1-q4","prompt":"What does Romans 8:32 teach us about God's heart?","options":["If God gave His Son, we can trust His generous care","God is unwilling to give anything","God only gives when we force Him","God's greatest gift was small"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Romans 8:32 teaches that the gift of Jesus proves God's generous heart and care for us."},{"id":"tltg-l1-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What is one gift from God you often take for granted?","explanation":"Recognizing God's gifts helps generosity grow from gratitude instead of pressure."}]}}
$tltg_l1_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'god-gives-first'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007c';


-- The Life That Gives — Lesson 2: The Circle of Blessing (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000909';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $tltg_l2_reader$
{"openingThought":"God's kingdom does not run on taking.\n\nIt runs on giving.\n\nSin turns life inward.\n\nIt asks, \"What can I get?\"\n\"What can I keep?\"\n\"How can this benefit me?\"\n\nBut God's love moves in the opposite direction.\n\nGod gives.\nWe receive.\nGrace changes us.\nThen we give.\n\nThat is the circle of blessing.\n\nTrue generosity does not end with us. It flows through us.","scriptureRef":"Luke 6:38","scriptureQuote":"Give, and it shall be given unto you; good measure, pressed down, and shaken together, and running over.\n\nFor with the same measure that ye mete withal it shall be measured to you again.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"This is not a selfish formula for getting rich.\n\nIt is a picture of how love works.\n\nWhen generosity moves outward, blessing multiplies.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Philippians 2:3-5","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"Let nothing be done through strife or vainglory; but in lowliness of mind let each esteem other better than themselves.\n\nLook not every man on his own things, but every man also on the things of others.\n\nLet this mind be in you, which was also in Christ Jesus.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"The mind of Christ looks beyond self-interest to the good of others.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"1 John 4:19","quote":"We love him, because he first loved us."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Acts 20:35","quote":"It is more blessed to give than to receive."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Giving creates a circle of blessing\n\nJesus says, \"Give, and it shall be given unto you.\"\n\nThis is not a selfish formula for getting rich.\n\nIt is a picture of how love works.\n\nWhen generosity moves outward, blessing multiplies.\n\nGiving opens the heart, blesses others, and reflects the character of God.","2. God's love moves us outward\n\nFirst John says we love because God first loved us.\n\nGod's love comes first.\n\nWe receive before we give.\n\nBut once God's love reaches us, it does not stay locked inside us.\n\nLove received becomes love returned to God and love extended to others.","3. The mind of Christ is others-centered\n\nPhilippians tells us to look not only to our own interests, but also to the interests of others.\n\nThat is the mind of Jesus.\n\nChrist did not use His position for selfish gain.\n\nHe gave Himself.\n\nThe life of Jesus shows that the highest kind of life is not self-protection, but self-giving love.","4. Giving is more blessed than taking\n\nJesus said it is more blessed to give than to receive.\n\nThat does not mean receiving is bad.\n\nWe receive every good gift from God.\n\nBut when receiving turns into hoarding, the soul shrinks.\n\nGiving expands the heart because it aligns us with God's own character."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Blessing","lemma":"εὐλογία","transliteration":"eulogia","meaning":"A gift, benefit, favor, or good that flows from God and brings life.","strongs":"G2129","reference":"Acts 20:35","note":"Blessing is not meant to stop with the receiver.\n\nGod blesses us so that His goodness can move through us to others.\n\nThe circle of blessing begins with God and continues as His love shapes us into givers."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The world often teaches a broken cycle:\n\nGet.\nKeep.\nProtect.\nConsume.\nRepeat.\n\nBut God's kingdom teaches a better way:\n\nReceive.\nGive thanks.\nShare.\nBless.\nReflect God.\nRepeat.\n\nThis is not only about money.\n\nIt includes attention, mercy, time, forgiveness, hospitality, encouragement, service, and resources.\n\nThe circle of blessing begins with God as the Great Giver.\n\nThen His grace creates generosity in us.\n\nWhen we give, we are not losing life.\n\nWe are participating in the way life was meant to flow."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"givingCirclePattern","title":"The Circle of Blessing","caption":"Grace received becomes love shared.","stages":[{"label":"God gives"},{"label":"We receive"},{"label":"We give thanks"},{"label":"We bless others"},{"label":"Others experience God's goodness"},{"label":"God is glorified"}]},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"The Circle of Blessing","references":[{"reference":"Luke 6:38"},{"reference":"Philippians 2:3-5"},{"reference":"1 John 4:19"},{"reference":"Acts 20:35"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: God gives first, and His love moves us outward into a life of generosity, service, and blessing."}],"reflectPrompt":"Where are you more tempted to take, keep, or protect than to give, share, or bless?\n\nThink about your time, money, attention, emotional energy, forgiveness, encouragement, or willingness to serve.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, practice the circle of blessing.\n\nChoose one gift God has given you and let it move through you.\n\nIf God has given you encouragement, encourage someone.\n\nIf God has given you time, serve someone.\n\nIf God has given you resources, share with someone.\n\nIf God has given you mercy, show mercy to someone.\n\nDo not overcomplicate it.\n\nAsk one simple question:\n\n\"How can I let God's goodness move through me today?\"\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for giving first. Thank You that Your love moves outward and creates blessing. Teach me to receive with gratitude and give with joy. Free me from the fear of losing and the habit of hoarding. Let Your generosity shape my heart so others can experience Your goodness through my life. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"God gives first, and His grace creates a circle of blessing. What we receive from Him is meant to flow through us to others.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"tltg-l2-q1","prompt":"According to Luke 6:38, what happens when we give?","options":["It shall be given unto us","God forgets us","Giving becomes meaningless","Love stops flowing"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus teaches that giving creates a circle of blessing. What we measure out is measured back to us."},{"id":"tltg-l2-q2","prompt":"According to 1 John 4:19, why do we love?","options":["Because God first loved us","Because we earned God's love","Because fear creates love","Because love begins with us"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"First John says we love because God first loved us. God's love is the source of our love."},{"id":"tltg-l2-q3","prompt":"According to Philippians 2:3-5, what mindset should believers have?","options":["The mind of Christ, looking also to the interests of others","A self-centered mind","A greedy mind","A careless mind"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Philippians calls believers to have the mind of Christ and to care about the interests of others."},{"id":"tltg-l2-q4","prompt":"According to Acts 20:35, what did Jesus say is more blessed?","options":["To give than to receive","To take than to give","To hoard than to share","To ignore others"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus said it is more blessed to give than to receive."},{"id":"tltg-l2-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What is one specific way you can let God's goodness move through you this week?","explanation":"The circle of blessing continues when we receive from God and then share His goodness with others."}]}}
$tltg_l2_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'the-circle-of-blessing'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007c';


-- The Life That Gives — Lesson 3: From Taking to Giving (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-00000000090a';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $tltg_l3_reader$
{"openingThought":"Sin turns the heart inward.\n\nIt teaches us to take, protect, compete, compare, and consume.\n\nBut Jesus came to rescue us from that way of living.\n\nThe gospel does not only forgive selfish people.\n\nIt transforms takers into givers.\n\nGod's love reaches us while we are curved inward, then begins turning us outward toward Him and others.\n\nThat is what grace does.\n\nIt does not simply cancel guilt.\n\nIt creates a new kind of life.","scriptureRef":"Titus 2:14","scriptureQuote":"Who gave himself for us, that he might redeem us from all iniquity, and purify unto himself a peculiar people, zealous of good works.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Jesus gave Himself to redeem and purify a people who begin to love what He loves.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Galatians 1:3-5","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"Grace be to you and peace from God the Father, and from our Lord Jesus Christ.\n\nWho gave himself for our sins, that he might deliver us from this present evil world.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"The gospel delivers us from a self-centered age into a new life in Christ.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"2 Timothy 3:1-5","quote":"For men shall be lovers of their own selves... lovers of pleasures more than lovers of God."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Philippians 2:4-5","quote":"Look not every man on his own things, but every man also on the things of others.\n\nLet this mind be in you, which was also in Christ Jesus."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Jesus gave Himself to redeem us\n\nTitus says Jesus gave Himself for us.\n\nThat is the foundation of Christian transformation.\n\nJesus did not give a small gift from a safe distance.\n\nHe gave Himself.\n\nHis self-giving love redeems us from lawlessness, lovelessness, and selfishness.","2. Redemption changes what we desire\n\nTitus says Jesus purifies a people who are zealous for good works.\n\nThat means grace does not leave the heart unchanged.\n\nJesus saves us from sin's guilt and from sin's pattern.\n\nHe begins creating a people who want to bless, serve, love, and give.","3. The present evil age is self-centered\n\nSecond Timothy describes a world where people are lovers of themselves, lovers of money, proud, unthankful, unholy, unloving, and lovers of pleasure more than lovers of God.\n\nThat is the spirit of taking.\n\nWhen self becomes the center, people become tools, resources become idols, and love becomes conditional.\n\nJesus delivers us from that way of life.","4. The mind of Christ moves outward\n\nPhilippians calls us to look not only to our own interests, but also to the interests of others.\n\nThat is the mind of Jesus.\n\nChrist's life moved outward in love.\n\nWhen His Spirit works in us, we begin to move outward too.\n\nWe become less obsessed with getting and more open to giving."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Redeem","lemma":"λυτρόω","transliteration":"lutroō","meaning":"To rescue, release, or deliver by paying a cost.","strongs":"G3084","reference":"Titus 2:14","note":"Jesus redeems us not only from guilt, but also from the selfish pattern of sin.\n\nHis self-giving love frees us from the life of taking and forms in us a new life of giving."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The gospel changes the direction of the heart.\n\nSin says:\n\nTake for yourself.\nProtect yourself.\nExalt yourself.\nPlease yourself.\nUse others for yourself.\n\nJesus says:\n\nReceive My love.\nTrust My grace.\nLet Me free you.\nServe in love.\nGive because God has given to you.\n\nThis is not natural to the fallen heart.\n\nIt is supernatural.\n\nOnly grace can turn a taker into a giver.\n\nOnly Jesus can break the inward curve of selfishness and teach us the joy of other-centered love."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"Grace changes the heart","accent":"sage","description":"Jesus redeems us from self-centered living into other-centered love.","payload":{"variant":"comparison","leftTitle":"Before grace","rightTitle":"After grace","leftItems":["taking","self-protection","pride","using others","fear"],"rightItems":["receiving","gratitude","service","generosity","love"]},"footnote":"Only grace can turn a taker into a giver."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"From Taking to Giving","references":[{"reference":"Titus 2:14"},{"reference":"Galatians 1:3-5"},{"reference":"2 Timothy 3:1-5"},{"reference":"Philippians 2:4-5"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Jesus gave Himself to redeem us from selfishness and form in us a life of love, service, and generosity."}],"reflectPrompt":"Where do you see the taking spirit of sin showing up in your own life?\n\nThink about control, money, attention, relationships, comfort, pride, comparison, or using people to meet your own needs.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, identify one place where your heart naturally turns inward.\n\nThen ask Jesus to turn it outward.\n\nUse this simple question:\n\n\"What would giving look like here?\"\n\nIf you usually protect your time, give someone your presence.\n\nIf you usually hold tightly to resources, share something.\n\nIf you usually seek attention, encourage someone else.\n\nIf you usually defend yourself, listen first.\n\nDo not try to manufacture generosity from guilt.\n\nStart with Jesus.\n\nHe gave Himself for you.\n\nLet His love become the power that moves you outward.\n\nPrayer:\n\nJesus, thank You for giving Yourself for me. Thank You for redeeming me from selfishness, fear, and the spirit of taking. Show me where my heart is still curved inward. Teach me to receive Your love deeply and reflect it freely. Make me generous, humble, and willing to bless others because You first blessed me. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"Jesus gave Himself to redeem us from the inward pull of sin. His grace turns takers into givers and forms a life of other-centered love.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"tltg-l3-q1","prompt":"According to Titus 2:14, what did Jesus give?","options":["Himself for us","A small leftover gift","A reason to be selfish","A message without sacrifice"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Titus says Jesus gave Himself for us to redeem and purify a people zealous for good works."},{"id":"tltg-l3-q2","prompt":"According to Galatians 1:4, what did Jesus give Himself for?","options":["Our sins, to deliver us from this present evil world","His own selfish gain","Human applause","A temporary example only"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Galatians says Jesus gave Himself for our sins so He might deliver us from this present evil world."},{"id":"tltg-l3-q3","prompt":"What does 2 Timothy 3 describe as a mark of the last days?","options":["People being lovers of their own selves","People naturally becoming perfect","A world without selfishness","Sin disappearing without Christ"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Second Timothy describes people as lovers of their own selves, showing the self-centered spirit of sin."},{"id":"tltg-l3-q4","prompt":"According to Philippians 2:4-5, what mind should believers have?","options":["The mind of Christ, looking also to the interests of others","A mind focused only on self","A mind ruled by greed","A mind that ignores others"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Paul calls believers to have the mind of Christ and to look also to the interests of others."},{"id":"tltg-l3-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What is one practical way Jesus is calling you to move from taking to giving?","explanation":"Grace changes the direction of the heart. Jesus redeems us from self-centered living and teaches us to love outwardly."}]}}
$tltg_l3_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'from-taking-to-giving'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007c';


-- The Life That Gives — Lesson 4: Everything Belongs to God (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-00000000090b';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $tltg_l4_reader$
{"openingThought":"Generosity begins with ownership.\n\nIf we believe everything belongs to us, giving will always feel like losing.\n\nBut if we believe everything belongs to God, giving becomes stewardship.\n\nScripture teaches that God owns the earth, the resources in it, and even the ability we have to produce wealth.\n\nThat means we are not owners in the ultimate sense.\n\nWe are stewards.\n\nA steward manages what belongs to someone else.\n\nWhen we understand that everything comes from God, generosity stops being a threat and becomes an act of trust.","scriptureRef":"Psalm 24:1","scriptureQuote":"The earth is the LORD'S, and the fulness thereof; the world, and they that dwell therein.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Everything we see, use, and depend on begins with God's ownership.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Haggai 2:8","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"The silver is mine, and the gold is mine, saith the LORD of hosts.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Material blessings belong to God, not to human pride.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Deuteronomy 8:18","quote":"But thou shalt remember the LORD thy God: for it is he that giveth thee power to get wealth."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"1 Chronicles 29:14","quote":"But who am I, and what is my people, that we should be able to offer so willingly after this sort?\n\nFor all things come of thee, and of thine own have we given thee."}],"teachingPoints":["1. The earth belongs to God\n\nPsalm 24 says the earth is the Lord's and everything in it.\n\nThat is a direct challenge to the way we often think.\n\nOur money, possessions, opportunities, skills, and resources do not begin with us.\n\nThey belong to God first.\n\nWe hold them temporarily, but He owns them ultimately.","2. Material blessings belong to God\n\nHaggai says the silver and gold belong to the Lord.\n\nGod is not against material blessing.\n\nBut He does want us to see material blessing correctly.\n\nMoney is not our master.\n\nPossessions are not our identity.\n\nResources are not meant to become idols.\n\nThey are gifts entrusted by God for faithful use.","3. God gives the ability to produce wealth\n\nDeuteronomy says God gives the power to get wealth.\n\nThis includes strength, intelligence, opportunity, timing, health, relationships, and ability.\n\nEven when we work hard, we are still dependent on God.\n\nHard work matters.\n\nBut hard work is not independent from grace.","4. Giving is returning what came from God\n\nDavid said, \"Of thine own have we given thee.\"\n\nThat is the heart of stewardship.\n\nWhen we give to God, we are not enriching Him.\n\nWe are returning from what He already gave.\n\nGenerosity is not God taking from us.\n\nIt is God inviting us to participate in His purpose with what was His all along."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Steward","lemma":"οἰκονόμος","transliteration":"oikonomos","meaning":"A person entrusted to manage what belongs to another.","strongs":"G3623","reference":"1 Chronicles 29:14","note":"A steward does not own everything independently.\n\nA steward manages faithfully.\n\nBiblical stewardship means recognizing that our lives, resources, abilities, and opportunities belong to God and are entrusted to us for His purposes."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"scripture_connection","body":"The question is not only, \"How much should I give?\"\n\nThe deeper question is:\n\n\"What do I believe I own?\"\n\nIf we believe everything is ours, giving feels like loss.\n\nIf we believe everything is God's, giving becomes worship.\n\nThis changes how we see money.\n\nMoney is not merely personal power.\n\nIt is entrusted influence.\n\nIt can serve selfishness, or it can serve love.\n\nIt can build ego, or it can bless people.\n\nIt can feed fear, or it can support mission.\n\nThe Bible's view is simple:\n\nGod owns everything.\n\nWe manage what He gives.\n\nFaithful generosity begins there."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"givingCirclePattern","title":"Stewardship","caption":"Stewardship begins when we realize everything belongs to God.","stages":[{"label":"God owns"},{"label":"God entrusts"},{"label":"We manage"},{"label":"We give"},{"label":"Others are blessed"},{"label":"God is honored"}]},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Everything Belongs to God","references":[{"reference":"Psalm 24:1"},{"reference":"Haggai 2:8"},{"reference":"Deuteronomy 8:18"},{"reference":"1 Chronicles 29:14"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: God owns all things and gives us the ability to produce and manage resources. Generosity is returning to God from what He already gave."}],"reflectPrompt":"What resource do you tend to treat as fully yours instead of entrusted by God?\n\nThink about money, time, skills, influence, possessions, career opportunities, or even your physical energy.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, choose one resource and consciously give it back to God.\n\nIt may be money.\nIt may be time.\nIt may be a skill.\nIt may be your schedule.\nIt may be your influence.\n\nPray over it and say:\n\n\"Lord, this came from You. Show me how to manage it faithfully.\"\n\nThen take one practical step.\n\nGive.\nServe.\nBudget with intention.\nStop wasting what God entrusted.\nUse your skill to bless someone.\nShare something you have been holding too tightly.\n\nStewardship starts when ownership is surrendered.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for every gift You have entrusted to me. The earth is Yours, and everything I have comes from You. Help me stop living like an owner and start living like a faithful steward. Teach me to manage my resources with wisdom, gratitude, and generosity. Use what You have placed in my hands to bless others and honor You. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"Everything belongs to God. We are not ultimate owners, but stewards entrusted with His gifts to manage faithfully and generously.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"tltg-l4-q1","prompt":"According to Psalm 24:1, who does the earth belong to?","options":["The Lord","Human pride","The strongest person","No one"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Psalm 24:1 says the earth is the Lord's and the fullness thereof."},{"id":"tltg-l4-q2","prompt":"According to Haggai 2:8, what belongs to the Lord?","options":["The silver and the gold","Only spiritual things","Nothing material","Only the temple furniture"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"God says the silver and the gold are His, showing that material blessings belong to Him."},{"id":"tltg-l4-q3","prompt":"According to Deuteronomy 8:18, who gives the power to get wealth?","options":["The Lord God","Human pride alone","Luck only","Money itself"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Deuteronomy says God gives the power to get wealth, reminding us that even our ability comes from Him."},{"id":"tltg-l4-q4","prompt":"According to 1 Chronicles 29:14, what did David recognize about giving?","options":["All things come from God, and we give back from what is His","Giving makes humans superior to God","God needs our money to survive","Resources have no connection to worship"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"David said, \"All things come of thee, and of thine own have we given thee.\""},{"id":"tltg-l4-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"How would your generosity change if you truly believed everything you have belongs to God?","explanation":"Stewardship begins by recognizing that God owns everything and we manage His gifts for His purposes."}]}}
$tltg_l4_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'everything-belongs-to-god'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007c';


-- The Life That Gives — Lesson 5: Returning the Tithe (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-00000000090c';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $tltg_l5_reader$
{"openingThought":"Tithe is not about God needing money.\n\nGod owns everything.\n\nThe earth is His.\nThe silver is His.\nThe gold is His.\nThe breath in our lungs is His.\n\nSo why does God ask His people to return tithe?\n\nBecause tithe teaches trust.\n\nIt reminds us that increase comes from God. It trains the heart to acknowledge Him first. It keeps money from becoming our master.\n\nTithe is not a bribe.\n\nIt is not a payment for salvation.\n\nIt is a faith response to the God who provides.","scriptureRef":"Leviticus 27:30","scriptureQuote":"And all the tithe of the land... is the LORD'S: it is holy unto the LORD.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"The tithe already belongs to God.\n\nReturning it is recognition, not invention.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Genesis 28:22","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"And of all that thou shalt give me I will surely give the tenth unto thee.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Jacob understood that giving back began with receiving from God.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Numbers 18:21","quote":"And, behold, I have given the children of Levi all the tenth in Israel for an inheritance, for their service."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Malachi 3:10","quote":"Bring ye all the tithes into the storehouse, that there may be meat in mine house."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Deuteronomy 8:18","quote":"But thou shalt remember the LORD thy God: for it is he that giveth thee power to get wealth."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Tithe belongs to the Lord\n\nLeviticus says the tithe is the Lord's and is holy unto Him.\n\nThat means tithe is not first described as something we invent.\n\nIt already belongs to God.\n\nReturning tithe is an act of recognition.\n\nWe acknowledge that God is the source of our increase and that everything we have comes from Him.","2. Tithe is connected to trust\n\nJacob promised to give a tenth of what God gave him.\n\nThe language is important:\n\n\"Of all that thou shalt give me.\"\n\nJacob understood that giving back began with receiving from God.\n\nTithe trains the heart to remember that provision is not self-created.\n\nGod gives first.","3. Tithe supports God's work\n\nNumbers shows that the tithe supported the Levites in their service.\n\nIn Scripture, tithe is connected to the work of ministry, worship, and spiritual service.\n\nIt is not random giving.\n\nIt is structured support for the work God appointed.\n\nTithe reminds us that God's mission is meant to be sustained by God's people.","4. Tithe confronts fear and self-reliance\n\nMalachi calls God's people to bring the tithes into the storehouse.\n\nThe issue was not only money.\n\nIt was trust.\n\nWhen we hold back what belongs to God, we often reveal fear underneath.\n\nFear that there will not be enough.\nFear that we must secure ourselves.\nFear that God cannot provide.\n\nTithe becomes a weekly or monthly confession:\n\nGod is my source."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Tithe","lemma":"מַעֲשֵׂר","transliteration":"ma'aser","meaning":"A tenth.","strongs":"H4643","reference":"Leviticus 27:30","note":"Tithe means returning one tenth of increase to God.\n\nIt is not about earning salvation or buying blessing.\n\nIt is an act of worship, trust, and stewardship that recognizes God as the source of all provision."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"scripture_connection","body":"Tithe is one of the clearest ways Scripture challenges the heart's relationship with money.\n\nMoney can make us feel powerful.\n\nIt can make us feel secure.\n\nIt can make us feel independent.\n\nBut tithe interrupts that illusion.\n\nIt says:\n\nGod gave first.\nGod owns everything.\nGod provides increase.\nGod's work matters.\nMoney is not my master.\n\nReturning tithe does not mean we are paying God back as if we could settle the account.\n\nWe cannot.\n\nIt means we are acknowledging Him first with what He has entrusted to us.\n\nThis keeps generosity from becoming vague.\n\nTithe gives faith a concrete practice."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"givingCirclePattern","title":"Returning the tithe","caption":"Tithe is returned, not paid. It belongs to the Lord.","stages":[{"label":"God gives increase"},{"label":"We recognize Him as source"},{"label":"We return the tenth"},{"label":"God's work is supported"},{"label":"The heart learns trust"}]},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Returning What Belongs to God","references":[{"reference":"Genesis 28:22"},{"reference":"Leviticus 27:30"},{"reference":"Numbers 18:21"},{"reference":"Malachi 3:10"},{"reference":"Deuteronomy 8:18"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Tithe is the tenth that belongs to the Lord. Returning it is an act of worship, trust, and support for God's work."}],"reflectPrompt":"What does your relationship with money reveal about your trust in God?\n\nThink about fear, control, generosity, anxiety, budgeting, giving, and whether you see God as your true source.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, look honestly at your increase and your giving.\n\nDo not start with shame.\n\nStart with surrender.\n\nAsk:\n\n\"Lord, am I recognizing You first with what You have entrusted to me?\"\n\nIf you already return tithe, ask God to keep it from becoming empty routine.\n\nIf you do not, ask God to help you take a faithful next step.\n\nTithe is not about God needing your money.\n\nIt is about your heart learning to trust the God who gives.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for being the source of all provision. Everything I have comes from You. Teach me to return tithe with faith, gratitude, and trust. Free me from fear, greed, and self-reliance. Help me honor You first and support Your work with a willing heart. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"Tithe belongs to the Lord. Returning it is not buying salvation, but acknowledging God as the source of every increase and supporting His work with trust.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"tltg-l5-q1","prompt":"According to Leviticus 27:30, who does the tithe belong to?","options":["The Lord","The strongest person","Human pride","No one"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Leviticus says the tithe is the Lord's and is holy unto Him."},{"id":"tltg-l5-q2","prompt":"What does the word \"tithe\" mean?","options":["A tenth","A random amount","A yearly tax only","A feeling of generosity"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Tithe means a tenth. It refers to returning one tenth of increase to God."},{"id":"tltg-l5-q3","prompt":"According to Numbers 18:21, what did the tithe support?","options":["The service of the Levites","The building of Egypt","Personal luxury only","Roman soldiers"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Numbers shows that the tithe supported the Levites in their spiritual service."},{"id":"tltg-l5-q4","prompt":"According to Deuteronomy 8:18, what should we remember?","options":["God gives the power to get wealth","Wealth comes only from luck","Money is our savior","God has no connection to provision"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Deuteronomy reminds us that God gives the power to get wealth."},{"id":"tltg-l5-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What is one practical step you can take to acknowledge God first with your resources?","explanation":"Tithe trains the heart to recognize God as the source of provision and to trust Him with what He has entrusted to us."}]}}
$tltg_l5_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'returning-the-tithe'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007c';


-- The Life That Gives — Lesson 6: Offerings From the Heart (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-00000000090d';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $tltg_l6_reader$
{"openingThought":"Tithe teaches us to recognize God as the source.\n\nOfferings teach us to respond with love.\n\nTithe is the tenth that belongs to the Lord.\n\nOfferings go beyond the tithe. They are gifts freely given from a grateful heart.\n\nGod does not want forced generosity.\n\nHe wants willing love.\n\nAn offering is not about impressing God, proving spirituality, or giving under pressure.\n\nIt is the heart saying:\n\n\"God has been generous to me, so I want my life to become generous too.\"","scriptureRef":"2 Corinthians 9:7","scriptureQuote":"Every man according as he purposeth in his heart, so let him give; not grudgingly, or of necessity: for God loveth a cheerful giver.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"God wants willing, thoughtful, love-shaped giving.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Deuteronomy 16:17","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"Every man shall give as he is able, according to the blessing of the LORD thy God which he hath given thee.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Offerings are faithful responses to God's blessing, not competitions for attention.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"2 Corinthians 9:6","quote":"He which soweth sparingly shall reap also sparingly; and he which soweth bountifully shall reap also bountifully."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Matthew 6:19-21","quote":"Lay not up for yourselves treasures upon earth.\n\nFor where your treasure is, there will your heart be also."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Offerings are given willingly\n\nPaul says giving should not be grudging or forced.\n\nThat matters.\n\nGod is not looking for generosity that comes from manipulation, guilt, or public pressure.\n\nHe wants the heart.\n\nA true offering is willing, thoughtful, and love-shaped.","2. Offerings are connected to ability\n\nDeuteronomy says every person should give as they are able, according to the blessing God has given.\n\nThis means offerings are not about comparison.\n\nOne person may give more. Another may give less.\n\nThe issue is not showing off.\n\nThe issue is faithful response to God's blessing.","3. Generosity is like sowing\n\nPaul compares giving to sowing seed.\n\nA person who sows sparingly reaps sparingly. A person who sows generously reaps generously.\n\nThis is not a selfish formula for getting rich.\n\nIt is a kingdom principle.\n\nGenerosity produces blessing, shapes the heart, supports God's work, and blesses people.","4. Giving reveals the heart\n\nJesus said where your treasure is, there your heart will be also.\n\nMoney follows worship.\n\nWhat we invest in reveals what we value.\n\nOfferings help train the heart away from selfish treasure and toward God's kingdom."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Cheerful","lemma":"ἱλαρός","transliteration":"hilaros","meaning":"Willing, glad, ready, and free from resentment.","strongs":"G2431","reference":"2 Corinthians 9:7","note":"God loves a cheerful giver because cheerful giving reflects trust.\n\nIt shows that the heart is not giving under fear or pressure, but in response to God's generosity and grace."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"Offerings reveal the difference between religious pressure and gospel generosity.\n\nPressure says:\n\n\"You have to give so God will approve of you.\"\n\nGrace says:\n\n\"God has already given to you, so now you are free to give with joy.\"\n\nOfferings are not meant to be careless or random.\n\nThey should be thoughtful.\n\nThey should be intentional.\n\nThey should come from a heart that recognizes God's blessing and wants to participate in His work.\n\nThis is why cheerful giving matters.\n\nGod is not only looking at the amount.\n\nHe is forming the heart behind the gift."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"Two kinds of giving","accent":"amber","description":"God forms the giver, not just the gift.","payload":{"variant":"comparison","leftTitle":"Forced giving","rightTitle":"Heart giving","leftItems":["pressure","guilt","comparison","resentment"],"rightItems":["gratitude","trust","joy","love"]},"footnote":"Offerings flow from grace, not religious pressure."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Giving From the Heart","references":[{"reference":"Deuteronomy 16:17"},{"reference":"2 Corinthians 9:6-10"},{"reference":"Matthew 6:19-21"},{"reference":"Acts 20:35"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Offerings are willing gifts that flow from gratitude, trust, and love for God's kingdom."}],"reflectPrompt":"When you give, what usually motivates you: gratitude, guilt, pressure, love, fear, or trust?\n\nBe honest. God is not only interested in the gift itself. He wants to heal and form the heart behind the giving.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, plan one offering from the heart.\n\nDo not make it random.\n\nPray first.\n\nAsk:\n\n\"Lord, what have You blessed me with, and how can I respond with generosity?\"\n\nThen give in a way that is willing and thoughtful.\n\nIt may be financial.\n\nIt may be practical.\n\nIt may be serving someone in need.\n\nIt may be supporting ministry.\n\nThe goal is not to impress anyone.\n\nThe goal is to let gratitude become action.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for every blessing You have given me. Teach me to give willingly, thoughtfully, and cheerfully. Free me from guilt-driven giving and selfish holding. Shape my heart through generosity, and help my resources reflect love for You and compassion for others. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"Offerings flow from the heart. God invites cheerful, willing generosity that reflects gratitude, trust, and love.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"tltg-l6-q1","prompt":"According to 2 Corinthians 9:7, what kind of giver does God love?","options":["A cheerful giver","A resentful giver","A forced giver","A boastful giver"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Paul says God loves a cheerful giver, meaning giving should come willingly from the heart."},{"id":"tltg-l6-q2","prompt":"According to Deuteronomy 16:17, how should each person give?","options":["As they are able, according to God's blessing","Only to impress others","Exactly the same amount as everyone else","With no thought or gratitude"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Deuteronomy teaches that giving should be according to ability and the blessing God has given."},{"id":"tltg-l6-q3","prompt":"What does 2 Corinthians 9:6 compare generosity to?","options":["Sowing seed","Hiding treasure","Losing faith","Buying salvation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Paul compares giving to sowing. Generous sowing leads to generous reaping."},{"id":"tltg-l6-q4","prompt":"According to Matthew 6:21, what follows our treasure?","options":["Our heart","Our fear only","Our confusion","Our pride alone"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus said where your treasure is, there your heart will be also."},{"id":"tltg-l6-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What is one way you can let gratitude become action this week?","explanation":"Offerings are not only about money. They are willing responses of love, trust, and gratitude toward God and others."}]}}
$tltg_l6_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'offerings-from-the-heart'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007c';


-- The Life That Gives — Lesson 7: Giving as Love in Action (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-00000000090e';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $tltg_l7_reader$
{"openingThought":"Love that never gives is only a theory.\n\nGod's love moved.\n\nGod gave life.\nGod gave breath.\nGod gave provision.\nGod gave His Son.\n\nWhen His love reaches us, it begins to move through us.\n\nGiving is not only about money.\n\nIt is about a life shaped by God's generosity.\n\nTithes, offerings, service, mercy, time, encouragement, and practical help all become ways love takes action.\n\nThe life that gives is not trying to impress God.\n\nIt is reflecting the God who gave first.","scriptureRef":"1 John 3:16-18","scriptureQuote":"Hereby perceive we the love of God, because he laid down his life for us.\n\nAnd we ought to lay down our lives for the brethren.\n\nMy little children, let us not love in word, neither in tongue; but in deed and in truth.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"God's love is seen in sacrifice, and ours should become visible in action too.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Acts 20:35","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"It is more blessed to give than to receive.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Giving blesses the giver because it aligns us with God's heart.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Matthew 23:23","quote":"Ye pay tithe of mint and anise and cummin, and have omitted the weightier matters of the law, judgment, mercy, and faith."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Luke 11:42","quote":"These ought ye to have done, and not to leave the other undone."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"2 Corinthians 9:8","quote":"And God is able to make all grace abound toward you."}],"teachingPoints":["1. God's love becomes visible through action\n\nJohn says we understand love because Jesus laid down His life for us.\n\nThen he calls believers to love not only in word, but in deed and truth.\n\nReal love moves beyond speech.\n\nIt becomes practical.\n\nIt gives.\nIt serves.\nIt notices needs.\nIt responds with compassion.","2. Giving includes more than money\n\nTithe and offerings matter, but generosity is bigger than financial giving.\n\nA giving life includes time, attention, encouragement, hospitality, mercy, forgiveness, service, and practical help.\n\nMoney is one part of stewardship.\n\nBut the whole life belongs to God.","3. God cares about both giving and mercy\n\nJesus corrected leaders who were careful about tithe but neglected judgment, mercy, and faith.\n\nHe did not say tithe was meaningless.\n\nHe said they should not leave mercy undone.\n\nThis matters.\n\nBiblical giving cannot be separated from love, justice, compassion, and faithfulness.\n\nGod wants the gift and the heart.","4. Grace supplies what generosity requires\n\nSecond Corinthians says God is able to make all grace abound toward us.\n\nGenerosity can feel risky.\n\nWe fear not having enough.\nWe fear being overlooked.\nWe fear losing control.\n\nBut grace teaches us that God is able to supply what He calls us to share.\n\nWe give because we trust the Giver."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Deed","lemma":"ἔργον","transliteration":"ergon","meaning":"An action, work, or something actually done.","strongs":"G2041","reference":"1 John 3:18","note":"First John says love should be shown in deed and truth.\n\nThis means biblical love is not only spoken.\n\nIt becomes visible through practical action, sacrifice, generosity, and faithful care for others."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The Life That Gives has shown one clear movement:\n\nGod gives first.\n\nHis grace creates a circle of blessing.\n\nJesus redeems us from taking and self-centeredness.\n\nEverything belongs to God.\n\nTithe teaches trust.\n\nOfferings express willing gratitude.\n\nNow the whole life becomes love in action.\n\nThis is the point.\n\nGod is not merely trying to get money from His people.\n\nHe is forming generous people.\n\nPeople who trust Him.\nPeople who steward faithfully.\nPeople who give cheerfully.\nPeople who care about mercy.\nPeople who bless others because they have been blessed.\n\nGenerosity is not a side topic.\n\nIt is a sign that God's love is reshaping the heart."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"The Life That Gives","accent":"amber","description":"Generosity is love made visible.","payload":{"variant":"sequence","steps":[{"label":"God gives first"},{"label":"We receive with gratitude"},{"label":"Grace turns us outward"},{"label":"We steward what belongs to God"},{"label":"We return tithe"},{"label":"We give offerings"},{"label":"Love becomes action"}]},"footnote":"God forms generous people who trust, steward, give, and love in deed and truth."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Love Made Visible","references":[{"reference":"1 John 3:16-18"},{"reference":"Acts 20:35"},{"reference":"Matthew 23:23"},{"reference":"2 Corinthians 9:8"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: God's love becomes visible through generosity, mercy, service, stewardship, and practical care for others."}],"reflectPrompt":"Where is God inviting you to make love more visible through giving?\n\nThink beyond money. Consider your time, attention, encouragement, mercy, service, resources, skills, and willingness to help someone in need.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, practice giving as love in action.\n\nChoose one concrete act of generosity.\n\nNot vague good intentions.\n\nSomething real.\n\nGive an offering.\nHelp someone quietly.\nEncourage someone who feels forgotten.\nShare a resource.\nServe without needing credit.\nReturn tithe with renewed trust.\nMake room in your schedule for someone else's need.\n\nBefore you do it, pray:\n\n\"Lord, let this reflect Your love, not my ego.\"\n\nThen give with humility.\n\nThe goal is not to be seen as generous.\n\nThe goal is to let God's generosity move through you.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for giving first. Thank You for the gift of Jesus and for every blessing You have entrusted to me. Make my life generous in deed and in truth. Teach me to return tithe with trust, give offerings with joy, serve with humility, and love people in practical ways. Let my life reflect Your giving heart. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"You have completed The Life That Gives. God gives first, and His grace forms generous people who return, share, serve, and love in action.","seriesCompleteScreen":{"title":"Study Complete","message":"You have completed The Life That Gives. God gives first, and His grace forms generous people who return, share, serve, and love in action.","primaryCta":{"label":"Back to Foundations Path","href":"/paths/foundations"},"secondaryCta":{"label":"Review this study","href":"/study-library/the-life-that-gives"}},"lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"tltg-l7-q1","prompt":"According to 1 John 3:16, how do we perceive the love of God?","options":["Jesus laid down His life for us","God ignored human need","Love never requires action","Giving is unnecessary"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"First John says we perceive the love of God because Jesus laid down His life for us."},{"id":"tltg-l7-q2","prompt":"According to 1 John 3:18, how should we love?","options":["In deed and in truth","In words only","In appearance only","With no practical action"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"John teaches that love should not remain only in word or tongue, but should be shown in deed and truth."},{"id":"tltg-l7-q3","prompt":"According to Acts 20:35, what did Jesus say is more blessed?","options":["To give than to receive","To hoard than to share","To take than to give","To ignore the needy"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus said it is more blessed to give than to receive."},{"id":"tltg-l7-q4","prompt":"What did Jesus say was neglected in Matthew 23:23?","options":["Judgment, mercy, and faith","Greed, pride, and selfishness","Wealth, power, and status","Appearance, applause, and control"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus corrected those who were careful with tithe but neglected the weightier matters of the law: judgment, mercy, and faith."},{"id":"tltg-l7-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What is one practical way you can love in deed and truth this week?","explanation":"Biblical love becomes visible through action. Giving, serving, mercy, and practical care show that God's love is shaping the heart."}]}}
$tltg_l7_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'giving-as-love-in-action'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007c';


-- Designed to Thrive — Lesson 1: Choices Have Consequences (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-00000000090f';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $dtt_l1_reader$
{"openingThought":"God designed life to work by principles.\n\nChoices matter.\n\nWhat we think, eat, drink, watch, say, practice, avoid, and repeat shapes real outcomes in our bodies, minds, relationships, and spiritual lives.\n\nThis is not because God is waiting to punish every mistake.\n\nIt is because love requires real freedom, and real freedom means our choices have real consequences.\n\nThe Bible calls this wisdom.\n\nWisdom is learning how life works under God's design and choosing the path that leads to life.","scriptureRef":"Galatians 6:7","scriptureQuote":"Be not deceived; God is not mocked: for whatsoever a man soweth, that shall he also reap.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Choices are seeds.\n\nOver time, what we sow begins to grow.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Proverbs 26:2","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"The curse causeless shall not come.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Life operates by real cause and effect, not random fate.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Proverbs 8:34-36","quote":"Blessed is the man that heareth me, watching daily at my gates, waiting at the posts of my doors.\n\nFor whoso findeth me findeth life, and shall obtain favour of the LORD.\n\nBut he that sinneth against me wrongeth his own soul: all they that hate me love death."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Deuteronomy 30:19","quote":"I have set before you life and death, blessing and cursing: therefore choose life."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Life operates by cause and effect\n\nGalatians says we reap what we sow.\n\nThis means actions produce outcomes.\n\nChoices are seeds.\n\nHabits are seeds.\n\nThoughts are seeds.\n\nAppetites are seeds.\n\nRelationships are seeds.\n\nOver time, what we sow begins to grow.\n\nGod is not arbitrary. He designed life with moral, spiritual, mental, relational, and physical cause and effect.","2. Wisdom understands how life works\n\nProverbs says the person who finds wisdom finds life.\n\nWisdom is not just knowing facts.\n\nWisdom is skill for living.\n\nIt means learning the principles God built into reality and making choices that align with life, health, peace, and love.\n\nA wise person asks:\n\n\"What will this produce if I keep choosing it?\"","3. Sin wounds the person who practices it\n\nProverbs says, \"He that sinneth against me wrongeth his own soul.\"\n\nThat is a serious line.\n\nSin is not just a broken rule.\n\nIt is a self-damaging path.\n\nChoices that move against God's design eventually wound the mind, body, relationships, conscience, and soul.\n\nGod warns us because He loves us.","4. God invites us to choose life\n\nDeuteronomy says, \"Choose life.\"\n\nGod does not force love, wisdom, or obedience.\n\nHe invites.\n\nHe teaches.\n\nHe warns.\n\nHe empowers.\n\nBecause God loves us, He cares about the direction our choices are taking us.\n\nHis commands are not random restrictions.\n\nThey are loving instructions for life."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Wisdom","lemma":"חָכְמָה","transliteration":"chokmah","meaning":"Skill for living, discernment, and the ability to understand life's principles and make good choices.","strongs":"H2451","reference":"Proverbs 8:35","note":"Biblical wisdom is not merely intelligence.\n\nIt is learning how life works under God's design and choosing what leads to life, blessing, health, and wholeness."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"scripture_connection","body":"Some people think God's blessings and consequences work like random lightning bolts.\n\nBe good, get a prize.\n\nMess up, get punished.\n\nBut Scripture presents something deeper.\n\nGod created a world where choices matter.\n\nA loving choice tends to produce relational health.\n\nA selfish choice tends to produce relational damage.\n\nA wise habit tends to produce strength.\n\nA destructive habit tends to produce weakness.\n\nA clear mind tends to support better decisions.\n\nA clouded mind tends to create confusion.\n\nThis is not about shame.\n\nIt is about reality.\n\nGod loves us too much to pretend our choices do not matter.\n\nHe teaches us wisdom because He wants us to live."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"Cause and effect","accent":"sage","description":"Choices are seeds. Over time, what we sow begins to grow.","payload":{"variant":"sequence","steps":[{"label":"Sow","subtitle":"thoughts, habits, choices, relationships"},{"label":"Grow","subtitle":"character, health, peace, damage, strength, weakness"},{"label":"Reap","subtitle":"life-giving outcomes or destructive outcomes"}]},"footnote":"God designed life with moral, spiritual, mental, relational, and physical cause and effect."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Choices Have Consequences","references":[{"reference":"Proverbs 26:2"},{"reference":"Galatians 6:7"},{"reference":"Proverbs 8:34-36"},{"reference":"Deuteronomy 30:19"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: God designed life with cause and effect. Wisdom means learning His principles and choosing the path that leads to life."}],"reflectPrompt":"What is one choice or habit in your life that is already producing visible fruit?\n\nThink about your health, thoughts, sleep, eating, relationships, spiritual life, screen time, emotions, or daily routines.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, identify one seed you are sowing.\n\nAsk:\n\n\"What will this produce if I keep choosing it?\"\n\nThen choose one small adjustment toward life.\n\nDrink more water.\nSleep earlier.\nPray before reacting.\nTake a walk.\nTell the truth.\nEat something nourishing.\nTurn off what is feeding anxiety.\nMake peace with someone.\nChoose Scripture over scrolling.\n\nDo not try to fix everything at once.\n\nWisdom often begins with one honest choice.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for designing life with wisdom and love. Help me see that my choices matter without falling into shame or fear. Teach me to understand cause and effect in my body, mind, relationships, and spiritual life. Give me wisdom to choose what leads to life, health, peace, and deeper trust in You. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"Choices are seeds. God designed life with cause and effect, and wisdom teaches us to choose what leads to life, health, and wholeness.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"dtt-l1-q1","prompt":"According to Galatians 6:7, what will a person reap?","options":["Whatever they sow","Whatever others choose for them","Nothing at all","Only random outcomes"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Galatians teaches the principle of cause and effect: whatever a person sows, they will also reap."},{"id":"dtt-l1-q2","prompt":"According to Proverbs 8:35, what does the person who finds wisdom find?","options":["Life","Confusion","Pride","Self-destruction"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Proverbs says whoever finds wisdom finds life and obtains favor from the Lord."},{"id":"dtt-l1-q3","prompt":"According to Proverbs 8:36, what happens when a person sins against wisdom?","options":["They wrong their own soul","They become healthier automatically","They avoid all consequences","They become free from responsibility"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Proverbs says the person who sins against wisdom wrongs his own soul, showing that foolish choices are self-damaging."},{"id":"dtt-l1-q4","prompt":"According to Deuteronomy 30:19, what does God invite His people to choose?","options":["Life","Death","Confusion","Bondage"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"God says, \"Choose life,\" revealing His desire for human flourishing and blessing."},{"id":"dtt-l1-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What is one small life-giving choice God may be inviting you to make this week?","explanation":"Wisdom begins with honest choices that align with God's design for life, health, peace, and wholeness."}]}}
$dtt_l1_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'choices-have-consequences'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007d';


-- Designed to Thrive — Lesson 2: Wisdom for Whole Life (guided reader JSON).
