where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000910';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $dtt_l2_reader$
{"openingThought":"Wisdom is more than knowing right answers.\n\nWisdom is knowing how life works.\n\nIt helps us see the connection between choices and outcomes, habits and health, thoughts and peace, appetites and consequences.\n\nGod does not give wisdom to limit our lives.\n\nHe gives wisdom to protect and bless our lives.\n\nBecause God loves us, He teaches us how to live in harmony with the way He designed us.\n\nHealth is not only about the body.\n\nIt involves the whole person.\n\nThe mind.\nThe heart.\nThe body.\nThe spirit.\n\nGod wants wisdom to shape all of it.","scriptureRef":"Proverbs 3:5-8","scriptureQuote":"Trust in the LORD with all thine heart; and lean not unto thine own understanding.\n\nIn all thy ways acknowledge him, and he shall direct thy paths.\n\nBe not wise in thine own eyes: fear the LORD, and depart from evil.\n\nIt shall be health to thy navel, and marrow to thy bones.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Wisdom begins with trust and reaches into every area of life.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Proverbs 4:20-23","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"My son, attend to my words; incline thine ear unto my sayings.\n\nFor they are life unto those that find them, and health to all their flesh.\n\nKeep thy heart with all diligence; for out of it are the issues of life.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"God's Word guards the inner life from which the outer life flows.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"James 1:5","quote":"If any of you lack wisdom, let him ask of God, that giveth to all men liberally."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Psalm 119:105","quote":"Thy word is a lamp unto my feet, and a light unto my path."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Wisdom begins with trust\n\nProverbs says to trust in the Lord with all your heart and not lean on your own understanding.\n\nThat is where wisdom begins.\n\nWe do not see everything clearly on our own.\n\nOur desires can mislead us.\n\nOur culture can normalize unhealthy patterns.\n\nOur emotions can make destructive choices feel reasonable.\n\nWisdom begins when we trust God more than our own limited perspective.","2. God's wisdom directs the path\n\nProverbs says that when we acknowledge God in all our ways, He directs our paths.\n\nGod cares about \"all\" our ways.\n\nNot just church.\nNot just prayer.\nNot just doctrine.\n\nHe cares about how we eat, rest, think, work, speak, spend, relate, and respond.\n\nWisdom brings God into every area of life.","3. God's Word brings life and health\n\nProverbs says God's words are life to those who find them and health to all their flesh.\n\nThis does not mean Scripture replaces wise medical care.\n\nIt means God's instruction leads us toward life.\n\nHis Word teaches principles that protect the mind, guide the heart, and shape healthier choices.","4. The heart must be guarded\n\nProverbs says to keep the heart with all diligence because the issues of life flow from it.\n\nThe heart is the inner life: thoughts, motives, desires, emotions, and loyalties.\n\nHealth is not only physical.\n\nA bitter heart, anxious mind, uncontrolled appetite, or restless spirit can damage the whole person.\n\nWisdom guards the inner life because the outer life flows from it."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Heart","lemma":"לֵב","transliteration":"leb","meaning":"The inner life of a person, including thoughts, motives, desires, emotions, and will.","strongs":"H3820","reference":"Proverbs 4:23","note":"In Scripture, the heart is not merely emotion.\n\nIt is the center of the person.\n\nGod's wisdom guards the heart because what happens inside us shapes what happens through us."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"scripture_connection","body":"Wisdom is whole-life discipleship.\n\nIt refuses to divide life into \"spiritual\" and \"ordinary\" categories.\n\nThe way we sleep matters.\nThe way we eat matters.\nThe way we handle stress matters.\nThe way we speak matters.\nThe way we think matters.\nThe way we treat the body matters.\nThe way we respond to God matters.\n\nNot because we are trying to earn salvation.\n\nBecause God loves the whole person.\n\nThe Bible connects wisdom with health because wise living aligns us with God's design.\n\nFoolishness ignores cause and effect.\n\nWisdom listens, learns, and adjusts."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"wholePersonHealthPattern","title":"Wisdom for the Whole Person","caption":"God's wisdom touches the whole life."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Wisdom and Health","references":[{"reference":"Proverbs 3:5-8"},{"reference":"Proverbs 4:20-23"},{"reference":"James 1:5"},{"reference":"Psalm 119:105"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Wisdom is skill for whole-life living. God gives wisdom to guide the heart, mind, body, and spirit toward life."}],"reflectPrompt":"Where are you leaning on your own understanding instead of asking God for wisdom?\n\nThink about your health choices, stress patterns, relationships, habits, schedule, thoughts, spiritual life, or emotional reactions.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, ask God for wisdom in one specific area of your life.\n\nDo not pray vaguely.\n\nName the area.\n\n\"Lord, give me wisdom with my sleep.\"\n\n\"Lord, give me wisdom with my eating.\"\n\n\"Lord, give me wisdom with my emotions.\"\n\n\"Lord, give me wisdom with my schedule.\"\n\n\"Lord, give me wisdom with this relationship.\"\n\nThen take one wise step.\n\nNot ten.\n\nOne.\n\nWisdom grows through repeated obedience to light God has already given.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for caring about my whole life. I confess that I often lean on my own understanding and ignore the wisdom You give. Teach me to trust You with my body, mind, heart, and spirit. Give me practical wisdom for the choices in front of me, and help me walk in the path that leads to life, health, and peace. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"Wisdom is skill for whole-life living. God gives wisdom to guide the heart, mind, body, and spirit toward life and wholeness.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"dtt-l2-q1","prompt":"According to Proverbs 3:5, what should we do with all our heart?","options":["Trust in the Lord","Trust only in ourselves","Ignore wisdom","Follow every feeling"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Proverbs says to trust in the Lord with all your heart and not lean on your own understanding."},{"id":"dtt-l2-q2","prompt":"According to Proverbs 3:6, what happens when we acknowledge God in all our ways?","options":["He directs our paths","He ignores our choices","He removes all responsibility","He makes wisdom unnecessary"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Proverbs says that when we acknowledge God in all our ways, He will direct our paths."},{"id":"dtt-l2-q3","prompt":"According to Proverbs 4:22, what are God's words to those who find them?","options":["Life and health","Confusion and fear","Empty ideas","A burden without purpose"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Proverbs says God's words are life to those who find them and health to all their flesh."},{"id":"dtt-l2-q4","prompt":"According to James 1:5, what should we do if we lack wisdom?","options":["Ask God","Pretend we already know everything","Ignore the need","Follow the loudest voice"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"James says that if anyone lacks wisdom, they should ask God, who gives generously."},{"id":"dtt-l2-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What is one area where you need God's wisdom for healthier living?","explanation":"God's wisdom is practical. It guides the whole person toward life, health, peace, and wholeness."}]}}
$dtt_l2_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'wisdom-for-whole-life'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007d';


-- Designed to Thrive — Lesson 3: Saved Means Made Whole (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000911';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $dtt_l3_reader$
{"openingThought":"Many people think salvation only means going to heaven one day.\n\nThat is true, but it is not the whole picture.\n\nIn Scripture, salvation is bigger than future escape.\n\nIt includes rescue, healing, restoration, freedom, and wholeness.\n\nJesus did not come only to forgive our record.\n\nHe came to restore our lives.\n\nHe cares about the whole person.\n\nThe heart.\nThe mind.\nThe body.\nThe spirit.\nThe way we live.\nThe way we think.\nThe way we treat what He has entrusted to us.\n\nGod's salvation reaches deeper than guilt.\n\nIt reaches into the places where sin has broken us and begins making us whole.","scriptureRef":"John 10:10","scriptureQuote":"I am come that they might have life, and that they might have it more abundantly.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Jesus came for life restored under God's love, not life without hardship.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"3 John 1:2","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"Beloved, I wish above all things that thou mayest prosper and be in health, even as thy soul prospereth.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"God cares about spiritual life and physical well-being together.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Romans 12:1-2","quote":"Present your bodies a living sacrifice, holy, acceptable unto God, which is your reasonable service.\n\nAnd be not conformed to this world: but be ye transformed by the renewing of your mind."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"1 Thessalonians 5:23","quote":"And the very God of peace sanctify you wholly.\n\nAnd I pray God your whole spirit and soul and body be preserved blameless unto the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Jesus came to give abundant life\n\nJesus said He came so we might have life more abundantly.\n\nThat does not mean life without hardship.\n\nIt means life restored under God's love and purpose.\n\nAbundant life is not shallow prosperity.\n\nIt is the life God designed us to live: forgiven, restored, surrendered, wise, and connected to Him.","2. God cares about our health\n\nThird John expresses a desire for health and soul prosperity.\n\nThis shows that spiritual life and physical well-being are not enemies.\n\nGod cares about the whole person.\n\nHe is not interested in saving the \"spiritual\" part of us while ignoring the body, mind, emotions, and daily life.","3. Salvation renews the mind\n\nRomans says we are transformed by the renewing of the mind.\n\nThis means God's work in us includes how we think.\n\nOur thoughts affect choices.\nOur choices affect habits.\nOur habits affect health.\nOur health affects service, clarity, and emotional resilience.\n\nGod's truth renews the mind so the whole life can be transformed.","4. God wants whole-person restoration\n\nFirst Thessalonians speaks of spirit, soul, and body.\n\nGod's saving work is not fragmented.\n\nHe wants to sanctify us wholly.\n\nThis does not mean perfect health in a broken world.\n\nIt means every part of our life belongs to God and can be brought under His healing, wisdom, and grace."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Whole","lemma":"ὁλοτελής","transliteration":"holoteles","meaning":"Complete, restored, integrated, and not divided into disconnected parts.","strongs":"G3651","reference":"1 Thessalonians 5:23","note":"Biblical wholeness means God cares about the entire person.\n\nSalvation includes forgiveness, but it also reaches into the body, mind, heart, habits, relationships, and spiritual life.\n\nGod restores people from the inside out."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The Bible does not treat human beings like disconnected pieces.\n\nYou are not a soul trapped inside a disposable body.\n\nYou are a whole person.\n\nWhat affects the body can affect the mind.\n\nWhat affects the mind can affect the emotions.\n\nWhat affects the heart can affect the body.\n\nWhat affects the spirit can shape the whole life.\n\nThis is why health matters in discipleship.\n\nNot because healthy habits save us.\n\nJesus saves.\n\nBut healthy choices can help us live with greater clarity, energy, peace, and usefulness in God's hands.\n\nHealth is stewardship.\n\nWholeness is God's desire.\n\nAnd salvation is God's work of restoring what sin has damaged."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"wholePersonHealthPattern","title":"Saved Means Made Whole","caption":"God's salvation reaches the whole person.","highlightedIndices":[3]},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Made Whole","references":[{"reference":"John 10:10"},{"reference":"3 John 1:2"},{"reference":"Romans 12:1-2"},{"reference":"1 Thessalonians 5:23"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Salvation is more than future hope. Jesus came to restore the whole person and lead us into abundant life."}],"reflectPrompt":"What part of your life most needs God's restoring work right now?\n\nThink about your body, thoughts, emotions, habits, relationships, spiritual life, or the way you respond to stress.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, invite God into one area of your whole life.\n\nDo not separate it from your faith.\n\nIf it is sleep, bring it to God.\n\nIf it is anxiety, bring it to God.\n\nIf it is eating, bring it to God.\n\nIf it is bitterness, bring it to God.\n\nIf it is screen time, bring it to God.\n\nIf it is neglecting your body, bring it to God.\n\nAsk:\n\n\"Lord, what would restoration look like here?\"\n\nThen take one small step toward wholeness.\n\nNot perfection.\n\nOne faithful step.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You that salvation is not shallow. Thank You for caring about my whole life: body, mind, heart, and spirit. Help me receive the abundant life Jesus came to give. Renew my mind, heal what is broken, and teach me to steward my life with wisdom and grace. Make me whole in You. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"Salvation is more than future hope. Jesus came to bring abundant life and restore the whole person: body, mind, heart, and spirit.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"dtt-l3-q1","prompt":"According to John 10:10, why did Jesus come?","options":["That we might have life more abundantly","That we might live without purpose","That health would never matter","That wisdom would be unnecessary"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus said He came so that we might have life and have it more abundantly."},{"id":"dtt-l3-q2","prompt":"According to 3 John 1:2, what does John desire for the believer?","options":["That they prosper and be in health","That they ignore the body","That they reject wisdom","That they live without restoration"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"John expresses a desire for health and soul prosperity, showing concern for whole-person well-being."},{"id":"dtt-l3-q3","prompt":"According to Romans 12:2, how are we transformed?","options":["By the renewing of the mind","By ignoring our thoughts","By copying the world","By avoiding God's wisdom"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Romans says we are transformed by the renewing of the mind."},{"id":"dtt-l3-q4","prompt":"According to 1 Thessalonians 5:23, what does God want to sanctify?","options":["The whole person","Only outward appearance","Only public behavior","Nothing in the present life"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Paul prays that God would sanctify believers wholly: spirit, soul, and body."},{"id":"dtt-l3-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What is one area where you want Jesus to bring deeper wholeness?","explanation":"Jesus came to restore the whole person. God's healing work reaches the body, mind, heart, and spirit."}]}}
$dtt_l3_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'saved-means-made-whole'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007d';


-- Designed to Thrive — Lesson 4: Your Body Matters to God (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000912';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $dtt_l4_reader$
{"openingThought":"Your body is not an accident.\n\nIt is not disposable.\n\nIt is not separate from your spiritual life.\n\nGod made you as a whole person, and your body is part of the life He entrusted to you.\n\nThat means health is not about vanity.\n\nIt is not about worshiping appearance.\n\nIt is not about trying to earn salvation by perfect habits.\n\nHealth is stewardship.\n\nYour body matters because God made it, Jesus redeemed it, and the Spirit desires to dwell in you.\n\nThe question is not, \"How do I become perfect?\"\n\nThe better question is:\n\n\"How can I honor God with the body and life He has given me?\"","scriptureRef":"1 Corinthians 6:19-20","scriptureQuote":"What? know ye not that your body is the temple of the Holy Ghost which is in you, which ye have of God?\n\nAnd ye are not your own. For ye are bought with a price: therefore glorify God in your body, and in your spirit, which are God's.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"The body has sacred value because it belongs to God.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"1 Corinthians 10:31","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"Whether therefore ye eat, or drink, or whatsoever ye do, do all to the glory of God.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Faith reaches into everyday choices, not only dramatic spiritual moments.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Romans 12:1","quote":"Present your bodies a living sacrifice, holy, acceptable unto God, which is your reasonable service."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Genesis 1:27","quote":"So God created man in his own image."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Your body belongs to God\n\nPaul says, \"Ye are not your own.\"\n\nThat is not meant to make us feel trapped.\n\nIt is meant to remind us that our lives have sacred value.\n\nWe belong to the God who created us and redeemed us.\n\nThe body is not meaningless.\n\nIt belongs to God.","2. The body is a temple\n\nPaul calls the body the temple of the Holy Spirit.\n\nA temple is a place set apart for God's presence.\n\nThat means how we treat the body matters.\n\nFood, rest, movement, sexual purity, substances, stress, and habits are not disconnected from discipleship.\n\nGod cares about the whole person.","3. Everyday choices can glorify God\n\nFirst Corinthians says even eating and drinking can be done to God's glory.\n\nThat brings faith into daily life.\n\nHealth is not limited to dramatic spiritual moments.\n\nIt shows up in ordinary choices.\n\nWhat we consume.\nHow we rest.\nHow we move.\nHow we manage appetite.\nHow we care for what God entrusted to us.","4. Stewardship is not perfectionism\n\nRomans calls us to present our bodies to God.\n\nThis is not a call to shame, obsession, or comparison.\n\nSome people face sickness, disability, trauma, limitations, or circumstances beyond their control.\n\nGod is not asking for image-driven perfection.\n\nHe is inviting faithful stewardship.\n\nWe honor Him with the measure of light, strength, ability, and opportunity we have."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Temple","lemma":"ναός","transliteration":"naos","meaning":"A sacred place set apart for God's presence.","strongs":"G3485","reference":"1 Corinthians 6:19","note":"Calling the body a temple gives the body spiritual dignity.\n\nIt means the body is not something to worship or neglect.\n\nIt is something to steward because it belongs to God and is meant to glorify Him."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"scripture_connection","body":"The Bible does not teach body worship.\n\nIt teaches body stewardship.\n\nThere is a difference.\n\nBody worship says:\n\n\"My appearance is my identity.\"\n\nBody neglect says:\n\n\"My body does not matter.\"\n\nBiblical stewardship says:\n\n\"My body belongs to God, and I want to care for it faithfully.\"\n\nThat balance matters.\n\nHealth should never become pride.\n\nIt should never become legalism.\n\nIt should never become shame.\n\nBut it also should not be ignored.\n\nIf the body is God's temple, then caring for it is one way we respond to His love."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"wholePersonHealthPattern","title":"Your Body Matters to God","caption":"The body is part of whole-person stewardship.","highlightedIndices":[0],"areas":[{"area":"Body","items":["nutrition","movement","rest","temperance"]},{"area":"Mind","items":["clarity","focus","thoughts","wisdom"]},{"area":"Heart","items":["peace","relationships","emotions","healing"]},{"area":"Spirit","items":["worship","surrender","trust","purpose"]}]},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Glorify God in Your Body","references":[{"reference":"Genesis 1:27"},{"reference":"Romans 12:1"},{"reference":"1 Corinthians 6:19-20"},{"reference":"1 Corinthians 10:31"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Your body matters to God because He created it, redeemed it, and calls you to glorify Him through faithful stewardship."}],"reflectPrompt":"Do you tend to neglect your body, obsess over your body, or steward your body?\n\nThink honestly about your habits, motives, routines, appetite, rest, exercise, appearance, and how your view of God shapes the way you care for yourself.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, choose one simple way to honor God with your body.\n\nDo not try to overhaul everything at once.\n\nChoose one faithful step.\n\nDrink more water.\nGo for a walk.\nSleep earlier.\nEat one nourishing meal.\nStretch.\nAvoid something that clouds your mind.\nRest without guilt.\nPray before eating.\nStop speaking harshly about your body.\n\nThen say:\n\n\"Lord, this body belongs to You. Help me steward it with gratitude.\"\n\nThe goal is not perfection.\n\nThe goal is worshipful care.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for creating and redeeming me as a whole person. Help me see my body as something entrusted to me, not something to worship or neglect. Teach me to glorify You in what I eat, drink, do, and choose. Free me from shame, pride, and carelessness. Give me wisdom to steward my body with gratitude and grace. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"Your body matters to God. It is part of the life He created, redeemed, and entrusted to you for faithful stewardship.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"dtt-l4-q1","prompt":"According to 1 Corinthians 6:19, what is the body called?","options":["The temple of the Holy Ghost","Something meaningless","A tool for selfishness only","A burden God ignores"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Paul says the body is the temple of the Holy Ghost, showing that the body has sacred value."},{"id":"dtt-l4-q2","prompt":"According to 1 Corinthians 6:20, why should believers glorify God in their body?","options":["They were bought with a price","Their body belongs only to culture","Appearance saves them","Health earns salvation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Paul says believers are bought with a price, therefore they should glorify God in body and spirit."},{"id":"dtt-l4-q3","prompt":"According to 1 Corinthians 10:31, what should be done to the glory of God?","options":["Eating, drinking, and whatever we do","Only public worship services","Nothing physical","Only preaching"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Paul says even eating and drinking should be done to the glory of God."},{"id":"dtt-l4-q4","prompt":"What does this lesson teach about health stewardship?","options":["It is a response to God's love, not a way to earn salvation","It is about pride and appearance","It means God loves healthy people more","It has nothing to do with faith"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Health stewardship honors God with the life He has entrusted to us. It is not a way to earn salvation."},{"id":"dtt-l4-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What is one practical way you can glorify God in your body this week?","explanation":"The body belongs to God. Faithful stewardship begins with one wise, worshipful choice."}]}}
$dtt_l4_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'your-body-matters-to-god'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007d';


-- Designed to Thrive — Lesson 5: The Original Design for Food (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000913';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $dtt_l5_reader$
{"openingThought":"Food is not only fuel.\n\nIt is part of God's design for life.\n\nBefore sin entered the world, before sickness damaged the body, and before appetite became distorted, God gave humanity food from the earth.\n\nThe first diet was simple, life-giving, and connected to creation.\n\nThat matters.\n\nThe Bible does not present eating as a way to earn salvation. Jesus saves us by grace.\n\nBut Scripture does invite us to think wisely about what we put into the body God has entrusted to us.\n\nFood can either support life or work against it.\n\nGod's original design points us toward wisdom, gratitude, and care.","scriptureRef":"Genesis 1:29","scriptureQuote":"And God said, Behold, I have given you every herb bearing seed, which is upon the face of all the earth, and every tree, in the which is the fruit of a tree yielding seed.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"God's first gift of food was simple, life-giving, and rooted in creation.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Daniel 1:8","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"But Daniel purposed in his heart that he would not defile himself with the portion of the king's meat.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Daniel chose faithfulness over pressure when appetite and culture pushed another way.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Daniel 1:12","quote":"Prove thy servants, I beseech thee, ten days; and let them give us pulse to eat, and water to drink."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Daniel 1:15","quote":"And at the end of ten days their countenances appeared fairer and fatter in flesh than all the children which did eat the portion of the king's meat."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Proverbs 23:20-21","quote":"Be not among winebibbers; among riotous eaters of flesh.\n\nFor the drunkard and the glutton shall come to poverty."}],"teachingPoints":["1. God gave humanity food from creation\n\nGenesis 1 shows God giving humanity plants, fruits, seeds, and food from the earth.\n\nThis was not random.\n\nIt was part of His original design.\n\nBefore sin, God provided food that supported life, strength, and flourishing.\n\nThe original diet reminds us that God cares about what sustains the body.","2. Food is a gift to receive with gratitude\n\nGod says, \"I have given you.\"\n\nThat phrase matters.\n\nFood is not only something we consume.\n\nIt is something we receive.\n\nEvery meal is a reminder that life depends on God's provision.\n\nWhen we eat with gratitude, food becomes more than appetite.\n\nIt becomes stewardship.","3. Daniel chose principle over pressure\n\nDaniel lived in a culture where compromise would have been easy.\n\nBut he purposed in his heart not to defile himself.\n\nHis food choice was not about pride.\n\nIt was about faithfulness, clarity, discipline, and loyalty to God.\n\nDaniel teaches us that appetite should not rule the conscience.","4. Appetite needs wisdom and self-control\n\nProverbs warns against drunkenness and gluttony.\n\nThis is not body-shaming.\n\nIt is wisdom.\n\nUncontrolled appetite can damage judgment, health, finances, relationships, and spiritual sensitivity.\n\nGod wants us free, not enslaved by cravings."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Temperance","lemma":"ἐγκράτεια","transliteration":"egkrateia","meaning":"Self-control, balance, and wise restraint.","strongs":"G1466","reference":"Galatians 5:23","note":"Temperance is not harsh self-punishment.\n\nIt is Spirit-shaped self-control.\n\nGod wants appetite to serve life, not rule the person. Wise eating is part of caring for the body He has entrusted to us."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"scripture_connection","body":"The Bible's teaching on food should be held with balance.\n\nFood choices do not save us.\n\nJesus saves.\n\nA healthy diet does not make someone spiritually superior.\n\nAnd people should not be shamed for their background, health struggles, access to food, or where they are starting.\n\nBut the opposite error is also dangerous.\n\nWe should not pretend food has no effect.\n\nWhat we eat can affect energy, clarity, mood, long-term health, discipline, and ability to serve.\n\nGod's original design invites us to choose food that supports life.\n\nDaniel's example invites us to make choices based on conviction, not pressure.\n\nProverbs invites us to avoid excess and appetite-driven living.\n\nHealth is not salvation.\n\nHealth is stewardship."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"Food and wisdom","accent":"stone","description":"Food choices do not save us, but they can help us steward life wisely.","payload":{"variant":"sequence","steps":[{"label":"God's original design","subtitle":"simple, life-giving, creation-based food"},{"label":"Daniel's choice","subtitle":"conviction, clarity, discipline"},{"label":"Wisdom's warning","subtitle":"avoid excess, avoid appetite ruling the life"}]},"footnote":"Health is not salvation. Health is stewardship."},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Food and Wisdom","references":[{"reference":"Genesis 1:29"},{"reference":"Daniel 1:8-16"},{"reference":"Proverbs 23:20-21"},{"reference":"1 Corinthians 10:31"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: God's original design for food points toward life, gratitude, and stewardship. We eat wisely not to earn salvation, but to honor the life God has given."}],"reflectPrompt":"What does your current relationship with food reveal about your habits, appetite, gratitude, or self-control?\n\nBe honest without shame. Think about nourishment, excess, restriction, emotional eating, convenience, discipline, gratitude, and whether your choices are helping or hurting your whole life.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, make one food choice that moves you closer to wisdom.\n\nKeep it simple.\n\nAdd more fruit or vegetables.\nDrink more water.\nPrepare one nourishing meal.\nEat with gratitude instead of rushing.\nAvoid eating past fullness.\nPause before using food to numb stress.\nChoose clarity over craving.\n\nDo not turn this into perfectionism.\n\nAsk one question:\n\n\"Will this choice help me care for the life God has entrusted to me?\"\n\nStart there.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for providing food as a gift. Help me see eating as part of whole-life stewardship, not shame or pride. Teach me wisdom, gratitude, and self-control. Give me freedom from appetite ruling my choices, and help me care for my body in a way that honors You. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"Food is part of God's design for life. Wise eating does not earn salvation, but it helps us steward the body and life God has entrusted to us.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"dtt-l5-q1","prompt":"According to Genesis 1:29, what did God give humanity for food?","options":["Herbs bearing seed and fruit from trees","Only processed foods","Nothing from creation","Food that had no connection to life"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Genesis 1:29 shows God giving humanity plants, seeds, and fruit from the earth as part of His original design."},{"id":"dtt-l5-q2","prompt":"What did Daniel purpose in his heart?","options":["That he would not defile himself with the king's food","That appetite should always rule him","That food choices never matter","That he should reject God's wisdom"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Daniel 1:8 says Daniel purposed in his heart not to defile himself, showing conviction and faithfulness."},{"id":"dtt-l5-q3","prompt":"What did Daniel request to eat and drink for ten days?","options":["Pulse and water","Wine and sweets","The king's full table","Nothing at all"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Daniel requested pulse, meaning plant-based food, and water for ten days."},{"id":"dtt-l5-q4","prompt":"What does Proverbs 23 warn against?","options":["Drunkenness and gluttony","Gratitude","Wisdom","Self-control"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Proverbs warns against winebibbing and riotous eating, showing the danger of uncontrolled appetite."},{"id":"dtt-l5-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What is one simple food choice you can make this week to practice better stewardship?","explanation":"Food choices do not save us, but wise choices can support health, clarity, gratitude, and faithful stewardship."}]}}
$dtt_l5_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'the-original-design-for-food'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007d';


-- Designed to Thrive — Lesson 6: Clear Mind, Clean Life (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000914';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $dtt_l6_reader$
{"openingThought":"God wants His people to live with clarity.\n\nA clear mind matters.\n\nWhat clouds the mind can weaken judgment.\nWhat weakens judgment can shape choices.\nWhat shapes choices can affect the whole life.\n\nThat is why Scripture speaks plainly about temperance, self-control, and avoiding what damages the body or dulls spiritual sensitivity.\n\nThis is not about shame.\n\nIt is about freedom.\n\nGod does not call us away from destructive habits because He wants less joy for us.\n\nHe calls us away because He wants us whole, awake, wise, and free.","scriptureRef":"Proverbs 20:1","scriptureQuote":"Wine is a mocker, strong drink is raging: and whosoever is deceived thereby is not wise.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Scripture warns plainly about what clouds judgment and deceives the mind.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"1 Corinthians 6:11","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"And such were some of you: but ye are washed, but ye are sanctified, but ye are justified.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"The gospel restores people from what once mastered them. In Christ, freedom begins.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Proverbs 23:29-32","quote":"Who hath woe? who hath sorrow? who hath contentions? who hath babbling? who hath wounds without cause? who hath redness of eyes?\n\nThey that tarry long at the wine; they that go to seek mixed wine.\n\nAt the last it biteth like a serpent, and stingeth like an adder."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"1 Corinthians 6:12","quote":"All things are lawful unto me, but I will not be brought under the power of any."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Scripture warns about what clouds judgment\n\nProverbs says wine is a mocker and strong drink is raging.\n\nThe warning is not random.\n\nAnything that clouds judgment can lead people into decisions they would not make with a clear mind.\n\nGod's wisdom protects us from being deceived by what promises pleasure but produces damage.","2. Destructive habits often promise relief but create bondage\n\nProverbs 23 shows the sorrow, conflict, wounds, and regret that can come from intoxicating habits.\n\nThis principle reaches beyond alcohol.\n\nAnything that masters the person can become bondage.\n\nSubstances, cravings, entertainment, lust, anger, food, screens, or unhealthy patterns can promise escape while quietly taking control.","3. Jesus restores people from the old life\n\nFirst Corinthians says, \"such were some of you.\"\n\nThat phrase is full of hope.\n\nGod does not define believers by what once mastered them.\n\nThrough Christ, people can be washed, sanctified, and justified.\n\nThe gospel does not only forgive.\n\nIt begins freedom.","4. Freedom means not being mastered\n\nPaul says, \"I will not be brought under the power of any.\"\n\nThat is a clear principle for health and holiness.\n\nThe question is not only, \"Can I do this?\"\n\nThe better question is:\n\n\"Is this mastering me?\"\n\nGod wants His people free, clear-minded, and able to respond to His Spirit."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Temperance","lemma":"ἐγκράτεια","transliteration":"egkrateia","meaning":"Self-control, wise restraint, and freedom from being ruled by appetite or habit.","strongs":"G1466","reference":"Galatians 5:23","note":"Temperance is not joyless restriction.\n\nIt is Spirit-shaped freedom.\n\nGod teaches us to avoid what masters, clouds, weakens, or destroys so we can live with clarity, wisdom, and love."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"scripture_connection","body":"The Bible's call to clear-minded living is not about looking down on people who struggle.\n\nEveryone has something that can master them.\n\nFor one person, it may be alcohol.\n\nFor another, it may be food, anger, lust, shopping, approval, screens, or constant distraction.\n\nThe principle is the same:\n\nGod does not want anything ruling the life except His love.\n\nTemperance is about freedom.\n\nA clear mind helps us hear God's word more honestly.\n\nA clean life helps us avoid unnecessary harm.\n\nA surrendered heart helps us break patterns that once controlled us.\n\nJesus does not shame the bound.\n\nHe sets captives free."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"Freedom check","accent":"sky","description":"Freedom means refusing to be mastered by what damages the life God gave you.","payload":{"variant":"sequence","steps":[{"label":"Ask","subtitle":"Does this cloud my judgment?"},{"label":"Ask","subtitle":"Does this control my appetite?"},{"label":"Ask","subtitle":"Does this weaken my witness?"},{"label":"Ask","subtitle":"Does this harm my body, mind, or relationships?"},{"label":"Ask","subtitle":"Does this pull me away from God?"}]}},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Clear Mind, Clean Life","references":[{"reference":"Proverbs 20:1"},{"reference":"Proverbs 23:29-35"},{"reference":"1 Corinthians 6:11-12"},{"reference":"Galatians 5:22-23"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: God calls us to temperance and clear-minded living, not to shame us, but to free us from what clouds, controls, and damages."}],"reflectPrompt":"What is one habit, appetite, or pattern that may be mastering you more than you want to admit?\n\nThink honestly without shame. Consider substances, food, scrolling, entertainment, lust, anger, spending, approval, or anything that weakens your clarity and freedom.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, do a simple freedom check.\n\nChoose one habit and ask:\n\n\"Is this helping me live clear, free, and whole?\"\n\nIf the honest answer is no, take one small step.\n\nRemove the trigger.\nSet a limit.\nAsk for accountability.\nReplace the habit with prayer, movement, rest, Scripture, or a healthier routine.\nGet help if the pattern is bigger than willpower.\n\nDo not confuse conviction with condemnation.\n\nConviction invites you toward freedom.\n\nCondemnation tells you to hide.\n\nJesus invites you into the light so He can heal what has been ruling you.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for calling me to freedom, not bondage. Show me what is clouding my mind, weakening my choices, or mastering my life. Give me wisdom, self-control, and courage to walk away from what destroys. Thank You that in Jesus I can be washed, restored, and made free. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"God calls us to clear-minded, free, and whole living. Temperance is not shame. It is freedom from what clouds the mind and masters the life.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"dtt-l6-q1","prompt":"According to Proverbs 20:1, what is not wise?","options":["Being deceived by wine or strong drink","Seeking wisdom","Living with a clear mind","Asking God for help"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Proverbs warns that wine is a mocker and strong drink is raging, and whoever is deceived by it is not wise."},{"id":"dtt-l6-q2","prompt":"What does Proverbs 23 describe as the result of lingering long with wine?","options":["Sorrow, conflict, wounds, and harm","Perfect wisdom","Spiritual clarity","Guaranteed health"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Proverbs 23 describes the painful consequences connected with intoxicating habits."},{"id":"dtt-l6-q3","prompt":"According to 1 Corinthians 6:11, what has God done for believers?","options":["Washed, sanctified, and justified them","Left them trapped forever","Ignored their need for freedom","Told them change is impossible"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Paul says believers have been washed, sanctified, and justified, showing the restoring work of Christ."},{"id":"dtt-l6-q4","prompt":"According to 1 Corinthians 6:12, what should not happen to us?","options":["We should not be brought under the power of anything","We should be mastered by every appetite","We should ignore self-control","We should reject freedom"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Paul says he will not be brought under the power of any, giving a principle of freedom from being mastered."},{"id":"dtt-l6-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What is one practical step you can take toward clearer, freer living this week?","explanation":"Temperance is Spirit-shaped freedom. God invites us to step away from what clouds, controls, or damages us."}]}}
$dtt_l6_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'clear-mind-clean-life'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007d';


-- Designed to Thrive — Lesson 7: Jesus the Healer (guided reader JSON).
delete from public.lesson_sections
where lesson_id = 'd3000007-0000-4000-8000-000000000915';

update public.lessons
set reader_content = $dtt_l7_reader$
{"openingThought":"Jesus did not come into the world as a distant teacher with good ideas.\n\nHe came near.\n\nHe touched the sick.\nHe restored the broken.\nHe forgave sinners.\nHe lifted the ashamed.\nHe opened blind eyes.\nHe gave peace to troubled hearts.\n\nHis healing ministry shows us something important about God:\n\nGod cares about the whole person.\n\nJesus came to save us from sin, but His salvation reaches every place sin has damaged: body, mind, heart, relationships, and spirit.\n\nHe is not only interested in getting people to heaven one day.\n\nHe is also restoring people into wholeness now.","scriptureRef":"Acts 10:38","scriptureQuote":"God anointed Jesus of Nazareth with the Holy Ghost and with power: who went about doing good, and healing all that were oppressed of the devil; for God was with him.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Jesus went about doing good and healing those oppressed by the devil, revealing God's heart for suffering people.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Isaiah 53:5","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"But he was wounded for our transgressions, he was bruised for our iniquities.\n\nAnd with his stripes we are healed.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Christ bore our sin and its wounds so healing could reach what sin had broken.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Matthew 1:21","quote":"And she shall bring forth a son, and thou shalt call his name JESUS: for he shall save his people from their sins."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"John 10:10","quote":"I am come that they might have life, and that they might have it more abundantly."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Matthew 9:35","quote":"And Jesus went about all the cities and villages, teaching in their synagogues, and preaching the gospel of the kingdom, and healing every sickness and every disease among the people."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Jesus came to save from sin\n\nMatthew 1 says Jesus came to save His people from their sins.\n\nThis is the center of His mission.\n\nSin is the deepest disease.\n\nIt separates us from God, damages the heart, corrupts desire, and brings death.\n\nJesus did not come merely to improve our habits.\n\nHe came to rescue us from sin itself.","2. Jesus healed because God cares about suffering\n\nActs says Jesus went about doing good and healing those oppressed by the devil.\n\nHis miracles were not random displays of power.\n\nThey revealed God's heart.\n\nEvery healing showed that sickness, oppression, pain, and brokenness were never part of God's original design.\n\nJesus came to confront what sin had done.","3. Healing points to whole-person restoration\n\nMatthew 9 shows Jesus teaching, preaching, and healing.\n\nHe addressed the mind with truth.\n\nHe addressed the heart with the gospel.\n\nHe addressed the body with healing.\n\nHe addressed the spirit with restoration.\n\nJesus' ministry was whole-person ministry.\n\nHe came to make people whole, not merely informed.","4. Final healing is still ahead\n\nJesus healed many people during His earthly ministry, but not every sickness disappeared from the world immediately.\n\nThat matters.\n\nWe should not shame people who are still sick, struggling, disabled, grieving, or waiting.\n\nIn this life, healing may come in different ways and at different times.\n\nBut the resurrection promises complete restoration.\n\nJesus' healing ministry is a preview of the day when sin, sickness, pain, and death will be gone forever."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Heal","lemma":"רָפָא","transliteration":"rapha","meaning":"To restore, make whole, mend, or bring back to health.","strongs":"H7495","reference":"Isaiah 53:5","note":"Biblical healing is bigger than physical recovery alone.\n\nJesus heals by forgiving sin, restoring relationship with God, renewing the mind, freeing the heart, and one day making all things completely whole."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"Designed to Thrive has shown one clear truth:\n\nGod cares about the whole life.\n\nChoices have consequences.\n\nWisdom guides the whole person.\n\nSalvation includes restoration and wholeness.\n\nThe body matters to God.\n\nFood can be received with wisdom and gratitude.\n\nTemperance protects clarity and freedom.\n\nAnd Jesus stands at the center as the Healer.\n\nThis keeps health from becoming legalism.\n\nWe do not care for the body to earn salvation.\n\nWe care for the body because Jesus has redeemed the whole person.\n\nWe do not pursue health to feel superior.\n\nWe pursue wisdom because God's design is good.\n\nWe do not shame people who struggle.\n\nWe point people to the Savior who restores.\n\nJesus is the Healer of body, mind, heart, and spirit."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"wholePersonHealthPattern","title":"Jesus Restores the Whole Person","caption":"Jesus came to save, heal, restore, and make whole.","highlightedIndices":[0,1,2,3],"areas":[{"area":"Body","items":["healing","stewardship","strength","care"]},{"area":"Mind","items":["truth","clarity","renewal","wisdom"]},{"area":"Heart","items":["peace","forgiveness","emotional healing","love"]},{"area":"Spirit","items":["salvation","trust","worship","surrender"]}]},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Jesus the Healer","references":[{"reference":"Isaiah 53:5"},{"reference":"Matthew 1:21"},{"reference":"Acts 10:38"},{"reference":"Matthew 9:35"},{"reference":"John 10:10"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Jesus came to save from sin and restore the whole person. His healing ministry reveals God's desire for life, freedom, and wholeness."}],"reflectPrompt":"Where do you most need Jesus' healing work right now?\n\nThink about sin, guilt, health, habits, emotional wounds, anxiety, relationships, shame, weariness, or spiritual distance from God.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, bring one area of brokenness to Jesus honestly.\n\nDo not hide it.\n\nDo not spiritualize it away.\n\nDo not pretend it is not affecting you.\n\nPray:\n\n\"Jesus, this is where I need Your healing.\"\n\nThen take one wise step in cooperation with His grace.\n\nConfess sin.\nAsk for help.\nRest.\nForgive.\nSeek counsel.\nStart a healthier habit.\nRemove what is damaging you.\nReturn to Scripture.\nMake a medical appointment if needed.\nTalk to someone trustworthy.\n\nHealing is not always instant.\n\nBut coming to Jesus is always the right place to begin.\n\nPrayer:\n\nJesus, thank You for coming near to broken people. Thank You for saving from sin, healing the wounded, restoring the weary, and caring about the whole person. I bring You the places in me that need Your touch. Give me wisdom, courage, and faith to cooperate with Your restoring work. Make me whole in Your love. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"You have completed Designed to Thrive. God cares about the whole person. Through wisdom, stewardship, temperance, and the healing grace of Jesus, He invites us into life, wholeness, and restoration.","seriesCompleteScreen":{"title":"Study Complete","message":"You have completed Designed to Thrive. God cares about the whole person. Through wisdom, stewardship, temperance, and the healing grace of Jesus, He invites us into life, wholeness, and restoration.","primaryCta":{"label":"Back to Foundations Path","href":"/paths/foundations"},"secondaryCta":{"label":"Review this study","href":"/study-library/designed-to-thrive"}},"lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"dtt-l7-q1","prompt":"According to Acts 10:38, what did Jesus go about doing?","options":["Doing good and healing those oppressed of the devil","Ignoring human suffering","Teaching without compassion","Avoiding broken people"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Acts says Jesus went about doing good and healing those oppressed of the devil, showing God's care for suffering people."},{"id":"dtt-l7-q2","prompt":"According to Matthew 1:21, what did Jesus come to save His people from?","options":["Their sins","Wisdom","Health","Compassion"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Matthew says Jesus would save His people from their sins, showing the deepest purpose of His mission."},{"id":"dtt-l7-q3","prompt":"According to Matthew 9:35, what did Jesus do among the people?","options":["Taught, preached, and healed","Only criticized them","Avoided the sick","Rejected the needy"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Matthew says Jesus taught, preached the gospel of the kingdom, and healed sickness and disease."},{"id":"dtt-l7-q4","prompt":"What does this lesson teach about health and salvation?","options":["Jesus saves and restores the whole person","Health habits earn salvation","Sickness means God has rejected someone","The body does not matter to God"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The lesson shows that Jesus saves from sin and restores the whole person: body, mind, heart, and spirit."},{"id":"dtt-l7-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What is one area where you need to invite Jesus' healing and wisdom this week?","explanation":"Jesus is the Healer. He invites us to bring our brokenness to Him and cooperate with His restoring grace."}]}}
$dtt_l7_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'jesus-the-healer'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007d';


-- Buried and Raised — Lesson 1: Loved Into New Life (guided reader JSON).
update public.lessons
set reader_content = $bar_l1_reader$
{"openingThought":"God does not begin transformation by threatening us.\n\nHe begins by loving us.\n\nBefore baptism is about water, it is about love.\n\nGod sees the real you.\n\nNot the filtered version.\nNot the religious version.\nNot the version you wish people saw.\n\nThe real you.\n\nAnd still, He says:\n\n\"I have loved you with an everlasting love.\"\n\nThat love does not erase your identity.\n\nIt restores it.\n\nGod does not want to turn you into a spiritual robot. He wants to heal what sin has damaged and grow you into the person He created you to become.\n\nBaptism begins here:\n\nGod loves you, draws you, and invites you into new life.","scriptureRef":"Jeremiah 31:3","scriptureQuote":"Yea, I have loved thee with an everlasting love: therefore with lovingkindness have I drawn thee.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"God's love is not temporary. He draws the heart with kindness, not coercion.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Luke 15:20","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"But when he was yet a great way off, his father saw him, and had compassion, and ran.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"The father in the parable shows God's heart: compassion runs toward the returning sinner.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"John 17:23","quote":"That the world may know that thou hast sent me, and hast loved them, as thou hast loved me."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"1 John 3:16","quote":"Hereby perceive we the love of God, because he laid down his life for us."}],"teachingPoints":["1. God loves the real you\n\nJeremiah says God loves with an everlasting love.\n\nThat means His love is not temporary, shallow, or easily exhausted.\n\nGod does not love an imaginary version of you.\n\nHe loves you.\n\nHe sees what sin has damaged, but He also sees what grace can restore.\n\nHis love is honest enough to confront what harms you and strong enough to heal what is broken.","2. God draws instead of forcing\n\nGod says, \"With lovingkindness have I drawn thee.\"\n\nThat word matters.\n\nGod draws.\n\nHe does not coerce.\n\nHe does not manipulate.\n\nHe does not bypass the will.\n\nHe invites the heart through love.\n\nBaptism is meaningful because it is a free response to God's love, not a forced religious act.","3. God runs toward the returning heart\n\nIn Luke 15, the father sees the son while he is still far away and runs toward him.\n\nThat picture shows the heart of God.\n\nGod is not coldly waiting for sinners to crawl back.\n\nHe moves toward the one who turns home.\n\nHis compassion comes before the son's full explanation.\n\nGrace runs first.","4. The cross reveals our value\n\nFirst John says we understand God's love because Jesus laid down His life for us.\n\nThe cross shows what you are worth to God.\n\nNot because you earned it.\n\nNot because you were perfect.\n\nBut because God's love is that deep.\n\nBaptism is our public response to the love revealed at the cross."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Drawn","lemma":"מָשַׁךְ","transliteration":"mashak","meaning":"To pull near, attract, invite, or bring close.","strongs":"H4900","reference":"Jeremiah 31:3","note":"God draws us with lovingkindness.\n\nHe does not force love because forced love is not love.\n\nBaptism is a voluntary response to the God who lovingly draws us into restored relationship with Himself."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"God's goal is not to erase you.\n\nGod's goal is to restore you.\n\nSin damages the person.\n\nIt twists desire.\nIt wounds identity.\nIt creates shame.\nIt breaks trust.\nIt bends the heart toward fear, pride, selfishness, and hiding.\n\nBut God's love does not come to destroy your individuality.\n\nIt comes to heal it.\n\nThe gospel says:\n\nGod loves you.\nGod draws you.\nGod values you.\nGod gave Himself for you.\nGod wants to make you new.\n\nBaptism is not the beginning of God's love for you.\n\nIt is your response to the love that has already been pursuing you."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"Loved into new life","accent":"sage","description":"God does not force transformation. He draws us with love.","payload":{"variant":"sequence","steps":[{"label":"God's love","subtitle":"draws, heals, restores, frees"},{"label":"Sin","subtitle":"hides, wounds, distorts, enslaves"},{"label":"Baptism","subtitle":"my public yes to God's restoring love"}]}},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Loved Into New Life","references":[{"reference":"Jeremiah 31:3"},{"reference":"Luke 15:20"},{"reference":"John 17:23"},{"reference":"1 John 3:16"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: God loves the real you, draws you with kindness, and invites you into new life through Jesus."}],"reflectPrompt":"Do you find it easy or difficult to believe that God loves the real you?\n\nThink about shame, past mistakes, insecurity, religious pressure, fear, or the parts of yourself you try to hide from others.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, sit with Jeremiah 31:3 for a few minutes.\n\nRead it slowly:\n\n\"I have loved thee with an everlasting love.\"\n\nThen ask:\n\n\"What would change if I believed God really loves me like this?\"\n\nDo not rush to perform.\n\nDo not rush to prove.\n\nStart with receiving.\n\nThen write one sentence:\n\n\"Because God loves me, I can…\"\n\nFinish the sentence honestly.\n\nMaybe:\n\nBecause God loves me, I can stop hiding.\n\nBecause God loves me, I can come home.\n\nBecause God loves me, I can surrender.\n\nBecause God loves me, I can begin again.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for loving me with everlasting love. Thank You for drawing me with kindness instead of forcing me with fear. Help me believe that You love the real me and that Your love can restore what sin has damaged. Prepare my heart to respond fully to Jesus and to walk in the new life You are calling me into. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"God loves the real you with everlasting love. Baptism begins as a response to the God who draws, restores, and invites you into new life.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"bar-l1-q1","prompt":"According to Jeremiah 31:3, what kind of love does God have?","options":["Everlasting love","Temporary love","Forced love","Shallow love"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jeremiah says God loves with an everlasting love and draws us with lovingkindness."},{"id":"bar-l1-q2","prompt":"According to Luke 15:20, what did the father do when he saw his son far away?","options":["He had compassion and ran to him","He ignored him","He rejected him","He waited with anger only"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The father in the parable represents God's compassionate heart toward the returning sinner."},{"id":"bar-l1-q3","prompt":"According to John 17:23, how does the Father love believers?","options":["As He loved Jesus","Less than He loves Jesus","Only if they are perfect","Only from a distance"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus said the Father loves His people as He loves His Son, revealing the depth of God's love."},{"id":"bar-l1-q4","prompt":"According to 1 John 3:16, how do we perceive the love of God?","options":["Jesus laid down His life for us","We earned it by performance","God ignored our need","Love never sacrifices"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"First John says we perceive God's love because Jesus laid down His life for us."},{"id":"bar-l1-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What part of your life do you need to bring into the light of God's everlasting love?","explanation":"God draws with lovingkindness. His love does not erase who we are; it restores us into new life."}]}}
$bar_l1_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'loved-into-new-life'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007e';


-- Buried and Raised — Lesson 2: Born Again (guided reader JSON).
update public.lessons
set reader_content = $bar_l2_reader$
{"openingThought":"Jesus once told a deeply religious man that he needed to be born again.\n\nNot improved.\n\nNot polished.\n\nNot merely better informed.\n\nBorn again.\n\nThat sounds strange at first because we usually think change means behavior management.\n\nTry harder.\nDo better.\nStop messing up.\nFix yourself.\n\nBut Jesus goes deeper.\n\nHe says we need a new beginning from the inside out.\n\nThe new birth is not God deleting who you are.\n\nIt is God restoring who you were always meant to become.","scriptureRef":"John 3:3","scriptureQuote":"Except a man be born again, he cannot see the kingdom of God.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Jesus goes deeper than behavior. He speaks of a new spiritual beginning.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"John 3:5-6","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"Except a man be born of water and of the Spirit, he cannot enter into the kingdom of God.\n\nThat which is born of the flesh is flesh; and that which is born of the Spirit is spirit.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"New birth joins outward commitment with the Spirit's inward work.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"John 3:16-17","quote":"For God so loved the world, that he gave his only begotten Son.\n\nFor God sent not his Son into the world to condemn the world; but that the world through him might be saved."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"2 Corinthians 5:17","quote":"Therefore if any man be in Christ, he is a new creature: old things are passed away; behold, all things are become new."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Religious knowledge is not enough\n\nNicodemus knew Scripture.\n\nHe was respected, educated, and religious.\n\nBut Jesus still told him he needed to be born again.\n\nThat means spiritual transformation is deeper than information.\n\nWe do not only need to know religious ideas.\n\nWe need the heart renewed by God.","2. New birth is an inward transformation\n\nJesus was not talking about physical rebirth.\n\nHe was talking about a new spiritual beginning.\n\nThe mind is renewed.\n\nThe heart is softened.\n\nThe motives are changed.\n\nThe will is surrendered.\n\nThe life begins moving in a new direction because God is working within.","3. The Spirit makes new life possible\n\nJesus said we must be born of water and of the Spirit.\n\nWater points us toward baptism.\n\nThe Spirit points us toward God's inward work.\n\nBaptism without the Spirit would be only an outward symbol.\n\nThe Spirit without a willing response would not honor human freedom.\n\nGod invites us into both: outward commitment and inward renewal.","4. God's purpose is salvation, not condemnation\n\nJohn 3 says God loved the world and gave His Son.\n\nThen it says God did not send His Son to condemn the world, but to save it.\n\nThat means the new birth is not driven by fear.\n\nIt is driven by love.\n\nGod is not trying to crush the sinner.\n\nHe is trying to restore the sinner into new life."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Born Again","lemma":"γεννάω","transliteration":"gennao","meaning":"A new beginning produced by God's Spirit, restoring the heart, mind, motives, and life.","strongs":"G1080","reference":"John 3:3","note":"To be born again is not to become a different person in the sense of losing yourself.\n\nIt is to become renewed by God from the inside out.\n\nThe old life of guilt, hiding, and sin gives way to new life in Christ."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"The new birth corrects a common misunderstanding about Christianity.\n\nChristianity is not merely behavior modification.\n\nIt is not just cleaning up the outside.\n\nIt is not pretending to be better than others.\n\nIt is not learning religious language while the heart remains unchanged.\n\nJesus calls us into new life.\n\nThat new life begins with God's love, moves through the work of the Holy Spirit, and becomes visible in a surrendered life.\n\nBaptism connects to this because baptism is the public sign that we are saying yes to new birth.\n\nThe old life is being left behind.\n\nA new life in Christ is beginning."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"Born again","accent":"sky","description":"The new birth is God restoring the life from the inside out.","payload":{"variant":"sequence","steps":[{"label":"Old life","subtitle":"guilt, hiding, self-rule, sin, fear"},{"label":"God's love","subtitle":"draws, forgives, restores"},{"label":"New life","subtitle":"renewed heart, Spirit-led mind, surrendered will, life in Christ"}]}},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Born Again","references":[{"reference":"John 3:1-17"},{"reference":"John 3:16-17"},{"reference":"2 Corinthians 5:17"},{"reference":"Titus 3:5"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Jesus calls us to be born again: renewed by the Spirit and brought into a new life shaped by God's love."}],"reflectPrompt":"Where do you most need a new beginning with God?\n\nThink about your thoughts, habits, motives, guilt, relationships, fears, hidden struggles, or the parts of life where behavior change alone has not been enough.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, stop asking only, \"What behavior do I need to fix?\"\n\nAsk a deeper question:\n\n\"Lord, what part of me needs to be made new?\"\n\nThen sit with that honestly.\n\nDo not hide.\n\nDo not perform.\n\nDo not pretend you can manufacture new birth by willpower.\n\nBring that area to Jesus and ask for the work of the Spirit.\n\nA new beginning starts when we stop defending the old life and open ourselves to the life God wants to give.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for loving me enough to offer me a new beginning. I confess that I cannot make myself new by effort alone. I need Your Spirit to renew my heart, mind, motives, and will. Help me receive the new life Jesus came to give. Prepare me to follow Him fully and publicly in faith. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"Jesus calls us to be born again. New life is not surface-level behavior change. It is God restoring the heart from the inside out.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"bar-l2-q1","prompt":"According to John 3:3, what did Jesus say a person must experience?","options":["Being born again","Becoming famous","Earning salvation","Avoiding all questions"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus told Nicodemus that unless a person is born again, they cannot see the kingdom of God."},{"id":"bar-l2-q2","prompt":"According to John 3:5, what must a person be born of?","options":["Water and the Spirit","Fear and pressure","Reputation and effort","Wealth and status"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus said a person must be born of water and the Spirit, pointing to baptism and the Spirit's inward work."},{"id":"bar-l2-q3","prompt":"According to John 3:16, what motivated God to give His Son?","options":["Love","Condemnation","Manipulation","Indifference"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"John 3:16 says God so loved the world that He gave His only begotten Son."},{"id":"bar-l2-q4","prompt":"According to 2 Corinthians 5:17, what is true of someone in Christ?","options":["They are a new creature","They remain hopeless forever","They must save themselves","They are rejected by God"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Paul says that if anyone is in Christ, they are a new creature and old things are passed away."},{"id":"bar-l2-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What old pattern do you want God to replace with new life?","explanation":"The new birth is God's work of renewal from the inside out. He restores the heart, mind, motives, and life through His Spirit."}]}}
$bar_l2_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'born-again'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007e';


-- Buried and Raised — Lesson 3: Love, Not Condemnation (guided reader JSON).
update public.lessons
set reader_content = $bar_l3_reader$
{"openingThought":"God does not manipulate people into transformation.\n\nHe loves them into it.\n\nThat matters because many people imagine God as mainly angry, disappointed, or ready to condemn.\n\nBut Jesus gives us a clearer picture.\n\nGod gave His Son because He loved the world.\n\nGod sent His Son not to condemn the world, but to save it.\n\nCondemnation crushes.\n\nLove restores.\n\nFear may pressure behavior for a moment, but love reaches the heart and changes the direction of the life.\n\nBaptism is not a response to spiritual bullying.\n\nIt is a response to divine love.","scriptureRef":"John 3:16-17","scriptureQuote":"For God so loved the world, that he gave his only begotten Son.\n\nFor God sent not his Son into the world to condemn the world; but that the world through him might be saved.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"God's motive is love, and His mission is salvation, not condemnation.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Romans 2:4","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"The goodness of God leadeth thee to repentance.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Repentance is awakened when we see God's goodness clearly, not when we are manipulated by fear.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"1 John 4:16","quote":"God is love; and he that dwelleth in love dwelleth in God, and God in him."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"1 John 4:18-19","quote":"There is no fear in love; but perfect love casteth out fear.\n\nWe love him, because he first loved us."}],"teachingPoints":["1. God's motive is love\n\nJohn 3:16 begins with God's love.\n\nThat is the foundation.\n\nGod did not send Jesus because He hated the world.\n\nHe gave His Son because He loved the world.\n\nThe cross is not proof that God was reluctant to save.\n\nThe cross is proof that God's love moved first.","2. God's mission is salvation, not condemnation\n\nJohn 3:17 says God did not send His Son to condemn the world, but to save it.\n\nJesus does not deny sin.\n\nHe came because sin is real.\n\nBut His purpose is not to crush sinners under despair.\n\nHis purpose is to rescue, heal, restore, and bring people into life.","3. God's goodness leads to repentance\n\nRomans says the goodness of God leads us to repentance.\n\nRepentance is not produced by manipulation.\n\nIt is awakened when we see God clearly.\n\nWhen we understand His goodness, our minds begin to change.\n\nWe stop believing lies about Him.\n\nWe stop running from Him.\n\nWe start turning toward Him.","4. Perfect love casts out fear\n\nFirst John says perfect love casts out fear.\n\nFear-based religion may create outward compliance, but it does not create deep trust.\n\nGod wants more than frightened behavior.\n\nHe wants a restored relationship.\n\nHis love casts out the fear that makes us hide, perform, or pretend."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Repentance","lemma":"μετάνοια","transliteration":"metanoia","meaning":"A change of mind, a turning of the heart, and a reorientation of life toward God.","strongs":"G3341","reference":"Romans 2:4","note":"Repentance is not merely feeling bad.\n\nIt is seeing God's goodness clearly enough that the mind changes, the heart turns, and the life begins moving in a new direction.\n\nGod's goodness leads us there."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"Condemnation and conviction are not the same thing.\n\nCondemnation says:\n\n\"You are hopeless.\"\n\nConviction says:\n\n\"Come into the light so you can be healed.\"\n\nCondemnation drives people into hiding.\n\nConviction invites people into freedom.\n\nCondemnation attacks identity.\n\nConviction exposes what is damaging the person so grace can restore them.\n\nGod does not use condemnation to create new birth.\n\nHe uses love.\n\nThis matters for baptism.\n\nA person should not be baptized because they were pressured, scared, manipulated, or embarrassed into it.\n\nBaptism should be a free, thoughtful, personal yes to the God whose love has won the heart."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"Love, not condemnation","accent":"rose","description":"God does not force transformation. His goodness leads the heart to repentance.","payload":{"variant":"comparison","leftTitle":"Condemnation","rightTitle":"God's love","leftItems":["fear","hiding","pressure","despair","control"],"rightItems":["goodness","repentance","freedom","trust","new life"]}},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Love, Not Condemnation","references":[{"reference":"John 3:16-17"},{"reference":"Romans 2:4"},{"reference":"1 John 4:16"},{"reference":"1 John 4:18-19"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: God transforms through love, not fear. His goodness leads us to repentance and prepares the heart for new life."}],"reflectPrompt":"Have you been more shaped by fear of God or trust in God's love?\n\nThink about your view of repentance, obedience, baptism, confession, and whether you come to God freely or hide from Him in fear.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, pay attention to the voice you hear when you think about God.\n\nDoes it sound like condemnation or conviction?\n\nCondemnation says:\n\n\"Hide. You are too far gone.\"\n\nConviction says:\n\n\"Come. God is ready to restore.\"\n\nWhen you feel guilt or fear, bring it into prayer and ask:\n\n\"Father, what are You lovingly inviting me to surrender?\"\n\nThen respond honestly.\n\nNot because you are being forced.\n\nBecause His goodness is drawing you.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for loving the world enough to give Your Son. Thank You that Jesus came not to condemn, but to save. Free me from fear-based religion and help me see Your goodness clearly. Let Your love lead me to repentance, trust, surrender, and new life. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"God does not transform by condemnation. His love reveals His goodness, leads us to repentance, and invites us freely into new life.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"bar-l3-q1","prompt":"According to John 3:16, what motivated God to give His Son?","options":["Love","Hatred","Indifference","Pressure"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"John 3:16 says God so loved the world that He gave His only begotten Son."},{"id":"bar-l3-q2","prompt":"According to John 3:17, why did God send His Son into the world?","options":["That the world through Him might be saved","To condemn the world","To ignore the world","To confuse the world"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"John 3:17 says God did not send His Son to condemn the world, but that the world through Him might be saved."},{"id":"bar-l3-q3","prompt":"According to Romans 2:4, what leads us to repentance?","options":["The goodness of God","Human pride","Condemnation only","Fear without love"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Romans says the goodness of God leads us to repentance."},{"id":"bar-l3-q4","prompt":"According to 1 John 4:18, what does perfect love cast out?","options":["Fear","Hope","Trust","Grace"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"First John says perfect love casts out fear."},{"id":"bar-l3-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What lie about God's character do you need to let His love replace?","explanation":"God's love leads to repentance by showing us who He really is. His goodness draws us into trust and new life."}]}}
$bar_l3_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'love-not-condemnation'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007e';


-- Buried and Raised — Lesson 4: Water and Spirit (guided reader JSON).
update public.lessons
set reader_content = $bar_l4_reader$
{"openingThought":"Jesus said new birth involves water and the Spirit.\n\nThat is not random language.\n\nWater points us to baptism.\n\nThe Spirit points us to inner transformation.\n\nBaptism is the visible sign.\n\nThe Holy Spirit is the inward power.\n\nOne without the other misses the point.\n\nWater without the Spirit becomes empty ritual.\n\nSpirit without surrender avoids the public step of faith Jesus gave His followers.\n\nGod calls us into a new life that is both inwardly real and outwardly confessed.","scriptureRef":"John 3:5","scriptureQuote":"Except a man be born of water and of the Spirit, he cannot enter into the kingdom of God.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"New birth joins the Spirit's inward work with baptism as the outward sign of surrender.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Matthew 3:16-17","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"And Jesus, when he was baptized, went up straightway out of the water.\n\nAnd lo a voice from heaven, saying, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Jesus was baptized as our example, and the Father publicly affirmed Him as His beloved Son.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Acts 2:38","quote":"Repent, and be baptized every one of you in the name of Jesus Christ for the remission of sins.\n\nAnd ye shall receive the gift of the Holy Ghost."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Titus 3:5","quote":"According to his mercy he saved us, by the washing of regeneration, and renewing of the Holy Ghost."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Jesus connects new birth with water and Spirit\n\nIn John 3, Jesus says we must be born of water and of the Spirit.\n\nThis shows that new life is not merely private feeling.\n\nGod works within the heart by His Spirit, and baptism becomes the outward sign of that inward surrender.\n\nBaptism is not magic.\n\nIt is a visible response to God's saving work.","2. Jesus Himself was baptized\n\nJesus did not need baptism because He had sinned.\n\nHe was baptized as our example.\n\nWhen He came up out of the water, the Father publicly affirmed Him as His beloved Son.\n\nBaptism marks a person's public identification with God's love, purpose, and will.","3. Baptism is connected to repentance\n\nPeter said, \"Repent, and be baptized.\"\n\nRepentance means a change of mind and direction.\n\nIt is turning from sin and turning toward God.\n\nBaptism is not a casual religious ceremony.\n\nIt is the public sign that a person is responding to the gospel and choosing a new life with Jesus.","4. The Holy Spirit gives power for new life\n\nActs 2 connects baptism with receiving the gift of the Holy Spirit.\n\nTitus speaks of washing and renewal by the Holy Spirit.\n\nThis matters because baptism is not merely about leaving an old life behind.\n\nIt is about receiving power for a new life.\n\nGod does not call us into transformation and then leave us alone."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Spirit","lemma":"πνεῦμα","transliteration":"pneuma","meaning":"The Holy Spirit, God's personal presence working within the heart to renew, guide, convict, comfort, and empower.","strongs":"G4151","reference":"John 3:5","note":"The Holy Spirit makes the new birth real.\n\nBaptism is the outward sign, but the Spirit does the inward work of changing the heart, renewing the mind, and empowering the life."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"Baptism holds together two truths:\n\nGod works inside.\n\nWe respond outside.\n\nThe Spirit convicts, draws, renews, and empowers.\n\nThe water testifies that we are saying yes.\n\nThis is why baptism is not supposed to be forced, rushed, or treated like a meaningless tradition.\n\nIt should be a thoughtful response to Jesus.\n\nA person is saying:\n\nI am turning from the old life.\n\nI am receiving the cleansing grace of Christ.\n\nI am identifying with Jesus.\n\nI am opening my life to the Holy Spirit.\n\nI am stepping publicly into the new life God gives."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"Water and Spirit","accent":"sky","description":"Baptism is the visible sign. The Spirit is the inward power.","payload":{"variant":"sequence","steps":[{"label":"Water","subtitle":"public sign, baptism, surrender, cleansing symbol"},{"label":"Spirit","subtitle":"inner renewal, conviction, power, new life"},{"label":"Together","subtitle":"born again into life with Christ"}]}},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Water and Spirit","references":[{"reference":"John 3:5"},{"reference":"Matthew 3:13-17"},{"reference":"Acts 2:38"},{"reference":"Titus 3:5"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Jesus connects new birth with water and the Spirit. Baptism is the outward sign of surrender, and the Holy Spirit brings inward renewal."}],"reflectPrompt":"Have you thought of baptism more as an outward ceremony or as a response to the Spirit's inward work?\n\nThink about repentance, surrender, public commitment, and whether you are allowing the Holy Spirit to renew your heart and direction.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, ask God to show you where the Spirit is inviting you into deeper surrender.\n\nPray:\n\n\"Holy Spirit, what are You renewing in me?\"\n\nThen listen honestly.\n\nMaybe He is calling you to confess something.\n\nMaybe He is calling you to let go of an old pattern.\n\nMaybe He is calling you to take baptism seriously.\n\nMaybe He is calling you to stop delaying obedience.\n\nDo not reduce baptism to a symbol only.\n\nAsk what the symbol is calling you to receive and live.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for the new birth Jesus described. Thank You for baptism as a visible sign of surrender and for the Holy Spirit as the power of inward renewal. Teach me to respond honestly to Your love. Wash me, renew me, and lead me into the life You are calling me to live in Christ. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"Jesus calls us to be born of water and the Spirit. Baptism is the outward sign of surrender, and the Holy Spirit brings inward renewal.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"bar-l4-q1","prompt":"According to John 3:5, what must a person be born of?","options":["Water and the Spirit","Fear and pressure","Reputation and effort","Tradition only"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus said a person must be born of water and of the Spirit, connecting new birth with baptism and the Spirit's inward work."},{"id":"bar-l4-q2","prompt":"What happened when Jesus was baptized in Matthew 3?","options":["He came up out of the water and the Father affirmed Him","He avoided the water completely","He rejected baptism","He said baptism had no meaning"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Matthew says Jesus came up out of the water, and the Father declared Him His beloved Son."},{"id":"bar-l4-q3","prompt":"According to Acts 2:38, what did Peter tell the people to do?","options":["Repent and be baptized","Ignore repentance","Avoid the Holy Spirit","Wait until they were perfect"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Peter called the people to repent and be baptized in the name of Jesus Christ."},{"id":"bar-l4-q4","prompt":"According to Titus 3:5, what renews us?","options":["The Holy Ghost","Human pride","Fear alone","Empty ritual"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Titus speaks of the washing of regeneration and renewing of the Holy Ghost."},{"id":"bar-l4-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"Where is the Holy Spirit inviting you to surrender more fully to Jesus?","explanation":"Baptism is the outward sign of a heart responding to God's inward work through the Holy Spirit."}]}}
$bar_l4_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'water-and-spirit'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007e';


-- Buried and Raised — Lesson 5: Buried With Christ (guided reader JSON).
update public.lessons
set reader_content = $bar_l5_reader$
{"openingThought":"Baptism is not just getting wet.\n\nIt is a picture of death, burial, and resurrection.\n\nWhen a person goes under the water, baptism points to the burial of the old life.\n\nWhen a person comes up from the water, baptism points to new life in Christ.\n\nThis is why the Bible connects baptism with Jesus' death and resurrection.\n\nBaptism says:\n\nThe old life does not own me anymore.\n\nI belong to Jesus.\n\nI am being raised to walk in newness of life.","scriptureRef":"Romans 6:4","scriptureQuote":"Therefore we are buried with him by baptism into death.\n\nThat like as Christ was raised up from the dead by the glory of the Father, even so we also should walk in newness of life.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Baptism pictures burial with Christ and resurrection to walk in newness of life.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Romans 6:6","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"Knowing this, that our old man is crucified with him, that the body of sin might be destroyed.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"The old life is crucified with Christ. Sin no longer has final authority over those who belong to Him.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Romans 6:11","quote":"Likewise reckon ye also yourselves to be dead indeed unto sin, but alive unto God through Jesus Christ our Lord."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Colossians 2:12","quote":"Buried with him in baptism, wherein also ye are risen with him through the faith of the operation of God."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Galatians 2:20","quote":"I am crucified with Christ: nevertheless I live; yet not I, but Christ liveth in me."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Baptism points to burial with Christ\n\nRomans says we are buried with Christ by baptism into death.\n\nThis is why baptism by immersion matters.\n\nGoing under the water clearly pictures burial.\n\nThe old life of sin, self-rule, guilt, and separation is being surrendered to Jesus.\n\nBaptism is not a casual religious gesture.\n\nIt is a visible sign that the old life is being laid down.","2. Baptism points to resurrection with Christ\n\nRomans also says that just as Christ was raised from the dead, we should walk in newness of life.\n\nComing up from the water points to resurrection.\n\nThe person is not only saying goodbye to the old life.\n\nThey are saying yes to a new life with Jesus.\n\nBaptism looks backward to Christ's resurrection and forward to a transformed life.","3. The old self no longer has final authority\n\nRomans says the \"old man\" is crucified with Christ.\n\nThat does not mean believers never struggle again.\n\nIt means the old life is no longer master.\n\nSin may tempt.\n\nOld patterns may speak.\n\nBut in Christ, they do not have the final claim.\n\nBaptism declares a new allegiance.","4. New life is lived through faith\n\nGalatians says, \"Christ liveth in me.\"\n\nThe baptized life is not powered by human effort alone.\n\nIt is lived by faith in Jesus.\n\nWe die to self-rule and rise to a life where Christ becomes the center.\n\nBaptism is the beginning of a daily walk with Him."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Immersion","lemma":"βαπτίζω","transliteration":"baptizo","meaning":"Being fully placed under the water.","strongs":"G907","reference":"Romans 6:4","note":"Baptism by immersion best reflects the biblical picture of burial and resurrection.\n\nGoing under the water symbolizes burial with Christ.\n\nComing up from the water symbolizes being raised to new life in Him."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"Baptism is one of the clearest pictures of the gospel.\n\nJesus died.\n\nJesus was buried.\n\nJesus rose again.\n\nIn baptism, the believer identifies with that story.\n\nGoing under the water says:\n\nMy old life is surrendered to Christ.\n\nComing up from the water says:\n\nI am raised to live a new life in Him.\n\nThis does not mean baptism saves by itself.\n\nJesus saves.\n\nBut baptism is the public sign that a person has received His saving grace and is choosing to belong to Him.\n\nIt is a visible sermon.\n\nA death.\nA burial.\nA resurrection.\nA new beginning."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"baptismMeaningPattern","title":"Buried and Raised With Christ","caption":"Baptism is a public sign that the old life is buried and new life begins in Christ.","highlightedIndices":[0,1,2,3]},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"Buried and Raised","references":[{"reference":"Romans 6:1-11"},{"reference":"Colossians 2:12"},{"reference":"Galatians 2:20"},{"reference":"2 Corinthians 5:17"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Baptism identifies us with Jesus' death, burial, and resurrection. The old life is surrendered, and new life begins in Christ."}],"reflectPrompt":"What part of the old life do you sense God calling you to surrender to Jesus?\n\nThink about old patterns, guilt, fear, hidden sin, self-rule, shame, unhealthy relationships, or anything that keeps pulling you away from Christ.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, think about baptism as more than a symbol.\n\nThink of it as a public yes to a new life.\n\nAsk yourself:\n\n\"What needs to be buried with Christ?\"\n\nThen be honest.\n\nDo not protect the old life.\n\nDo not dress it up.\n\nDo not negotiate with what God is calling you to surrender.\n\nBring it to Jesus and say:\n\n\"Lord, I do not want this to rule me anymore.\"\n\nThen ask Him to raise you into a new way of living.\n\nPrayer:\n\nJesus, thank You for dying, being buried, and rising again for me. Thank You that baptism shows the old life being surrendered and new life beginning in You. Help me stop protecting what needs to be buried. Give me faith to walk in newness of life, with You at the center of my heart, choices, and future. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"Baptism points to death, burial, and resurrection with Christ. The old life is surrendered, and new life begins in Jesus.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"bar-l5-q1","prompt":"According to Romans 6:4, what are believers buried with Christ by?","options":["Baptism","Fear","Popularity","Human pride"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Romans says believers are buried with Christ by baptism into death."},{"id":"bar-l5-q2","prompt":"What does coming up from the water symbolize?","options":["Being raised to walk in newness of life","Returning to the old life","Rejecting Jesus","Avoiding transformation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Romans 6:4 connects baptism with being raised to walk in newness of life."},{"id":"bar-l5-q3","prompt":"According to Colossians 2:12, what are believers in baptism?","options":["Buried with Christ and risen with Him","Separated from Christ forever","Saved by water apart from Jesus","Unchanged by grace"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Colossians says believers are buried with Christ in baptism and risen with Him through faith."},{"id":"bar-l5-q4","prompt":"According to Galatians 2:20, who lives in the believer?","options":["Christ","Fear","The old life as master","Condemnation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Paul says, \"Christ liveth in me,\" showing that the new life is centered in Jesus."},{"id":"bar-l5-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What would it mean for you to walk in newness of life this week?","explanation":"Baptism points to more than leaving the old life behind. It points to a new life lived by faith in Jesus."}]}}
$bar_l5_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'buried-with-christ'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007e';


-- Buried and Raised — Lesson 6: In the Name of God (guided reader JSON).
update public.lessons
set reader_content = $bar_l6_reader$
{"openingThought":"Baptism is not only about going into water.\n\nIt is about belonging.\n\nJesus told His disciples to baptize people in the name of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit.\n\nThat means baptism is not an empty ceremony.\n\nIt is an entrance into a new identity, a new relationship, and a new life shaped by God's love.\n\nTo be baptized in God's name means we are identifying with who He is.\n\nHis character.\nHis authority.\nHis love.\nHis mission.\n\nBaptism says:\n\nI belong to the God who made me, redeemed me, and is making me new.","scriptureRef":"Matthew 28:19-20","scriptureQuote":"Go ye therefore, and teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Ghost.\n\nTeaching them to observe all things whatsoever I have commanded you.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Baptism is commanded in the name of the triune God and connected to a life of discipleship.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"John 17:26","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"And I have declared unto them thy name, and will declare it: that the love wherewith thou hast loved me may be in them, and I in them.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Jesus declared the Father's name so His disciples could know God's character and love.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"1 John 4:8","quote":"He that loveth not knoweth not God; for God is love."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"2 Corinthians 13:14","quote":"The grace of the Lord Jesus Christ, and the love of God, and the communion of the Holy Ghost, be with you all."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Baptism is in the name of God\n\nJesus commanded baptism in the name of the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit.\n\nThis shows that baptism is deeply relational.\n\nWe are not baptized into a vague idea.\n\nWe are baptized into the life, love, authority, and mission of the living God.\n\nBaptism declares that our identity now belongs to Him.","2. God's name reveals His character\n\nIn Scripture, a name often points to character.\n\nJesus said He declared the Father's name.\n\nThat means He revealed what God is like.\n\nWhen we are baptized in God's name, we are identifying with His character.\n\nAnd Scripture tells us God is love.","3. Baptism is connected to discipleship\n\nJesus did not only say to baptize.\n\nHe also said to teach.\n\nBaptism is not the finish line of faith.\n\nIt is the beginning of a life of learning, following, obeying, growing, and becoming more like Jesus.\n\nThe baptized life is a discipleship life.","4. God gives grace, love, and fellowship\n\nSecond Corinthians speaks of the grace of Jesus, the love of God, and the communion of the Holy Spirit.\n\nThis gives us a beautiful picture of the life we enter.\n\nWe are not walking alone.\n\nThe Father loves.\nThe Son gives grace.\nThe Spirit brings fellowship and power.\n\nBaptism places our lives under the care and character of God."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Name","lemma":"ὄνομα","transliteration":"onoma","meaning":"In Scripture, a name can represent identity, character, authority, and relationship.","strongs":"G3686","reference":"Matthew 28:19","note":"Being baptized in God's name means more than having words spoken over us.\n\nIt means identifying with God's character and entering a life under His authority, love, and mission."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"Baptism in the name of the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit shows that salvation is not isolated from relationship.\n\nGod does not merely forgive us and send us away.\n\nHe brings us into His life.\n\nThe Father receives us.\n\nThe Son redeems us.\n\nThe Holy Spirit renews us.\n\nThis means baptism is not only about what we leave behind.\n\nIt is also about who we now belong to.\n\nWe are not defined by the old life.\n\nWe are not defined by shame.\n\nWe are not defined by sin's claim.\n\nWe are named by God.\n\nAnd the God whose name we bear is love."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"studyVisual","title":"Baptized in the name","accent":"stone","description":"Baptism declares who we belong to and whose character is shaping us.","payload":{"variant":"sequence","steps":[{"label":"Father","subtitle":"love, belonging, care"},{"label":"Son","subtitle":"grace, redemption, forgiveness"},{"label":"Holy Spirit","subtitle":"renewal, power, fellowship"}]}},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"In the Name of God","references":[{"reference":"Matthew 28:19-20"},{"reference":"John 17:26"},{"reference":"1 John 4:8"},{"reference":"2 Corinthians 13:14"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Baptism in God's name means belonging to the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit and entering a life shaped by God's love."}],"reflectPrompt":"What does it mean to you that baptism is not only about leaving the old life, but also about belonging to God?\n\nThink about identity, shame, purpose, discipleship, and what it means to live under the name and character of God.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, practice living as someone who belongs to God.\n\nWhen shame speaks, answer:\n\n\"I belong to the God who loves me.\"\n\nWhen fear speaks, answer:\n\n\"I belong to the God who redeems me.\"\n\nWhen temptation speaks, answer:\n\n\"I belong to the God who is making me new.\"\n\nThen take one action that reflects God's name.\n\nTell the truth.\nShow mercy.\nForgive.\nServe.\nPray.\nChoose purity.\nKeep your word.\nEncourage someone.\n\nIf you bear God's name, let your life begin to reflect His character.\n\nPrayer:\n\nFather, thank You for calling me into belonging. Jesus, thank You for redeeming me by grace. Holy Spirit, thank You for renewing and guiding me. Help me understand baptism as a life lived in Your name. Shape my identity, choices, and relationships by Your love. Teach me to follow You as a true disciple. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"Baptism in God's name means belonging to the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit. It marks the beginning of a life shaped by God's love and mission.","lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"bar-l6-q1","prompt":"According to Matthew 28:19, in whose name did Jesus command baptism?","options":["The Father, the Son, and the Holy Ghost","Moses only","The disciples only","Human tradition only"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus commanded His disciples to baptize in the name of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Ghost."},{"id":"bar-l6-q2","prompt":"What does \"name\" often represent in Scripture?","options":["Identity, character, authority, and relationship","Random sound only","Human popularity","A meaningless label"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"In Scripture, a name often points to identity, character, authority, and relationship."},{"id":"bar-l6-q3","prompt":"According to 1 John 4:8, what is God?","options":["Love","Fear","Confusion","Selfishness"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"First John says God is love. Baptism in God's name means identifying with the God whose character is love."},{"id":"bar-l6-q4","prompt":"What does Matthew 28:20 connect with baptism?","options":["Teaching and discipleship","Spiritual laziness","Ignoring Jesus' commands","Rejecting growth"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus said to baptize and teach people to observe all things He commanded, showing that baptism begins a life of discipleship."},{"id":"bar-l6-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What is one way your life can better reflect the name and character of God this week?","explanation":"Baptism declares belonging to God. The baptized life grows in reflecting His love, grace, truth, and mission."}]}}
$bar_l6_reader$::jsonb
where slug = 'in-the-name-of-god'
  and study_series_id = 'e4000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000007e';


-- Buried and Raised — Lesson 7: A Public Yes to Jesus (guided reader JSON).
update public.lessons
set reader_content = $bar_l7_reader$
{"openingThought":"Baptism is personal, but it is not meant to stay hidden.\n\nIt is a public yes to Jesus.\n\nA person may believe quietly at first.\n\nThey may wrestle privately.\nThey may pray privately.\nThey may repent privately.\nThey may sense God drawing them privately.\n\nBut baptism brings that decision into the open.\n\nIt says:\n\nI belong to Jesus.\n\nI am turning from the old life.\n\nI am choosing to follow Him.\n\nI am not ashamed to identify with His death, burial, and resurrection.\n\nBaptism is not the finish line.\n\nIt is the beginning of a visible life with Christ.","scriptureRef":"Acts 8:36-38","scriptureQuote":"And as they went on their way, they came unto a certain water: and the eunuch said, See, here is water; what doth hinder me to be baptized?\n\nAnd he answered and said, I believe that Jesus Christ is the Son of God.\n\nAnd he commanded the chariot to stand still: and they went down both into the water... and he baptized him.","scriptureAfterPrimary":"Faith came first. Baptism followed as a visible response to the gospel.","scriptureSecondaryRef":"Acts 22:16","scriptureSecondaryQuote":"And now why tarriest thou? arise, and be baptized, and wash away thy sins, calling on the name of the Lord.","scriptureAfterSecondary":"Baptism is connected with calling on the name of the Lord and receiving His cleansing grace.","scriptureSupplementBlocks":[{"type":"scripture","reference":"Matthew 28:19-20","quote":"Go ye therefore, and teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Ghost.\n\nTeaching them to observe all things whatsoever I have commanded you."},{"type":"scripture","reference":"Romans 6:4","quote":"Even so we also should walk in newness of life."}],"teachingPoints":["1. Baptism follows personal faith\n\nIn Acts 8, the Ethiopian heard the message of Jesus and responded with faith.\n\nHe asked, \"What doth hinder me to be baptized?\"\n\nHis baptism was not empty tradition.\n\nIt was a personal response to the gospel.\n\nBaptism matters most when it comes from a heart that believes and is ready to follow Jesus.","2. Baptism is a public step\n\nThe Ethiopian did not keep his response hidden.\n\nHe stopped the chariot and went down into the water.\n\nBaptism takes an inward decision and makes it visible.\n\nIt is not about showing off.\n\nIt is about confessing openly that Jesus is Lord and that the old life is being left behind.","3. Baptism calls on the name of the Lord\n\nActs 22 connects baptism with calling on the name of the Lord.\n\nThis means baptism is not merely joining a religious group.\n\nIt is a response to Jesus Himself.\n\nThe person being baptized is trusting Christ, receiving His cleansing grace, and publicly identifying with His name.","4. Baptism begins a life of discipleship\n\nJesus connected baptism with teaching people to observe His commands.\n\nThat means baptism is not the end of growth.\n\nIt is the beginning of a lifelong walk.\n\nAfter baptism, the believer continues learning, surrendering, growing, obeying, serving, and walking in newness of life."],"wordStudyBeforeStudyInsight":true,"wordStudyStepTitle":"Word Insight","wordStudyStepIntro":"","wordStudy":{"surface":"Disciple","lemma":"μαθητής","transliteration":"mathetes","meaning":"A learner, follower, or apprentice.","strongs":"G3101","reference":"Matthew 28:19","note":"A disciple is not someone who merely knows facts about Jesus.\n\nA disciple follows Jesus, learns from Him, and grows into a life shaped by His teaching, character, and mission.\n\nBaptism marks the public beginning of that discipleship journey."},"studyInsight":{"title":"Study Insight","placement":"after_teaching","variant":"christ_connection","body":"Buried and Raised has shown the meaning of baptism from the inside out.\n\nGod loves us with everlasting love.\n\nJesus calls us to be born again.\n\nGod leads us by love, not condemnation.\n\nNew birth involves water and the Spirit.\n\nBaptism identifies us with Christ's death, burial, and resurrection.\n\nBaptism in God's name means belonging to the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit.\n\nNow we see baptism as a public yes to Jesus.\n\nThis keeps baptism balanced.\n\nIt is not a magic ritual.\n\nIt is not a way to earn salvation.\n\nIt is not religious pressure.\n\nIt is not the end of the Christian life.\n\nBaptism is the visible confession of a heart responding to Jesus.\n\nIt says:\n\nI believe.\nI repent.\nI belong.\nI surrender.\nI am beginning a new life in Christ."},"studyInsightBlocks":[{"type":"baptismMeaningPattern","title":"A Public Yes to Jesus","caption":"Baptism is a public sign that the old life is buried and new life begins in Christ.","highlightedIndices":[0,1,2,3,4],"stages":[{"label":"Old life","subtitle":"surrendered to Jesus"},{"label":"Going under the water","subtitle":"buried with Christ"},{"label":"Coming up from the water","subtitle":"raised to new life"},{"label":"New life","subtitle":"lived by faith through the Spirit"},{"label":"Public meaning","subtitle":"I belong to Jesus and choose to follow Him"}]},{"type":"scriptureChain","title":"A Public Yes","references":[{"reference":"Acts 8:35-38"},{"reference":"Acts 22:16"},{"reference":"Matthew 28:19-20"},{"reference":"Romans 6:4"}]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Takeaway: Baptism is a personal and public response to Jesus, marking the beginning of a new life of discipleship."}],"reflectPrompt":"What does it mean for you to publicly say yes to Jesus?\n\nThink about faith, repentance, surrender, identity, baptism, discipleship, and whether there is anything holding you back from fully following Him.","lifeApplicationStepTitle":"Life Application and Prayer","lifeApplication":"This week, honestly consider your response to Jesus.\n\nAsk yourself:\n\n\"Have I personally said yes to Him?\"\n\n\"Have I surrendered the old life to Him?\"\n\n\"Have I been baptized as a public response to His grace?\"\n\n\"Am I walking as His disciple now?\"\n\nDo not answer with pressure.\n\nAnswer with honesty.\n\nIf you have not been baptized, pray about taking that step.\n\nIf you have been baptized, return to what it means.\n\nThe old life is buried.\n\nNew life has begun.\n\nNow walk with Jesus daily.\n\nPrayer:\n\nJesus, thank You for calling me into new life. Thank You for loving me, drawing me, forgiving me, and inviting me to follow You. Help me respond with honest faith, not fear or pressure. If baptism is the step I need to take, give me clarity and courage. If I have already been baptized, help me live faithfully from that public yes. Teach me to walk in newness of life with You. Amen.","prayer":"","completionSummary":"You have completed Buried and Raised. Baptism is a public yes to Jesus, showing that the old life is buried and new life has begun through faith in Christ.","seriesCompleteScreen":{"title":"Study Complete","message":"You have completed Buried and Raised. Baptism is a public yes to Jesus, showing that the old life is buried and new life has begun through faith in Christ.","primaryCta":{"label":"Back to Foundations Path","href":"/paths/foundations"},"secondaryCta":{"label":"Review this study","href":"/study-library/buried-and-raised"}},"lessonQuiz":{"eyebrow":"CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING","title":"Quick Review","description":"Check your understanding before moving on.","settings":{"quizRequired":false,"passingScore":70,"allowRetake":true},"questions":[{"id":"bar-l7-q1","prompt":"In Acts 8, what did the Ethiopian ask when he saw water?","options":["\"What doth hinder me to be baptized?\"","\"Why should I avoid Jesus?\"","\"Can I follow God without faith?\"","\"Does baptism mean nothing?\""],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"After hearing the message of Jesus, the Ethiopian asked what prevented him from being baptized."},{"id":"bar-l7-q2","prompt":"What did the Ethiopian confess before baptism?","options":["\"I believe that Jesus Christ is the Son of God\"","\"I do not believe\"","\"I want to stay the same\"","\"The old life is better\""],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"His confession showed personal faith in Jesus before baptism."},{"id":"bar-l7-q3","prompt":"According to Acts 22:16, baptism is connected with calling on whose name?","options":["The name of the Lord","The name of Caesar","The name of tradition only","The name of self"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Acts 22:16 connects baptism with calling on the name of the Lord."},{"id":"bar-l7-q4","prompt":"According to Matthew 28:19-20, what is connected with baptism?","options":["Teaching and discipleship","Hiding faith forever","Ignoring Jesus' commands","Ending spiritual growth"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jesus commanded His followers to baptize and teach, showing that baptism begins a life of discipleship."},{"id":"bar-l7-q5","kind":"reflection","prompt":"What is one step you need to take to live more fully from your yes to Jesus?","explanation":"Baptism is a public beginning, but discipleship continues daily as we walk in newness of life with Christ."}]}}
